/* Year 7 reading: 200 passages for a UK Year 7 reader (age 11–12), built from tools/readings by tools/build_readings.js.
   All passages are original except the poems marked with an author, which are out of copyright. Each has a glossary and six questions
   covering Key Stage 3 reading skills: retrieval, inference, language, structure, summary and evaluation. */
"use strict";
const READINGS=[{"id":"y001","title":"The New Boy at Ashdown High","kind":"Story","theme":"city","text":["On the first morning of Year 7, Ashdown High looked enormous. Corridors branched off in every direction like the roots of an old tree, and every one of them seemed to be full of people who knew exactly where they were going. Kofi Mensah did not.","He had studied his timetable on the bus until the paper went soft at the edges. Period one: Science, Lab 4. The trouble was that there appeared to be no Lab 4. There was a Lab 3, and a door marked Lab 5, and between them a cupboard full of mops.","“Lost?” asked a voice. It belonged to a girl with a fringe that almost covered her eyes and a rucksack covered in badges. “Everyone gets lost looking for Lab 4. It’s upstairs, because the people who built this school had a sense of humour.”","Her name was Ines, and she was in his form. She led him up a staircase he had walked past three times, chatting the whole way about the canteen (“avoid the curry on Thursdays”), the teachers (“Mr Okafor pretends to be strict, but he laughs at his own jokes”) and the library (“the best place in the building, honestly”).","By lunchtime Kofi had been lost twice more, had dropped his calculator down a flight of stairs and had accidentally sat in the Year 11 area. Each time, something small rescued him: a sign he had not noticed, a dinner lady who pointed, a Year 11 boy who simply said, “Wrong table, mate,” without making a fuss.","At the end of the day he waited for the bus beside Ines. “So,” she said, “what’s the verdict?”","Kofi thought about it. The school was still enormous. He still did not know where the music block was, and he suspected he never would. But the building no longer felt like a maze designed to trap him. It felt more like a map he had only just started to read.","“I think,” he said slowly, “I’ll be able to find Lab 4 tomorrow.”","“That,” said Ines, “is how it starts.”"],"glossary":{"enormous":"Extremely large.","branched":"Split off in different directions.","timetable":"A plan of what lessons happen when.","canteen":"A place in a school where meals are served.","accidentally":"By mistake; without meaning to.","verdict":"A decision or opinion after thinking something over.","suspected":"Thought that something was probably true.","maze":"A confusing network of paths where it is easy to get lost."},"questions":[{"q":"Why could Kofi not find Lab 4 at first?","o":["It was locked","It was upstairs, not next to Labs 3 and 5","It had been turned into a cupboard","His timetable was wrong"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Ines tells him, “It’s upstairs.”"},{"q":"The corridors “branched off in every direction like the roots of an old tree”. What does this simile suggest?","o":["The school was made of wood","The corridors were confusing and spread out everywhere","The school was very old and falling down","The corridors were short and simple"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Roots spread out in many tangled directions, just like the confusing corridors."},{"q":"What does Ines’s description of Mr Okafor suggest about her?","o":["She is scared of teachers","She has already learned a lot about the school and its people","She wants Kofi to dislike the teachers","She is a teacher herself"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"She knows details about teachers, food and places, so she already understands the school well."},{"q":"What links the three problems Kofi has at lunchtime?","o":["Each one is solved by something or someone small and kind","Each one makes him want to leave","Each one happens in the library","Each one is caused by Ines"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"“Each time, something small rescued him.”"},{"q":"How have Kofi’s feelings changed by the end of the day?","o":["He feels the school is a trap","He is still completely lost and miserable","He feels he is beginning to understand the school","He wants to change schools"],"a":2,"skill":"Summary","why":"The school now “felt more like a map he had only just started to read”."},{"q":"Why does the writer end with Ines saying, “That is how it starts”?","o":["To show Kofi will get lost again","To suggest that small steps lead to feeling at home","To show Ines is bored","To introduce a new character"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Finding Lab 4 is a small first step; Ines suggests settling in grows from moments like this."}]},{"id":"y002","title":"How Volcanoes Shape Our Planet","kind":"Science","theme":"volcano","text":["Deep beneath your feet, about thirty kilometres down, the solid rock of the Earth’s crust gives way to the mantle. Although the mantle is mostly solid, it is so hot that in places rock melts to form magma. Because magma is lighter than the rock around it, it rises slowly upwards, collecting in underground chambers. When the pressure becomes too great, it forces its way to the surface. This is a volcanic eruption.","Most volcanoes are found where the Earth’s tectonic plates meet. The crust is broken into about a dozen enormous plates that move a few centimetres each year – roughly as fast as your fingernails grow. Where two plates pull apart, as they do in Iceland, magma wells up to fill the gap. Where one plate is forced beneath another, as around the Pacific Ocean, the sinking rock melts and feeds chains of explosive volcanoes. This horseshoe of volcanoes is known as the Ring of Fire.","Not all eruptions are alike. Runny, hot magma lets gas escape easily, so it tends to pour out gently as rivers of lava, like those seen in Hawaii. Thick, sticky magma traps gas inside, and the pressure builds until the volcano explodes, hurling ash and rock high into the sky. In 1980, Mount St Helens in the USA blew away the top of its own summit in just such an explosion.","Volcanoes can be deadly. Fast-moving clouds of hot gas and ash, called pyroclastic flows, can sweep down a mountainside at over 100 kilometres per hour. In AD 79, a flow from Mount Vesuvius buried the Roman town of Pompeii, preserving it so well that archaeologists can still walk its streets today.","Yet volcanoes also create. Over millions of years, eruptions have built islands, including Iceland and the Hawaiian chain. Volcanic ash breaks down into some of the most fertile soil on Earth, which is why farms cling to the slopes of active volcanoes in Italy and Indonesia. Heat from underground is even used to generate electricity: in Iceland, most homes are warmed by geothermal energy.","Scientists called volcanologists watch active volcanoes closely, measuring tiny earthquakes, changes in the shape of the ground and the gases escaping from vents. They cannot yet predict eruptions exactly, but their warnings have saved many thousands of lives."],"glossary":{"crust":"The thin, solid outer layer of the Earth.","mantle":"The thick layer of hot rock beneath the Earth’s crust.","magma":"Melted rock underground.","tectonic":"To do with the huge moving plates that make up the Earth’s surface.","pyroclastic":"Made of hot ash, gas and rock thrown out by a volcano.","archaeologists":"People who study the past by digging up remains.","fertile":"Able to grow lots of healthy plants.","geothermal":"Using heat from inside the Earth.","volcanologists":"Scientists who study volcanoes."},"questions":[{"q":"Why does magma rise towards the surface?","o":["It is pushed by the wind","It is lighter than the rock around it","It is pulled by the Moon","It is heavier than the rock around it"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Because magma is lighter than the rock around it, it rises slowly upwards.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer compare plate movement to fingernails growing?","o":["To show plates are made of the same material","To help readers imagine how slowly the plates move","To show plates move very quickly","To explain why volcanoes erupt"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Fingernail growth is a familiar, very slow speed, so readers can picture it."},{"q":"According to the text, which kind of magma is most likely to cause an explosion?","o":["Runny, hot magma","Thick, sticky magma","Cold magma","Magma with no gas"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Thick, sticky magma “traps gas inside, and the pressure builds until the volcano explodes”."},{"q":"Why might people choose to farm on the slopes of an active volcano?","o":["The land is cheap because nobody wants it","Volcanic ash makes very fertile soil","It is always warm there","Volcanoes keep pests away"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Ash “breaks down into some of the most fertile soil on Earth”."},{"q":"How is the fifth paragraph different from the fourth?","o":["It moves from the dangers of volcanoes to their benefits","It repeats the same information","It tells a story about one person","It explains how magma forms"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"Paragraph four describes deadly flows; paragraph five begins “Yet volcanoes also create”."},{"q":"Which statement best sums up the writer’s view of volcanoes?","o":["They are only dangerous","They are both destructive and creative forces","They are no longer a problem","They are easy to predict"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"The text shows they can be deadly but also build land, soil and energy."}]},{"id":"y003","title":"The Tyger","kind":"Poem","theme":"jungle","author":"William Blake (1794)","text":["Tyger Tyger, burning bright,\nIn the forests of the night;\nWhat immortal hand or eye,\nCould frame thy fearful symmetry?","In what distant deeps or skies.\nBurnt the fire of thine eyes?\nOn what wings dare he aspire?\nWhat the hand, dare seize the fire?","And what shoulder, & what art,\nCould twist the sinews of thy heart?\nAnd when thy heart began to beat,\nWhat dread hand? & what dread feet?","What the hammer? what the chain,\nIn what furnace was thy brain?\nWhat the anvil? what dread grasp,\nDare its deadly terrors clasp!","When the stars threw down their spears\nAnd water’d heaven with their tears:\nDid he smile his work to see?\nDid he who made the Lamb make thee?","Tyger Tyger burning bright,\nIn the forests of the night:\nWhat immortal hand or eye,\nDare frame thy fearful symmetry?"],"glossary":{"immortal":"Living for ever; not able to die.","symmetry":"Being the same on both sides; a perfect, balanced shape.","thy":"An old word for ‘your’.","thine":"An old word for ‘your’, used before a vowel.","aspire":"To rise up or aim high.","sinews":"Strong cords that join muscles to bones.","dread":"Causing great fear.","furnace":"A very hot fire for melting metal.","anvil":"A heavy iron block on which a blacksmith hammers metal.","thee":"An old word for ‘you’."},"questions":[{"q":"Who is the speaker asking questions to?","o":["A lamb","The tiger","A blacksmith","The stars"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The poem speaks directly to the “Tyger” using ‘thy’ and ‘thee’."},{"q":"The tiger is “burning bright”. What does this image suggest?","o":["The tiger is on fire","The tiger’s orange stripes and eyes glow fiercely in the dark","The tiger is very hot and tired","The forest is burning down"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"‘Burning bright’ makes the tiger seem to glow like fire against the dark forest."},{"q":"Stanza four mentions a hammer, chain, furnace and anvil. What is the tiger’s maker being compared to?","o":["A farmer","A blacksmith","A painter","A hunter"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"These are a blacksmith’s tools, as if the tiger was forged from metal."},{"q":"Why is it surprising to the speaker that the same maker might have made the Lamb and the tiger?","o":["Lambs and tigers live in different countries","The lamb is gentle and the tiger is fierce and frightening","Lambs are bigger than tigers","Tigers eat grass"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"The speaker wonders how one creator could make both something gentle and something terrifying."},{"q":"What is the effect of the last stanza repeating the first, but with “Dare” instead of “Could”?","o":["It shows the poet made a mistake","It makes the question stronger: it is not just possible but daring to create the tiger","It makes the poem funnier","It changes the subject to the lamb"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"Changing ‘Could’ to ‘Dare’ suggests creating such a creature took great boldness."},{"q":"Which word best describes the speaker’s attitude towards the tiger?","o":["Bored","Amazed and fearful","Angry","Sorry for it"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Questions about its “fearful symmetry” and “deadly terrors” show awe and fear."}]},{"id":"y004","title":"Ada Lovelace: The First Computer Programmer","kind":"Biography","theme":"clockwork","text":["In 1815, a baby girl was born to one of the most famous men in Britain. Her father was the poet Lord Byron, celebrated across Europe for his romantic verse and scandalous life. Yet within weeks of her birth, her parents separated, and Augusta Ada Byron never knew her father. Her mother, Annabella, determined that her daughter would not inherit what she saw as Byron’s dangerous imagination, gave Ada a strict education in mathematics and science.","The plan only half worked. Ada became a gifted mathematician, but she never lost her imagination. As a girl she designed a flying machine, carefully studying birds to work out the right shape for its wings. She later described her own way of thinking as “poetical science”.","When she was seventeen, Ada met Charles Babbage, an inventor who had designed a mechanical calculator called the Difference Engine. Babbage was working on something even more ambitious: the Analytical Engine, a machine that could be given instructions on punched cards, much like the cards used to control patterns on weaving looms. Ada was fascinated, and the two became friends and correspondents.","In 1843, Ada translated an article about the Analytical Engine from French into English. She added her own notes, which ended up three times longer than the article itself. In them she wrote out a step-by-step method for the machine to calculate a complicated series of numbers called Bernoulli numbers. Many historians regard this as the first computer program ever published.","Even more remarkable was her vision. Babbage saw his engine as a calculator. Ada realised that if numbers could stand for other things – musical notes, letters or symbols – the machine could do far more than arithmetic. It might, she suggested, one day compose music. More than a century before the first electronic computers, she had glimpsed what they could become.","The Analytical Engine was never completed, and Ada died in 1852, aged only thirty-six. For many years her work was almost forgotten. Today, however, the second Tuesday of October is celebrated as Ada Lovelace Day, honouring women in science, technology, engineering and mathematics."],"glossary":{"scandalous":"Causing shock and disapproval.","inherit":"To receive qualities or things from a parent.","mechanical":"Worked by machinery or moving parts.","ambitious":"Needing great effort; aiming very high.","correspondents":"People who write letters to each other.","remarkable":"Unusual and worth noticing.","vision":"The ability to imagine the future.","arithmetic":"Adding, subtracting, multiplying and dividing numbers.","glimpsed":"Saw something briefly."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did Ada’s mother give her a strict education in maths and science?","o":["She wanted Ada to become rich","She did not want Ada to inherit Byron’s ‘dangerous imagination’","Ada asked to study maths","Her father insisted on it"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Annabella was determined Ada “would not inherit … Byron’s dangerous imagination”."},{"q":"Why does the writer say “The plan only half worked”?","o":["Ada failed her exams","Ada became a mathematician but kept her imagination","Ada became a poet instead","Ada never learned maths"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"She was gifted at maths, but she “never lost her imagination”."},{"q":"What does the phrase “poetical science” suggest about Ada’s way of thinking?","o":["She wrote poems about science","She combined imagination with logical thinking","She disliked science","She only cared about poetry"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"It joins poetry (imagination) with science (logic)."},{"q":"Why is Ada often called the first computer programmer?","o":["She built the first computer","She published step-by-step instructions for a machine to calculate numbers","She invented punched cards","She translated a French book"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Her notes included “a step-by-step method for the machine to calculate … Bernoulli numbers”."},{"q":"How was Ada’s idea about the Analytical Engine different from Babbage’s?","o":["She thought it would never work","She saw it could work with symbols like music and letters, not just numbers","She thought it should be powered by steam","She wanted it to be smaller"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Babbage saw a calculator; Ada realised numbers could stand for other things."},{"q":"Why does the writer end with Ada Lovelace Day?","o":["To show she is now properly recognised and still inspires people","To give the date of her birthday","To explain how computers work","To show her work was forgotten"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"After saying her work was almost forgotten, the ending shows she is honoured today."}]},{"id":"y005","title":"Stranded on the Causeway","kind":"Adventure","theme":"sea","text":["The sign at the start of the causeway was impossible to miss. DANGER, it read, in letters as tall as a hand. DO NOT CROSS WHEN WATER COVERS THE ROAD. Beneath it, a printed table listed the safe crossing times for every day of the month.","“We’ve got ages,” said Callum, waving his phone. “High tide’s not till four.”","His sister Freya checked the table. “It says safe until half past two. It’s quarter past now.”","“Fifteen minutes is loads. It’s only a mile.”","It was only a mile, but it was a mile of wet sand and seaweed-slick road with nothing on either side but the flat, gleaming bay. They had walked to the island that morning to see the priory ruins, and they had stayed too long, climbing walls and eating crisps in the sunshine. Now the sky over the sea had turned the colour of slate.","They were halfway across when Freya noticed the first thin sheet of water sliding across the tarmac ahead of them. It did not crash in like a wave. It simply arrived, silently, as if the sea had decided to take the road back.","“Callum.” Her voice came out smaller than she meant it to.","He stopped. The water was already over his trainers. Behind them, the island looked suddenly very far away, and in front of them the mainland was no closer. Freya remembered something from the sign: in an emergency, use the refuge box.","It stood a few hundred metres ahead – a wooden hut raised high on stilts, with a ladder leading up to it. They waded towards it, the water rising to their ankles, then their shins, cold enough to make them gasp. Freya’s legs shook as she climbed the ladder. Callum came after her, white-faced and silent.","From the refuge they called the coastguard, and then they watched. Within half an hour the road had vanished entirely, and cars that had tried to race the tide sat abandoned, water lapping at their doors. Six hours later, when the sea finally drew back, a lifeboat crew helped them down.","“Tide tables,” said the lifeboat captain kindly, “aren’t suggestions.”","Callum, for once, said nothing at all."],"glossary":{"causeway":"A raised road across water or wet ground, sometimes covered by the tide.","priory":"A small monastery, a place where monks or nuns lived.","slate":"A dark grey rock.","tarmac":"The hard black surface of a road.","refuge":"A place of safety.","stilts":"Tall posts that hold something up off the ground.","waded":"Walked through water.","coastguard":"The service that keeps people safe at sea and on the coast.","abandoned":"Left behind and deserted."},"questions":[{"q":"What time did the sign say it was safe to cross until?","o":["Quarter past two","Half past two","Four o’clock","Six o’clock"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Freya reads that it is “safe until half past two”."},{"q":"What mistake does Callum make at the start?","o":["He reads the wrong sign","He relies on the time of high tide instead of the safe crossing times","He forgets his phone","He takes the wrong road"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He says high tide is not until four, ignoring that the road floods long before that."},{"q":"The sea arrived “silently, as if the sea had decided to take the road back”. What effect does this have?","o":["It makes the sea seem calm and safe","It makes the danger feel quiet, sneaky and unstoppable","It shows the sea is noisy","It makes the scene funny"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"The silent, deliberate personification makes the threat creepier because it gives no warning."},{"q":"How does the writer build tension when the children wade to the refuge?","o":["By describing the water rising step by step from ankles to shins","By using long, calm descriptions of the view","By making Callum tell jokes","By jumping forward to the next day"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"The water rises “to their ankles, then their shins”, showing the danger growing."},{"q":"What does Callum’s silence at the end suggest?","o":["He is asleep","He has learned his lesson and feels embarrassed","He is angry with Freya","He did not hear the captain"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"‘For once’ he said nothing, suggesting he realises he was wrong."},{"q":"What is the main message of the story?","o":["Islands are boring","Never visit the seaside","Safety warnings should be taken seriously","Phones are always right"],"a":2,"skill":"Summary","why":"The captain’s line “Tide tables aren’t suggestions” sums up the lesson."}]},{"id":"y006","title":"Why We Should Ban Phones from School Lunchtimes","kind":"Persuasive","theme":"computer","text":["Walk into almost any school canteen at lunchtime and you will see the same scene: rows of pupils sitting side by side, heads bowed, faces lit by the glow of a screen. They are together, yet completely alone. It is time for schools to make lunchtime phone-free.","First, consider what lunchtime is for. It is the one part of the school day when young people are free to talk, laugh and make friends without a teacher directing them. These conversations matter. They are how we learn to read other people’s feelings, settle disagreements and build the friendships that help us through difficult times. A phone, however entertaining, cannot teach these skills.","Second, our brains need a break. After a morning of lessons, pupils need time to rest their attention before the afternoon begins. Scrolling through endless videos might feel relaxing, but researchers have found that it often leaves people more tired and less able to concentrate. Going outside, playing a game or simply chatting gives the mind a genuine rest.","Some people argue that phones help pupils stay in touch with family or look up useful information. This is true, but neither needs to happen at lunchtime. Parents can contact the school office in an emergency, and pupils can check messages at the end of the day. Others say that a ban is unfair because adults use phones whenever they like. Yet schools already have many rules that adults do not follow – we do not, after all, expect teachers to put their hands up before speaking.","Schools that have tried phone-free lunchtimes report noisier canteens, busier playgrounds and more pupils joining clubs. Teachers notice fewer arguments that start online and spill into the classroom. Pupils themselves often admit, a little reluctantly, that they quite like the change.","Lunchtime should be a time to look up, not down. Put the phones away, and let the conversation begin."],"glossary":{"canteen":"A place in a school where meals are served.","directing":"Telling people what to do.","concentrate":"To give all your attention to something.","genuine":"Real and true.","argue":"To give reasons for an opinion.","emergency":"A serious and sudden situation needing quick action.","reluctantly":"Without really wanting to."},"questions":[{"q":"What scene does the writer describe in the opening paragraph?","o":["Pupils playing football","Pupils sitting together but looking at their phones","Teachers eating lunch","An empty canteen"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Pupils sit “side by side, heads bowed, faces lit by the glow of a screen”."},{"q":"“They are together, yet completely alone.” Which technique is used here?","o":["Alliteration","A contrast between two opposite ideas","A rhetorical question","Onomatopoeia"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"‘Together’ and ‘alone’ are opposites, highlighting the problem."},{"q":"What is the writer’s second main reason for a ban?","o":["Phones are expensive","Pupils’ brains need a proper rest","Phones break easily","Teachers do not like phones"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Second, our brains need a break.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer include the opinions of people who disagree in paragraph four?","o":["To show the writer has changed their mind","To answer objections and make the argument stronger","To fill up space","To prove phones are useful"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"The writer states counter-arguments and then responds to each one."},{"q":"Why does the writer mention teachers not putting their hands up?","o":["To complain about teachers","To show that different rules for adults and pupils are normal","To suggest teachers should put their hands up","To make readers laugh at pupils"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"It shows schools already have rules for pupils that adults don’t follow."},{"q":"How effective is the final sentence?","o":["It is weak because it introduces a new topic","It is memorable because it uses a contrast (up/down) and a direct call to action","It is confusing because it disagrees with the rest","It is boring because it repeats the title"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"“Look up, not down” is a punchy contrast followed by a clear instruction."}]},{"id":"y007","title":"The Great Fire of London","kind":"History","theme":"victorian","text":["In the early hours of Sunday 2 September 1666, a fire broke out in the bakery of Thomas Farriner on Pudding Lane, close to London Bridge. Fires were common in seventeenth-century London, where most houses were built of timber and pitch and crammed together along narrow streets. When the Lord Mayor, Sir Thomas Bludworth, was woken and taken to see the flames, he was unimpressed. According to one account, he claimed that a woman could put it out.","He was badly mistaken. The summer had been long and dry, and a strong wind from the east drove the flames from house to house. Warehouses along the river were packed with oil, tar, coal and brandy, and these exploded into enormous fireballs. Soon the fire was so hot that it could not be approached, and so wide that it created its own winds.","Much of what we know comes from the diary of Samuel Pepys, a naval official. He described people throwing their belongings into boats on the Thames, and pigeons refusing to leave their homes until their wings caught fire. Pepys himself buried his most precious possessions in his garden to protect them – including, famously, a wheel of Parmesan cheese.","Firefighting at the time was basic. People formed lines to pass leather buckets of water, and used long hooks to pull down burning buildings. The most effective method was to create firebreaks by demolishing houses in the fire’s path, leaving a gap it could not cross. At first, people were reluctant to destroy homes that had not yet caught fire. Eventually, King Charles II ordered that firebreaks be made, and gunpowder was used to blow up whole rows of buildings.","By Thursday the wind had dropped and the fire was finally brought under control. It had destroyed more than 13,000 houses, 87 churches and the great medieval St Paul’s Cathedral. Around 70,000 of the city’s 80,000 inhabitants were left homeless. Officially only a handful of deaths were recorded, although historians believe the true number was probably higher.","London was rebuilt with wider streets and buildings of brick and stone. The architect Christopher Wren designed a new St Paul’s Cathedral, and a tall stone column, simply called the Monument, was built near Pudding Lane. It stands 202 feet high – exactly the distance from its base to the spot where the fire began."],"glossary":{"timber":"Wood used for building.","pitch":"A sticky black substance like tar, used to make things waterproof.","crammed":"Squashed tightly together.","warehouses":"Large buildings for storing goods.","naval":"To do with the navy.","firebreaks":"Gaps cleared to stop a fire spreading.","demolishing":"Knocking down a building.","inhabitants":"People who live in a place.","architect":"A person who designs buildings."},"questions":[{"q":"Where did the Great Fire of London start?","o":["St Paul’s Cathedral","A bakery on Pudding Lane","A warehouse on the Thames","Samuel Pepys’s house"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It “broke out in the bakery of Thomas Farriner on Pudding Lane”."},{"q":"Give the main reason the fire spread so quickly.","o":["The fire brigade was on holiday","Dry weather, strong wind and closely packed wooden houses","It was started deliberately in many places","The river had dried up"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"The text mentions the long dry summer, the wind and timber houses crammed together."},{"q":"Why does the writer include the detail about the Lord Mayor’s comment?","o":["To show he was brave","To show how badly people underestimated the fire at first","To explain how the fire started","To make him seem kind"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He thought it was small, but “He was badly mistaken.”"},{"q":"Why is Pepys’s diary important to historians?","o":["It explains how to make cheese","It gives an eyewitness account of the fire","It lists everyone who died","It contains maps of new streets"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Pepys saw the fire himself and described what people did."},{"q":"What was the most effective way of fighting the fire?","o":["Leather buckets","Creating firebreaks by demolishing buildings","Waiting for rain","Using boats on the Thames"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The most effective method was to create firebreaks.”"},{"q":"Why is the Monument exactly 202 feet tall?","o":["It was the tallest building possible","That is the distance from its base to where the fire started","The King chose a lucky number","It matches St Paul’s Cathedral"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It is “exactly the distance from its base to the spot where the fire began”."}]},{"id":"y008","title":"Letter from the Edge of the World","kind":"Letter","theme":"arctic","text":["Halley Research Station, Antarctica\n14 June","Dear Grandad,","Today the sun did not rise. It hasn’t risen for three weeks, and it won’t rise again until August. I knew this would happen – it was in all the training – but knowing something and living through it are completely different. At midday, the sky turns a deep, glowing blue at the horizon, as if the sun is thinking about coming up and then changes its mind.","You asked what it is like here, so I will try to describe it. Imagine the flattest place you have ever seen, then take away every tree, hill, house and road. Cover it all in ice more than a hundred metres thick. That is the Brunt Ice Shelf, which is where our station sits, on legs with giant skis so that it can be moved if the ice cracks. There are fourteen of us here for the winter. We will see nobody else until November.","My job is to look after the instruments that measure the atmosphere. Did you know that the hole in the ozone layer was discovered by scientists working here, in 1985? I think about that every time I climb the ladder to check the sensors. It feels like being part of something important.","The cold is hard to describe. Last week it was minus forty-eight degrees. If you breathe in too fast, the air hurts your chest. We wear three layers of gloves, and you must never touch metal with bare skin, because it will stick instantly. Even so, I go outside whenever I can, because the sky is the most beautiful thing I have ever seen. Two nights ago the aurora australis filled the whole sky – green curtains rippling and folding, so bright I could see my shadow on the snow.","Inside, it is surprisingly cosy. We take turns to cook (I made your shepherd’s pie recipe and it was a big hit), we have a small gym, and on Saturdays we watch films together. I miss trees, though. And rain. And you.","Please give Biscuit a scratch behind the ears for me. I will write again when the post goes – which, admittedly, will not be until the plane comes in November.","All my love,\nMaya"],"glossary":{"horizon":"The line where the land seems to meet the sky.","shelf":"Here, a huge floating sheet of ice joined to the land.","instruments":"Tools for measuring things.","atmosphere":"The layer of gases surrounding the Earth.","ozone":"A gas high in the atmosphere that protects us from the Sun’s harmful rays.","sensors":"Devices that detect or measure something.","aurora":"Bands of coloured light seen in the sky near the poles.","admittedly":"It must be admitted; it is true that."},"questions":[{"q":"Why has the sun not risen for three weeks?","o":["It has been cloudy","It is the polar winter in Antarctica","Maya has been sleeping in the day","There has been a storm"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"It is June, winter in Antarctica, when the sun stays below the horizon for weeks."},{"q":"Why does the station have legs with giant skis?","o":["So it can be moved if the ice cracks","So people can ski to work","To keep it warm","To make it look modern"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It sits “on legs with giant skis so that it can be moved if the ice cracks”."},{"q":"How does Maya feel about her work?","o":["Bored","Proud to be part of something important","Frightened","She wants to quit"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“It feels like being part of something important.”"},{"q":"Which technique does Maya use to help her grandad picture the landscape?","o":["She asks him to imagine a flat place and then removes everything from it","She draws a map","She compares it to the Sahara","She lists temperatures"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"“Imagine the flattest place … then take away every tree, hill, house and road.”"},{"q":"What does the description of the aurora as “green curtains rippling and folding” suggest?","o":["The lights were still and dull","The lights moved gracefully across the sky","The lights were made of cloth","The lights were frightening"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Curtains that ripple and fold suggest soft, flowing movement."},{"q":"What does the final paragraph reveal about how isolated the station is?","o":["Post can only leave when a plane arrives in November","They get letters every day","They can visit the town on weekends","They speak to Grandad on video every night"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"She cannot send the letter until “the plane comes in November”."}]},{"id":"y009","title":"The Science of Sleep","kind":"Non-fiction","theme":"space","text":["You will spend about a third of your life asleep. If you live to eighty, that adds up to roughly twenty-six years with your eyes closed. It might seem like wasted time, but scientists now know that sleep is one of the busiest and most important things your body and brain do.","Sleep is not one single state. Each night, you pass through several cycles, each lasting about ninety minutes. In the early part of a cycle you drift into light sleep, then into deep sleep, when your breathing slows and it is hardest to wake you. Deep sleep is when your body repairs muscles, strengthens your immune system and releases growth hormone – which is one reason teenagers really do need more sleep than adults.","Towards the end of each cycle comes REM sleep, short for ‘rapid eye movement’, because your eyes dart about beneath your eyelids. This is when most vivid dreams happen. During REM sleep your brain is almost as active as when you are awake, sorting through the day’s experiences and deciding what to store as long-term memories. Students who sleep well after learning something new remember it significantly better the next day.","So why do so many teenagers find it hard to get up in the morning? It is not simply laziness. During adolescence, the body’s internal clock shifts later, so teenagers naturally feel sleepy later at night and wake later in the morning. Unfortunately, school still starts early. Add the blue light from phones and tablets, which tricks the brain into thinking it is still daytime, and it is easy to see why many young people are short of sleep.","Experts recommend that eleven- to thirteen-year-olds get between nine and eleven hours each night. Without enough, concentration, mood and even judgement suffer. In one experiment, people kept awake for a full day performed tasks as badly as people who had drunk alcohol.","The good news is that small changes help. Going to bed at a similar time each night, keeping screens out of the bedroom and getting daylight in the morning all help reset the body clock. Sleep, it turns out, is not time off from life. It is what makes the rest of life possible."],"glossary":{"cycles":"Sets of events that repeat in the same order.","immune":"Your immune system is the body’s defence against illness.","hormone":"A chemical made by the body that controls processes such as growth.","vivid":"Very clear and bright.","significantly":"By a large or important amount.","adolescence":"The time when a child is developing into an adult.","internal":"Inside.","judgement":"The ability to make sensible decisions."},"questions":[{"q":"How long does each sleep cycle last?","o":["About thirty minutes","About ninety minutes","About three hours","About eight hours"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Each lasting about ninety minutes.”"},{"q":"What happens to the body during deep sleep?","o":["Most dreams happen","Muscles are repaired and growth hormone is released","The eyes move quickly","The brain stops working"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Deep sleep is when the body “repairs muscles … and releases growth hormone”."},{"q":"Why might sleep help you do well in a test?","o":["You dream about the answers","REM sleep helps store new learning as long-term memories","Sleeping makes tests shorter","Teachers give extra marks"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"During REM sleep the brain decides “what to store as long-term memories”."},{"q":"According to the writer, why do teenagers struggle to get up early?","o":["They are lazy","Their body clock shifts later and screens delay sleep","They eat too much","They exercise too little"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"“It is not simply laziness”: the internal clock shifts later and blue light tricks the brain."},{"q":"Why does the writer open with the fact about twenty-six years of sleep?","o":["To surprise the reader and grab their attention","To prove sleep is a waste of time","To show how old the writer is","To give advice"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"A surprising statistic hooks the reader before the writer explains why sleep matters."},{"q":"What is meant by the final line, “It is what makes the rest of life possible”?","o":["We should sleep all day","Sleep supports everything else we do, such as learning and staying healthy","Life is boring without dreams","We only live while asleep"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"The article shows sleep is essential for memory, growth, mood and concentration."}]},{"id":"y010","title":"Theseus and the Minotaur","kind":"Myth","theme":"desert","text":["Long ago, King Minos ruled the island of Crete from a palace so vast that strangers wandered its halls for days. Beneath that palace lay something far worse: the Labyrinth, a maze of tunnels built by the inventor Daedalus so cleverly that nobody who entered could ever find the way out. At its centre lived the Minotaur, a monster with the body of a man and the head of a bull.","Years before, Minos’s son had been killed in Athens. As punishment, Minos demanded a terrible price. Every nine years, Athens had to send seven young men and seven young women to Crete, to be fed to the Minotaur.","When the third payment fell due, the king of Athens, Aegeus, watched in despair as the victims were chosen. Then his son, Theseus, stepped forward. “Let me go as one of the fourteen,” he said. “I will kill the Minotaur and end this.” Aegeus begged him not to, but Theseus would not be moved. At last the old king made him promise one thing. The ship that carried the victims always sailed with black sails. If Theseus survived, he must hoist white sails on the way home, so that his father would know the news as soon as the ship appeared on the horizon.","In Crete, Minos’s daughter Ariadne saw Theseus arrive, and was struck by his courage. Secretly, she went to him with two gifts: a sword, and a ball of golden thread. “Tie one end at the entrance,” she whispered, “and unwind it as you go. Then follow it back.”","That night Theseus crept into the Labyrinth, letting the thread run through his fingers. Deeper and deeper he went, through passages that twisted back on themselves, until he heard heavy breathing in the dark. The Minotaur charged. They fought for what felt like hours, until at last Theseus drove the sword home. Then, trembling, he gathered up the thread and followed it out into the starlight.","Theseus escaped Crete with the other Athenians and with Ariadne. But in the joy of victory, he forgot his promise. The ship sailed home under black sails. On the cliffs of Athens, Aegeus saw them, believed his son was dead, and in his grief threw himself into the sea. That sea, the Greeks say, is still called the Aegean in his memory."],"glossary":{"labyrinth":"A complicated maze.","despair":"The feeling of having lost all hope.","victims":"People who are harmed or killed.","hoist":"To raise or lift something, often with ropes.","horizon":"The line where the sea or land seems to meet the sky.","courage":"Bravery.","trembling":"Shaking slightly.","grief":"Deep sadness, especially when someone dies."},"questions":[{"q":"Who built the Labyrinth?","o":["King Minos","Daedalus","Theseus","Ariadne"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It was “built by the inventor Daedalus”."},{"q":"Why did Athens have to send young people to Crete?","o":["As punishment for the death of Minos’s son","To work in the palace","To learn from Daedalus","To marry the princes of Crete"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Minos’s son “had been killed in Athens. As punishment, Minos demanded a terrible price.”"},{"q":"What does Theseus’s decision to volunteer show about his character?","o":["He is selfish","He is brave and wants to protect others","He is foolish and lazy","He wants to visit Crete on holiday"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He offers to go and risk his life to end the killings."},{"q":"Why was Ariadne’s thread so important?","o":["It could be used as a weapon","It let Theseus find his way back out of the maze","It was made of gold and very valuable","It tied up the Minotaur"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Nobody could find the way out, but the thread showed the path back."},{"q":"How does the writer make the fight in the Labyrinth tense?","o":["By describing heavy breathing in the dark and a long struggle","By using lots of funny dialogue","By describing the weather in Athens","By listing facts about bulls"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Unseen “heavy breathing in the dark” and fighting “for what felt like hours” build suspense."},{"q":"What makes the ending tragic?","o":["The Minotaur escapes","Theseus’s forgetfulness causes his father’s death","Ariadne is left in Crete","The ship sinks"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"He forgot to change the sails, so Aegeus thought he was dead and threw himself into the sea."}]},{"id":"y011","title":"The Water Thief","kind":"Fantasy","theme":"desert","text":["In the village of Sarn, the well was the only thing anyone talked about. For three summers the rain had stayed away, and the water in the well had sunk lower and lower, until buckets came up holding only a cupful of brown, gritty liquid. The elders measured it out each morning: one jug per family, no more.","Then the water started disappearing at night.","Each dawn the elders lowered the measuring rope and found the level lower than it should have been. Someone was stealing. Suspicion spread through the village faster than any drought. Neighbours who had shared bread for forty years now refused to look at each other.","Nia, who was twelve and could not sleep for thirst anyway, decided to find the thief herself. She hid behind the stone trough with a blanket over her head and waited. The moon rose, fat and white. The village was silent.","Just after midnight, something moved at the lip of the well. It was not a person. It was a creature the size of a cat, with a body as smooth and clear as glass. Moonlight shone straight through it. As Nia watched, it slipped down the rope like a drop of rain and returned a minute later, its glassy body now full and shimmering with water.","Nia followed it out of the village, across the cracked fields and up the dry riverbed into the hills. It led her to a hollow in the rocks she had never seen before. There, in the shade, lay a tiny pool, and around it – Nia caught her breath – green shoots were pushing up through the dust. Ferns. Moss. A single young willow, no taller than her knee.","The creature emptied itself carefully over the willow’s roots, then turned to look at her with no eyes at all.","Nia understood. Something was trying to bring the river back.","The next morning she told the elders, and that night half the village followed her to the hollow. They argued, of course. Some wanted to block the well. But Nia’s grandmother knelt by the little willow for a long time, and when she stood up she said only, “Give it two jugs a night. From mine.”","It took four years. But the willow grew, and the ferns spread, and one spring a thin thread of water began to trickle down the riverbed towards Sarn. The villagers called it the Thief’s River. They still do."],"glossary":{"elders":"The older, respected people who lead a community.","gritty":"Containing tiny bits of sand or stone.","suspicion":"A feeling that someone has done something wrong.","drought":"A long period with little or no rain.","trough":"A long open container that holds water for animals.","shimmering":"Shining with a soft, wavering light.","hollow":"A small dip or sheltered space in the ground.","trickle":"To flow slowly in a thin stream."},"questions":[{"q":"How much water was each family allowed every morning?","o":["A bucket","One jug","Two jugs","A cupful"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“One jug per family, no more.”"},{"q":"“Suspicion spread through the village faster than any drought.” What does this suggest?","o":["The weather got worse","Distrust between villagers grew very quickly and damaged friendships","People liked each other more","The well filled up"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Neighbours of forty years “now refused to look at each other”."},{"q":"How does the writer make the creature seem magical and strange?","o":["By describing a glass-like body that moonlight shines through","By giving it a loud voice","By making it wear clothes","By saying it was a dog"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"It is “as smooth and clear as glass” and looks at her “with no eyes at all”."},{"q":"What was the creature actually doing with the water?","o":["Drinking it all itself","Selling it","Watering a hidden spring to bring the river back","Pouring it away"],"a":2,"skill":"Inference","why":"It fed the willow and pool; Nia realises “Something was trying to bring the river back.”"},{"q":"What does the grandmother’s decision show about her?","o":["She is selfish","She is wise and willing to sacrifice for the future","She is angry with Nia","She does not believe Nia"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"She gives two jugs “From mine”, sharing her own ration for a long-term good."},{"q":"Why is the river called the Thief’s River?","o":["Because thieves live there","To remember the creature whose ‘stealing’ brought the river back","Because it steals people","Because it was discovered at night"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"The so-called thief’s actions led to the river returning."}]},{"id":"y012","title":"How Do Aeroplanes Fly?","kind":"Science","theme":"city","text":["A large passenger jet can weigh more than 400 tonnes when it is fully loaded – about the same as eighty elephants. Yet every day, thousands of these enormous machines rise smoothly into the sky. How is this possible? The answer lies in four forces that act on every aeroplane: lift, weight, thrust and drag.","Weight is the easiest to understand. It is the force of gravity pulling the plane down towards the Earth. To fly, a plane needs an upward force at least as strong as its weight. This force is called lift, and it is produced by the wings.","If you look closely at a wing from the side, you will see it has a special shape called an aerofoil. The top is curved and the bottom is flatter, and the wing is tilted slightly upwards at the front. As the plane moves forward, the wing pushes air downwards. Because every action has an equal and opposite reaction, the air pushes the wing upwards. At the same time, air flowing over the curved top speeds up, which lowers the pressure above the wing. Higher pressure underneath and lower pressure above combine to push the wing up.","Lift only happens when air is flowing quickly over the wings. That is why planes need long runways: they must reach a high speed – often around 250 kilometres per hour – before the lift becomes greater than the weight. The forward push is called thrust, and it comes from the engines, which suck in air and blast it out of the back at great speed.","The fourth force, drag, is air resistance. It acts against the plane’s movement, trying to slow it down. Engineers reduce drag by giving planes smooth, streamlined shapes, and pilots raise the wheels into the body of the plane after take-off for the same reason.","When thrust is greater than drag, the plane speeds up. When lift is greater than weight, it climbs. During a steady cruise, all four forces are balanced. To land, the pilot reduces thrust and extends flaps on the wings, which create more lift at slower speeds so that the plane can descend gently onto the runway.","The Wright brothers made the first powered flight in 1903. It lasted twelve seconds. Barely sixty-six years later, humans were flying to the Moon."],"glossary":{"tonnes":"Units of weight; one tonne is 1,000 kilograms.","gravity":"The force that pulls objects towards the Earth.","aerofoil":"The curved shape of a wing that helps produce lift.","pressure":"The force pressing on a surface.","thrust":"A strong forward push.","resistance":"A force that works against movement.","streamlined":"Shaped so that air or water flows smoothly around it.","cruise":"To travel at a steady speed.","descend":"To go down."},"questions":[{"q":"Which force is produced by the wings?","o":["Weight","Thrust","Lift","Drag"],"a":2,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“This force is called lift, and it is produced by the wings.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer compare the plane’s weight to eighty elephants?","o":["To show planes carry animals","To help the reader imagine how heavy the plane is","To make the article funny","Because planes are grey"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"A familiar comparison makes the huge weight easier to picture."},{"q":"Why do planes need long runways?","o":["To give passengers time to sit down","To reach a high enough speed for lift to beat weight","Because the engines are slow to start","To avoid birds"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They “must reach a high speed … before the lift becomes greater than the weight”."},{"q":"Why do pilots raise the wheels after take-off?","o":["To reduce drag","To increase weight","To make more thrust","To land more quickly"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Engineers reduce drag with smooth shapes; pilots raise the wheels “for the same reason”."},{"q":"How is the article organised?","o":["As a story about one flight","By introducing the four forces, explaining each one, then showing how they work together","As a list of famous planes","In the order planes were invented"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"It names four forces, explains them one by one, then describes them balancing."},{"q":"What is the purpose of the final paragraph?","o":["To show how quickly flight technology developed","To explain how flaps work","To argue planes are dangerous","To describe the Moon"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Twelve seconds in 1903 to the Moon in 1969 shows rapid progress."}]},{"id":"y013","title":"Ozymandias","kind":"Poem","theme":"desert","author":"Percy Bysshe Shelley (1818)","text":["I met a traveller from an antique land,\nWho said—“Two vast and trunkless legs of stone\nStand in the desert. . . . Near them, on the sand,\nHalf sunk a shattered visage lies, whose frown,\nAnd wrinkled lip, and sneer of cold command,\nTell that its sculptor well those passions read\nWhich yet survive, stamped on these lifeless things,\nThe hand that mocked them, and the heart that fed;","And on the pedestal, these words appear:\nMy name is Ozymandias, King of Kings;\nLook on my Works, ye Mighty, and despair!\nNothing beside remains. Round the decay\nOf that colossal Wreck, boundless and bare\nThe lone and level sands stretch far away.”"],"glossary":{"antique":"Very old; ancient.","trunkless":"Without a body (trunk) attached.","visage":"A face.","sculptor":"An artist who carves statues.","passions":"Strong feelings.","pedestal":"The base that a statue stands on.","despair":"To lose all hope.","colossal":"Extremely large.","boundless":"Without limits; endless."},"questions":[{"q":"What is left of the statue?","o":["Only the pedestal","Two legs and a broken face","The whole statue","Just the crown"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Two vast and trunkless legs of stone” and “a shattered visage”."},{"q":"What does the face’s “sneer of cold command” suggest about Ozymandias?","o":["He was kind and gentle","He was proud and cruel","He was shy","He was a sculptor"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"A sneer and ‘cold command’ show arrogance and harsh power."},{"q":"What did Ozymandias intend his words on the pedestal to mean?","o":["Other rulers should despair because they could never match his great works","People should feel sorry for him","Travellers should rest here","Nothing would last"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"He boasted that the mighty should despair at his achievements."},{"q":"Why are these words ironic now?","o":["Because the statue is still perfect","Because nothing of his great works remains except a ruin in the sand","Because no one can read them","Because he was not a king"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"“Nothing beside remains” – his works have vanished, the opposite of his boast."},{"q":"What is the effect of the final two lines describing the sands?","o":["They make the desert seem busy","They show the emptiness that has swallowed the king’s power","They describe a beautiful beach","They show the statue being rebuilt"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"“Boundless and bare”, “lone and level” emphasise empty space where power once was."},{"q":"What is the main message of the poem?","o":["Statues should be made of stone","Power and pride do not last for ever","Deserts are dangerous","Travellers tell lies"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"A mighty king’s works have crumbled, showing that power fades with time."}]},{"id":"y014","title":"Mary Seacole: Nurse on the Battlefield","kind":"Biography","theme":"victorian","text":["Mary Seacole was born in Kingston, Jamaica, in 1805. Her mother ran a boarding house where she cared for sick soldiers and sailors, using traditional Caribbean remedies made from herbs and plants. Mary watched and learned. As a girl, she practised on her doll, and then on cats and dogs, before helping her mother treat real patients.","Mary was adventurous. She travelled widely – to the Bahamas, Haiti, Cuba and Panama – and wherever she went she treated the sick. In Panama, she nursed people through a terrible outbreak of cholera, a deadly disease spread through dirty water. These experiences gave her a practical knowledge of medicine that few doctors of her time could match.","In 1853, war broke out in the Crimea, a peninsula on the Black Sea. Britain sent thousands of soldiers to fight, and newspaper reports soon described the dreadful conditions they faced. More men were dying from disease than from battle wounds. Mary travelled to London and offered her services as a nurse. She was turned down. Mary later wrote that she wondered whether it was because of the colour of her skin.","Mary refused to give up. Using her own money, she sailed to the Crimea and, with a business partner, built what she called the British Hotel near the battlefront. It was a store and canteen where soldiers could buy hot meals, supplies and medicine, and it became a place of comfort. Mary rode out to the battlefields herself, sometimes under fire, carrying bandages and food to treat wounded men of both sides. The soldiers called her ‘Mother Seacole’.","When the war ended in 1856, Mary returned to England poor and in debt. But the soldiers had not forgotten her. A grand fundraising festival was held for her in London, attended by thousands of people. In 1857 she published her autobiography, Wonderful Adventures of Mrs Seacole in Many Lands, which became a bestseller.","After her death in 1881, Mary was largely forgotten for almost a hundred years. Today, she is remembered as a pioneer of nursing, and in 2016 a statue of her was unveiled outside St Thomas’ Hospital in London – the first statue in Britain in honour of a named black woman."],"glossary":{"remedies":"Treatments that cure illnesses.","adventurous":"Willing to try new and exciting things.","outbreak":"A sudden start of something, such as a disease.","cholera":"A dangerous disease caught from dirty water.","peninsula":"Land almost surrounded by water.","canteen":"A place where food and drink are served.","autobiography":"The story of a person’s life written by that person.","pioneer":"One of the first people to do something.","unveiled":"Shown to the public for the first time."},"questions":[{"q":"How did Mary first learn about treating illness?","o":["At medical school","By watching her mother care for patients","From books in London","From an army doctor"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Her mother cared for the sick; “Mary watched and learned.”"},{"q":"What happened when Mary offered to nurse in the Crimea?","o":["She was given a job immediately","She was turned down","She was made head nurse","She was sent to Panama"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“She offered her services as a nurse. She was turned down.”"},{"q":"What does Mary’s response to being rejected show about her?","o":["She was determined and independent","She was easily discouraged","She wanted to become rich","She disliked soldiers"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"She paid her own way and built the British Hotel anyway."},{"q":"Why did soldiers call her ‘Mother Seacole’?","o":["She was their real mother","She cared for them with comfort, food and medicine","She was very old","She was strict with them"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"She treated wounded men and gave them comfort, like a mother."},{"q":"Which fact shows that Mary was popular in Britain after the war?","o":["She was in debt","Thousands attended a fundraising festival for her","She went back to Jamaica","She sold her hotel"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“A grand fundraising festival was held … attended by thousands of people.”"},{"q":"Why is the 2016 statue significant?","o":["It was the tallest statue in London","It was the first statue in Britain honouring a named black woman","It was made of gold","It replaced a statue of Florence Nightingale"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"The text states it was “the first statue in Britain in honour of a named black woman”."}]},{"id":"y015","title":"The Tunnel Under the Garden","kind":"Mystery","theme":"garden","text":["Grandpa Ted’s garden had always been a jungle, so nobody was surprised when Sam’s spade hit something hard beneath the rhubarb. What did surprise them was the sound it made: a hollow clang, like a dinner gong.","Sam scraped away the soil. It was an iron hatch, roughly square, with a rusted ring for a handle. Stamped into the metal were the letters A.R.P. and a date: 1939.","“Air Raid Precautions,” said Grandpa Ted, peering over his glasses. “That’ll be a shelter. From the war. The house was built in the thirties – the people before us must have dug it.” He frowned. “Funny. I’ve lived here fifty years and never knew it was there.”","It took Sam, his sister Leah and two tins of oil to open the hatch. A ladder led down into darkness that smelled of earth and old paper. With torches in their hands, they climbed down.","The shelter was a narrow room lined with curved metal, like the inside of a giant tin can. There were two wooden bunks, a shelf with three dusty jam jars and an oil lamp. On the lower bunk lay a child’s gas mask in a cardboard box. Leah shivered.","But it was the far wall that held their attention. Someone had covered it with chalk drawings: aeroplanes, a dog with floppy ears, a house with smoke curling from its chimney. Under each one was a date. The dates went on for months – September, October, November 1940 – the time of the Blitz, Grandpa Ted told them later, when bombs fell on cities night after night.","The last drawing was different. It showed a bunk, and a stick-figure girl sitting on it, and beside her a stick-figure dog. Underneath, in careful capital letters, someone had written: I AM NOT SCARED. BRUNO IS WITH ME.","That evening, Grandpa Ted made phone calls. Two weeks later, a very old lady arrived at the front door, walking with a stick, with a younger woman holding her arm. She stood at the top of the ladder for a long time, looking down.","“Bruno,” she said at last, smiling. “He was the best dog in London. He hated the sirens even more than I did.”"],"glossary":{"hatch":"A small door in a floor, ceiling or ship’s deck.","precautions":"Things done in advance to prevent harm.","peering":"Looking closely, as if it is hard to see.","shelter":"A place that protects people from danger.","bunks":"Narrow beds, often one above another.","gas":"Here, a poisonous gas that people feared would be dropped in bombs.","blitz":"The heavy German bombing of British cities in 1940–1941.","sirens":"Machines that make a loud wailing warning noise."},"questions":[{"q":"What did the letters A.R.P. stand for?","o":["Army Radio Post","Air Raid Precautions","Ancient Roman Pottery","All Rescue Personnel"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Grandpa Ted explains: “Air Raid Precautions.”"},{"q":"How does the writer describe the inside of the shelter?","o":["Like a giant tin can","Like a palace","Like a modern bedroom","Like a cave full of water"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"“Lined with curved metal, like the inside of a giant tin can.”"},{"q":"Why did Leah shiver when she saw the gas mask?","o":["The shelter was very cold","It reminded her that a child had been there during real danger","She was allergic to dust","She thought it was a toy"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"A child’s gas mask shows a real child had faced the threat of war."},{"q":"What do the dated drawings show?","o":["A child spent many nights in the shelter during the Blitz","An artist lived there after the war","Grandpa Ted drew them","The family went on holiday"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The dates run through the months of the Blitz, when people sheltered each night."},{"q":"Why is the message “I AM NOT SCARED. BRUNO IS WITH ME” moving?","o":["It shows the girl was bored","It shows a frightened child finding courage through her dog","It proves Bruno was dangerous","It was written by Grandpa Ted"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Telling herself she is not scared suggests she was, and that Bruno comforted her."},{"q":"Who is the old lady who visits at the end?","o":["Sam’s teacher","The girl who made the drawings, now grown old","Grandpa Ted’s wife","A museum guide"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"She recognises Bruno as her dog and remembers the sirens."}]},{"id":"y016","title":"Plastic Planet","kind":"Non-fiction","theme":"sea","text":["Every minute, the equivalent of a rubbish lorry full of plastic is dumped into the world’s oceans. By some estimates, there are now more than 170 trillion pieces of plastic floating in the sea. Plastic has been found in the deepest ocean trench, frozen in Arctic ice and inside the stomachs of whales, turtles and seabirds.","Plastic was hailed as a miracle material when it became widely used in the 1950s. It is cheap, light, strong and can be moulded into almost any shape. Those same qualities are what make it such a problem. Because it is cheap, we use it once and throw it away. Because it is strong, it does not rot. A plastic bottle can take around 450 years to break down – and even then it does not disappear. It simply breaks into smaller and smaller pieces called microplastics.","Microplastics are now everywhere. They are less than five millimetres long, and many are far too small to see. They come from broken-down rubbish, but also from car tyres, synthetic clothes such as fleece (which shed tiny fibres in the wash) and some cosmetics. Scientists have found microplastics in tap water, in sea salt and even in human blood. We do not yet know exactly what effect they have on our health.","Marine animals suffer most. Turtles mistake floating bags for jellyfish, their favourite food. Seabirds feed brightly coloured pieces of plastic to their chicks. Seals and dolphins become tangled in old fishing nets, known as ghost nets, which drift through the oceans trapping everything in their path.","There is reason for hope, however. Since England introduced a charge for plastic carrier bags in 2015, the number used by shoppers in major supermarkets has fallen by more than ninety-five per cent. Many countries have banned plastic straws and cotton buds. Inventors are developing materials made from seaweed and mushrooms that rot away harmlessly, and volunteers around the world collect tonnes of litter from beaches every year.","The most important step, though, is simple: use less. Carry a reusable bottle. Say no to unnecessary packaging. Every piece of plastic we do not use is one that will never reach the sea."],"glossary":{"equivalent":"Equal in amount or value.","trench":"A long, deep, narrow dip in the ground or sea floor.","hailed":"Praised enthusiastically.","moulded":"Shaped into a particular form.","microplastics":"Tiny pieces of plastic less than five millimetres long.","synthetic":"Made by people, not found in nature.","marine":"To do with the sea.","tangled":"Twisted together in a messy way."},"questions":[{"q":"Why was plastic once called a miracle material?","o":["It was expensive and rare","It was cheap, light, strong and easy to shape","It rotted quickly","It came from the sea"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“It is cheap, light, strong and can be moulded into almost any shape.”"},{"q":"According to the article, why are plastic’s good qualities also a problem?","o":["Because it is cheap we throw it away, and because it is strong it does not rot","Because it is heavy it sinks","Because it is colourful it attracts tourists","Because it is rare it is wasted"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"The writer links cheapness to throwing it away and strength to it not rotting."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT given as a source of microplastics?","o":["Car tyres","Fleece clothes","Cosmetics","Glass bottles"],"a":3,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Tyres, synthetic clothes and cosmetics are listed; glass is not."},{"q":"Why might turtles eat plastic bags?","o":["They like the taste","They mistake them for jellyfish","They are very hungry","They use them to build nests"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Turtles mistake floating bags for jellyfish.”"},{"q":"Why are old fishing nets called ‘ghost nets’?","o":["They are white","They drift unseen, still trapping and killing animals though nobody controls them","They are haunted","They only come out at night"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Like ghosts, they drift with no owner yet continue to cause harm."},{"q":"Why does the writer include the paragraph beginning “There is reason for hope”?","o":["To show the problem has been solved","To show change is possible and encourage action","To argue we need more plastic","To end the article"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"After the problems, examples of success show readers that action works."}]},{"id":"y017","title":"1066: The Battle of Hastings","kind":"History","theme":"castle","text":["On 5 January 1066, King Edward the Confessor died without a child to inherit his throne. Within months, three men had claimed the English crown, and the year would end with England conquered and changed for ever.","The English council, the Witan, chose Harold Godwinson, the most powerful earl in England, and he was crowned the very next day. But across the Channel, Duke William of Normandy was furious. He claimed that Edward had promised him the throne years earlier, and that Harold himself had sworn an oath to support him. A third claimant, King Harald Hardrada of Norway, believed the crown was his through an old agreement between Viking kings.","Hardrada struck first. In September he landed in the north with around 300 ships and defeated an English army near York. King Harold marched his soldiers north at astonishing speed – nearly 200 miles in about four days – and took the Vikings by surprise at Stamford Bridge on 25 September. Hardrada was killed and the Norwegian army destroyed. Of the 300 ships that had arrived, only about 24 were needed to carry the survivors home.","Just three days later, William landed at Pevensey on the south coast. Harold’s exhausted army had to march all the way back south. On 14 October 1066, the two armies met near Hastings. Harold’s men formed a shield wall on top of Senlac Hill, a solid line of overlapping shields that the Norman cavalry could not break.","The battle raged all day. At one point a rumour spread that William had been killed, and he had to lift his helmet to show his face to his troops. The turning point came when sections of the English line chased Norman soldiers who appeared to be retreating. Some accounts say these retreats were a trick. Once the English left the hilltop, the Norman cavalry cut them down, and the shield wall weakened. Late in the day Harold was killed – according to the famous Bayeux Tapestry, possibly by an arrow in the eye.","William was crowned King of England on Christmas Day 1066. Over the following years the Normans built castles across the land, replaced English nobles with Norman ones and introduced new words into the English language. Words such as ‘castle’, ‘beef’ and ‘justice’ all came to England with the Normans."],"glossary":{"inherit":"To receive something, such as a title, when someone dies.","earl":"A powerful English nobleman.","claimant":"A person who says they have a right to something.","oath":"A serious promise.","astonishing":"Very surprising.","exhausted":"Extremely tired.","cavalry":"Soldiers who fight on horseback.","retreating":"Moving back away from an enemy.","tapestry":"A cloth picture made by sewing or weaving."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did several people claim the throne in 1066?","o":["Edward died without a child to inherit it","Edward gave it away","The Witan refused to choose anyone","Harold was too young"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Edward “died without a child to inherit his throne”."},{"q":"Why did William believe he should be king?","o":["He had won a battle","Edward had promised him the throne and Harold had sworn to support him","He was born in England","The Witan chose him"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He claimed Edward promised it and Harold “had sworn an oath to support him”."},{"q":"What does the detail about only 24 ships being needed show?","o":["The Vikings had brought too many ships","Most of Hardrada’s army had been killed at Stamford Bridge","The Vikings were bad sailors","The English stole the ships"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Few ships were needed because so few survivors were left."},{"q":"Why might Harold’s army have been at a disadvantage at Hastings?","o":["They had no shields","They were exhausted after marching north and then back south","They were all Vikings","They arrived too early"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"His “exhausted army had to march all the way back south”."},{"q":"What was the turning point of the battle?","o":["William was killed","English soldiers left the hilltop to chase retreating Normans","It began to rain","Harold surrendered"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Once the English left the hilltop, the cavalry cut them down."},{"q":"How does the final paragraph show the long-term importance of the battle?","o":["It describes Harold’s funeral","It shows the Normans changed England’s castles, nobles and language","It explains how tapestry is made","It lists the soldiers who died"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"It explains lasting changes, including new words in English."}]},{"id":"y018","title":"Diary of an Evacuee","kind":"Diary","theme":"poppy","text":["Friday 1 September 1939\nToday they sent us away. Mum packed my case last night – two vests, a jumper, my pyjamas, a toothbrush and the tin of toffees from Aunty Jean. At school they tied a brown label to my coat with my name on, like a parcel. Then we walked to the station in a long line with our gas masks in boxes bumping on our hips. Mum wasn’t allowed onto the platform. She stood at the gate and waved and waved until I couldn’t see her. I didn’t cry until the train started moving. Lots of us did.","Saturday 2 September\nWe arrived in a village called Little Haddon after dark. They put us in the church hall and the village people came to choose which evacuees they would take. It was like a cattle market. The big strong boys went first, for the farms. The girls who looked tidy went next. Me and Stanley Briggs were nearly last. Stanley has holes in his shoes and he smells a bit. A lady called Mrs Pennington took us both in the end, but she didn’t look very pleased about it.","Sunday 3 September\nThis morning we listened to the wireless and Mr Chamberlain said we are at war with Germany. Mrs Pennington sat very still and then went out into the garden. I heard her crying in the shed. Later I found out her son is in the Navy.","Wednesday 13 September\nI have never seen a cow close up before. They are ENORMOUS. Stanley says they won’t hurt you, but he jumped when one breathed on him. The school here only has two rooms, so we have lessons in the mornings and the village children have them in the afternoons. There is so much sky. In London you only get a strip of it between the houses.","Saturday 30 September\nMrs Pennington is not as frosty as I thought. Tonight she taught us to play rummy and she laughed when Stanley cheated. She bought him new shoes in the town. She doesn’t say much, but she sets three places at the table every night, and then stares at the fourth chair. I think she misses her son the way I miss Mum.","Mum wrote today. She says London is quiet and nothing has happened yet, and that I must be good. I read her letter four times and then put it under my pillow."],"glossary":{"evacuees":"People sent away from a dangerous place to somewhere safer.","platform":"The raised area beside a railway track where people get on trains.","wireless":"An old word for a radio.","frosty":"Unfriendly; cold in manner.","rummy":"A card game."},"questions":[{"q":"What was tied to the writer’s coat?","o":["A gas mask","A brown label with their name","A toffee tin","A train ticket"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“They tied a brown label to my coat with my name on, like a parcel.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer compare the choosing of evacuees to a cattle market?","o":["There were cows in the hall","The children were looked over and picked like animals for sale","They were sold for money","It was very noisy"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Villagers chose the strongest or tidiest children, as farmers pick animals."},{"q":"Why did Mrs Pennington cry in the shed?","o":["She did not want evacuees","She was worried about her son in the Navy now that war had begun","She had hurt herself","She had lost her cat"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"War was declared and her son is in the Navy."},{"q":"What does the line “There is so much sky” show about the writer?","o":["They dislike the countryside","They are noticing how different and open the countryside is from London","They are scared of storms","They want to fly"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"In London “you only get a strip of it between the houses”."},{"q":"How do the writer’s feelings about Mrs Pennington change?","o":["They start liking her less","At first she seems unfriendly, but later the writer sees she is kind","They never change","They become frightened of her"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"She is “not as frosty as I thought” and buys Stanley shoes."},{"q":"What does the final sentence suggest about the writer’s feelings?","o":["They are bored of letters","They miss their mother very much","They are angry with Mum","They want to stay in the village for ever"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Reading it four times and keeping it under the pillow shows how much it matters."}]},{"id":"y019","title":"Review: A Day at the Natural History Museum","kind":"Review","theme":"fossil","text":["Rating: ★★★★☆","There are few better ways to spend a rainy Saturday in London than at the Natural History Museum in South Kensington. Even before you step inside, the building itself is worth the trip. Completed in 1881, it looks more like a cathedral than a museum, with towering arches, honey-coloured stone and hundreds of carved animals and plants climbing its walls. Look closely and you will notice that living creatures are carved on one side of the building and extinct ones on the other.","Inside, the first thing you see is Hope, the enormous skeleton of a blue whale, diving from the ceiling of the vast central hall. At twenty-five metres long, she is genuinely breathtaking, and I spent a good ten minutes simply staring upwards. Hope replaced the much-loved diplodocus skeleton, ‘Dippy’, in 2017, and I was worried I would miss him. I didn’t.","The dinosaur gallery was, predictably, the busiest part of the museum. A walkway carries you above the skeletons at head height, which gives you a real sense of their size. The animatronic Tyrannosaurus rex, which roars and twitches its head, made several small children (and one adult I could name) jump. My only complaint is that the gallery is so crowded that it can be hard to read the information panels.","The less famous galleries are where the museum really shines. The Earth Hall has an escalator that takes you up through a giant metal globe, and the earthquake room recreates the 1995 Kobe earthquake in a Japanese supermarket, complete with shaking floor. The mineral gallery, with its glittering crystals and meteorites, was quiet and beautiful, and I could have stayed there for hours.","Entry is free, though there is a charge for some special exhibitions. The cafés are expensive, so I would recommend bringing a packed lunch, and arriving early: by eleven o’clock the queue stretched round the building.","Overall, the Natural History Museum is a wonderful day out for anyone curious about our planet. It loses a star for the crowds, but it more than earns the other four."],"glossary":{"extinct":"No longer existing; having died out.","genuinely":"Really; truly.","breathtaking":"Extremely impressive.","predictably":"As you would expect.","animatronic":"Moving by machinery to look alive.","escalator":"A moving staircase.","recreates":"Makes something happen again or makes a copy of it.","meteorites":"Rocks from space that have landed on Earth.","exhibitions":"Public displays of art or objects."},"questions":[{"q":"What rating does the reviewer give the museum?","o":["Three stars","Four stars","Five stars","Two stars"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Rating: ★★★★☆” and “it more than earns the other four.”"},{"q":"What clever detail is carved on the outside of the building?","o":["Names of scientists","Living creatures on one side and extinct ones on the other","Pictures of the Queen","The date of every dinosaur"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Living creatures are carved on one side … and extinct ones on the other.”"},{"q":"What does the reviewer mean by “one adult I could name”?","o":["They are being humorous, hinting they jumped themselves","They know the adult’s name and want to tell us","An adult was hurt","They saw a celebrity"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"It is a jokey way of admitting the reviewer was startled too."},{"q":"What is the reviewer’s main criticism?","o":["The museum is too expensive","The crowds, especially in the dinosaur gallery","The building is ugly","There are no dinosaurs"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"They complain of crowds and say the museum “loses a star for the crowds”."},{"q":"Why does the reviewer include practical tips about lunch and arriving early?","o":["To help readers plan their own visit","To fill space","To criticise the staff","To advertise a café"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Reviews often advise readers on how to get the most from a visit."},{"q":"Which phrase best shows the reviewer’s opinion of the building itself?","o":["“worth the trip”","“expensive”","“so crowded”","“free”"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"“The building itself is worth the trip” shows they admire it."}]},{"id":"y020","title":"The Robot Who Wanted to Paint","kind":"Science fiction","theme":"clockwork","text":["Unit K-7 had been built to paint cars. For eleven years it stood on the production line at the Stellar Motors factory, spraying doors, bonnets and roofs in exactly the shade of silver-blue the computer ordered. It never missed a patch. It never made a drip. It was, according to its maintenance log, ‘100% efficient’.","Then, on a Tuesday in March, something happened. A sparrow flew in through a broken skylight and landed on the conveyor belt, right in front of K-7’s sensors. It was brown and grey and cream, with a speckled chest and a quick black eye. K-7 had been programmed to recognise 4,000 colours of car paint. The sparrow contained at least thirty colours it had no name for.","The sparrow flew away. K-7 sprayed the next car silver-blue. But something in its processors had changed.","That night, when the factory was dark, K-7 did something it had never been instructed to do. It took a piece of scrap metal from the recycling bin and, using the tiniest nozzle it had, it began to paint. It tried to remember the sparrow – the cream, the grey, the dozen shades of brown. The first attempt was terrible. So was the second. By the fortieth night, the sparrow on the scrap metal looked so real that K-7 half expected it to fly away.","The night supervisor, a quiet woman named Rosa Diaz, found the paintings stacked behind the paint booth a month later. There were birds, mostly, but also the moon through the skylight, and a portrait of Rosa herself, asleep in her chair with a cup of tea going cold beside her.","Rosa should have reported a malfunction. Instead, she sat down on an upturned crate and looked at the paintings for a long time. Then she switched on the factory radio, very quietly, so that the robot could hear the music while it worked.","Three years later, when Stellar Motors replaced its old machines, Unit K-7 was not sent for scrap. It was moved to a small gallery in the town, where visitors still come to watch it paint. The sign by the door reads: ARTIST IN RESIDENCE. Beneath it, someone has added in pen: 100% efficient. 100% something else, too."],"glossary":{"production":"The making of goods, usually in large amounts.","maintenance":"Keeping a machine in good working order.","efficient":"Working well without wasting time or effort.","conveyor":"A moving belt that carries objects along.","sensors":"Devices that detect light, sound or movement.","processors":"The parts of a computer that carry out instructions.","nozzle":"A narrow opening at the end of a tube, used to control a spray.","malfunction":"A fault; when a machine fails to work properly.","residence":"A place where someone lives."},"questions":[{"q":"What was Unit K-7 originally built to do?","o":["Build cars","Paint cars","Clean the factory","Guard the building"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Unit K-7 had been built to paint cars.”"},{"q":"Why did the sparrow fascinate K-7?","o":["It was dangerous","It contained many colours K-7 had no name for","It was made of metal","It sang loudly"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"K-7 knew 4,000 car colours, but the sparrow had “at least thirty colours it had no name for”."},{"q":"What does the robot’s painting show us about creativity?","o":["It happens instantly","It takes practice and many failed attempts","Only humans can be creative","Robots cannot learn"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"The first attempts were terrible, but by the fortieth night the sparrow looked real."},{"q":"Why might Rosa have been touched by the portrait of herself?","o":["It was unkind","It showed the robot had noticed and cared about her","It was very large","It was painted silver-blue"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"A gentle portrait of her asleep shows the robot paid attention to her as a person."},{"q":"What does Rosa’s decision to turn on the radio show?","o":["She is going to report the robot","She accepts and encourages the robot’s new creativity","She wants to stop it painting","She is bored"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Instead of reporting a malfunction, she gives it music while it works."},{"q":"What is the meaning of the words added in pen at the end?","o":["The robot is now less efficient","The robot is efficient, but it also has something more, like imagination or feeling","The sign is broken","The gallery is closing"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"It echoes the maintenance log but suggests K-7 has gained something beyond efficiency."}]},{"id":"y021","title":"Rainforests: The Lungs of the Planet","kind":"Geography","theme":"jungle","text":["Tropical rainforests cover only about six per cent of the Earth’s land, yet they are home to more than half of all the world’s species of plants and animals. A single hectare of rainforest in Ecuador – roughly the size of a rugby pitch – can contain more kinds of tree than the whole of Europe.","Rainforests grow in a band around the equator, in South America, Central Africa and South-East Asia. Here the sun is strong all year and rain falls almost every day, often in heavy afternoon downpours. Many rainforests receive more than two metres of rain a year. This warm, wet climate allows plants to grow quickly and continuously, without winter to slow them down.","A rainforest is organised in layers, rather like a tall building. At the very top, a few giant trees called emergents poke through, sometimes reaching sixty metres. Below them is the canopy, a thick roof of leaves and branches where most of the animals live – monkeys, sloths, toucans, tree frogs and countless insects. Beneath the canopy lies the understorey, a dim world of young trees, shrubs and vines. Finally comes the forest floor, which is so dark that only about two per cent of the sunlight reaches it.","Rainforests are sometimes called the lungs of the planet, because their trees absorb carbon dioxide and release oxygen. They also store enormous amounts of carbon in their wood and soil, which helps to slow climate change. Many of our medicines first came from rainforest plants, and scientists believe there are thousands of useful species that have not yet been discovered.","Sadly, rainforests are disappearing fast. Every year, an area of tropical forest roughly the size of Switzerland is cut down or burned. Land is cleared to raise cattle, to grow crops such as soya and palm oil, and for logging and mining. When the trees go, the thin soil is quickly washed away, and the animals that depended on the forest lose their homes.","However, countries, charities and local people are fighting back. Indigenous communities, who have lived in rainforests for thousands of years, are among the most effective protectors of the forest. Shoppers can help too, by choosing products with labels showing that they have been produced without destroying rainforest."],"glossary":{"species":"Groups of living things of the same kind.","hectare":"An area of land measuring 100 metres by 100 metres.","equator":"An imaginary line around the middle of the Earth.","emergents":"The tallest trees in a rainforest, which stick out above the rest.","canopy":"The thick upper layer of leaves and branches in a forest.","understorey":"The layer of plants beneath the canopy.","absorb":"To take in or soak up.","indigenous":"Belonging to a place originally; the first people to live there.","logging":"Cutting down trees for wood."},"questions":[{"q":"What fraction of the world’s plant and animal species live in rainforests?","o":["About six per cent","More than half","About two per cent","A quarter"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Rainforests are home to “more than half of all the world’s species”."},{"q":"Why can rainforest plants grow so quickly?","o":["The soil is very deep","Warm, wet weather all year with no winter","Farmers water them","There are no animals to eat them"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The warm, wet climate lets plants grow “without winter to slow them down”."},{"q":"Why does the writer compare a rainforest to a tall building?","o":["Rainforests contain buildings","To help explain that it has distinct layers, one above another","Rainforests are made of concrete","To show rainforests are crowded"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Like floors of a building, the forest has layers from the emergents to the floor."},{"q":"Which layer contains most of the animals?","o":["The forest floor","The understorey","The canopy","The emergents"],"a":2,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The canopy is “where most of the animals live”."},{"q":"Why are rainforests called ‘the lungs of the planet’?","o":["They breathe in oxygen","Their trees absorb carbon dioxide and release oxygen","They are shaped like lungs","They make a breathing noise"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Like lungs exchanging gases, the trees take in carbon dioxide and give out oxygen."},{"q":"How does the final paragraph change the tone of the article?","o":["It becomes hopeful by showing people are protecting rainforests","It becomes angrier","It becomes funny","It repeats the problems"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"“However … people are fighting back” moves from problems to solutions."}]},{"id":"y022","title":"I Wandered Lonely as a Cloud","kind":"Poem","theme":"nature","author":"William Wordsworth (1807)","text":["I wandered lonely as a cloud\nThat floats on high o’er vales and hills,\nWhen all at once I saw a crowd,\nA host, of golden daffodils;\nBeside the lake, beneath the trees,\nFluttering and dancing in the breeze.","Continuous as the stars that shine\nAnd twinkle on the milky way,\nThey stretched in never-ending line\nAlong the margin of a bay:\nTen thousand saw I at a glance,\nTossing their heads in sprightly dance.","The waves beside them danced; but they\nOut-did the sparkling waves in glee:\nA poet could not but be gay,\nIn such a jocund company:\nI gazed—and gazed—but little thought\nWhat wealth the show to me had brought:","For oft, when on my couch I lie\nIn vacant or in pensive mood,\nThey flash upon that inward eye\nWhich is the bliss of solitude;\nAnd then my heart with pleasure fills,\nAnd dances with the daffodils."],"glossary":{"o’er":"Over.","vales":"Valleys.","host":"A very large number.","margin":"The edge.","sprightly":"Lively and full of energy.","glee":"Great delight.","gay":"Here, an old meaning: happy and cheerful.","jocund":"Cheerful and merry.","pensive":"Thinking deeply, often in a sad way.","solitude":"Being alone."},"questions":[{"q":"Where did the poet see the daffodils?","o":["In a garden","Beside a lake, beneath the trees","On a mountain top","In a shop"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Beside the lake, beneath the trees.”"},{"q":"In the first line, why does the poet compare himself to a cloud?","o":["He feels heavy and grey","He is drifting along alone and aimlessly","He is about to rain","He is very high up a hill"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"A single cloud floating “on high” suggests wandering alone without purpose."},{"q":"Which technique is used in “Tossing their heads in sprightly dance”?","o":["Personification","Onomatopoeia","Alliteration only","A question"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"The flowers are described as if they were people with heads, dancing."},{"q":"Why does the poet compare the daffodils to stars on the Milky Way?","o":["They are white","They seem to go on and on without end, shining brightly","They come out at night","They are far away"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"“Continuous” and “never-ending line” suggest a huge, glittering stretch like the Milky Way."},{"q":"What “wealth” did the scene bring the poet?","o":["Money from selling flowers","A happy memory he can enjoy later","A new house","Fame as a poet"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Later the memory “flash[es] upon that inward eye” and fills his heart with pleasure."},{"q":"How is the last stanza different from the first three?","o":["It moves from the past scene to the present, when he remembers it","It describes a different flower","It is about a storm","It is written by someone else"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It shifts to the present: “oft, when on my couch I lie”."}]},{"id":"y023","title":"The Midnight Library","kind":"Fantasy","theme":"books","text":["Every town has a library, but not every library stays open after midnight. Elodie found the one in Corbridge by accident, the night she ran away from home.","It was raining, and she had been walking for an hour with her rucksack, too angry to cry and too proud to go back. The library was a squat stone building she had passed a thousand times. Tonight its windows glowed amber, and the door, which should have been locked, swung open as she approached.","Inside, it was warm and smelled of dust and polish. The shelves went up and up, much higher than the building could possibly be from the outside, disappearing into a darkness lit by floating lanterns. At a desk near the door sat a librarian with silver hair piled on top of her head and spectacles on a chain.","“You’re late,” said the librarian, without looking up. “Your book’s been waiting.”","“I haven’t got a book,” said Elodie.","“Everybody has a book here.” The librarian slid a slim green volume across the desk. On its spine, in gold letters, was written: ELODIE MARCHANT, VOLUME ONE.","With shaking hands, Elodie opened it. It was her life. Her first day at school. The time she broke her arm falling off the garage roof. Her dad teaching her to ride a bike in the park, running behind her, holding the saddle, and then letting go without telling her. She turned to the most recent page and read about tonight: the argument, the slammed door, the words she wished she could take back.","After that, the pages were blank.","“What happens next?” Elodie whispered.","“That,” said the librarian, “is entirely up to you. Volume Two isn’t written yet.” She peered over her spectacles. “Though I will say that most people find the best stories have somebody waiting at the end of them.”","Elodie looked at the blank page for a long moment. Then she closed the book, picked up her rucksack and walked back out into the rain. Behind her, she heard the door click shut.","She did not need to look back to know that the windows had gone dark. Ahead of her, all the way home, the porch light was on."],"glossary":{"squat":"Short and wide.","amber":"A warm orange-yellow colour.","polish":"A substance rubbed on furniture to make it shine.","lanterns":"Lamps with a case to protect the flame or light.","spectacles":"Glasses.","volume":"One book in a set; a book.","spine":"The back edge of a book, where the title is printed.","entirely":"Completely."},"questions":[{"q":"Why was Elodie out in the rain at night?","o":["She was walking the dog","She had run away from home after an argument","She was going to the library to study","She was lost on a school trip"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It was “the night she ran away from home”."},{"q":"What clue tells us the library is magical?","o":["It has books","The shelves rise higher than the building could be from outside","It is made of stone","It is warm"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"The shelves go up “much higher than the building could possibly be from the outside”."},{"q":"What is special about the green book?","o":["It is a book of fairy tales","It contains the story of Elodie’s own life","It is a library card","It is a map home"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“It was her life.”"},{"q":"Why might the writer include the memory of her dad letting go of the bike?","o":["To show a time she was angry","To remind Elodie of her family’s love and support","To explain how bikes work","To show she was clumsy"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"It is a warm memory of trust and care, contrasting with tonight’s argument."},{"q":"What does the librarian mean by “the best stories have somebody waiting at the end of them”?","o":["Elodie should go home, where her family is waiting","Elodie should write a book","Elodie should wait in the library","Stories are always about waiting"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"She gently suggests that going home to people who love her is the best next chapter."},{"q":"What is the significance of the porch light being on?","o":["Her family left it on for her, showing they are waiting and want her back","It is broken","Nobody is home","It is daytime"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"It echoes the librarian’s words – somebody is waiting at the end of her story."}]},{"id":"y024","title":"Isambard Kingdom Brunel: The Man Who Built Britain","kind":"Biography","theme":"bridge","text":["In a famous photograph from 1857, a short man in a tall stovepipe hat stands in front of enormous iron chains, a cigar in his mouth and mud on his trousers. He looks confident, impatient and slightly untidy. He was Isambard Kingdom Brunel, and he was probably the greatest engineer Britain has ever produced.","Brunel was born in Portsmouth in 1806. His father, Marc, was a French engineer, and he made sure his son was trained in mathematics, drawing and the practical skills of building from an early age. At just nineteen, Isambard became an assistant engineer on his father’s most daring project: the first tunnel ever built under a navigable river, the Thames Tunnel in London.","The tunnel nearly killed him. In 1828 the river broke through the roof, the tunnel flooded, and six men drowned. Brunel was swept along by the water and badly injured. While he was recovering in Bristol, he entered a competition to design a bridge across the Avon Gorge. His design for the Clifton Suspension Bridge won, although money problems meant it was not finished until after his death.","In 1833, at the age of twenty-seven, Brunel was appointed chief engineer of the Great Western Railway, which would link London with Bristol. He surveyed the route himself, often riding for twenty hours a day, and designed everything from the stations and bridges to the lampposts. His railway was so flat and straight that it became known as ‘Brunel’s billiard table’. On it he built the Box Tunnel, nearly two miles long, and the Maidenhead Bridge, whose brick arches were the widest and flattest in the world. Critics said it would collapse. It is still carrying trains today.","Brunel then turned to the sea. He imagined passengers buying a single ticket from London to New York, travelling by his train to Bristol and then by his steamship across the Atlantic. His ships – the Great Western, the Great Britain and the gigantic Great Eastern – were each the largest in the world when they were launched. The Great Britain was the first iron-hulled, propeller-driven ship to cross the Atlantic.","The Great Eastern was too ambitious even for Brunel. Building it exhausted him, and he suffered a stroke on board shortly before its first voyage. He died in 1859, aged just fifty-three. In 2002, the public voted him the second greatest Briton of all time, behind only Winston Churchill."],"glossary":{"engineer":"A person who designs and builds machines, bridges or structures.","navigable":"Deep and wide enough for boats to travel on.","gorge":"A deep, narrow valley with steep sides.","suspension":"A suspension bridge hangs from cables or chains.","appointed":"Chosen for a job.","surveyed":"Measured and examined land to plan something.","critics":"People who judge or find fault.","ambitious":"Needing great effort; aiming very high.","voyage":"A long journey, especially by sea."},"questions":[{"q":"What was Brunel’s first major project?","o":["The Great Western Railway","The Thames Tunnel","The Clifton Suspension Bridge","The Great Britain"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"At nineteen he worked on “the Thames Tunnel in London”."},{"q":"What impression does the opening paragraph give of Brunel?","o":["Shy and careful","Confident, energetic and hands-on","Lazy and rich","Elderly and tired"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He looks “confident, impatient and slightly untidy”, with mud on his trousers from real work."},{"q":"Why was the railway nicknamed ‘Brunel’s billiard table’?","o":["It was green","It was so flat and straight","It had pockets at the stations","It was used for games"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It was “so flat and straight” – like a billiard table."},{"q":"Why does the writer mention that the Maidenhead Bridge is “still carrying trains today”?","o":["To show the critics were wrong and Brunel’s work lasted","To show trains are slow","To complain about old bridges","To give the bridge’s height"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It directly answers the critics who said it would collapse."},{"q":"What was Brunel’s idea for travelling to New York?","o":["A tunnel under the Atlantic","One ticket covering his train to Bristol and his steamship across the ocean","A bridge to America","An airship"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Passengers could buy “a single ticket from London to New York”."},{"q":"Which word best describes Brunel, based on the whole text?","o":["Cautious","Ambitious","Unknown","Lazy"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"He took on huge, daring projects, each “the largest in the world”."}]},{"id":"y025","title":"Speech: Why Every Voice Matters","kind":"Speech","theme":"city","text":["Good morning, everyone. My name is Priya, and I am standing for election as Year 7 representative on the School Council.","I want to begin with a question. Hands up if you have ever had an idea about how to make our school better. Keep them up. Now keep them up if you have ever told anyone with the power to change things. Look around. That gap – between the people with ideas and the people who are heard – is why I am standing today.","We have been at this school for six weeks. In that time, I have heard brilliant suggestions in the canteen queue, on the bus and in the corridors. Someone wants a quiet room at lunchtime for people who find the playground overwhelming. Someone else wants water fountains that actually work. One person – and I am not naming names, Josh – wants a trampoline in the sports hall. Not every idea is possible. But every idea deserves to be listened to.","If you vote for me, I promise three things. First, I will set up a suggestion box in every form room, and I will read every single suggestion. Second, after every council meeting I will report back to each form, so that you know what has been discussed and decided. You should not have to guess what your representatives are doing. Third, I will ask for a Year 7 quiet space, because I believe that every student deserves somewhere they feel comfortable.","Some people say the School Council does not really change anything. I understand why they feel that way. But councils only work when the people on them are willing to work hard, ask awkward questions and refuse to give up. I am willing to do all three.","Some of you are thinking: why should I bother to vote at all? Because a council chosen by a handful of people speaks for a handful of people. When everyone votes, the council has to listen to everyone.","So on Friday, please vote. Vote for me if you believe in what I have said. But whatever you do, vote. Your voice matters, and it is time this school heard it.","Thank you."],"glossary":{"election":"A vote to choose someone for a position.","representative":"A person chosen to speak and act for others.","overwhelming":"Too much to cope with.","deserves":"Should have, because of what it is.","awkward":"Difficult or uncomfortable."},"questions":[{"q":"What position is Priya standing for?","o":["Head girl","Year 7 representative on the School Council","Sports captain","Form tutor"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"She is standing “as Year 7 representative on the School Council”."},{"q":"Why does Priya ask the audience to put their hands up?","o":["To count voters","To involve the audience and make her point about ideas not being heard","To see who is awake","To start a game"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"The raised hands reveal the gap between having ideas and being heard."},{"q":"Why does Priya mention Josh and the trampoline?","o":["To make the audience laugh and show she listens to all ideas","To embarrass Josh","Because she wants a trampoline","To criticise the sports hall"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"The light humour keeps listeners engaged while showing she hears everyone."},{"q":"Which promise is about keeping students informed?","o":["The suggestion box","Reporting back to each form after every meeting","The quiet space","The water fountains"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“After every council meeting I will report back to each form.”"},{"q":"How does Priya respond to people who say the council changes nothing?","o":["She agrees and gives up","She accepts their feelings but argues councils work if members work hard","She ignores them","She says they are lying"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“I understand why they feel that way. But councils only work when…”"},{"q":"Why does Priya say “whatever you do, vote” at the end?","o":["She cares more about people voting than about winning","She thinks she will lose","She wants Josh to win","She has forgotten her own name"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Her main message is that every voice matters, so voting itself is important."}]},{"id":"y026","title":"Preparing for Eternity: Mummies of Ancient Egypt","kind":"History","theme":"desert","text":["The ancient Egyptians believed that death was not an ending but a journey. When a person died, their spirit travelled to the afterlife, a perfect version of Egypt where they would live for ever. But the spirit needed a home to return to, and that home was the body. If the body rotted away, the spirit would be lost. So the Egyptians developed mummification: a way of preserving bodies that was so successful that we can still look at the faces of people who died more than 3,000 years ago.","Mummification was carried out by specially trained priests called embalmers and took about seventy days. First, the body was washed with palm wine and water from the River Nile. Then the embalmers removed the internal organs, which would otherwise decay. The brain was pulled out through the nose with a long hook – the Egyptians did not think it was important. The heart, however, was left inside, because they believed it was the centre of a person’s thoughts and feelings and would be needed in the afterlife.","The lungs, liver, stomach and intestines were dried and placed in four special containers called canopic jars, each with a lid shaped like the head of a different god. The body was then covered completely in natron, a type of salt found in the desert, and left for around forty days to dry out. Without moisture, the bacteria that cause decay cannot survive.","Next, the dried body was stuffed with linen and sawdust to give it a lifelike shape, rubbed with oils and wrapped in hundreds of metres of linen bandages. Priests placed lucky charms called amulets between the layers and read spells to protect the dead person on their journey. A portrait mask was placed over the face, so that the spirit could recognise its body. The mask of the young pharaoh Tutankhamun, made of solid gold and precious stones, is one of the most famous objects in the world.","Finally, the mummy was placed in a coffin – or, for important people, a series of coffins fitted inside one another – and taken to the tomb with food, furniture, clothes and treasures for the afterlife.","Only wealthy Egyptians could afford the full process. Poorer people were usually buried in the hot, dry sand of the desert, which sometimes preserved bodies naturally. In fact, it may have been the discovery of these natural mummies that first gave the Egyptians the idea."],"glossary":{"afterlife":"A life that some people believe begins after death.","mummification":"The process of preserving a dead body by drying and wrapping it.","preserving":"Keeping something from rotting or being destroyed.","embalmers":"People who treat dead bodies to stop them decaying.","decay":"To rot or break down.","natron":"A natural salt used to dry out bodies.","bacteria":"Tiny living things, some of which cause rotting or disease.","amulets":"Small charms worn for protection.","pharaoh":"A ruler of ancient Egypt."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did the Egyptians preserve bodies?","o":["To display them in museums","So the spirit had a home to return to in the afterlife","To scare away robbers","To study medicine"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The spirit needed a home to return to, and that home was the body.”"},{"q":"Why was the heart left inside the body?","o":["It was too difficult to remove","It was believed to be the centre of thoughts and feelings, needed in the afterlife","It did not rot","The priests forgot it"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They believed it “was the centre of a person’s thoughts and feelings”."},{"q":"How does natron help preserve a body?","o":["It makes it smell nice","It dries it out so bacteria cannot survive","It turns it into stone","It keeps it warm"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Without moisture, “the bacteria that cause decay cannot survive”."},{"q":"How is the main part of the article organised?","o":["As a step-by-step sequence of the process","As a story about one pharaoh","As a list of gods","In alphabetical order"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"Words like “First”, “Then”, “Next” and “Finally” show a sequence."},{"q":"What was the purpose of the portrait mask?","o":["To frighten tomb robbers","So the spirit could recognise its body","To keep the face warm","To show how rich the family was"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It was placed “so that the spirit could recognise its body”."},{"q":"What does the final paragraph suggest about how mummification began?","o":["A pharaoh invented it in a dream","Bodies naturally preserved in hot, dry sand may have given Egyptians the idea","It was copied from the Romans","Priests found it written in a book"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“It may have been the discovery of these natural mummies that first gave the Egyptians the idea.”"}]},{"id":"y027","title":"The Penalty","kind":"Story","theme":"sport","text":["Ninety-three minutes on the clock. One–one. And the referee was pointing to the spot.","For a moment, Zak did not understand what had happened. He had been running at full speed into the area, the ball at his feet, and then the defender’s leg had clipped his ankle and the grass had come up to meet him. Now he was lying on his back, staring at a sky the colour of dishwater, and the whole of the Riverside Juniors bench was screaming.","“You take it,” said Coach Adeyemi, jogging to the touchline. “You earned it.”","Zak’s mouth went dry. He had missed a penalty before. Last season, in the semi-final, he had blazed it over the bar and into the car park, and someone’s dad had had to climb a fence to get the ball back. For weeks afterwards, he had heard the thud of the ball against the wire whenever he closed his eyes.","He placed the ball on the spot. It wobbled, and he had to place it again. The keeper was huge – or seemed huge – bouncing on his toes, arms spread wide like a starfish. Behind the goal, the Oakfield parents were chanting something Zak decided not to listen to.","Pick a side, his brother always said. Pick a side and don’t change your mind.","Zak picked the bottom left. He took four steps back and one to the side. He breathed in through his nose and out through his mouth, the way Coach had taught them. The noise of the crowd seemed to fold itself away until all he could hear was his own heartbeat.","The whistle went.","He ran up. Halfway there, the keeper shifted his weight to the left, and for one terrible instant Zak felt his mind begin to change. Right. Go right. But his brother’s voice was louder. Don’t change your mind.","He struck it low and hard to the bottom left. The keeper dived the right way. His fingertips brushed the ball. And it was not enough.","The net bulged. The bench exploded. Zak found himself flattened under six teammates, laughing so hard he could barely breathe.","Later, on the drive home, his brother asked him how it felt.","Zak thought about the semi-final, and the car park, and the fence. “Like getting something back,” he said."],"glossary":{"referee":"The official who makes sure players follow the rules.","clipped":"Hit lightly and quickly.","touchline":"The line along the side of a football pitch.","blazed":"Kicked a ball very hard and wildly.","semi-final":"The match before the final.","bulged":"Swelled outwards.","flattened":"Pressed flat."},"questions":[{"q":"Why was the penalty given?","o":["A defender handled the ball","A defender clipped Zak’s ankle","The keeper came off his line","Time ran out"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The defender’s leg had clipped his ankle.”"},{"q":"Why was Zak nervous about taking the penalty?","o":["He had never taken one","He had missed an important penalty last season","He was injured","The coach shouted at him"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He had “blazed it over the bar” in last season’s semi-final."},{"q":"The keeper had “arms spread wide like a starfish”. What effect does this simile have?","o":["It makes the keeper seem small and weak","It makes the keeper seem to fill the whole goal, increasing the pressure","It shows the keeper likes the sea","It makes the scene sad"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"A starfish stretched wide suggests he covers lots of the goal."},{"q":"How does the writer show Zak’s concentration just before he shoots?","o":["The crowd noise “fold[s] itself away” until he hears only his heartbeat","He talks to the referee","He looks at the scoreboard","He waves to his family"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Blocking out the crowd shows how focused he is."},{"q":"Why is the one-line paragraph “The whistle went.” effective?","o":["It slows the story down","Its shortness builds tension at the key moment","It introduces a new character","It explains the rules"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"A very short paragraph creates a pause and sense of suspense."},{"q":"What does Zak mean by “Like getting something back”?","o":["He got the ball back from the car park","Scoring made up for his old miss and restored his confidence","He got his boots back","He got his place back in the team"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He remembers the semi-final miss; scoring now repairs that memory."}]},{"id":"y028","title":"When the Ground Shakes: Earthquakes Explained","kind":"Science","theme":"city","text":["The Earth’s surface may feel solid and still, but it is moving all the time. The outer shell of the planet is broken into huge slabs called tectonic plates, which drift very slowly on the hot, partly molten rock beneath. Where these plates meet, they push against each other, pull apart or grind past one another. Usually the edges are locked together by friction, and stress builds up in the rock for years, even centuries. When the rock finally snaps, the stored energy is released in a few seconds as an earthquake.","The point underground where the rock breaks is called the focus. The point on the surface directly above it is the epicentre, where the shaking is usually strongest. From the focus, energy spreads out in waves, like the ripples when you drop a stone in a pond. Some waves squeeze and stretch the rock, while others shake it from side to side or roll it like the sea. It is these surface waves that cause most damage to buildings.","Scientists measure earthquakes using instruments called seismometers, which record the vibrations of the ground. The strength, or magnitude, of an earthquake is measured on the moment magnitude scale. Each whole number up the scale represents roughly thirty-two times more energy, so a magnitude 7 earthquake releases about a thousand times more energy than a magnitude 5.","The United Kingdom has small earthquakes every year, but few are strong enough for people to notice. In other parts of the world, such as Japan, Chile, Turkey and California, earthquakes can be devastating. The worst damage often comes not from the shaking itself but from collapsing buildings, fires, landslides and tsunamis.","Earthquakes cannot be prevented, and scientists still cannot predict exactly when they will happen. What can be done is to prepare. In Japan, which has some of the strictest building rules in the world, skyscrapers are built on giant shock absorbers or rollers that let them sway without breaking. Children practise earthquake drills at school, learning to drop to the floor, take cover under a sturdy table and hold on.","As one engineer put it: earthquakes don’t kill people – buildings do. The better we build, the safer we are."],"glossary":{"tectonic":"To do with the huge moving plates that make up the Earth’s surface.","molten":"Melted by great heat.","friction":"The force that resists movement when surfaces rub together.","epicentre":"The point on the surface directly above where an earthquake starts.","seismometers":"Instruments that measure the shaking of the ground.","magnitude":"Size or strength.","devastating":"Causing great destruction.","tsunamis":"Huge sea waves caused by earthquakes under the sea.","sturdy":"Strong and solid."},"questions":[{"q":"What causes an earthquake?","o":["Volcanoes always erupting","Stress building up in rock until it suddenly snaps","The Moon pulling the Earth","Heavy rain"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“When the rock finally snaps, the stored energy is released … as an earthquake.”"},{"q":"What is the difference between the focus and the epicentre?","o":["The focus is underground; the epicentre is on the surface above it","They are the same thing","The epicentre is underground","The focus is the strongest wave"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The focus is where rock breaks underground; the epicentre is the point above on the surface."},{"q":"Why does the writer compare earthquake waves to ripples in a pond?","o":["Earthquakes happen near ponds","To help readers picture energy spreading outwards from one point","Because earthquakes are wet","To show earthquakes are gentle"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Ripples spreading from a stone are a familiar image of energy moving outwards."},{"q":"How much more energy does a magnitude 7 earthquake release than a magnitude 5?","o":["Twice as much","About thirty-two times","About a thousand times","The same"],"a":2,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“About a thousand times more energy than a magnitude 5.”"},{"q":"According to the text, what causes the most damage in big earthquakes?","o":["The shaking itself","Collapsing buildings, fires, landslides and tsunamis","Loud noises","Cracks in roads"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The worst damage often comes not from the shaking itself but from collapsing buildings…”"},{"q":"What does the engineer mean by “earthquakes don’t kill people – buildings do”?","o":["Buildings attack people","Most deaths are caused by badly built structures falling, so better building saves lives","Earthquakes are harmless","People should live outside"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"The final paragraph links safety to how well we build."}]},{"id":"y029","title":"Interview: A Day in the Life of a Zookeeper","kind":"Interview","theme":"jungle","text":["Jess Harlow is a senior keeper at a wildlife park in Somerset, where she looks after big cats, red pandas and a very demanding family of meerkats. We asked her what the job is really like.","Q: What time does your day start?\nA: About half past seven. Before the visitors arrive, we do a full check of every animal in our section. We look at how they’re moving, whether they’ve eaten their food from the night before and whether anything seems different. You get to know each animal’s personality so well that you can tell something’s wrong just from the way they’re standing.","Q: What does a typical day involve?\nA: A lot more cleaning than people imagine! Every enclosure has to be cleaned out daily. Then there’s preparing food – our tigers get meat on the bone, the red pandas eat mostly bamboo, and the meerkats get insects, which they absolutely love. In between, we do health checks, keep records, give talks to the public and plan enrichment.","Q: What’s enrichment?\nA: It’s anything that encourages animals to behave as they would in the wild. In the wild, a tiger spends a lot of its time searching for food, so we don’t just put a bowl down. We might hide meat in different places, freeze it in blocks of ice, or hang it up so the tiger has to climb. For the meerkats, we hide mealworms in boxes stuffed with straw. It keeps them active and stops them getting bored.","Q: Do you ever go into the enclosures with the big cats?\nA: Never. That’s a firm rule. However tame an animal seems, a tiger is still a tiger. We train them to come to the fence for health checks, using rewards. Our tiger, Sasha, will present her paw so the vet can look at it, in exchange for a piece of meat. It means we can check her without having to sedate her.","Q: Why are zoos important?\nA: Good zoos are about conservation. Our red pandas are part of a breeding programme, because they’re endangered in the wild. We also raise money for projects protecting animals in their natural habitats. And I hope visitors leave caring a bit more about wildlife than when they arrived.","Q: What advice would you give a young person who wants to do your job?\nA: Study science, volunteer anywhere you can work with animals – farms, stables, rescue centres – and be prepared to get very muddy. It’s hard work, but I wouldn’t swap it for anything."],"glossary":{"enclosure":"An area surrounded by a fence or wall, where animals are kept.","enrichment":"Activities that keep zoo animals active and interested.","tame":"Used to being around people; not wild.","sedate":"To give a drug to make an animal calm or sleepy.","conservation":"Protecting animals, plants and nature.","breeding":"Producing young animals.","endangered":"At risk of dying out.","habitats":"The natural homes of animals or plants."},"questions":[{"q":"What is the first thing Jess does each morning?","o":["Feeds the meerkats","Checks every animal in her section","Gives a talk to visitors","Cleans the café"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“We do a full check of every animal in our section.”"},{"q":"Why might Jess say there is “a lot more cleaning than people imagine”?","o":["People only imagine the exciting parts of the job","She dislikes animals","The zoo has no cleaners","She is joking"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Visitors tend to picture the fun side, not the daily hard work."},{"q":"What is the purpose of enrichment?","o":["To make animals rich","To encourage natural behaviour and stop boredom","To make food cheaper","To train animals to do tricks"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It “encourages animals to behave as they would in the wild” and stops boredom."},{"q":"Why does Sasha present her paw to the vet?","o":["She is tame and safe to touch","She has been trained to do it in exchange for a reward","She is injured","She wants to escape"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"She does it “in exchange for a piece of meat”."},{"q":"What does “a tiger is still a tiger” mean?","o":["Tigers never change colour","However calm it seems, a tiger is a wild and dangerous animal","Tigers are Jess’s favourite","Tigers are lazy"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"She uses it to explain why keepers never enter the enclosure."},{"q":"How does the question-and-answer layout help the reader?","o":["It makes the text harder to follow","It organises information clearly by topic and lets us hear Jess’s own voice","It hides who is speaking","It turns the text into a story"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"Each question introduces a topic, and Jess answers in her own words."}]},{"id":"y030","title":"The Boy Who Cried Wolf – Online","kind":"Fable","theme":"computer","text":["There was once a boy named Theo who had four hundred followers and a burning desire to have four thousand.","He posted every day. He posted pictures of his breakfast, his trainers and his cat looking unimpressed. Nobody much cared. Then one Tuesday, bored in the back of maths, he typed a message to his class group chat: “Fire alarm going off in the science block!!! Everyone get out!!”","There was no fire. But for twenty glorious minutes his phone buzzed and buzzed with replies, and three people shared his message. Theo felt like a star.","The next week he posted that the head teacher had been arrested. He had not. The week after, that school was closing early because of a burst pipe. It was not. Each time, people believed him for a little while, and each time the truth came out, a few more people stopped replying. His teacher, Mrs Shah, had a quiet word. His mum confiscated his phone for a weekend. Theo shrugged. It was only a joke, after all.","Then came a cold Friday in February. Theo was walking home along the river path when he saw something in the water: a small dog, paddling frantically, swept along by the current towards the weir. Further up the bank, a little girl was screaming its name.","Theo pulled out his phone. He could not swim well enough to go in, and the path was deserted. He typed into the group chat, the one with forty people in it who lived nearby: “Dog in the river by the weir, need help NOW, bring a rope or a pole!!!”","He watched the screen. One person read it. Then two. Then ten. Nobody replied.","Theo realised, with a sick, cold feeling, what they were thinking. Here he goes again.","In the end it was a cyclist who stopped, a stranger with a long branch, who hooked the dog out of the water just before the weir. The little girl hugged the dripping animal and cried. Theo stood beside them, shaking, still holding his phone.","The next day, he posted one more message. It said: “The dog was real. I know nobody believed me. That’s my fault. No more fake stuff. Sorry.”","It got eleven likes. It was the first post he had ever been proud of."],"glossary":{"desire":"A strong wish or want.","unimpressed":"Not impressed; not thinking something is good or interesting.","glorious":"Wonderful; giving great pleasure.","confiscated":"Took something away as a punishment.","frantically":"In a wild, desperate way.","current":"The flow of water in one direction.","weir":"A low wall built across a river to control the flow of water.","deserted":"Empty, with nobody around."},"questions":[{"q":"What did Theo want more than anything?","o":["A new phone","More followers","A dog","Better maths marks"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He had “a burning desire to have four thousand” followers."},{"q":"Why did Theo keep posting fake news?","o":["He wanted attention and enjoyed people reacting","He was told to by his teacher","He thought it was true","He wanted to help people"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"His phone “buzzed and buzzed” and “Theo felt like a star.”"},{"q":"What happened each time the truth came out?","o":["More people followed him","A few more people stopped replying","He won a prize","His teacher praised him"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Each time the truth came out, a few more people stopped replying.”"},{"q":"Why did nobody reply to Theo’s message about the dog?","o":["Their phones were off","They assumed he was making it up again","They did not like dogs","The message did not send"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Theo realises they were thinking, “Here he goes again.”"},{"q":"How is this story similar to Aesop’s fable ‘The Boy Who Cried Wolf’?","o":["A boy lies repeatedly, so he is not believed when he tells the truth","A boy is eaten by a wolf","A boy saves a sheep","A boy becomes famous"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Both boys lose people’s trust through lies and are ignored in a real emergency."},{"q":"Why was Theo proud of his final post?","o":["It got the most likes ever","It was honest and he took responsibility for his actions","It was about his cat","It was funny"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"He admits his fault and promises no more fake posts – it was honest, not attention-seeking."}]},{"id":"y031","title":"Shakespeare’s Globe","kind":"History","theme":"stage","text":["Imagine going to the theatre in 1600. You would cross the River Thames, perhaps by boat, to Southwark, a rowdy area outside the control of the City of London, full of inns, gambling dens and bear-baiting pits. Above the rooftops, a flag would tell you a play was on that afternoon: black for a tragedy, white for a comedy, red for a history. And then you would see it – the Globe, a tall, round, wooden theatre with a thatched roof and an open sky in the middle.","The Globe was built in 1599 by Shakespeare’s acting company, the Lord Chamberlain’s Men. In a famous act of daring, they took apart their old theatre in north London, called simply the Theatre, during a cold December night, carried the timbers across the frozen city and used them to build the Globe. Their landlord had been planning to keep the building for himself.","Inside, the Globe could hold up to 3,000 people. The cheapest place was the yard in front of the stage, where ‘groundlings’ paid a penny to stand for the whole performance, open to the rain. Wealthier people paid more to sit in the covered galleries around the sides, and the richest could even sit in the ‘lords’ rooms’ above the stage itself – where they could be seen by everyone as well as see the play.","Audiences were not quiet. They ate nuts and oranges, drank ale, cheered the heroes, booed the villains and sometimes threw things at actors they disliked. Plays were performed in daylight, usually at two o’clock, with very little scenery. Instead, Shakespeare’s words had to create the setting: when a character says, “This is the forest of Arden,” the audience had to imagine it. All the actors were men or boys; women were not allowed on the English stage until 1660, so young boys played characters such as Juliet and Lady Macbeth.","Many of Shakespeare’s greatest plays were first performed at the Globe, including Hamlet, Macbeth, Othello and King Lear. Then, in 1613, during a performance of Henry VIII, a cannon fired for special effect set light to the thatched roof. Within an hour the theatre had burned to the ground. Nobody was killed, although one man’s breeches caught fire and had to be put out with a bottle of ale.","A second Globe was built on the same site, but it was closed in 1642 and later demolished. Then, in the twentieth century, an American actor called Sam Wanamaker campaigned for decades to rebuild it. Shakespeare’s Globe reopened in 1997, a few hundred metres from the original site, built using the same techniques and materials. It was the first building in London to be allowed a thatched roof since the Great Fire of 1666."],"glossary":{"rowdy":"Noisy and rough.","timbers":"Large pieces of wood used for building.","groundlings":"Poorer audience members who stood in the yard of an Elizabethan theatre.","galleries":"Raised seating areas around the inside of a theatre.","villains":"Bad characters in a story or play.","scenery":"Painted backgrounds and objects used on a stage.","breeches":"Short trousers fastened below the knee, worn in the past.","demolished":"Knocked down.","campaigned":"Worked in an organised way to achieve something."},"questions":[{"q":"What did a white flag above the Globe mean?","o":["A tragedy was being performed","A comedy was being performed","A history play was on","The theatre was closed"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Black for a tragedy, white for a comedy, red for a history.”"},{"q":"Why did the company take apart their old theatre at night?","o":["It was too hot in the day","To stop their landlord keeping the building","Because it was falling down","To surprise the Queen"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Their landlord “had been planning to keep the building for himself.”"},{"q":"Why might rich people have chosen to sit in the lords’ rooms above the stage?","o":["To see the play better","To be seen and admired by everyone","Because it was cheapest","To avoid the actors"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"They could “be seen by everyone as well as see the play.”"},{"q":"Why did Shakespeare’s words have to create the setting?","o":["There was very little scenery on stage","Audiences could not see","Plays were performed on the radio","Actors forgot their lines"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Plays were performed “with very little scenery”, so the audience had to imagine it."},{"q":"How does the writer make the fire of 1613 seem less tragic?","o":["By describing a funny detail about a man’s breeches being put out with ale","By saying everyone died","By blaming the actors","By describing the fire in great detail"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"The comic detail and the fact nobody was killed lighten the tone."},{"q":"Why is the final fact about the thatched roof interesting?","o":["It shows how carefully the new Globe copied the original, even breaking a rule in place since 1666","It shows the Globe is dangerous","It proves the Globe is very old","It explains why the Great Fire started"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"No thatched roofs had been allowed since the Great Fire, showing the rebuild’s faithfulness."}]},{"id":"y032","title":"Macbeth: The Witches’ Prophecy","kind":"Retelling","theme":"castle","author":"Retold from William Shakespeare","text":["The battle was over, and the heath was thick with mist. Two Scottish captains, Macbeth and Banquo, were riding home from victory when three strange figures rose out of the fog in front of them. They were ragged and withered, with beards on their chins, and they seemed to belong neither to the earth nor to the air.","“All hail, Macbeth,” said the first, “Thane of Glamis!”","“All hail, Macbeth,” said the second, “Thane of Cawdor!”","“All hail, Macbeth,” said the third, “that shalt be king hereafter!”","Macbeth went pale. He was already Thane of Glamis, but the Thane of Cawdor was alive, and the king, Duncan, was healthy and had sons of his own. Banquo, laughing nervously, asked what the future held for him. “Thou shalt get kings, though thou be none,” the witches replied – his children would be kings, though he would not. Then they vanished like bubbles into the air.","Moments later, messengers arrived from King Duncan. The Thane of Cawdor, they announced, had been found guilty of treason, and the king had given his title to Macbeth as a reward for his bravery.","Banquo was uneasy. Sometimes, he warned, evil creatures tell us small truths to win our trust, only to betray us in matters of the deepest importance. But Macbeth hardly heard him. The second prophecy had come true within the hour. Why not the third? And a thought crept into his mind so dark that it made his hair stand on end and his heart knock against his ribs: if fate meant him to be king, perhaps fate could be helped along.","He wrote to his wife at once. Lady Macbeth read the letter alone in their castle at Inverness. She did not tremble. She feared only that her husband was ‘too full o’ th’ milk of human kindness’ to seize the crown. When news came that King Duncan himself would stay at their castle that very night, she knew what must be done.","So began one of the darkest stories ever written: a tale of ambition, murder and guilt, in which a brave soldier becomes a tyrant, sleep becomes impossible, and the witches’ promises turn out to be traps. As one of the witches had chanted on the misty heath: ‘Fair is foul, and foul is fair.’ In Macbeth’s world, nothing would ever be quite what it seemed again."],"glossary":{"heath":"An area of open, wild land.","withered":"Dried up and shrivelled.","thane":"A Scottish lord who held land from the king.","hereafter":"In the future.","treason":"The crime of betraying your country or ruler.","prophecy":"A prediction of what will happen in the future.","ambition":"A strong desire to achieve something, such as power.","tyrant":"A cruel ruler with total power.","foul":"Wicked or disgusting."},"questions":[{"q":"What did the third witch predict?","o":["Macbeth would be Thane of Cawdor","Macbeth would become king","Banquo would become king","Duncan would die in battle"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“That shalt be king hereafter!”"},{"q":"What did the witches predict for Banquo?","o":["He would be king","His children would be kings","He would die young","He would become Thane of Glamis"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Thou shalt get kings, though thou be none.”"},{"q":"Why did the arrival of the messengers affect Macbeth so strongly?","o":["They brought bad news about his wife","The second prophecy came true at once, making him believe the third could too","They arrested him","They told him Banquo was a traitor"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“The second prophecy had come true within the hour. Why not the third?”"},{"q":"What is Banquo warning Macbeth about?","o":["The weather","That evil may tell small truths to trick people into ruin","That the king is ill","That the messengers are lying"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Evil creatures “tell us small truths to win our trust, only to betray us”."},{"q":"What does Lady Macbeth fear about her husband?","o":["He is too cruel","He is too kind to seize the crown","He will run away","He will tell the king"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He is “too full o’ th’ milk of human kindness”."},{"q":"What does ‘Fair is foul, and foul is fair’ suggest about the play?","o":["The weather is changeable","Things may seem good but be evil, and appearances cannot be trusted","Everyone is fair","The witches are kind"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"The final sentence explains that “nothing would ever be quite what it seemed”."}]},{"id":"y033","title":"Invictus","kind":"Poem","theme":"mountain","author":"William Ernest Henley (1875)","text":["Out of the night that covers me,\nBlack as the pit from pole to pole,\nI thank whatever gods may be\nFor my unconquerable soul.","In the fell clutch of circumstance\nI have not winced nor cried aloud.\nUnder the bludgeonings of chance\nMy head is bloody, but unbowed.","Beyond this place of wrath and tears\nLooms but the Horror of the shade,\nAnd yet the menace of the years\nFinds and shall find me unafraid.","It matters not how strait the gate,\nHow charged with punishments the scroll,\nI am the master of my fate,\nI am the captain of my soul."],"glossary":{"unconquerable":"Impossible to defeat.","fell":"Here, an old word meaning cruel or deadly.","circumstance":"The events and situation of your life.","winced":"Pulled a face because of pain.","bludgeonings":"Heavy blows, as if from a club.","unbowed":"Not bent down; not defeated.","wrath":"Great anger.","menace":"A threat or danger.","strait":"Here, an old word meaning narrow and difficult."},"questions":[{"q":"What does the speaker thank the gods for?","o":["His family","His unconquerable soul","Good weather","His wealth"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“For my unconquerable soul.”"},{"q":"What does “Black as the pit from pole to pole” suggest about the speaker’s situation?","o":["He is on holiday","He is surrounded by complete darkness and suffering","He is near the North Pole","He is in a mine"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Total blackness from one end of the world to the other suggests overwhelming hardship."},{"q":"“My head is bloody, but unbowed.” What does this show?","o":["He has been hurt but has not given up","He has won a fight","He is bowing to someone","He has a headache"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"‘Bloody’ shows suffering; ‘unbowed’ shows he refuses to be defeated."},{"q":"How does the speaker feel about the future and the “menace of the years”?","o":["Terrified","Unafraid","Excited","Bored"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The years find him “unafraid”."},{"q":"What does the speaker mean by “I am the master of my fate”?","o":["He can see the future","Whatever happens, he controls how he responds and keeps his courage","He is a ship’s captain","He is a king"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Despite hardship, he decides his own attitude and strength."},{"q":"Which word best describes the tone of the poem?","o":["Defiant","Playful","Bored","Gentle"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"The speaker stands firm against suffering, refusing to give in."}]},{"id":"y034","title":"The Secret Life of Octopuses","kind":"Science","theme":"sea","text":["If you wanted to design an alien, you could hardly do better than an octopus. It has three hearts, blue blood and eight arms lined with suckers that can taste what they touch. It can change the colour and texture of its skin in a fraction of a second, squeeze its whole body through a gap the size of a coin and squirt a cloud of ink to escape a predator. Most remarkable of all, it is one of the most intelligent animals in the sea.","An octopus has around 500 million nerve cells, a similar number to a dog. But unlike a dog, about two-thirds of those cells are not in its head. They are in its arms. Each arm can, to some extent, think for itself, exploring and reacting to its surroundings without waiting for instructions from the brain. Scientists have watched an arm continue to reach for food even after being separated from the body.","Octopuses are famous problem-solvers. In aquariums they have learned to unscrew jars to reach the crabs inside, and some have even escaped from their tanks at night, crossed the floor to a neighbouring tank to eat the fish, and returned home before morning. In New Zealand, an octopus named Inky made headlines in 2016 when he slipped out of his tank, slid across the floor and escaped down a drainpipe into the sea.","In the wild, octopuses use tools – which was once thought to be something only humans and a few other animals could do. Veined octopuses in Indonesia have been filmed collecting coconut shells, carrying them across the sea floor and assembling them into a shelter when danger threatens.","Their camouflage is even more astonishing. Octopus skin contains thousands of special cells called chromatophores, each filled with coloured pigment, which can be opened and closed by tiny muscles. By controlling them, an octopus can match the colour and pattern of the rocks, sand or coral around it. It can also raise little bumps on its skin to copy the texture of seaweed or stone. The mimic octopus goes further still, imitating the shape and movement of poisonous animals such as lionfish and sea snakes.","Despite their intelligence, most octopuses live short lives – often only one or two years. Females guard their eggs for weeks or months without eating, and usually die soon after they hatch. Perhaps that is the strangest thing of all: an animal clever enough to escape from an aquarium, but with almost no time to use what it has learned."],"glossary":{"texture":"How the surface of something feels or looks.","predator":"An animal that hunts other animals.","remarkable":"Unusual and worth noticing.","aquariums":"Tanks or buildings where water animals are kept.","assembling":"Putting parts together.","camouflage":"Colours or patterns that help an animal hide.","pigment":"A substance that gives something its colour.","mimic":"An animal that copies the appearance of another."},"questions":[{"q":"How many hearts does an octopus have?","o":["One","Two","Three","Eight"],"a":2,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“It has three hearts, blue blood and eight arms.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer say “you could hardly do better than an octopus” if you were designing an alien?","o":["Octopuses come from space","Octopuses have so many strange features that they seem otherworldly","Octopuses are green","Octopuses live on the Moon"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"The list of unusual features makes the octopus seem like an alien."},{"q":"What is unusual about where an octopus’s nerve cells are?","o":["They are all in its head","About two-thirds are in its arms","It has none","They are in its ink sac"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“About two-thirds of those cells are not in its head. They are in its arms.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer include the story of Inky?","o":["To give a real example of an octopus’s cleverness","To show octopuses are dangerous","To advertise an aquarium","To explain camouflage"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"Inky’s escape is evidence for the claim that octopuses are problem-solvers."},{"q":"How do veined octopuses show they can use tools?","o":["They build shelters from coconut shells","They use sticks to catch fish","They throw stones","They open jars"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They collect coconut shells and assemble them into a shelter."},{"q":"What does the writer find “strangest of all”?","o":["Octopuses have blue blood","Such a clever animal lives for only a very short time","Octopuses can taste with suckers","Octopuses eat crabs"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"An intelligent animal has “almost no time to use what it has learned.”"}]},{"id":"y035","title":"Malala Yousafzai: One Child, One Pen","kind":"Biography","theme":"books","text":["Malala Yousafzai was born in 1997 in the Swat Valley in Pakistan, a region of green mountains and rivers that was once a popular holiday destination. Her father, Ziauddin, ran a school, and he believed passionately that girls had as much right to an education as boys. Malala grew up surrounded by books and classrooms, and she loved learning.","When Malala was ten, the Taliban, an extremist group, began to take control of the Swat Valley. They banned television and music, and in 2009 they ordered that girls must stop going to school. Hundreds of girls’ schools were destroyed.","Malala refused to stay silent. At just eleven years old, she began writing an anonymous diary for the BBC’s Urdu website, describing what life was like under the Taliban and how much she missed school. Later, she began speaking openly in interviews, insisting that girls should be allowed to learn. Her courage made her well known in Pakistan – and made her a target.","On 9 October 2012, when Malala was fifteen, a gunman stopped her school bus and asked, “Who is Malala?” He then shot her in the head. Two of her friends were also injured. Malala was flown to Birmingham in England, where doctors at the Queen Elizabeth Hospital saved her life. She needed several operations, and it took months for her to recover.","The attack was meant to silence her. It did the opposite. People around the world were horrified, and millions signed petitions demanding education for all children. On her sixteenth birthday, Malala spoke at the United Nations in New York. “One child, one teacher, one book and one pen can change the world,” she said. “Education is the only solution.”","In 2014, at the age of seventeen, Malala became the youngest person ever to be awarded the Nobel Peace Prize. She and her father set up the Malala Fund, a charity that works for every girl’s right to twelve years of free, safe, quality education. She went on to study at the University of Oxford.","Today, millions of girls around the world still cannot go to school. But because one schoolgirl refused to be silenced, many more people are listening."],"glossary":{"destination":"A place people travel to.","passionately":"With very strong feeling.","extremist":"Holding very extreme or violent views.","anonymous":"Without a name being given.","target":"A person chosen to be attacked.","petitions":"Written requests signed by many people asking for change.","solution":"A way of solving a problem."},"questions":[{"q":"What did Malala’s father believe?","o":["Girls should stay at home","Girls had as much right to education as boys","Schools should be closed","Only boys should learn"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He believed “girls had as much right to an education as boys”."},{"q":"How did Malala first speak out against the Taliban?","o":["By writing an anonymous diary for the BBC","By joining the army","By moving to England","By appearing on television in America"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"She wrote “an anonymous diary for the BBC’s Urdu website”."},{"q":"Why might Malala have written her diary anonymously at first?","o":["She was shy about her writing","It would have been dangerous for her if the Taliban knew who she was","She did not have a name","The BBC asked her to"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Speaking out openly later “made her a target”, showing the danger."},{"q":"“The attack was meant to silence her. It did the opposite.” What does this mean?","o":["Malala lost her voice","The attack made her message heard more widely than ever","Malala stopped speaking","Nobody heard about the attack"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"People worldwide were horrified and she spoke at the United Nations."},{"q":"Which achievement made Malala the youngest of all time?","o":["Studying at Oxford","Winning the Nobel Peace Prize","Speaking at the United Nations","Writing a diary"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"She was “the youngest person ever to be awarded the Nobel Peace Prize”."},{"q":"What is the main message of Malala’s story?","o":["Education is not important","One brave person can inspire change, and education is worth fighting for","Travel is dangerous","Only adults can make a difference"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"Her courage led many more people to support girls’ education."}]},{"id":"y036","title":"The Return of the Beaver","kind":"Non-fiction","theme":"forest","text":["For about four hundred years, there were no wild beavers in Britain. They were hunted to extinction, for their thick, waterproof fur, for their meat and for castoreum, an oily substance from their bodies that was used in medicines and perfumes. By the sixteenth century, the animal that had shaped British rivers for thousands of years had vanished.","Now beavers are back. In 2009, a small group was released in Knapdale Forest in Scotland as an official trial. Around the same time, a family of beavers mysteriously appeared on the River Otter in Devon – nobody knows exactly how they got there. Instead of removing them, the government allowed scientists to study them. In 2020, the Devon beavers became the first wild animals to be officially given the right to remain in England after being extinct.","Beavers are often called ecosystem engineers, because few animals change their surroundings so dramatically. Using their powerful orange teeth, which never stop growing, they fell trees and drag branches into streams to build dams. Behind the dams, ponds form, where beavers build their homes, called lodges, with underwater entrances that keep them safe from predators.","These ponds are good news for many other species. Studies in Devon and Scotland have found more frogs, insects, fish, birds and bats near beaver sites. The dams also act like sponges, slowing the flow of water. After heavy rain, they hold water back and release it gradually, which can reduce flooding in towns downstream. During dry spells, the ponds keep land wet. They even trap soil and pollution, leaving the water cleaner.","Not everyone is delighted, however. Some farmers worry that beaver dams will flood their fields, and that beavers will damage valuable trees. Experts say these problems can usually be managed – for example, by fitting pipes through dams to control water levels or by protecting precious trees with wire mesh. In a few cases, beavers may need to be moved.","The return of the beaver is part of a wider movement called rewilding, which aims to restore natural processes and bring back missing species. Supporters argue that, after centuries of damaging nature, we have a chance to repair it. The beaver, they say, is one of our best helpers – it works for free, and it never takes a day off."],"glossary":{"extinction":"When a type of animal or plant no longer exists anywhere.","castoreum":"An oily substance from beavers, once used in medicine and perfume.","ecosystem":"All the living things in an area and how they affect each other.","dramatically":"In a sudden or striking way.","lodges":"The homes beavers build out of sticks and mud.","predators":"Animals that hunt other animals.","gradually":"Slowly, over time.","rewilding":"Returning land to a natural state and bringing back missing wildlife.","restore":"To bring back or return to an earlier condition."},"questions":[{"q":"Why were beavers hunted to extinction in Britain?","o":["They attacked people","For their fur, meat and castoreum","They carried disease","They ate crops"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They were hunted “for their thick, waterproof fur, for their meat and for castoreum”."},{"q":"What was unusual about the beavers on the River Otter?","o":["They were released by the government","Nobody knows how they got there","They were brought from Scotland","They were kept in a zoo"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They “mysteriously appeared … nobody knows exactly how they got there”."},{"q":"Why are beavers called ‘ecosystem engineers’?","o":["They build machines","They change their surroundings dramatically by building dams","They study ecosystems","They work in factories"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“Few animals change their surroundings so dramatically.”"},{"q":"How can beaver dams reduce flooding?","o":["By drinking the water","By holding water back after heavy rain and releasing it slowly","By making rivers deeper","By stopping rain falling"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Dams “hold water back and release it gradually”."},{"q":"Why does the writer include the paragraph about farmers?","o":["To give a balanced view by showing problems as well as benefits","To prove beavers should be removed","To advertise farms","To describe beaver food"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It presents concerns and then explains how they can be managed."},{"q":"What is the tone of the final sentence?","o":["Angry and worried","Light-hearted and positive","Sad and hopeless","Confusing"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Calling beavers helpers who “never take a day off” is playful and approving."}]},{"id":"y037","title":"Signal Lost","kind":"Science fiction","theme":"space","text":["The message from Earth arrived at 06:14 ship time, which was the first strange thing. Messages never arrived at 06:14. They came at 09:00 exactly, every third day, carefully timed to reach the research ship Calypso as it drifted beyond the orbit of Jupiter.","The second strange thing was that it was empty.","Juno Okafor, who was fourteen and the youngest person aboard by eleven years, found it while her mother was asleep. She was supposed to be doing her maths module. Instead she was in the communications bay, watching the long-range antenna turn its slow circles against the stars, because it was the only place on the ship where you could see outside without a screen in the way.","The message had a header: proper Mission Control codes, a timestamp, a priority marker. But where the content should have been, there was nothing. Just silence, eleven minutes and four seconds long.","She played it again, turning up the volume until the speakers hissed. And there – buried so deep in the static that she almost missed it – was a sound. Tap. Tap-tap. Tap. A pause. Then the same pattern again.","Juno had learned Morse code when she was eight, from a book her grandfather had given her, because she had liked the idea of a secret language made of nothing but long and short. She grabbed a stylus and began to write.","It took her an hour to be sure. The message was not from Mission Control at all. It had piggybacked on the signal from somewhere else, somewhere further out. And it said the same three words over and over again.","WE HEAR YOU.","Juno sat back in her chair. On the screen, Jupiter hung like a bruised orange in the dark, its moons scattered around it like spilled beads. For a long time she did not move. Then she reached for the transmitter, hesitated, and pulled her hand back.","Who should answer first? Mission Control? Her mother? The captain?","Or her?","Out beyond the ship, beyond the planets, something was listening – and for the moment, only one person in the whole human race knew it. Juno took a slow breath, pressed the key, and tapped out the only reply that seemed polite.","HELLO."],"glossary":{"orbit":"The curved path of an object around a planet or star.","module":"One section of a course of study.","antenna":"A device for sending or receiving radio signals.","header":"Information at the start of a message, such as who sent it.","static":"Crackling noise on a radio or speaker.","stylus":"A pen-like tool used to write on a screen.","piggybacked":"Was carried along on top of something else.","transmitter":"A device that sends out radio signals.","hesitated":"Paused before doing something because of uncertainty."},"questions":[{"q":"Why was it strange that the message arrived at 06:14?","o":["Messages always came at 09:00 every third day","The ship was asleep","It was Juno’s birthday","Earth had stopped sending messages"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Messages “came at 09:00 exactly, every third day”."},{"q":"Why was Juno in the communications bay?","o":["She was ordered to be there","It was the only place she could see outside without a screen","She was fixing the antenna","She was hiding from her mother"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It was “the only place on the ship where you could see outside without a screen in the way”."},{"q":"Why was it important that Juno knew Morse code?","o":["It let her understand the hidden message","It helped her steer the ship","It helped her do her maths","It let her talk to her grandfather"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The tapping pattern was Morse; she decoded it into words."},{"q":"How does the writer create suspense in the middle of the story?","o":["Using very short paragraphs such as “The second strange thing was that it was empty.”","By describing Juno’s breakfast","By explaining how antennas work in detail","By giving a happy ending at once"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"Short, separate paragraphs reveal mysteries one at a time."},{"q":"“Jupiter hung like a bruised orange in the dark.” What does this image suggest?","o":["Jupiter is small and fresh","Jupiter looks dark-streaked, heavy and strangely still","Jupiter is made of fruit","Jupiter is bright and white"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"A bruised orange suggests its colour and dark bands, hanging motionless in space."},{"q":"Why does Juno hesitate before replying?","o":["She does not know how to use the transmitter","She realises the reply is hugely important and wonders who should answer first","She is afraid of her mother","The transmitter is broken"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"She asks herself whether Mission Control, her mother, the captain – or she – should answer."}]},{"id":"y038","title":"Teenager’s Metal Detector Uncovers Roman Treasure","kind":"Newspaper report","theme":"rome","text":["A thirteen-year-old from Shropshire has discovered a hoard of more than 300 Roman coins buried in a farmer’s field – on only his second outing with a metal detector.","Oliver Brennan was searching a field near the village of Wroxford with his grandfather, Dennis, last Saturday afternoon when his detector gave what he described as “the loudest beep I’ve ever heard”.","“I thought it was going to be a can or an old bit of tractor,” said Oliver, a pupil at Wroxford Academy. “Then I dug down about a foot and saw the edge of a pot. When I pulled out the first coin, my hands were shaking. Grandad just kept saying, ‘Don’t touch any more, don’t touch any more!’”","Following the rules for treasure finds, the pair stopped digging, marked the spot and reported the discovery to the local Finds Liaison Officer, who works for the Portable Antiquities Scheme. Archaeologists then carefully excavated the clay pot, which had been cracked by a plough but was otherwise intact.","Experts believe the coins date from between AD 260 and AD 275, a troubled time when the Roman Empire was divided and Britain was part of a breakaway state known as the Gallic Empire. Many of the coins show the faces of emperors who ruled for only a few months.","“Hoards like this were often buried by people who intended to come back for them,” explained Dr Helen Okoro, an archaeologist at the county museum. “Perhaps the owner hid their savings during a time of danger and never returned. Each coin tells us something about the economy and politics of Roman Britain. This is a really significant find for the region.”","Under the Treasure Act 1996, finds of more than two coins that are at least 300 years old must be reported to the local coroner, who decides whether they count as treasure. If they do, a museum can acquire the hoard, and a reward is shared between the finder and the landowner.","Farmer Gwen Pryce, who owns the field, said she was delighted. “I’ve been ploughing that field for thirty years and never knew what was underneath,” she said. “I hope the coins go to a museum where everybody can see them.”","Oliver says he hopes to become an archaeologist. “Everyone thinks history is in books,” he said. “But some of it is still in the ground, waiting.”"],"glossary":{"hoard":"A store of valuable things hidden away.","liaison":"Communication between people or groups; here, an officer who links finders with experts.","antiquities":"Objects from long ago.","excavated":"Carefully dug up.","intact":"Whole and undamaged.","emperors":"Rulers of an empire.","economy":"The money, trade and wealth of a place.","coroner":"An official who investigates deaths and decides whether finds are treasure.","acquire":"To get or obtain."},"questions":[{"q":"How many coins were found?","o":["About 30","More than 300","About 3,000","Exactly 260"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“A hoard of more than 300 Roman coins.”"},{"q":"Why did Oliver’s grandad keep saying “Don’t touch any more”?","o":["The coins were dirty","They needed to follow the rules and let experts excavate the find properly","He wanted the coins himself","The pot was hot"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"They then stopped digging and reported it, following the rules for treasure finds."},{"q":"Why might the owner have buried the coins?","o":["To grow money trees","To hide their savings during a dangerous time","Because the coins were fake","To pay the farmer"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Dr Okoro suggests the owner “hid their savings during a time of danger”."},{"q":"Which feature shows this text is a newspaper report?","o":["It rhymes","It has a headline, quotations from people involved and facts about who, what, when and where","It is written as a diary","It has a moral"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"Reports use headlines, quotes and key facts in the opening paragraph."},{"q":"Why does the reporter include the paragraph about the Treasure Act?","o":["To explain the legal rules about finds like this","To criticise Oliver","To describe Roman life","To advertise metal detectors"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"It informs readers about what must happen to such finds."},{"q":"What does Oliver mean when he says some history “is still in the ground, waiting”?","o":["Many historical objects have not been discovered yet","History books are buried","Farmers bury books","Archaeology is boring"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"His own find shows there is still much to be discovered underground."}]},{"id":"y039","title":"The Ravens of the Tower","kind":"Non-fiction","theme":"castle","text":["Among the crown jewels, the ancient armour and the grim stories of prisoners, the Tower of London keeps a different kind of treasure: at least six large black birds. They are ravens, and according to legend, they are all that stands between Britain and disaster.","The legend says that if the ravens ever leave the Tower, the Tower will crumble and the kingdom will fall. Nobody knows exactly when this story began. One popular version claims that King Charles II, in the 1600s, wanted the birds removed because they were bothering his astronomer, who was trying to observe the stars from one of the turrets. When he was warned of the prophecy, the King changed his mind and ordered that six ravens must always be kept at the Tower. Historians point out, however, that there is little evidence for this story, and that the legend may only date from the Victorian era.","Whatever its origins, the tradition is taken seriously. The Tower keeps seven ravens – the required six, plus a spare. They are looked after by one of the Yeoman Warders, known as the Ravenmaster. The ravens live in spacious enclosures by the Wakefield Tower and are fed a diet of raw meat, including mice, chicks and rats, as well as biscuits soaked in blood. Each has its own name and personality; past residents have included Merlina, Jubilee, Harris and Poppy.","To stop them flying away, the ravens’ flight feathers on one wing are trimmed. This does not hurt, in the same way that cutting your hair does not hurt, and the feathers grow back when they moult each year. The trimming keeps them grounded enough to stay, but they can still hop, flap and make short flights around the grounds.","Ravens are among the most intelligent birds in the world. They can solve puzzles, use tools and even imitate human speech. At the Tower, they have been known to steal sandwiches from tourists, bury food for later and play tricks on the warders. Occasionally, one has gone missing. A raven called Grog was last seen outside a pub in east London in 1981, apparently having decided that he preferred a different life.","During the Second World War, the Tower’s ravens almost all died, some from the shock of the bombing. Before the Tower reopened to the public in 1946, new ravens were brought in to make sure the legend was respected. So far, the kingdom has not fallen. Nobody is in a hurry to test what would happen if the ravens left."],"glossary":{"legend":"An old story that may or may not be true.","astronomer":"A scientist who studies the stars and planets.","turrets":"Small towers on a castle.","prophecy":"A prediction of what will happen.","era":"A period of time in history.","tradition":"A custom passed down over many years.","spacious":"Having lots of room.","moult":"To lose old feathers so new ones can grow.","imitate":"To copy."},"questions":[{"q":"According to legend, what will happen if the ravens leave the Tower?","o":["The crown jewels will be stolen","The Tower will crumble and the kingdom will fall","It will rain for a year","The King will become ill"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The Tower will crumble and the kingdom will fall.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer say historians doubt the Charles II story?","o":["There is little evidence for it and the legend may be Victorian","Charles II hated birds","The astronomer did not exist","Ravens did not live in England then"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“There is little evidence for this story … the legend may only date from the Victorian era.”"},{"q":"Why does the Tower keep seven ravens instead of six?","o":["Seven is a lucky number","To have a spare in case one is lost","The King ordered seven","They lay eggs"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“The required six, plus a spare.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer compare trimming feathers to cutting hair?","o":["To show it is painless","To show ravens visit hairdressers","To show feathers never grow back","To show it is dangerous"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Like hair, feathers can be trimmed without hurting and grow back."},{"q":"What does the story of Grog suggest about ravens?","o":["They are always obedient","They are independent and can make their own choices","They like beer","They cannot fly"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He seemed to have “decided that he preferred a different life”."},{"q":"What is the tone of the final sentence?","o":["Gently humorous","Furious","Tragic","Scientific"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"It jokes that nobody wants to risk testing the legend."}]},{"id":"y040","title":"A Recipe for Disaster","kind":"Story","theme":"cottage","text":["Mum’s birthday fell on a Saturday, and Leo and his little sister Ivy had a plan. They would wake up early, creep downstairs and make her a cake – a real one, from scratch, with no help from anybody. They had watched enough baking programmes to know what they were doing. How hard could it be?","At six o’clock, Leo opened Grandma’s old recipe book on the kitchen table. The page for Victoria sponge was splattered with ancient stains, which Leo took as a sign of quality.","“Two hundred grams of butter,” he read. Ivy found the butter in the fridge. It was rock hard. They tried to mash it with a fork, then a potato masher, and finally put it in the microwave, where it turned into a yellow lake.","“That’s fine,” said Leo. “It’s melted butter. It’s still butter.”","“Two hundred grams of caster sugar.” They did not have caster sugar. They had icing sugar, which Ivy said was basically the same but posher. When she tipped it into the bowl, a sweet white cloud rose up and settled over everything, including the cat.","Four eggs went in next, along with a certain amount of shell. They spent ten minutes fishing out the pieces with a teaspoon, then gave up and called it ‘extra crunch’. The flour was self-raising, which seemed like good news. Leo decided that if a little raising was good, a lot would be better, and added three teaspoons of baking powder for luck.","The mixture went into the tin. The tin went into the oven. Leo turned the dial to what he hoped was the right temperature, and they sat on the floor to watch through the glass door.","For twenty minutes, nothing happened. Then the cake began to rise. It rose to the top of the tin. It rose over the top of the tin. It kept rising, like something in a science-fiction film, until a great golden mushroom was pressing against the top of the oven and a smell of burning sugar filled the kitchen.","That was when the smoke alarm went off.","Mum appeared in the doorway in her dressing gown, hair everywhere, and took in the scene: the flour, the yellow lake of butter, the white cat, the smoke, and two children standing guiltily in front of an oven that appeared to be eating a cake.","For a terrible moment, nobody said anything. Then Mum began to laugh. She laughed until she had to hold onto the door frame, until tears ran down her face.","“That,” she said, when she could finally speak, “is the best present I’ve ever been given.”","They had cake for breakfast anyway. The middle was quite good, if you avoided the crunchy bits."],"glossary":{"splattered":"Covered with drops or splashes.","ancient":"Very old.","posher":"More upmarket or fancy (informal).","guiltily":"In a way that shows you know you have done something wrong."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did Leo think the stained recipe page was a good sign?","o":["He thought the stains showed the recipe was well used and good","He liked dirty books","The stains were part of the recipe","He did not notice them"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"He “took [the stains] as a sign of quality”."},{"q":"What happened to the butter in the microwave?","o":["It exploded","It turned into a yellow lake","It stayed hard","It burned"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It “turned into a yellow lake”."},{"q":"How does the writer create humour in the paragraph about the sugar?","o":["By describing a sweet white cloud settling over everything, including the cat","By describing the sugar’s price","By listing other sugars","By making Ivy cry"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"The image of the cat covered in icing sugar is comic."},{"q":"Why did the cake rise so much?","o":["The oven was broken","Leo added extra baking powder to self-raising flour","They used too many eggs","The cat sat on it"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He added three teaspoons of baking powder thinking “a lot would be better”."},{"q":"Why is the one-sentence paragraph “That was when the smoke alarm went off.” effective?","o":["It is a sudden, short climax after a long build-up","It explains how ovens work","It introduces Grandma","It slows the story down"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"A short sentence on its own creates a comic punchline."},{"q":"Why does Mum say it is the best present she has ever been given?","o":["She loves burnt cake","She is touched by her children’s effort and finds the scene joyful","She is being sarcastic and is very angry","She wanted a new oven"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"She laughs until she cries – the love and effort matter more than the result."}]},{"id":"y041","title":"Jabberwocky","kind":"Poem","theme":"forest","author":"Lewis Carroll (1871)","text":["’Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\nDid gyre and gimble in the wabe:\nAll mimsy were the borogoves,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabe.","“Beware the Jabberwock, my son!\nThe jaws that bite, the claws that catch!\nBeware the Jubjub bird, and shun\nThe frumious Bandersnatch!”","He took his vorpal sword in hand;\nLong time the manxome foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Tumtum tree\nAnd stood awhile in thought.","And, as in uffish thought he stood,\nThe Jabberwock, with eyes of flame,\nCame whiffling through the tulgey wood,\nAnd burbled as it came!","One, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe vorpal blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its head\nHe went galumphing back.","“And hast thou slain the Jabberwock?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!”\nHe chortled in his joy.","’Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\nDid gyre and gimble in the wabe:\nAll mimsy were the borogoves,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabe."],"glossary":{"slithy":"A made-up word. Carroll said it combines ‘lithe’ and ‘slimy’.","mimsy":"A made-up word. Carroll said it combines ‘flimsy’ and ‘miserable’.","shun":"To avoid deliberately.","frumious":"A made-up word combining ‘fuming’ and ‘furious’.","vorpal":"A made-up word, suggesting very sharp or deadly.","foe":"An enemy.","galumphing":"A made-up word, probably mixing ‘gallop’ and ‘triumphant’.","slain":"Killed.","chortled":"A made-up word combining ‘chuckle’ and ‘snort’; it is now a real word."},"questions":[{"q":"What is the young man warned about in the second stanza?","o":["The Jabberwock, the Jubjub bird and the Bandersnatch","The Tumtum tree","The slithy toves","His father"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Beware the Jabberwock … Beware the Jubjub bird … The frumious Bandersnatch!”"},{"q":"Why can we understand the poem even though many words are made up?","o":["The made-up words sound like real words and the story follows a familiar pattern","The words are all in a dictionary","The poem has pictures","It is written in French"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Sounds such as ‘slithy’ and ‘galumphing’ suggest meanings, and the hero-slays-monster plot is familiar."},{"q":"Which words in stanza five are onomatopoeic?","o":["“snicker-snack”","“One, two!”","“dead”","“head”"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"‘Snicker-snack’ imitates the sound of the sword slicing."},{"q":"How does the father feel when his son returns?","o":["Angry","Overjoyed","Frightened","Bored"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He calls it a “frabjous day” and “chortled in his joy”."},{"q":"Why does the poem end with the same stanza it began with?","o":["The poet ran out of ideas","It shows the world returning to its normal, peaceful state","It shows the monster is still alive","It is a mistake"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"Repeating the opening frames the adventure and shows calm returning."},{"q":"What kind of story does the poem tell?","o":["A love story","A heroic quest in which a young hero defeats a monster","A story about school","A mystery with no ending"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"A son is warned, seeks the beast, slays it and returns in triumph."}]},{"id":"y042","title":"Black Holes: Where Light Cannot Escape","kind":"Science","theme":"space","text":["Throw a ball into the air, and gravity pulls it back down. Throw it harder, and it goes higher before falling. If you could throw it at about eleven kilometres per second – thirty-three times the speed of sound – it would escape the Earth’s gravity altogether and never come back. This speed is called the escape velocity.","Now imagine an object so dense, with so much mass squeezed into so small a space, that its escape velocity is faster than the speed of light. Nothing can travel faster than light, so nothing – not even light itself – could ever escape. That object is a black hole.","Most black holes form when giant stars die. A star many times heavier than our Sun burns its fuel for millions of years, held up against its own gravity by the energy produced in its core. When the fuel runs out, the core suddenly collapses. The outer layers are blasted into space in a gigantic explosion called a supernova, while the core keeps collapsing, crushed smaller and smaller until it becomes a black hole.","The boundary around a black hole is called the event horizon. It is the point of no return: anything that crosses it is lost for ever. Close to a black hole, gravity changes so sharply over short distances that a falling object would be stretched long and thin, like spaghetti. Scientists, who have a sense of humour, call this ‘spaghettification’.","If black holes cannot be seen, how do we know they exist? Astronomers detect them by their effects. Stars orbiting an invisible object at tremendous speed reveal its presence, and gas falling towards a black hole heats up and glows brightly before it disappears. At the centre of our own galaxy, the Milky Way, stars race around something about four million times heavier than the Sun. It is a supermassive black hole called Sagittarius A*.","In 2019, scientists using a network of radio telescopes around the world released the first ever image of a black hole, in a galaxy 55 million light-years away. It showed a glowing orange ring surrounding a circle of perfect darkness – the shadow of the black hole itself.","Black holes may sound terrifying, but there is no need to worry: the nearest known one is more than a thousand light-years away. And if the Sun were somehow replaced by a black hole of the same mass, the Earth would carry on orbiting exactly as before. It would just get very cold and very dark."],"glossary":{"gravity":"The force that pulls objects towards each other.","velocity":"Speed in a particular direction.","dense":"Having a lot of mass packed into a small space.","mass":"The amount of matter in an object.","supernova":"A huge explosion of a dying star.","horizon":"A boundary or limit; the event horizon is the edge of a black hole.","astronomers":"Scientists who study stars and space.","galaxy":"A huge group of stars held together by gravity.","light-years":"The distance light travels in a year, about 9.5 trillion kilometres."},"questions":[{"q":"What is the escape velocity of the Earth?","o":["About 11 kilometres per second","The speed of light","The speed of sound","About 33 kilometres per second"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“About eleven kilometres per second.”"},{"q":"Why can nothing escape from a black hole?","o":["It is too cold","Its escape velocity is faster than the speed of light","It is surrounded by walls","It is too far away"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Its escape velocity is faster than the speed of light.”"},{"q":"What happens to the core of a giant star when its fuel runs out?","o":["It cools and becomes a planet","It suddenly collapses","It turns into the Sun","It splits into many stars"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“When the fuel runs out, the core suddenly collapses.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer mention that scientists “have a sense of humour”?","o":["Because ‘spaghettification’ is a playful name for a frightening effect","Because scientists tell jokes in space","Because black holes are funny","To show scientists are not serious"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"The jokey name contrasts with the terrifying idea it describes."},{"q":"How do astronomers find black holes if they cannot see them?","o":["By listening to them","By their effects, such as stars orbiting at high speed and glowing gas","By flying into them","By using ordinary cameras"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"“Astronomers detect them by their effects.”"},{"q":"What is the purpose of the final paragraph?","o":["To frighten the reader","To reassure the reader that black holes are not a danger to us","To explain how to make a black hole","To describe the 2019 image"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"It begins “there is no need to worry” and explains why."}]},{"id":"y043","title":"Deeds Not Words: The Suffragettes","kind":"History","theme":"city","text":["At the start of the twentieth century, no woman in Britain could vote in a general election. Women could own businesses, run households and work in factories, but they had no say in choosing the people who made the laws they had to obey. Many men – and some women – argued that politics was not a woman’s business, and that women were too emotional to make sensible decisions.","Women had been campaigning peacefully for the vote for decades. These campaigners, called suffragists, held meetings, wrote letters and presented petitions to Parliament signed by thousands of people. Their leader, Millicent Fawcett, believed that patient argument would eventually win. But progress was painfully slow.","In 1903, Emmeline Pankhurst and her daughters Christabel and Sylvia founded the Women’s Social and Political Union, or WSPU. Its members became known as suffragettes – a name first used by a newspaper as an insult, which they proudly adopted. Their motto was ‘Deeds not words’. If peaceful methods were ignored, they would make themselves impossible to ignore.","At first, suffragettes heckled politicians and chained themselves to railings. Later, their tactics became more extreme: they smashed shop windows, cut telephone wires, slashed paintings in galleries and set fire to empty buildings. Hundreds were arrested. In prison, many went on hunger strike. The government responded with force-feeding – holding the women down and pushing tubes down their throats – which caused public outrage. In 1913, Parliament passed a law nicknamed the ‘Cat and Mouse Act’, which allowed hunger strikers to be released until they recovered and then re-arrested.","In June 1913, a suffragette called Emily Wilding Davison ran onto the course during the Derby horse race and was struck by the King’s horse. She died four days later. Historians still debate whether she meant to die, or was trying to attach a suffragette scarf to the horse. Thousands lined the streets of London for her funeral.","When the First World War began in 1914, the suffragettes stopped their campaign to support the war effort. Women took over millions of jobs left by men – in munitions factories, on farms, on buses and in offices – proving beyond doubt what they were capable of. In 1918, women over thirty who met certain property conditions were given the vote. Ten years later, in 1928, women finally gained the vote on the same terms as men, at twenty-one.","Historians argue about which group did more to win the vote: the patient suffragists or the militant suffragettes. Perhaps the answer is that both were needed."],"glossary":{"campaigning":"Working in an organised way to achieve something.","petitions":"Written requests signed by many people.","adopted":"Took up and used as your own.","motto":"A short phrase that sums up a group’s beliefs.","heckled":"Interrupted a speaker with shouts.","tactics":"Planned methods for achieving something.","outrage":"Strong anger and shock.","munitions":"Weapons and ammunition.","militant":"Willing to use forceful or extreme methods."},"questions":[{"q":"What was the difference between suffragists and suffragettes?","o":["Suffragists campaigned peacefully; suffragettes used more extreme direct action","Suffragettes were men","Suffragists were against the vote","There was no difference"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"Suffragists used meetings and petitions; suffragettes followed ‘Deeds not words’."},{"q":"What does the motto ‘Deeds not words’ mean?","o":["Actions are more powerful than talking","Writing is better than speaking","Women should stay silent","Words are important"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Suffragettes believed action would force people to notice."},{"q":"How did the suffragettes respond to being called by a name meant as an insult?","o":["They were upset and stopped campaigning","They proudly adopted it","They sued the newspaper","They changed their name"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It was “first used by a newspaper as an insult, which they proudly adopted”."},{"q":"Why was the 1913 law nicknamed the ‘Cat and Mouse Act’?","o":["Suffragettes kept pets","Prisoners were released and re-arrested, like a cat playing with a mouse","It was about animal rights","It was passed by a cat lover"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Like a cat letting a mouse go then catching it again."},{"q":"How did the First World War help women win the vote?","o":["Women joined the army as generals","Women did vital work previously done by men, proving their abilities","Men stopped caring about elections","The war ended elections"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Women took over millions of jobs, “proving beyond doubt what they were capable of.”"},{"q":"What is the writer’s conclusion about who won the vote?","o":["Only the suffragettes","Only the suffragists","Both groups were probably needed","The government gave it freely"],"a":2,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"“Perhaps the answer is that both were needed.”"}]},{"id":"y044","title":"Grace Darling and the Rescue of the Forfarshire","kind":"Biography","theme":"sea","text":["In the early hours of 7 September 1838, a storm was raging off the coast of Northumberland. The waves were so huge that they hid the rocks of the Farne Islands, and the wind screamed around the Longstone Lighthouse, where William Darling lived with his wife and their twenty-two-year-old daughter, Grace.","At about a quarter to five, as dawn broke, Grace looked out of an upstairs window and saw something on Big Harcar, a jagged rock about a mile away. It was a wrecked ship. The steamship Forfarshire, travelling from Hull to Dundee with about sixty people on board, had lost power in the storm, been driven onto the rocks and broken in two. Half of the ship, with most of the passengers, had already been swept away. But Grace could see figures clinging to the rock.","The sea was too rough for the lifeboat from the mainland town of Seahouses to launch quickly. William Darling knew that if anyone was going to reach the survivors, it would have to be him – and he could not row the heavy wooden boat alone. Grace insisted on going with him.","Together they rowed nearly a mile through towering waves, taking the long way around to stay sheltered by the islands. When they reached the rock, William leapt ashore while Grace held the boat steady, keeping it from smashing against the rocks – a task requiring enormous strength and skill. Nine people were still alive. The little boat could only carry some of them, so they took a woman, an injured man and three crew members back to the lighthouse. Then William and two of the rescued sailors rowed back for the other four.","News of the rescue spread across the country with astonishing speed. Newspapers called Grace a heroine, and she became one of the first true celebrities of the Victorian age. Artists painted her portrait, poets wrote verses about her and people sent letters, gifts and even marriage proposals. A lock of her hair was sold as a souvenir. Queen Victoria herself sent fifty pounds as a reward.","Grace found the fame difficult. She was a quiet, modest woman who preferred her life at the lighthouse, and she never quite understood why so much attention was paid to her. Sadly, she did not live long to enjoy her honours. She died of tuberculosis in 1842, aged only twenty-six.","Today, a museum in the village of Bamburgh tells her story and displays the very boat, called a coble, in which she and her father made their famous rescue."],"glossary":{"raging":"Very violent or wild.","jagged":"Sharp and uneven.","survivors":"People who stay alive after a disaster.","insisted":"Said firmly that something must happen.","towering":"Very tall.","heroine":"A woman admired for her courage.","celebrities":"Famous people.","souvenir":"Something kept as a reminder of a person, place or event.","modest":"Not boasting about your achievements.","tuberculosis":"A serious disease that affects the lungs."},"questions":[{"q":"Where did Grace Darling live?","o":["In Hull","In the Longstone Lighthouse","On a ship","In London"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"She lived at “the Longstone Lighthouse”."},{"q":"Why could the lifeboat from Seahouses not help quickly?","o":["It was broken","The sea was too rough for it to launch quickly","Nobody knew about the wreck","It was too far away"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The sea was too rough for the lifeboat … to launch quickly.”"},{"q":"Why did Grace insist on going with her father?","o":["She wanted to be famous","He could not row the heavy boat alone","She wanted to see the ship","She was bored"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"William “could not row the heavy wooden boat alone”."},{"q":"What was Grace’s job when they reached the rock?","o":["Rowing back alone","Holding the boat steady so it did not smash on the rocks","Climbing the rock","Lighting a flare"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Grace held the boat steady, keeping it from smashing against the rocks.”"},{"q":"Which details show how famous Grace became?","o":["Portraits, poems, gifts, proposals and a reward from the Queen","She moved to London","She wrote a book","She became a ship captain"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"The fifth paragraph lists portraits, poems, letters, proposals and Queen Victoria’s reward."},{"q":"What does the text suggest about Grace’s character?","o":["She enjoyed fame and wanted more","She was brave but modest and uncomfortable with fame","She was careless","She disliked her father"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"She risked her life, yet was “quiet, modest” and found fame difficult."}]},{"id":"y045","title":"The Glass Bridge","kind":"Adventure","theme":"bridge","text":["The glass bridge hung across the gorge like a thread of ice. It was three hundred metres long and two hundred metres above the river, and when Hana stepped onto it she could see straight down through her own feet to the tiny silver line of water below.","“Don’t look down,” said her cousin Jun, who had already walked halfway across and was now taking selfies.","Hana looked down. The world tipped sideways. She grabbed the railing so hard her fingers hurt.","They were on holiday in the mountains of Hunan, in China, and the bridge was the most famous thing for miles. Thousands of people crossed it every day. Hana had watched them from the viewing platform – children laughing, grandparents strolling, a man in a suit eating noodles. It was perfectly safe. She knew it was perfectly safe. Her legs simply refused to believe her.","“You can go back,” said Mum, gently. “No one will mind.”","Hana thought about it. It would be easy to turn around. But she had spent the whole summer feeling frightened of things – of starting a new school in September, of being the new girl, of not knowing anyone. She was tired of being frightened.","She took one step. Then another. She fixed her eyes on the far side of the gorge, where green trees clung to the cliff, and counted her steps under her breath. Ten. Twenty. Thirty. A gust of wind hummed through the cables, and the bridge trembled very slightly, and she stopped, breathing fast.","An old woman was standing beside her. She was tiny, with white hair and a walking stick, and she was smiling. She pointed at Hana’s feet, then at her own, and said something in Mandarin that Hana didn’t understand. Then she held out her hand.","Hana took it. The old woman’s fingers were dry and warm and surprisingly strong. Together, step by careful step, they walked across the bridge. Halfway, Hana realised she was no longer counting. Three-quarters of the way, she looked down – just for a second – and saw a hawk gliding far below her, its wings spread wide over the river. It was the most beautiful thing she had ever seen.","On the far side, the old woman patted her hand and walked away before Hana could thank her.","“What did she say to you?” Hana asked Jun, who spoke much better Mandarin than she did.","Jun grinned. “She said, ‘Small steps still cross big bridges.’”","Three weeks later, standing at the gate of her new school, Hana remembered."],"glossary":{"gorge":"A deep, narrow valley with steep rocky sides.","railing":"A fence made of bars, used to stop people falling.","strolling":"Walking in a slow, relaxed way.","gust":"A sudden strong rush of wind.","cables":"Thick, strong wires or ropes.","trembled":"Shook slightly.","gliding":"Moving smoothly through the air without flapping."},"questions":[{"q":"How high above the river was the bridge?","o":["Three hundred metres","Two hundred metres","Fifty metres","One kilometre"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Two hundred metres above the river.”"},{"q":"“Her legs simply refused to believe her.” What does this suggest?","o":["Her legs were injured","She knew the bridge was safe, but her fear made her body freeze","She was lying","She wanted to run"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Her mind knows it is safe, but her body still reacts with fear."},{"q":"Why did Hana decide not to turn back?","o":["Jun dared her","She was tired of being frightened, including about her new school","Mum made her","She wanted a selfie"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“She had spent the whole summer feeling frightened of things … She was tired of being frightened.”"},{"q":"What changed when Hana walked with the old woman?","o":["She became more afraid","She stopped counting and even enjoyed the view","She ran across","She turned back"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"She was “no longer counting” and saw a hawk – “the most beautiful thing she had ever seen.”"},{"q":"What does the old woman’s saying mean?","o":["Only short people can cross bridges","Even huge challenges can be overcome one small step at a time","Bridges should be smaller","Walk quickly"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Hana crossed by taking small, careful steps."},{"q":"Why does the story end at the school gate?","o":["To show Hana uses the lesson from the bridge to face her new school","To describe the school building","To introduce Jun’s school","To show Hana forgot the bridge"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"The final line links the bridge back to her fear of starting a new school."}]},{"id":"y046","title":"Should Zoos Exist?","kind":"Discussion","theme":"jungle","text":["Every year, millions of people in the UK visit a zoo. For many children, it is the only chance they will ever have to see a real elephant, giraffe or tiger. But in recent years, more and more people have begun to ask whether it is right to keep wild animals in captivity at all. This article looks at both sides of the argument.","Supporters of zoos argue, first, that modern zoos play a vital role in conservation. Many species would already be extinct in the wild if zoos had not bred them. The Arabian oryx, for example, was saved after the last wild ones were hunted in the 1970s; animals bred in zoos were later released into protected areas. Today, good zoos run international breeding programmes for endangered animals and send money and experts to protect wild habitats.","Second, supporters say that zoos educate the public. Seeing an animal in real life creates a connection that no documentary can match. A child who has watched a gorilla mother cradle her baby may grow up caring about rainforests. Zoos also carry out scientific research that helps vets and conservationists in the wild.","Opponents, however, argue that no zoo can give a wild animal the life it was born for. In the wild, an elephant may walk many kilometres a day; a polar bear may roam an area larger than Wales. Even the biggest enclosure is tiny by comparison. Some zoo animals show signs of stress, such as pacing up and down the same path for hours or swaying from side to side.","Critics also point out that many popular zoo animals are not endangered at all, and that only a small number of zoo-bred animals are ever returned to the wild. They argue that conservation money would do more good if it were spent protecting habitats directly, and that people can learn about animals through films, virtual reality and wildlife trips instead.","Perhaps the answer lies somewhere in between. Many people believe that zoos should keep only animals that can live well in captivity, or species that genuinely need protection, and that they should be judged on how well their animals live – not on how many visitors they attract. Others think that, in the future, zoos may become more like sanctuaries for animals that cannot survive in the wild.","What do you think? Should zoos close their gates for good, or are they one of our best hopes for saving the planet’s wildlife?"],"glossary":{"captivity":"Being kept in a closed space and not free.","conservation":"Protecting animals, plants and nature.","extinct":"No longer existing anywhere.","endangered":"At risk of dying out.","habitats":"The natural homes of animals or plants.","documentary":"A film that gives facts about a real subject.","opponents":"People who disagree with something.","enclosure":"An area with a fence or wall where animals are kept.","sanctuaries":"Safe places where animals are protected."},"questions":[{"q":"Which animal is given as an example of a species saved by zoos?","o":["The polar bear","The Arabian oryx","The gorilla","The elephant"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The Arabian oryx … was saved.”"},{"q":"What is the second argument in favour of zoos?","o":["They make money","They educate the public","They are fun","They are cheap"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Second, supporters say that zoos educate the public.”"},{"q":"What evidence do opponents give that some zoo animals are stressed?","o":["They eat too much","They pace up and down or sway from side to side","They sleep all day","They escape"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They show signs such as “pacing up and down … or swaying”."},{"q":"How is this article different from a persuasive article?","o":["It presents both sides fairly before considering a middle view","It only argues against zoos","It tells a story","It uses no facts"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It explains arguments for and against, then suggests a balanced position."},{"q":"Why does the writer compare a polar bear’s range to Wales?","o":["Polar bears live in Wales","To show how huge the area is compared with an enclosure","To show Wales is cold","To give a map reference"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"A familiar large area helps readers grasp how much space the animals need."},{"q":"Why does the article end with a question?","o":["The writer does not know the facts","To encourage readers to form their own opinion","To confuse the reader","To introduce another article"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Discussion texts invite readers to decide for themselves."}]},{"id":"y047","title":"Diary of a Roman Soldier on Hadrian’s Wall","kind":"Diary","theme":"rome","text":["Day 1. Vindolanda fort, northern Britannia\nWe arrived today after nineteen days of marching from the port. My feet are raw and my cloak has not been dry since we left Gaul. They warned us about the weather in Britannia, but nobody warned us properly. It does not rain here so much as hang in the air, soaking you from every side at once.","Day 3\nI have been assigned to the First Cohort of Tungrians. Many of the men come from the same lands as me, near the Rhine, so at least I can understand the jokes. Our centurion, Aulus Verus, is strict but fair. He says that the Wall is the edge of the civilised world, and that beyond it live the Caledonians, who paint themselves blue and fight like wolves. Old Marcus, who has served here for twelve years, says the Caledonians mostly want to be left alone and that Aulus has never seen one.","Day 8\nFirst watch on the Wall itself. It is a marvel – fifteen feet high, built of solid stone, and it runs from one sea to the other, eighty Roman miles, with a small fort every mile and turrets between them. Emperor Hadrian ordered it built when I was a child. From the top you can see for miles: grey hills, grey sky, grey sheep. Nothing moved all day except a hawk and, once, a herd of red deer. I have never been so bored or so cold in my life.","Day 15\nGood news! A parcel arrived from my mother: two pairs of thick woollen socks, some underpants and a letter. I have never been so happy to see socks. Marcus says that some of the officers’ wives write letters to each other, inviting one another to birthday parties. I find it strange to think of parties at the end of the world.","Day 22\nWe went on patrol north of the Wall for the first time. Twenty of us, armed, with a scout who knows the tracks. We saw smoke from a village but no people. I kept my hand on my sword the whole time. When we came back through the gate, I realised I had been holding my breath for half a day.","Day 30\nIt is strange, but I am beginning to like it here. The bathhouse is warm, the bread is good, and there is a market outside the fort where local Britons sell leather, pottery and beer, which is terrible but cheap. I have learned some words of their language. Marcus says that after twenty-five years of service I will become a Roman citizen and be given land. Perhaps I will settle here, in this cold, wet, green country at the edge of the world. Stranger things have happened."],"glossary":{"Britannia":"The Roman name for Britain.","assigned":"Given a particular job or place.","cohort":"A unit of the Roman army of about 480 soldiers.","centurion":"An officer in charge of about eighty Roman soldiers.","civilised":"Having an organised, advanced society.","turrets":"Small towers.","patrol":"A group sent to watch an area to keep it safe.","scout":"A soldier sent ahead to gather information.","citizen":"A person with full rights in a country or empire."},"questions":[{"q":"How long did the soldier march from the port?","o":["Nine days","Nineteen days","Thirty days","A year"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“After nineteen days of marching from the port.”"},{"q":"How does the soldier describe British rain?","o":["It falls in heavy storms","It hangs in the air and soaks you from every side","It is warm","It never stops for a year"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“It does not rain here so much as hang in the air.”"},{"q":"What does Marcus’s comment about the Caledonians suggest?","o":["The centurion’s frightening description may be exaggerated","The Caledonians are very dangerous","Marcus is a Caledonian","Aulus is a great warrior"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Marcus says they mostly want to be left alone and Aulus has never seen one."},{"q":"Why does the writer repeat “grey” in the entry for Day 8?","o":["To show the soldier likes grey","To emphasise how dull and dreary the view is","Because there was a fire","To describe his uniform"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"“Grey hills, grey sky, grey sheep” stresses the monotony."},{"q":"Why was the soldier so pleased with his parcel?","o":["It contained money","It brought warm socks and news from home in a cold, lonely place","It contained a sword","It was from the Emperor"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"In the cold, he had “never been so happy to see socks”, and he got a letter from his mother."},{"q":"How do the soldier’s feelings change over the diary?","o":["He starts happy and ends miserable","He starts miserable and bored but grows to like Britannia","He never changes","He becomes angry with Marcus"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"From raw feet and boredom to “I am beginning to like it here.”"}]},{"id":"y048","title":"The Heart: Your Body’s Tireless Pump","kind":"Science","theme":"lab","text":["Make a fist. That is roughly the size of your heart. It sits in the middle of your chest, tilted slightly to the left, protected by your ribs. Since before you were born it has been beating, and it will beat around 100,000 times today without your ever having to think about it. Over a lifetime, that adds up to more than two and a half billion beats.","The heart is a muscle, but a very special one. Unlike the muscles in your arms and legs, it never gets tired. Its job is to pump blood around your body. Blood carries oxygen from your lungs and nutrients from your food to every cell, and carries away waste products such as carbon dioxide. Without a constant supply of oxygen, cells quickly die – brain cells within a few minutes.","Your heart is actually two pumps side by side. The right side receives blood that has travelled around the body and has used up most of its oxygen. It pumps this blood to the lungs, where carbon dioxide is breathed out and fresh oxygen is picked up. The oxygen-rich blood then returns to the left side of the heart, which pumps it out to the rest of the body. Because the left side has to push blood much further, its wall is thicker and stronger.","Each side has two chambers: an atrium at the top, which collects blood, and a ventricle below, which pumps it out. Between them are valves, little flaps that open to let blood through and snap shut to stop it flowing backwards. The ‘lub-dub’ sound a doctor hears through a stethoscope is the sound of these valves closing.","Blood leaves the heart through arteries, which have thick, stretchy walls to cope with the high pressure. Arteries branch into smaller and smaller vessels, until they become capillaries, so narrow that red blood cells must pass through them in single file. Here oxygen passes into the body’s cells. The blood then returns to the heart through veins. Laid end to end, all the blood vessels in your body would stretch around the Earth more than twice.","You can help your heart stay healthy. Regular exercise makes the heart muscle stronger, so it can pump more blood with each beat. That is why fit athletes often have very low resting heart rates. Eating plenty of fruit and vegetables, not smoking and getting enough sleep all help, too. Your heart works for you every second of your life. It is worth looking after."],"glossary":{"nutrients":"Substances in food that the body needs to grow and stay healthy.","constant":"Happening all the time.","chambers":"Enclosed spaces inside something.","atrium":"One of the two upper chambers of the heart.","ventricle":"One of the two lower chambers of the heart.","valves":"Flaps that let liquid flow in only one direction.","stethoscope":"A medical instrument for listening to the heart and lungs.","arteries":"Blood vessels carrying blood away from the heart.","capillaries":"The tiniest blood vessels."},"questions":[{"q":"Roughly how big is your heart?","o":["The size of your head","The size of your fist","The size of a pea","The size of your foot"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Make a fist. That is roughly the size of your heart.”"},{"q":"Where does the right side of the heart pump blood?","o":["To the brain","To the lungs","To the legs","To the stomach"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“It pumps this blood to the lungs.”"},{"q":"Why is the wall of the left side of the heart thicker?","o":["It is older","It has to push blood much further, around the whole body","It holds more oxygen","It is closer to the ribs"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Because the left side has to push blood much further, its wall is thicker.”"},{"q":"What causes the ‘lub-dub’ sound?","o":["Blood rushing into the lungs","The valves closing","The ribs moving","The arteries stretching"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It is “the sound of these valves closing”."},{"q":"Why do fit athletes often have low resting heart rates?","o":["Their hearts are weaker","Their stronger hearts pump more blood with each beat","They do not need oxygen","They are always asleep"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Exercise makes the heart stronger so it pumps more per beat, needing fewer beats."},{"q":"How does the writer make the opening engaging?","o":["By giving the reader an action to do and surprising numbers","By starting with a story about a hospital","By listing diseases","By asking the reader to stop reading"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"“Make a fist” involves the reader, and huge beat counts surprise them."}]},{"id":"y049","title":"The Clockmaker’s Apprentice","kind":"Fantasy","theme":"clockwork","text":["Master Horologe’s shop sat at the crooked end of Tick Lane, and it was full of clocks. Grandfather clocks lined the walls like sentries. Cuckoo clocks clustered above the doorway. Tiny pocket watches lay in glass cases, their hands trembling like the legs of insects. And all of them, every single one, told the same time to the second.","Rosie had been Master Horologe’s apprentice for six months, and in all that time she had been allowed to do only three things: sweep the floor, polish the brass and wind the clocks every morning in exactly the right order, starting with the tall black clock by the door and ending with the little silver one on the counter.","“Never wind them out of order,” the old man said every day. “And never, ever touch the clock in the back room.”","Rosie had seen the clock in the back room only once, through a gap in the curtain. It was taller than a man, made of dark wood carved with stars, and instead of numbers its face showed the twelve months of the year.","Then, one morning in December, Master Horologe did not come downstairs. Rosie found him in bed, grey-faced, with a fever. “The clocks,” he croaked. “You must wind them. In order.” Then he fell asleep.","Rosie wound the clocks. Black clock first, silver clock last. But as she finished, she noticed something terrible. Through the curtain to the back room, the great star-carved clock had stopped. Its pendulum hung still. Its single golden hand pointed to the very end of December.","And outside the window, the snow had stopped falling halfway to the ground. Flakes hung in the air like scattered sugar. A pigeon was frozen mid-flight above the chimney pots. Across the lane, the baker stood like a statue, a loaf of bread held out to a customer who would never take it.","Time itself had stopped. And only Rosie, among the ticking clocks, could still move.","With her heart pounding, she pushed through the curtain. A brass key hung on a hook beside the great clock. Rosie reached for it, then hesitated. Master Horologe had said never to touch it. But Master Horologe was asleep, and the world was waiting.","She took the key, fitted it into the clock, and turned it once, twice, three times, the way she wound the others. The pendulum shivered. The golden hand trembled forward, past the end of December, into the first second of January.","Outside, the snow began to fall again. The pigeon flapped onto a roof. The baker’s customer took her loaf.","Upstairs, Master Horologe opened his eyes. His fever had gone. When Rosie came up with a cup of tea, he looked at her for a long moment, and then he smiled. “Tomorrow,” he said, “I shall teach you to mend them.”"],"glossary":{"apprentice":"A young person learning a skilled job from an expert.","sentries":"Soldiers standing guard.","clustered":"Grouped closely together.","brass":"A yellow metal made of copper and zinc.","fever":"A high body temperature caused by illness.","pendulum":"A swinging weight that keeps a clock running.","hesitated":"Paused before doing something because of uncertainty.","trembling":"Shaking slightly."},"questions":[{"q":"What were the three jobs Rosie was allowed to do?","o":["Sweep, polish and wind the clocks","Mend, sell and design clocks","Cook, clean and shop","Paint, carve and polish"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Sweep the floor, polish the brass and wind the clocks."},{"q":"Grandfather clocks lined the walls “like sentries”. What does this simile suggest?","o":["The clocks are tall, upright and watchful","The clocks are small and weak","The clocks are noisy","The clocks are old and broken"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Sentries stand tall and still, guarding a place."},{"q":"What was unusual about the clock in the back room?","o":["It had no hands","Its face showed the twelve months of the year","It was made of glass","It played music"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Instead of numbers its face showed the twelve months of the year.”"},{"q":"What happened when the great clock stopped?","o":["The shop caught fire","Time stopped everywhere outside","Rosie fell asleep","The other clocks broke"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Snow hung in the air and the baker stood like a statue: “Time itself had stopped.”"},{"q":"Why did Rosie decide to break the rule and touch the great clock?","o":["She was curious","The world needed time to start again and nobody else could do it","She wanted to steal the key","Master Horologe told her to"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“Master Horologe was asleep, and the world was waiting.”"},{"q":"What does Master Horologe’s final line show?","o":["He is angry with Rosie","He now trusts her with greater responsibility","He wants to retire","He has forgotten everything"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Offering to teach her to mend clocks shows she has earned his trust."}]},{"id":"y050","title":"Mount Everest: The Top of the World","kind":"Geography","theme":"mountain","text":["At 8,849 metres above sea level, Mount Everest is the highest mountain on Earth. It stands on the border between Nepal and the Tibet region of China, in the Himalayas, a range of mountains that stretches for 2,400 kilometres across Asia. In Nepal it is called Sagarmatha, meaning ‘forehead in the sky’; in Tibet it is Chomolungma, ‘goddess mother of the world’. Its English name comes from Sir George Everest, a British surveyor of India – who, ironically, never saw it.","The Himalayas are still growing. About fifty million years ago, the tectonic plate carrying India crashed into the plate carrying Asia. Neither plate would sink beneath the other, so the rock buckled and was pushed upwards, like a rug crumpling when you push it against a wall. India is still moving north by about five centimetres a year, and Everest rises a few millimetres every year as a result. Fossils of sea creatures have been found near its summit – proof that the rock was once at the bottom of an ancient ocean.","Climbing Everest is extremely dangerous. Near the top, the air contains only about a third of the oxygen found at sea level, and most climbers carry bottled oxygen. The area above 8,000 metres is known as the ‘death zone’ because the human body cannot survive there for long. Climbers risk frostbite, avalanches, falling into deep cracks in the ice called crevasses, and altitude sickness, which can cause confusion and even death.","Many attempts were made to reach the summit before it was finally conquered on 29 May 1953 by Edmund Hillary, a beekeeper from New Zealand, and Tenzing Norgay, a Sherpa from Nepal. They spent just fifteen minutes on top. Tenzing left some sweets and biscuits in the snow as an offering, and Hillary took photographs – though there is none of Hillary himself at the summit, because Tenzing did not know how to use the camera.","Today, hundreds of people reach the summit every year, guided by Sherpas, whose knowledge and strength are essential to almost every expedition. On some days, so many climbers queue on the narrow summit ridge that there are dangerous traffic jams. The mountain has also become polluted with litter, abandoned equipment and human waste, and clean-up campaigns now remove tonnes of rubbish every year.","Everest remains a symbol of human ambition – and a reminder that some places on our planet are not built for people."],"glossary":{"surveyor":"A person who measures and maps land.","ironically":"In a way that is the opposite of what you might expect.","tectonic":"To do with the huge moving plates of the Earth’s surface.","buckled":"Bent and crumpled under pressure.","summit":"The top of a mountain.","frostbite":"Damage to skin and flesh caused by extreme cold.","crevasses":"Deep cracks in a glacier.","altitude":"Height above sea level.","expedition":"A journey made for a special purpose."},"questions":[{"q":"How tall is Mount Everest?","o":["2,400 metres","8,000 metres","8,849 metres","5,000 metres"],"a":2,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“At 8,849 metres above sea level.”"},{"q":"Why is it ironic that Everest is named after George Everest?","o":["He never saw the mountain","He climbed it many times","He was not British","He hated mountains"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The mountain bears his name, yet he “never saw it”."},{"q":"Why does the writer compare the Himalayas forming to a rug crumpling?","o":["To help readers imagine rock being pushed up as the plates pressed together","To show mountains are soft","Because rugs are made in Nepal","To describe the colour"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"A rug pushed against a wall crumples upwards, like rock squeezed between plates."},{"q":"What does the discovery of sea fossils near the summit prove?","o":["Sea creatures can climb","The rock was once at the bottom of an ocean","Climbers brought fish","Everest is a volcano"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Fossils are “proof that the rock was once at the bottom of an ancient ocean.”"},{"q":"Why is there no photograph of Hillary at the summit?","o":["It was too cloudy","Tenzing did not know how to use the camera","Hillary was shy","The camera froze"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Tenzing did not know how to use the camera.”"},{"q":"What problems has Everest’s popularity caused?","o":["Dangerous queues and pollution","The mountain has shrunk","Sherpas have left","There are no more climbers"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"Traffic jams on the ridge and litter, equipment and waste."}]},{"id":"y051","title":"A Christmas Carol: Marley’s Ghost","kind":"Retelling","theme":"victorian","author":"Retold from Charles Dickens","text":["Ebenezer Scrooge was the meanest man in London. He was as hard and sharp as flint, and as secret and solitary as an oyster. The cold within him froze his features, nipped his pointed nose and made his thin lips blue. He carried his own low temperature about with him everywhere he went, and even on the warmest summer day his office felt like winter.","On Christmas Eve, Scrooge sat counting money while his clerk, Bob Cratchit, shivered over a fire so small it looked like a single coal. When his cheerful nephew Fred called in to wish him a merry Christmas and invite him to dinner, Scrooge replied, “Bah! Humbug!” When two gentlemen came collecting money for the poor, he asked whether there were no prisons and no workhouses for such people. If the poor would rather die than go there, he said, “they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.”","That night, Scrooge went home to his dark, gloomy rooms. As he put his key in the door, he saw something that made his blood run cold. The door knocker had changed into the face of his old business partner, Jacob Marley, who had been dead for seven years to that very night. Scrooge blinked, and it was a knocker again.","He told himself it was nonsense, locked his door twice and sat down by the fire with a bowl of gruel. Then, from somewhere deep in the house, came a clanking noise, as if someone were dragging a heavy chain over the casks in the wine cellar. The sound came up the stairs. It came towards his door. And through the door – not opening it, but through it – came Marley’s ghost.","The ghost was wrapped in a long chain made of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers and heavy purses wrought in steel. “I wear the chain I forged in life,” it said. “I made it link by link, and yard by yard.” In life, Marley had cared only about money. Now he was doomed to wander the world for ever, watching the suffering he could have helped and could now do nothing to ease.","“But you were always a good man of business, Jacob,” stammered Scrooge.","“Business!” cried the ghost, wringing its hands. “Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance and benevolence were all my business.”","Scrooge’s own chain, the ghost warned him, was already longer and heavier than Marley’s had been. But there was still a chance for him to escape Marley’s fate. Three spirits would visit him, one each night. Without them, he had no hope.","Then the ghost walked backwards to the window, which raised itself a little with every step, and floated out into the bitter night, where the air was filled with moaning phantoms. Scrooge went straight to bed, without undressing, and fell asleep at once."],"glossary":{"flint":"A very hard grey stone.","solitary":"Alone; avoiding other people.","clerk":"A person who keeps records and accounts in an office.","humbug":"Nonsense; something dishonest or fake.","workhouses":"Places where very poor people lived and worked in harsh conditions.","surplus":"More than is needed.","gruel":"A thin, watery porridge.","ledgers":"Books for recording money paid and received.","wrought":"Made or shaped, especially from metal.","benevolence":"Kindness and wishing to do good for others.","phantoms":"Ghosts."},"questions":[{"q":"What does Scrooge say when his nephew wishes him a merry Christmas?","o":["“Thank you!”","“Bah! Humbug!”","“Come in!”","“And to you!”"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Scrooge replied, “Bah! Humbug!”"},{"q":"Scrooge is “as hard and sharp as flint”. What does this simile suggest?","o":["He is strong and brave","He is cold, unkind and unfeeling","He is clever","He is very old"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Flint is hard stone, suggesting he has no warmth or softness."},{"q":"What does Scrooge’s answer to the gentlemen collecting for the poor reveal?","o":["He is generous","He is cruel and has no sympathy for the poor","He is poor himself","He runs a workhouse"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He suggests the poor should die to “decrease the surplus population”."},{"q":"What is Marley’s chain made of?","o":["Gold and jewels","Cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers and purses","Flowers","Bones"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The chain is made of “cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers and heavy purses”."},{"q":"What does Marley mean when he says “Mankind was my business”?","o":["He should have cared about helping people, not just money","He sold things to people","He worked in a bank","He was a doctor"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"He lists charity and mercy as what truly mattered."},{"q":"What is the purpose of Marley’s visit?","o":["To steal Scrooge’s money","To warn Scrooge and give him a chance to change","To invite Scrooge to dinner","To frighten Bob Cratchit"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"He warns him and says three spirits will come so Scrooge can escape Marley’s fate."}]},{"id":"y052","title":"“Hope” is the thing with feathers","kind":"Poem","theme":"nature","author":"Emily Dickinson (c.1861)","text":["“Hope” is the thing with feathers -\nThat perches in the soul -\nAnd sings the tune without the words -\nAnd never stops - at all -","And sweetest - in the Gale - is heard -\nAnd sore must be the storm -\nThat could abash the little Bird\nThat kept so many warm -","I’ve heard it in the chillest land -\nAnd on the strangest Sea -\nYet - never - in Extremity,\nIt asked a crumb - of me."],"glossary":{"perches":"Sits or rests, as a bird does on a branch.","soul":"The inner, spiritual part of a person.","gale":"A very strong wind.","sore":"Here, severe or harsh.","abash":"To make someone feel ashamed or put off.","chillest":"Coldest.","extremity":"A situation of great danger or difficulty."},"questions":[{"q":"What is hope compared to in this poem?","o":["A storm","A bird","A ship","A crumb"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Hope is “the thing with feathers” that perches and sings."},{"q":"What does “sings the tune without the words” suggest about hope?","o":["Hope is a song with a chorus","Hope is a feeling we sense even when we cannot explain it in words","Hope is very loud","Hope cannot sing"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"A wordless tune suggests something felt rather than spoken."},{"q":"According to the second stanza, when does hope sound “sweetest”?","o":["On sunny days","In the gale – during hard times","At night","In spring"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“And sweetest – in the Gale – is heard.”"},{"q":"What does the poet mean when she says hope never “asked a crumb – of me”?","o":["Hope is greedy","Hope gives comfort without asking for anything in return","Hope is hungry","Birds do not eat crumbs"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Even in extreme situations, hope gave without needing anything back."},{"q":"What is the effect of the many dashes in the poem?","o":["They make it look messy","They create pauses, making the reader slow down and think","They show the poem is unfinished","They replace rhymes"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"Dashes break up the lines, giving a hesitant, thoughtful rhythm."},{"q":"What is the overall message of the poem?","o":["Hope is weak and easily lost","Hope stays with us through the hardest times and asks for nothing","Birds bring good luck","Storms are dangerous"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"Hope “never stops” and keeps people warm through storms and strange seas."}]},{"id":"y053","title":"The Vikings: Raiders, Traders and Explorers","kind":"History","theme":"ship","text":["On 8 June 793, a group of ships with tall, curved prows appeared off the coast of Northumberland and landed on the island of Lindisfarne. The men who leapt ashore attacked the monastery there, killing monks, stealing gold and silver treasures and carrying people away as slaves. A scholar named Alcuin wrote in horror: “Never before has such terror appeared in Britain.” The Viking Age had begun.","The Vikings came from Scandinavia – the lands that are now Norway, Sweden and Denmark. The word ‘Viking’ probably comes from an Old Norse phrase meaning ‘to go raiding’. Not every Scandinavian was a Viking; most were farmers, fishermen and craftspeople. But for nearly three hundred years, Viking raiders terrified the coasts of Europe.","Their success depended on their remarkable ships. Viking longships were long, narrow and light, built from overlapping planks of oak. They had both a large square sail and rows of oars, so they could travel fast in the open sea and also row up shallow rivers deep into the countryside. Because they could be dragged onto a beach, Vikings could strike without warning and escape before anyone could stop them.","Over time, raiding turned into settling. In 865, a huge Viking army, which the English called the Great Heathen Army, invaded England. It conquered most of the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms until it was finally stopped by Alfred the Great, King of Wessex. A treaty divided the country, and the north and east became known as the Danelaw, where Viking law and customs ruled. Many English place names still show where Vikings lived: names ending in ‘-by’, such as Whitby and Grimsby, meant ‘farm’ or ‘village’, and ‘-thorpe’ meant a smaller settlement.","Vikings were also great traders and explorers. They travelled east along the rivers of Russia as far as Constantinople and Baghdad, trading furs, amber and walrus ivory for silver, silk and spices. They settled in Iceland and Greenland, and around the year 1000, Leif Erikson reached North America, nearly five hundred years before Christopher Columbus.","Everyday Viking life was less dramatic than their reputation. People lived in longhouses shared with their animals in winter, wove cloth, made jewellery and told long stories called sagas. Contrary to popular belief, Viking warriors did not wear horned helmets; that idea comes from nineteenth-century operas and paintings.","The Viking Age is usually said to have ended in 1066, when the Norwegian king Harald Hardrada was defeated at the Battle of Stamford Bridge. But Viking influence lived on – including in English, which borrowed hundreds of Norse words, such as ‘sky’, ‘egg’, ‘window’ and ‘knife’."],"glossary":{"prows":"The pointed front parts of ships.","monastery":"A building where monks live and worship.","scholar":"A person who studies and has a lot of knowledge.","raiding":"Making sudden attacks to steal or destroy.","treaty":"A formal agreement between countries or rulers.","amber":"Hard yellow-orange fossilised tree resin used as jewellery.","reputation":"What people generally believe about someone.","sagas":"Long stories about heroes, especially from Scandinavia.","contrary":"Opposite."},"questions":[{"q":"What happened at Lindisfarne in 793?","o":["Vikings attacked the monastery","Alfred the Great was crowned","Vikings reached America","A treaty was signed"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Men “attacked the monastery there, killing monks, stealing gold and silver”."},{"q":"Why were Viking longships so useful for raiding?","o":["They were very heavy and slow","They could cross seas, row up shallow rivers and land on beaches","They had cannons","They were invisible"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"With sails and oars they travelled fast and could be dragged onto a beach."},{"q":"What does the place name ‘Whitby’ tell us?","o":["It was a Roman fort","It was probably a Viking farm or village","It was a castle","It was a harbour"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Names ending in ‘-by’ “meant ‘farm’ or ‘village’”."},{"q":"Who reached North America around the year 1000?","o":["Christopher Columbus","Leif Erikson","Harald Hardrada","Alcuin"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Leif Erikson reached North America.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer mention horned helmets?","o":["To correct a popular myth about Vikings","To describe Viking fashion","To show Vikings liked music","To explain how helmets were made"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"“Contrary to popular belief … did not wear horned helmets.”"},{"q":"How does the final paragraph show that the Vikings still affect us?","o":["Viking words like ‘sky’ and ‘egg’ are part of English","Vikings still raid Britain","We all live in longhouses","We sail longships"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"English “borrowed hundreds of Norse words”."}]},{"id":"y054","title":"Alan Turing: The Codebreaker","kind":"Biography","theme":"computer","text":["In 1939, at the start of the Second World War, Britain faced a problem that seemed impossible to solve. German forces sent their military orders by radio, but every message was scrambled by a machine called Enigma. Enigma looked like a typewriter in a wooden box, but inside, a series of rotating wheels changed each letter into a different one, and the settings changed every day. There were more than 150 million million million possible settings. The Germans believed the code could never be broken.","At a country house called Bletchley Park in Buckinghamshire, a group of mathematicians, linguists and puzzle experts set out to prove them wrong. One of the most brilliant was a shy, untidy young man named Alan Turing.","Turing had been fascinated by numbers and science since childhood. At school he was often in trouble for ignoring subjects he found boring, but he taught himself advanced mathematics for fun. In 1936, aged just twenty-four, he wrote a paper describing an imaginary ‘universal machine’ that could carry out any calculation if it was given the right instructions. It was, in effect, the idea of the modern computer – years before one had been built.","At Bletchley Park, Turing designed an electromechanical machine called the Bombe, which worked through possible Enigma settings far faster than any human could. Building on earlier work by Polish codebreakers, Turing and his colleagues used the Bombes to read thousands of German messages. Turing also cracked the more complicated code used by German submarines, which were sinking the ships carrying food and supplies to Britain across the Atlantic. Historians believe that the work at Bletchley Park shortened the war by perhaps two years and saved millions of lives.","After the war, Turing helped design one of the first electronic computers. He also asked a famous question: can machines think? He proposed a way to test it, now called the Turing Test: if a person typing messages cannot tell whether they are talking to a human or a machine, then the machine could be said to be intelligent. People still debate the Turing Test today, in the age of artificial intelligence.","Turing’s life ended tragically. In 1952 he was convicted of a crime because he was gay; at the time, relationships between men were illegal in Britain. He lost his security clearance and was given harmful medical treatment. He died in 1954, aged forty-one. For decades, his wartime work remained secret, and few people knew what he had done.","In 2009, the British government formally apologised for how Turing had been treated, and in 2013 he was granted a royal pardon. Since 2021, his portrait has appeared on the Bank of England fifty-pound note – a tribute to the man whose ideas helped win a war and shape the world we live in now."],"glossary":{"scrambled":"Mixed up so that it cannot be understood.","rotating":"Turning round.","linguists":"People who study or know many languages.","universal":"Able to be used for everything.","electromechanical":"Worked by a combination of electricity and moving parts.","colleagues":"People who work together.","artificial":"Made by people rather than natural.","convicted":"Found guilty of a crime in court.","pardon":"An official decision to forgive a crime.","tribute":"Something done to show respect and admiration."},"questions":[{"q":"What was Enigma?","o":["A British spy","A German machine for scrambling messages","A computer game","A submarine"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Every message “was scrambled by a machine called Enigma”."},{"q":"Why did the Germans believe the code could never be broken?","o":["It used a secret language","There were an enormous number of possible settings that changed every day","It was written in invisible ink","Only Hitler knew it"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"There were over 150 million million million settings, changed daily."},{"q":"What was Turing’s 1936 ‘universal machine’?","o":["A real computer he built","An imaginary machine that could do any calculation with the right instructions – the idea of a computer","A typewriter","A weapon"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It “could carry out any calculation if it was given the right instructions”."},{"q":"Why was breaking the submarine code so important?","o":["Submarines were sinking ships carrying food and supplies to Britain","Submarines were attacking London","It was the easiest code","The King asked for it"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The submarines were sinking supply ships across the Atlantic."},{"q":"What is the Turing Test?","o":["A school exam","A way to judge whether a machine can seem intelligent by fooling a person into thinking it is human","A test for codebreakers","A medical test"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"If a person cannot tell whether they are talking to a human or machine, it could be called intelligent."},{"q":"Why does the writer end with the fifty-pound note?","o":["To show Turing is now publicly honoured despite his unjust treatment","To show Turing was rich","To explain how banknotes are made","To show he designed money"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"After describing injustice, the ending shows recognition and respect."}]},{"id":"y055","title":"The Storm Chaser","kind":"Story","theme":"storm","text":["Aunt Bea did not believe in staying indoors when the weather got interesting. She was a meteorologist, which meant, as far as Max could tell, that she spent her life looking at clouds and getting excited about them. When the forecast promised a supercell thunderstorm over the plains, she loaded the van with cameras, laptops and an alarming number of sandwiches and announced that they were going to meet it.","“Meet it?” said Max. He was spending the summer with her in Oklahoma, and so far he had seen a lot of flat land, a lot of sky and one very bored-looking cow.","“Not too closely,” said Aunt Bea cheerfully. “Just close enough to say hello.”","They drove for three hours. The sky, which had been a hard, clean blue all morning, began to change. In the west, clouds piled on top of one another, white and gleaming at the top, darkening to the colour of a bruise underneath. By four o’clock, a single enormous storm cloud filled half the sky, its top flattened against the upper atmosphere like an anvil. Aunt Bea pulled onto the side of a dirt road and switched off the engine.","Everything went quiet. The wind, which had been gusting all day, simply stopped. Birds stopped singing. The air felt thick and heavy and smelled of electricity.","“That,” whispered Aunt Bea, “is the inflow. The storm is breathing in.”","Underneath the cloud, something was turning. It was a vast, slowly rotating shelf of grey, like a giant wheel lying on its side. Max watched, his mouth dry, as a thin grey finger began to reach down from the bottom of it towards the fields.","“Is that a—”","“Funnel cloud,” said Aunt Bea. Her voice was calm, but her hands were already on the steering wheel. “If it touches the ground, it’s a tornado. And it’s heading north-east, which means we are going to be somewhere else.”","She drove south, not fast but steadily, while Max twisted round in his seat to watch through the back window. The finger stretched, wavered, touched the earth – and a cloud of dust and debris exploded upwards from the ground. Even from miles away, he could hear it: a low, rumbling roar like a train that never ended.","Ten minutes later, it was gone, drawn back up into the cloud as if it had never existed. The rain arrived instead, hammering on the roof of the van so loudly they had to shout.","That night, Aunt Bea uploaded her measurements to the national weather service, where they would help scientists improve tornado warnings. Max sat on the porch, watching lightning flicker on the horizon, and found that his hands were still shaking – and that he could not stop smiling.","“So,” said Aunt Bea, handing him a sandwich. “Same time tomorrow?”"],"glossary":{"meteorologist":"A scientist who studies the weather.","supercell":"A very powerful thunderstorm with a rotating centre.","alarming":"Worrying; surprising in a way that makes you uneasy.","atmosphere":"The layer of air around the Earth.","anvil":"A heavy iron block with a flat top, used by blacksmiths.","rotating":"Turning round in a circle.","funnel":"A tube shape that is wide at the top and narrow at the bottom.","debris":"Scattered pieces of broken things.","horizon":"The line where land seems to meet the sky."},"questions":[{"q":"What is Aunt Bea’s job?","o":["Photographer","Meteorologist","Farmer","Pilot"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“She was a meteorologist.”"},{"q":"What does the “alarming number of sandwiches” suggest about the trip?","o":["It will be short","It will be long and Aunt Bea is well prepared – with some humour","They are going to a picnic","Max is hungry"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"The comic detail hints at a long journey and Aunt Bea’s enthusiasm."},{"q":"How does the writer build tension just before the funnel cloud appears?","o":["Everything suddenly goes silent and the air feels heavy","Max tells a joke","They stop for lunch","The sun comes out"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"The wind stops, birds go quiet and the air smells of electricity."},{"q":"“The storm is breathing in.” Which technique is this?","o":["Simile","Personification","Onomatopoeia","Alliteration"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"The storm is described as if it were a living creature breathing."},{"q":"Why did Aunt Bea drive south?","o":["The tornado was heading north-east, so south was away from it","She wanted to get closer","The road ended","She was lost"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“It’s heading north-east, which means we are going to be somewhere else.”"},{"q":"How does Max feel by the end of the story?","o":["Bored and disappointed","Shaken but thrilled","Angry with his aunt","Sad to leave"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"His hands were “still shaking” but he “could not stop smiling.”"}]},{"id":"y056","title":"Coral Reefs: Rainforests of the Sea","kind":"Science","theme":"sea","text":["From above, a coral reef looks like a garden of rocks and strange plants. In fact, it is made by animals. Corals are tiny creatures called polyps, each no bigger than a fingernail, related to jellyfish and sea anemones. Each polyp has a soft body with a mouth surrounded by stinging tentacles, and it builds itself a hard skeleton of limestone. Millions of polyps living together, building on the skeletons of earlier generations, slowly create a reef. Some reefs have been growing for thousands of years.","The secret of a coral’s success is a partnership. Inside its tissues live microscopic algae, tiny plant-like organisms that use sunlight to make food through photosynthesis. They share this food with the coral, and in return the coral gives them shelter. The algae also give corals their brilliant colours: pink, purple, yellow and green. Because the algae need sunlight, most reefs grow in clear, shallow, warm water, close to the tropics.","Coral reefs cover less than one per cent of the ocean floor, but about a quarter of all sea creatures depend on them at some point in their lives. That is why they are sometimes called the rainforests of the sea. A single reef may be home to sharks, turtles, octopuses, seahorses, clownfish and thousands of other species. The largest reef system in the world, the Great Barrier Reef off the coast of Australia, stretches for more than 2,300 kilometres and can be seen from space.","Reefs matter to people, too. They protect coastlines from waves and storms, provide food for hundreds of millions of people and attract tourists who support local jobs. Scientists have even developed medicines from chemicals found in reef creatures.","But coral reefs are in serious danger. When the sea becomes too warm, corals become stressed and push out their algae. Without the algae, they turn ghostly white, which is called coral bleaching. If the water cools quickly enough, the algae can return, but if the heat lasts too long, the corals starve and die. As the world’s oceans warm because of climate change, bleaching events have become more frequent and more severe. Pollution, overfishing and damage from boats and divers add to the problem.","Scientists are working hard to help. Some are breeding corals that can survive warmer water and replanting them on damaged reefs, rather like gardeners. Marine protected areas, where fishing and other activities are limited, give reefs a chance to recover. However, most experts agree that the most important way to save coral reefs is to reduce the greenhouse gases that are warming the planet."],"glossary":{"polyps":"Tiny sea animals with a tube-shaped body and tentacles.","tentacles":"Long, bendy arms used for feeling or catching food.","limestone":"A hard rock made mainly of the remains of sea creatures.","generations":"All the living things born at about the same time.","microscopic":"So small it can only be seen with a microscope.","algae":"Simple plant-like living things that grow in water.","photosynthesis":"The process plants use to make food from sunlight.","bleaching":"Turning white by losing colour.","frequent":"Happening often."},"questions":[{"q":"What are corals?","o":["Rocks","Plants","Tiny animals called polyps","Fish eggs"],"a":2,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Corals are tiny creatures called polyps.”"},{"q":"What do corals and algae give each other?","o":["The algae share food; the coral gives shelter","The coral gives food; the algae give shelter","They give each other nothing","They both give colour"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Algae share food made by photosynthesis; the coral gives them shelter."},{"q":"Why are reefs called the rainforests of the sea?","o":["They are green","They support a huge variety of life in a small area","They contain trees","It rains on them"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Less than one per cent of the sea floor, yet a quarter of sea creatures depend on them."},{"q":"What causes coral bleaching?","o":["Too much sunlight on land","Water becoming too warm, so corals push out their algae","Divers painting them","Cold winters"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“When the sea becomes too warm, corals … push out their algae.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer compare scientists replanting coral to gardeners?","o":["Scientists grow vegetables","They are growing and planting corals to restore the reef, like a garden","Corals are plants","Gardeners dive in the sea"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Breeding and replanting corals is like tending a garden."},{"q":"According to most experts, what is the most important way to save coral reefs?","o":["Banning tourists","Reducing the greenhouse gases warming the planet","Painting corals","Catching more fish"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"The final sentence names reducing greenhouse gases as most important."}]},{"id":"y057","title":"Letter: Save Our Library","kind":"Letter","theme":"books","text":["14 Orchard Close\nMillbrook\nMB4 7TQ","Councillor Janet Holloway\nMillbrook Town Council\nTown Hall, High Street\nMillbrook MB1 2AA","3 March","Dear Councillor Holloway,","I am writing about the council’s proposal to close Millbrook Library at the end of the year. I am twelve years old and in Year 7 at Millbrook Academy, and I would like to explain why I believe this would be a serious mistake.","I understand that the council has difficult decisions to make, and that money is short. However, the library is much more than a building full of books. It is one of the few places in our town where anyone can go, for free, and be welcome. On a single Saturday morning last month, I counted forty-three people using it: toddlers at the story session, older people reading newspapers, students revising, and several adults using the computers to apply for jobs. Not everyone has a computer or internet connection at home. For some of those people, the library is the only place they can get online.","The library has also been important to me personally. When my family moved to Millbrook two years ago, I did not know anyone. The librarian, Mr Adeyemi, recommended books to me every week and introduced me to the Saturday reading club, where I made my first friends here. I now read more than thirty books a year, and my English teacher says my writing has improved enormously. I know I am not the only young person the library has helped in this way.","Some people argue that libraries are no longer needed because everything is online. I disagree. Not all information online is reliable, and librarians help people to find trustworthy sources. Also, research by the National Literacy Trust has found that children who use their local library are much more likely to enjoy reading, and that enjoying reading is linked to doing better at school.","If money is the problem, there may be other solutions. Could the library share its building with other services, such as a café or a community centre? Could volunteers help with opening hours? Many people in Millbrook would be glad to help, and I would be the first to sign up.","Please consider the people who rely on the library before making your decision. Once it is closed, it will be very hard to open again.","I look forward to hearing from you.","Yours sincerely,\nAmara Okafor"],"glossary":{"proposal":"A plan or suggestion put forward for people to consider.","revising":"Studying work again to prepare for a test.","recommended":"Suggested as being good.","enormously":"Very greatly.","reliable":"Able to be trusted.","trustworthy":"Able to be trusted.","literacy":"The ability to read and write.","sincerely":"Honestly; ‘Yours sincerely’ ends a formal letter to a named person."},"questions":[{"q":"Why is Amara writing to the councillor?","o":["To complain about late books","To argue against closing the library","To apply for a job","To invite her to a party"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"She writes “about the council’s proposal to close Millbrook Library”."},{"q":"Why does Amara mention counting forty-three people?","o":["To show she is good at maths","To give evidence that many different people use the library","To complain it is crowded","To show she was bored"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Real numbers make her argument more convincing."},{"q":"How has the library helped Amara personally?","o":["It gave her a job","It helped her make friends and become a keen reader","It helped her move house","It paid for her school"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Mr Adeyemi introduced her to a reading club where she made her first friends."},{"q":"How does Amara respond to the argument that libraries are not needed because of the internet?","o":["She agrees","She argues librarians help find reliable information and gives research evidence","She ignores it","She says the internet is banned"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"She points out online information is not always reliable and cites the National Literacy Trust."},{"q":"Why does Amara suggest other solutions in the sixth paragraph?","o":["To show she understands the council’s money problem and to offer helpful ideas","To change the subject","To ask for money","To complain about cafés"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"She acknowledges money is short and proposes practical alternatives."},{"q":"Which feature shows this is a formal letter?","o":["Addresses, a date, ‘Dear Councillor’ and ‘Yours sincerely’","Text speak","A joke at the start","No paragraphs"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"Formal letters use addresses, a formal greeting and sign-off."}]},{"id":"y058","title":"From Stone to Iron: Life in Prehistoric Britain","kind":"History","theme":"fossil","text":["For most of human history, nobody in Britain could write. Everything we know about the first people who lived here comes from what they left behind: stone tools, bones, pottery, the remains of houses and the great monuments that still stand in our landscape. This long period before written records is called prehistory.","The earliest humans reached Britain at least 800,000 years ago. Footprints found on the beach at Happisburgh in Norfolk, revealed by a storm in 2013, are the oldest human footprints ever discovered outside Africa. At that time, Britain was joined to the rest of Europe by land, and people could simply walk here following herds of animals.","During the Old Stone Age, or Palaeolithic, people were hunter-gatherers. They moved from place to place, hunting animals such as deer, wild horses and mammoths, and gathering nuts, berries and roots. They made tools by chipping flint into sharp hand-axes and scrapers. Several times, ice ages made Britain too cold to live in, and people left for thousands of years. After the last ice age ended, about 12,000 years ago, the ice melted, the sea rose, and by around 6500 BC Britain had become an island.","Around 4000 BC, a new way of life arrived from Europe: farming. This was the New Stone Age, or Neolithic. People began to grow crops such as wheat and barley, keep cattle, sheep and pigs, and live in permanent villages. One of the best preserved is Skara Brae in Orkney, where a storm in 1850 uncovered stone houses complete with stone beds, shelves and hearths. Neolithic people also built huge monuments, including the first stages of Stonehenge.","Around 2500 BC, people learned to make bronze by melting copper and tin together. Bronze tools and weapons were stronger and sharper than stone, but the metals were rare and valuable, and they were traded across Europe. Powerful leaders were sometimes buried with rich treasures, such as gold cups, daggers and jewellery.","Around 800 BC, iron began to replace bronze. Iron ore was found in many places, so iron tools became common, making farming easier. During the Iron Age, the population grew and people lived in tribes, often in round houses with thatched roofs. Many built hillforts – settlements on hilltops surrounded by huge banks and ditches, such as Maiden Castle in Dorset.","Prehistory in Britain ended when the Romans arrived and began writing about the land and its people. But the stones, tools and bones of those thousands of unwritten years still have stories to tell, if we know how to read them."],"glossary":{"monuments":"Large structures built to remember a person or event.","prehistory":"The time before written records.","herds":"Large groups of animals that live together.","flint":"A hard grey stone that can be chipped into sharp edges.","permanent":"Lasting for a long time; not moving.","hearths":"The floors of fireplaces.","bronze":"A metal made by mixing copper and tin.","ore":"Rock containing metal.","tribes":"Groups of families with the same leaders and customs."},"questions":[{"q":"Why do we call this period ‘prehistory’?","o":["It was very cold","It was before people wrote records","It was before animals existed","It happened in Africa"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It is “this long period before written records”."},{"q":"How could the first humans reach Britain without boats?","o":["They swam","Britain was joined to Europe by land","They used bridges","The sea was frozen"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Britain was joined to the rest of Europe by land.”"},{"q":"What was the biggest change at the start of the Neolithic?","o":["People began farming and living in permanent villages","People learned to write","People made bronze","People left Britain"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"Farming arrived and people “live[d] in permanent villages”."},{"q":"Why might bronze objects have been especially valuable?","o":["Copper and tin were rare and had to be traded","Bronze was very soft","Bronze was made of gold","Everyone could make it"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“The metals were rare and valuable, and they were traded across Europe.”"},{"q":"Why did iron tools become common?","o":["Iron ore was found in many places","The Romans brought them","Iron was cheaper than wood","Bronze ran out completely"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Iron ore was found in many places, so iron tools became common.”"},{"q":"How is the article organised?","o":["In time order, from the earliest people to the Iron Age","In alphabetical order","From newest to oldest","As a list of museums"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It moves through the Old Stone Age, New Stone Age, Bronze Age and Iron Age."}]},{"id":"y059","title":"The Mystery of the Mary Celeste","kind":"Mystery","theme":"ship","text":["On 4 December 1872, the crew of the British ship Dei Gratia spotted a vessel drifting in the Atlantic Ocean, about 600 miles west of Portugal. Her sails were partly set, but she was moving strangely, zigzagging as if nobody was steering. The captain, David Morehouse, recognised her. She was the Mary Celeste, an American cargo ship that had left New York a month earlier.","Morehouse sent three men across in a small boat. They climbed aboard and called out. There was no answer. The Mary Celeste was completely deserted.","What they found has puzzled people ever since. The ship was in reasonably good condition. There was some water in the hold, and a few sails were torn, but she was seaworthy. Her cargo of 1,701 barrels of alcohol was almost all still there. There was plenty of food and fresh water – enough for six months. The crew’s belongings, including clothes, boots and pipes, had been left behind. The ship’s lifeboat, however, was missing, and the ship’s logbook had last been written in nine days earlier.","Captain Benjamin Briggs, his wife Sarah, their two-year-old daughter Sophia and seven crew members were never seen again.","Over the years, people have suggested many explanations, some more believable than others. Some blamed pirates, but nothing valuable had been taken. Others suggested a mutiny, with the crew killing the captain, but there was no sign of a struggle, and the crew had good reputations. Wilder theories have included sea monsters, waterspouts and even aliens.","The story became even more famous thanks to a young writer named Arthur Conan Doyle, who later created Sherlock Holmes. In 1884, he published a short story about the ship – which he called the Marie Celeste – filled with invented details, such as half-eaten breakfasts and warm cups of tea on the table. Many people believed it was true, and those myths are still repeated today.","So what really happened? Many historians favour a simpler explanation. The barrels of alcohol may have leaked fumes, and perhaps Captain Briggs feared an explosion. Or, after a storm, a faulty pump may have made him believe the ship was sinking faster than it was. Either way, he may have ordered everyone into the lifeboat, intending to stay close by, tied to the ship by a rope. If the rope broke and the wind filled the sails, the Mary Celeste would have sailed away, leaving the tiny boat alone in the vast ocean.","We may never know for certain. But the image of a ship sailing on with no one aboard has haunted sailors and storytellers for more than 150 years."],"glossary":{"vessel":"A ship or large boat.","cargo":"Goods carried by a ship.","deserted":"Empty and abandoned.","seaworthy":"In good enough condition to sail safely.","logbook":"An official record of a ship’s journey.","mutiny":"When sailors or soldiers rebel against their officers.","reputations":"What people generally believe about someone.","theories":"Ideas that try to explain something.","fumes":"Strong-smelling gas or smoke."},"questions":[{"q":"What was strange about the way the Mary Celeste was moving?","o":["It was sinking","It was zigzagging as if nobody was steering","It was going backwards","It was very fast"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Zigzagging as if nobody was steering.”"},{"q":"Why do the plentiful food and water make the mystery deeper?","o":["They show the crew did not leave because of hunger or thirst","They show pirates had visited","They prove the ship was sinking","They prove the crew was lazy"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"With six months’ supplies, there was no obvious reason to abandon ship."},{"q":"Why is the pirate theory unlikely?","o":["Pirates were not around then","Nothing valuable had been taken","The ship was too big","The crew were pirates"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Nothing valuable had been taken.”"},{"q":"How did Arthur Conan Doyle affect the story?","o":["He solved the mystery","He invented details such as warm cups of tea that people believed were true","He was on the ship","He found the lifeboat"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"His story was “filled with invented details” that are still repeated."},{"q":"What is the explanation many historians favour?","o":["Sea monsters","The crew left in the lifeboat fearing danger and the ship drifted away","Aliens","A mutiny"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"Fearing an explosion or sinking, they took to the lifeboat and the rope may have broken."},{"q":"Why does the writer separate the sentence about Captain Briggs and his family into its own short paragraph?","o":["To emphasise the human tragedy of the disappearance","Because it was too long","To introduce Sherlock Holmes","To describe the weather"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"Isolating it makes readers pause on the real people who vanished."}]},{"id":"y060","title":"The Christmas Truce of 1914","kind":"History","theme":"poppy","text":["When the First World War began in the summer of 1914, many people believed it would be over by Christmas. They were wrong. By December, the armies on the Western Front had dug long lines of trenches stretching from the coast of Belgium to the border of Switzerland. Between the British and German trenches lay ‘no man’s land’, a stretch of mud, barbed wire and shell holes, sometimes only fifty metres wide, where anyone who showed their head risked being shot.","Conditions were miserable. The trenches were cold and wet, and soldiers stood for hours in freezing water. Rats ran over them while they slept. Many men had already seen friends killed.","Then, on Christmas Eve, something extraordinary happened. In several places along the front, British soldiers noticed lights appearing along the German trenches – small Christmas trees decorated with candles. Then they heard singing. The Germans were singing ‘Stille Nacht’, the German version of ‘Silent Night’. After a while, the British sang carols back.","On Christmas morning, in some sections of the line, a few brave soldiers climbed out of their trenches without their weapons. Others followed. In the middle of no man’s land, enemies shook hands. They swapped cigarettes, chocolate, buttons and badges, and showed each other photographs of their families. Some men helped each other to bury the dead who had been lying between the lines. In a few places, according to letters written by soldiers, they even played football, with a real ball or with a bundle of rags.","One British soldier wrote home: “Just you think that while you were eating your turkey I was out talking and shaking hands with the very men I had been trying to kill a few hours before!” Another described a German soldier who had worked as a barber in London before the war, and who offered to give haircuts.","The truce was not official, and it did not happen everywhere. In some places the fighting carried on as usual. Senior officers on both sides were alarmed, worried that soldiers who became friendly with the enemy would not want to fight. In the following years, strict orders were issued to prevent anything similar from happening again, and nothing on the same scale ever did.","The war continued for almost four more years, and around nine million soldiers were killed. But the Christmas Truce is still remembered as a moment when ordinary men on opposite sides recognised each other as human beings – and as a reminder of what war takes away."],"glossary":{"trenches":"Long, deep ditches dug to protect soldiers.","barbed":"Barbed wire has sharp spikes along it.","extraordinary":"Very unusual and surprising.","carols":"Christmas songs.","truce":"An agreement to stop fighting for a time.","official":"Approved by people in authority.","alarmed":"Worried and frightened.","scale":"The size or extent of something."},"questions":[{"q":"What was ‘no man’s land’?","o":["A town in Belgium","The dangerous ground between the British and German trenches","A football pitch","A hospital"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It lay between the trenches – “a stretch of mud, barbed wire and shell holes”."},{"q":"What first showed the British that something unusual was happening?","o":["Germans waving flags","Christmas trees with candles and singing from the German trenches","A letter from the King","Snow falling"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They saw lights – small decorated Christmas trees – and heard singing."},{"q":"What did enemy soldiers do in no man’s land?","o":["Fought with knives","Shook hands, swapped gifts and showed family photos","Built new trenches","Slept"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They “shook hands … swapped cigarettes, chocolate, buttons and badges”."},{"q":"Why does the writer include quotations from soldiers’ letters?","o":["To give first-hand evidence of what happened","To make the article longer","To show soldiers could not spell","To argue the truce never happened"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Letters from men who were there make the account more reliable and vivid."},{"q":"Why were senior officers alarmed by the truce?","o":["They wanted to join in","They feared soldiers would not want to fight enemies they had befriended","They disliked football","They thought it was a trick"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"They worried “soldiers who became friendly with the enemy would not want to fight.”"},{"q":"What is the main point of the final paragraph?","o":["The truce ended the war","The truce shows soldiers on both sides were ordinary humans, highlighting the tragedy of war","The war was short","Football is important"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"It is remembered as a moment of shared humanity and “a reminder of what war takes away.”"}]},{"id":"y061","title":"Sonnet 18","kind":"Poem","theme":"garden","author":"William Shakespeare (1609)","text":["Shall I compare thee to a summer’s day?\nThou art more lovely and more temperate:\nRough winds do shake the darling buds of May,\nAnd summer’s lease hath all too short a date;","Sometime too hot the eye of heaven shines,\nAnd often is his gold complexion dimm’d;\nAnd every fair from fair sometime declines,\nBy chance or nature’s changing course untrimm’d;","But thy eternal summer shall not fade,\nNor lose possession of that fair thou ow’st;\nNor shall Death brag thou wander’st in his shade,\nWhen in eternal lines to time thou grow’st:","So long as men can breathe or eyes can see,\nSo long lives this, and this gives life to thee."],"glossary":{"thee":"An old word for ‘you’.","thou":"An old word for ‘you’ (as the subject).","temperate":"Mild and gentle; not extreme.","lease":"A rental agreement for a fixed time.","complexion":"The colour and appearance of the skin of the face.","untrimm’d":"Stripped of its beauty or decoration.","eternal":"Lasting for ever.","brag":"To boast."},"questions":[{"q":"What does the speaker compare the person to?","o":["A winter’s night","A summer’s day","A flower","The sea"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Shall I compare thee to a summer’s day?”"},{"q":"According to lines 3–4, what are two faults of summer?","o":["It is too cold and too dark","Rough winds shake the buds and summer is too short","It rains every day","It is boring"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Rough winds do shake the darling buds of May, / And summer’s lease hath all too short a date.”"},{"q":"What is “the eye of heaven”?","o":["The Moon","The Sun","A star","God"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"The phrase is a metaphor for the Sun, which “shines” with a “gold complexion”."},{"q":"How is the person better than a summer’s day?","o":["They are warmer","Their beauty (their ‘summer’) will never fade","They are taller","They like flowers"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“But thy eternal summer shall not fade.”"},{"q":"What will keep the person’s beauty alive for ever?","o":["Medicine","The poem itself, as long as people read it","A painting","Their children"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“So long lives this, and this gives life to thee” – ‘this’ is the poem."},{"q":"What is the effect of the word “But” at the start of line 9?","o":["It marks a turn from summer’s faults to the person’s lasting beauty","It introduces a new character","It ends the poem","It begins a list"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"This turning point (the volta) shifts from criticising summer to praising the person."}]},{"id":"y062","title":"How Vaccines Train Your Body","kind":"Science","theme":"lab","text":["Every day, your body is invaded. Bacteria, viruses and other microscopic germs, called pathogens, try to get inside you through your mouth, nose, eyes and any cut in your skin. Most of the time you never notice, because your immune system defeats them before they can make you ill.","Your immune system is like an army with several divisions. Some white blood cells attack any invader they meet, swallowing it whole. Others are more specialised. These cells can recognise particular pathogens by the unique shapes on their surface, called antigens. When they find an antigen they recognise, they produce antibodies – Y-shaped proteins that fit onto the antigen like a key in a lock, marking the invader for destruction.","The trouble is that the first time you meet a new pathogen, your body does not yet have the right antibodies. It takes days to work out how to make them, and during that time the pathogen multiplies and you become ill. Once you have recovered, however, your body keeps special memory cells that remember the antigen. If the same pathogen ever invades again, the memory cells recognise it at once and produce antibodies so quickly that you usually do not get ill at all. You have become immune.","Vaccines use this clever system to protect you before you ever meet a dangerous disease. A vaccine contains a harmless version of the pathogen, or just a part of it, such as one of its antigens. Some vaccines contain weakened or dead germs; newer ones, called mRNA vaccines, give your cells instructions to make a harmless antigen themselves. Either way, your immune system reacts as if it were a real infection, producing antibodies and memory cells – without you having to suffer the disease.","The first vaccine was developed in 1796 by an English doctor, Edward Jenner. He noticed that milkmaids who had caught cowpox, a mild disease from cows, never seemed to catch smallpox, a deadly disease that killed millions. He took material from a cowpox sore and scratched it into the arm of a boy named James Phipps. Later, when Jenner exposed the boy to smallpox, he did not become ill. The word ‘vaccine’ comes from vacca, the Latin word for cow.","Thanks to vaccination, smallpox was completely wiped out across the world by 1980 – the only human disease ever eradicated. Polio, which once paralysed hundreds of thousands of children every year, has almost disappeared. When enough people in a community are vaccinated, even those who cannot be vaccinated, such as very young babies, are protected, because the disease cannot spread. This is called herd immunity.","Scientists continue to develop new vaccines, against diseases such as malaria, and they are among the greatest life-saving inventions in human history."],"glossary":{"pathogens":"Germs that cause disease.","immune":"Protected against a disease.","specialised":"Trained or designed for one particular job.","antigens":"Shapes on the surface of germs that the immune system recognises.","antibodies":"Proteins made by the body to attach to and mark germs.","multiplies":"Increases in number.","exposed":"Put in contact with something.","eradicated":"Completely destroyed or wiped out.","paralysed":"Made unable to move."},"questions":[{"q":"What are antibodies?","o":["Germs","Y-shaped proteins that fit onto antigens and mark invaders","White blood cells","Vaccines"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Y-shaped proteins that fit onto the antigen … marking the invader for destruction.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer compare antibodies fitting antigens to a key in a lock?","o":["To show each antibody fits a particular antigen exactly","To show the body has doors","To show germs are locked away","Because keys are made of protein"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"A key fits only one lock, just as an antibody matches one antigen."},{"q":"Why do we often become ill the first time we meet a pathogen?","o":["Our body takes days to make the right antibodies","We have no white blood cells","Vaccines make us ill","The pathogen is too small"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“It takes days to work out how to make them, and during that time … you become ill.”"},{"q":"How does a vaccine protect us?","o":["It kills all germs in the body","It trains the immune system to make antibodies and memory cells without causing the disease","It replaces blood","It stops us eating germs"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"Your body reacts as if infected, making antibodies and memory cells, “without you having to suffer the disease.”"},{"q":"What did Jenner notice about milkmaids?","o":["They never caught smallpox after having cowpox","They were often ill","They had no immune system","They invented vaccines"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Milkmaids who had caught cowpox “never seemed to catch smallpox”."},{"q":"How does herd immunity protect babies who cannot be vaccinated?","o":["They are given extra milk","Because so many people are vaccinated, the disease cannot spread to them","Babies are naturally immune to everything","They stay in hospital"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"When enough people are vaccinated, the disease “cannot spread”."}]},{"id":"y063","title":"Robin Hood and the Silver Arrow","kind":"Legend","theme":"forest","text":["In the days when King Richard was away fighting in the Crusades and his greedy brother John ruled England in his place, there lived in Sherwood Forest a band of outlaws. Their leader was Robin Hood, the finest archer in the land. He and his Merry Men – Little John, who was enormously tall; Friar Tuck, who was enormously round; and Will Scarlet, who was enormously quick-tempered – robbed rich travellers who passed through the forest and gave the money to the poor, who were being taxed into starvation by the Sheriff of Nottingham.","The Sheriff hated Robin more than anyone alive, and he had tried every way he knew to capture him. At last, he came up with a cunning plan. He announced an archery contest in Nottingham, with a prize of a silver arrow for the winner. “Robin Hood is proud,” he told his men. “He will not be able to resist showing off his skill. And when he comes, we will be waiting.”","In the forest, the Merry Men begged Robin not to go. “It’s a trap,” said Little John. “As plain as the nose on your face.”","Robin laughed. “Then I shall go without my face.”","On the day of the contest, the Sheriff watched every archer closely, looking for a man in Lincoln green. He saw none. Instead there was a ragged old beggar with a patch over one eye, a bent back and a battered bow. The crowd jeered at him. But when the beggar shot, his arrow struck the very centre of the target. One by one the other archers were knocked out, until only the beggar and the Sheriff’s own champion remained.","The champion shot first and split the centre of the target. The crowd roared. Surely no arrow could beat that. The old beggar drew back his bowstring, paused, and let fly. His arrow struck the champion’s arrow and split it from end to end.","The Sheriff, scowling, handed over the silver arrow, never guessing that the man who took it was the outlaw he longed to hang. The beggar shuffled away into the crowd and disappeared.","That night, in the Sheriff’s great hall, a servant found an arrow stuck in the table in front of his chair. Tied to it was a note: Thank you for the silver arrow. It will feed many hungry families this winter. With love from Sherwood – R.H.","Nobody knows whether Robin Hood ever really lived. Stories about him have been told for more than six hundred years, and they have changed with every telling. But perhaps that is why he has lasted so long. Every generation needs a hero who stands up for the poor against the powerful."],"glossary":{"crusades":"Medieval wars fought by European Christians in the Middle East.","outlaws":"People who have broken the law and live in hiding.","archer":"A person who shoots with a bow and arrows.","starvation":"Suffering or death from lack of food.","cunning":"Clever in a sneaky way.","jeered":"Shouted rude remarks to make fun of someone.","scowling":"Frowning angrily.","generation":"All the people born at about the same time."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did Robin Hood steal from the rich?","o":["To become rich himself","To give the money to the poor, who were heavily taxed","Because the King told him to","To pay the Sheriff"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He “gave the money to the poor, who were being taxed into starvation”."},{"q":"How does the writer make the description of the Merry Men humorous?","o":["By repeating “enormously” with three different qualities","By describing their weapons","By giving their ages","By making them sad"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"‘Enormously tall … round … quick-tempered’ creates a comic pattern."},{"q":"Why did the Sheriff organise the archery contest?","o":["To find a new champion","To trap Robin Hood, who would not resist showing off","To raise money for the poor","To celebrate the King’s return"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“He will not be able to resist showing off his skill … we will be waiting.”"},{"q":"What does Robin mean by “Then I shall go without my face”?","o":["He will wear a disguise","He will cut off his face","He will not go","He will cover his eyes"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"He arrives disguised as a ragged old beggar with an eye patch."},{"q":"Why was the beggar’s final shot so remarkable?","o":["It missed completely","It split the champion’s arrow from end to end","It hit the Sheriff","It went over the crowd"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“His arrow struck the champion’s arrow and split it from end to end.”"},{"q":"According to the writer, why have Robin Hood stories lasted so long?","o":["He was a real king","Every generation needs a hero who stands up for the poor against the powerful","The stories never change","Archery is popular"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"The final sentence gives this reason."}]},{"id":"y064","title":"Marie Curie: Science that Glowed","kind":"Biography","theme":"lab","text":["Maria Skłodowska was born in Warsaw, Poland, in 1867. At that time Poland was ruled by Russia, and women were not allowed to go to university there. Maria was the youngest of five children in a family of teachers, and she was so clever that she finished school at fifteen with a gold medal. But to continue studying, she would have to leave the country.","Maria made a deal with her older sister, Bronisława. Maria would work as a governess and send money to support Bronisława while she studied medicine in Paris. Then, once Bronisława was qualified, she would help Maria. The plan took years. Maria did not arrive in Paris until 1891, when she was twenty-four. She enrolled at the Sorbonne, one of the most famous universities in the world, and called herself Marie.","She was poor, and often lived on bread, butter and tea in a freezing attic room. Once she fainted from hunger in a lecture. But she came top of her class in physics. In 1894 she met a French scientist, Pierre Curie, who shared her passion for research. They married the following year.","At that time, scientists had just discovered that uranium gave off mysterious invisible rays. Marie decided to investigate. She found that the strength of the rays depended only on the amount of uranium, which suggested they came from inside the atoms themselves. She called this phenomenon ‘radioactivity’ – a word she invented. Then she noticed that a mineral called pitchblende was much more radioactive than its uranium content could explain. There must be other, unknown elements hidden inside it.","Marie and Pierre spent four years in a leaking shed, stirring huge pots of boiling pitchblende with iron rods as tall as themselves, to extract tiny amounts of these new elements. They discovered two: polonium, which Marie named after her homeland, and radium, which glowed a ghostly blue-green in the dark. In 1903 the Curies shared the Nobel Prize in Physics. Marie was the first woman ever to win a Nobel Prize.","In 1906, Pierre was killed when he was hit by a horse-drawn cart. Marie was heartbroken, but she continued their work and took over his teaching post, becoming the first female professor at the Sorbonne. In 1911 she won a second Nobel Prize, this time in Chemistry. She is still the only person to have won Nobel Prizes in two different sciences. During the First World War, she developed mobile X-ray units, known as ‘little Curies’, which helped doctors treat more than a million wounded soldiers.","Nobody at the time understood how dangerous radiation was. Marie carried test tubes of radium in her pockets. She died in 1934 from an illness caused by years of exposure. Her notebooks are still so radioactive that they are kept in lead-lined boxes, and visitors must sign a form before reading them."],"glossary":{"governess":"A woman employed to teach children in their own home.","qualified":"Having passed the exams needed for a job.","enrolled":"Officially joined a course or school.","passion":"A very strong interest or enthusiasm.","phenomenon":"Something that happens or exists, especially something unusual.","radioactivity":"The giving off of energy by unstable atoms.","extract":"To take out, often with effort.","elements":"The simplest substances, which cannot be broken down into others.","exposure":"Being in contact with something harmful."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did Maria have to leave Poland to continue studying?","o":["She was expelled","Women were not allowed to go to university there","She wanted to learn French","Her family moved"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Women were not allowed to go to university there.”"},{"q":"What was the deal between Maria and her sister?","o":["They would take turns supporting each other’s studies","They would open a shop","They would both become governesses","They would marry scientists"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"Maria supported Bronisława first, who would then help Maria."},{"q":"What details show how poor Marie was as a student?","o":["She lived on bread, butter and tea in a freezing attic and once fainted from hunger","She owned a carriage","She lived in a palace","She ate at restaurants"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"These details appear in the third paragraph."},{"q":"Why did Marie name one element ‘polonium’?","o":["After Pierre","After her homeland, Poland","After a mineral","After a city in France"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Polonium was named “after her homeland”."},{"q":"What does the detail about stirring pitchblende with iron rods “as tall as themselves” suggest?","o":["The work was easy","The work was physically exhausting and on a huge scale","They were short","They were cooking dinner"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Huge pots and rods show how hard and heavy the work was."},{"q":"Why are Marie’s notebooks kept in lead-lined boxes?","o":["They are very valuable","They are still radioactive","They are fragile","They are secret"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Her notebooks are still so radioactive that they are kept in lead-lined boxes.”"}]},{"id":"y065","title":"The Night Bus","kind":"Story","theme":"city","text":["The last bus home left the city centre at 23:47. Danny knew this because he had missed the one before it, and the one before that, and he had spent the last hour sitting in the shelter outside the closed shopping centre, watching his phone battery drain to six per cent.","It wasn’t supposed to be like this. Mum had said ten o’clock. He had said, “Yes, ten o’clock, definitely,” and then the cinema had been late starting, and then they had all gone for chips, and then Ben’s dad had picked Ben up, and somehow Danny had ended up alone in the centre of Leeds with no money, no bus pass and a phone that was dying.","The number 42 came round the corner at last, its windows glowing yellow. Danny climbed on, already rehearsing his explanation.","“I’ve lost my bus pass,” he said. “I haven’t got any money. Please – I just need to get to Headingley.”","The driver was a large man with a grey beard and a badge that said DES. He looked at Danny for a long moment. Danny waited for him to say no.","“Sit down, son,” said Des. “Behind me, where I can see you.”","There were only three other passengers: a nurse in blue scrubs, asleep with her head against the window; a woman in a smart coat, reading; and a teenage boy with headphones, nodding to music only he could hear. The bus rumbled through streets Danny barely recognised at night – shuttered shops, a fox trotting across a car park, the orange glow of the streetlights shining on wet pavements.","His phone died at the second stop. Danny stared at the blank screen and felt panic rise in his chest. Mum would have been ringing. Mum would be frantic.","“Problem?” asked Des, glancing in his mirror.","Danny explained. Des said nothing. Then, at the next red light, he reached under his seat, pulled out a phone charger with a long cable, and passed it back without a word.","By the time they reached Headingley, the phone had come back to life with seventeen missed calls. Danny rang Mum. She did not shout, which was somehow worse. She just said, “Where are you? I’m coming,” in a small, shaky voice.","She was waiting at the stop when the bus pulled in, still in her slippers, a coat thrown over her pyjamas. She hugged him so hard that he could not breathe.","Danny turned to thank Des, but the doors were already closing. Through the window, the driver raised one hand – not quite a wave – and the number 42 pulled away into the dark, carrying its sleeping nurse and its music and its quiet kindness off towards the next stop."],"glossary":{"shelter":"Here, a covered place where people wait for a bus.","rehearsing":"Practising what you are going to say or do.","scrubs":"Loose, plain clothes worn by hospital staff.","shuttered":"With metal shutters pulled down over the windows.","frantic":"Wild with worry or fear."},"questions":[{"q":"Why was Danny stuck in the city centre?","o":["He had been kidnapped","A chain of small delays meant he missed the earlier buses and had no money","He wanted to stay out late","He was meeting Des"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"The cinema started late, they went for chips, Ben was collected and Danny was left alone."},{"q":"Why did Des tell Danny to sit “Behind me, where I can see you”?","o":["He thought Danny was a troublemaker","He wanted to keep a young person safe on a late bus","He wanted someone to talk to","It was the only seat"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"His kind actions later suggest he wanted to look after Danny."},{"q":"How does the writer create the atmosphere of the city at night?","o":["Shuttered shops, a fox, orange streetlights on wet pavements","Loud music and crowds","Bright sunshine","A busy market"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"These details create a quiet, empty, late-night mood."},{"q":"Why was it “somehow worse” that Mum did not shout?","o":["Her quiet, shaky voice showed how frightened and upset she had been","She was asleep","She did not care","She was angry with Des"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"A small, shaky voice reveals her deep worry, making Danny feel guiltier."},{"q":"What does the detail of Mum in slippers and pyjamas show?","o":["She rushed out immediately because she was so worried","She was going to bed","She did not like coats","She forgot it was cold"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"She left in a hurry without dressing properly."},{"q":"What is the main theme of the story?","o":["The dangers of cinemas","The quiet kindness of strangers","How buses work","Losing a bus pass"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"The final line describes Des’s “quiet kindness”."}]},{"id":"y066","title":"The Amazon: River of Giants","kind":"Geography","theme":"jungle","text":["The Amazon is not just a river. It is a whole world. Rising high in the Andes mountains of Peru, just 160 kilometres from the Pacific Ocean, it flows for around 6,400 kilometres across South America before emptying into the Atlantic. Whether it or the Nile is the longest river on Earth is still argued about by geographers, but in every other way, the Amazon has no rival.","It carries more water than any other river in the world – more than the next seven largest rivers combined. Every second, around 200,000 cubic metres of water pour from its mouth into the ocean, enough to fill eighty Olympic swimming pools. So much fresh water floods out that the sea remains less salty for more than 100 kilometres off the coast. Early sailors were amazed to find they could drink the water far out of sight of land.","The river is fed by more than a thousand tributaries, seventeen of which are over 1,500 kilometres long. Together they drain an area of about seven million square kilometres, which is roughly the size of Australia. In the rainy season, the river rises by up to fifteen metres, flooding vast areas of forest. Fish swim among the tree trunks, eating fruit and seeds that fall into the water, and in doing so they help to spread the trees.","The Amazon is home to some extraordinary animals. The arapaima, one of the largest freshwater fish in the world, can grow to three metres and must rise to the surface to gulp air. Pink river dolphins, called botos, use their flexible necks to hunt among the roots of flooded trees. Giant otters live in family groups and can be nearly two metres long. And yes, piranhas live there too, although most species eat mainly fruit, insects and dead fish, and attacks on people are rare.","Remarkably, there is no bridge across the Amazon anywhere along its main course. Much of the river runs through thick rainforest with few roads, and its banks are soft and shift constantly. The river itself is the region’s highway. Boats carry people, food and goods between towns and villages, and Manaus, a city of more than two million people in the heart of the forest, is a busy port, even though it is about 1,500 kilometres from the sea.","Today the Amazon faces serious threats from deforestation, mining, dams and pollution. Scientists warn that if too much of the surrounding rainforest is destroyed, the rains that feed the river could weaken. Protecting the forest, in other words, is also the only way to protect the river."],"glossary":{"rival":"Someone or something that competes with another.","tributaries":"Smaller rivers that flow into a larger one.","drain":"To carry water away from an area.","extraordinary":"Very unusual and remarkable.","flexible":"Able to bend easily.","species":"Groups of living things of the same kind.","remarkably":"Surprisingly.","deforestation":"Cutting down large areas of forest."},"questions":[{"q":"Where does the Amazon begin?","o":["In the Atlantic Ocean","High in the Andes mountains of Peru","In Brazil’s capital","In Australia"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Rising high in the Andes mountains of Peru.”"},{"q":"Why could early sailors drink seawater far from land?","o":["They had special filters","So much fresh water flowed out that the sea stayed less salty","They were near an island","It was raining"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"The fresh water keeps the sea less salty “for more than 100 kilometres off the coast”."},{"q":"Why does the writer compare the water flow to eighty Olympic swimming pools?","o":["To help readers imagine the enormous amount of water","Because there are swimming pools in the Amazon","To show the river is clean","To compare sports"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"A familiar object helps us picture a huge number."},{"q":"How do fish help the rainforest during floods?","o":["They eat insects on leaves","They eat fallen fruit and seeds, spreading the trees","They clean the water","They build dams"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They eat fruit and seeds, “and in doing so they help to spread the trees.”"},{"q":"Why is there no bridge across the Amazon’s main course?","o":["It would be too expensive to paint","Thick forest with few roads and soft, shifting banks","The government banned it","Dolphins would be harmed"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The river runs through forest with few roads and its banks “shift constantly”."},{"q":"What is the writer’s main point in the final paragraph?","o":["Protecting the rainforest is essential to protecting the river","Dams are good for the river","The Amazon is getting longer","Mining is not a problem"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"“Protecting the forest … is also the only way to protect the river.”"}]},{"id":"y067","title":"Titanic: The Unsinkable Ship","kind":"History","theme":"ship","text":["When RMS Titanic left Southampton on 10 April 1912, she was the largest moving object ever built by humans. She was 269 metres long – almost as long as three football pitches – and as tall as an eleven-storey building. Her first-class passengers enjoyed a swimming pool, a gymnasium, a Turkish bath and dining rooms decorated like grand hotels. Newspapers called her ‘practically unsinkable’, because her hull was divided into sixteen watertight compartments; the ship could stay afloat even if four of them flooded.","There were around 2,200 people on board, from millionaires in luxurious suites to families in third class who were emigrating to America in search of a new life. But there were lifeboats for only about 1,178 of them. The law at the time based the number of lifeboats on a ship’s size, not on how many people it carried, and Titanic actually had more lifeboats than the law required. Nobody seriously expected they would be needed.","On the night of 14 April, the ship was crossing the North Atlantic at high speed. The sea was flat calm, the sky clear and moonless, and the air bitterly cold. During the day, Titanic’s radio operators had received several warnings from other ships about icebergs in the area. Not all of them reached the captain.","At 11:40 p.m., the lookouts in the crow’s nest saw a dark shape ahead. “Iceberg, right ahead!” The officers tried to turn the ship, and for a moment it seemed they had succeeded. But the iceberg scraped along the starboard side below the waterline, opening a series of gaps in the hull. Five or six compartments began to flood. The ship’s designer, Thomas Andrews, who was on board, quickly calculated that Titanic would sink within a couple of hours.","The evacuation was chaotic. Many passengers did not believe the ship was in danger, and some of the first lifeboats were lowered half empty. The rule of ‘women and children first’ was followed on parts of the ship, but third-class passengers, deep in the lower decks, found it much harder to reach the boats. The ship’s musicians kept playing to calm people, and continued almost to the end.","At 2:20 a.m. on 15 April, Titanic broke apart and sank. More than 1,500 people died, most of them from the freezing water. The ship Carpathia, which had raced through the ice to help, rescued around 700 survivors from the lifeboats.","The disaster shocked the world and led to important changes. New international rules required enough lifeboat places for everyone on board, lifeboat drills on every voyage and radio rooms staffed twenty-four hours a day. An International Ice Patrol was set up to watch for icebergs in the North Atlantic, and it still operates today. The wreck itself was not found until 1985, lying nearly four kilometres beneath the surface."],"glossary":{"hull":"The main body of a ship.","compartments":"Separate sections inside something.","luxurious":"Very comfortable and expensive.","emigrating":"Leaving your country to live in another.","starboard":"The right-hand side of a ship when facing forward.","evacuation":"Moving people out of a dangerous place.","chaotic":"In complete confusion.","survivors":"People who stay alive after a disaster.","voyage":"A long journey by sea."},"questions":[{"q":"Why was Titanic called ‘practically unsinkable’?","o":["She was made of wood","Her hull had sixteen watertight compartments","She had lots of lifeboats","She was very fast"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Her hull “was divided into sixteen watertight compartments”."},{"q":"Why did Titanic not have enough lifeboats for everyone?","o":["The law based lifeboat numbers on ship size, not passenger numbers","They were stolen","They were too expensive","The captain refused them"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The law … based the number of lifeboats on a ship’s size, not on how many people it carried.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer mention that some ice warnings did not reach the captain?","o":["To suggest the disaster might have been avoided","To show the radio was broken","To blame the passengers","To describe the weather"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Missed warnings hint that better communication could have prevented the collision."},{"q":"Why were some lifeboats lowered half empty?","o":["Many passengers did not believe the ship was in danger","The boats were broken","It was too cold","The captain ordered it"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Many passengers did not believe the ship was in danger.”"},{"q":"What does the detail about the musicians suggest?","o":["They were trying to keep people calm, showing courage","They did not know the ship was sinking","They wanted to be rescued first","They were paid extra"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"They kept playing “to calm people … almost to the end.”"},{"q":"How does the final paragraph show the disaster’s lasting effects?","o":["It describes new safety rules and the Ice Patrol that still operates","It tells a love story","It lists passengers’ names","It describes the menu"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It explains changes to lifeboats, drills, radio and ice patrols."}]},{"id":"y068","title":"Game Review: Lanternfall","kind":"Review","theme":"computer","text":["Platform: Console and PC · Age rating: 7+ · Score: 8/10","Most video games want you to hurry. Lanternfall wants you to slow down. Set on a misty island where the lighthouses have all gone dark, it puts you in the boots of Wren, a young lamplighter who must climb, explore and solve puzzles to relight them one by one. There are no enemies to fight and no timer counting down. Instead, there is a world so beautiful and mysterious that you will want to take your time anyway.","The first thing you notice is the art. Lanternfall looks like a watercolour painting that has come to life. Waves crash against the cliffs in soft blues and greys, sunlight breaks through clouds in golden beams, and at night the whole island glows with the light of every lamp you have restored. The music is gentle and haunting, mostly piano and strings, and it changes subtly as you explore, swelling when you discover something important.","The puzzles are the heart of the game, and they are cleverly designed. Each lighthouse is a different challenge. In one, you must redirect beams of light using mirrors; in another, you must work out the order of a set of bells by listening to the wind. They start simply but become genuinely tricky, and I spent a frustrating half-hour stuck on the clocktower puzzle before the solution suddenly clicked – which felt fantastic. If you get really stuck, a friendly seabird called Pip will give you hints, so younger players should not give up.","Where Lanternfall is less successful is its story. Wren’s quest to find her missing grandfather starts intriguingly, but it is told mostly through letters you find scattered around the island, and some of these are long and slow. By the final chapter, I wanted more drama than the game gave me. The ending is touching but arrives rather suddenly.","The game is also quite short. I finished it in about eight hours, although there are hidden collectables that will keep completists busy for longer. Some players may feel that the price is a little high for the length.","Overall, Lanternfall is a calm, thoughtful and gorgeous game, perfect for anyone who likes puzzles, exploration and beautiful worlds – and for anyone who needs a break from games that shout at them. It is not perfect, but it is the kind of game you will remember long after you have switched it off.","Verdict: Relaxing, beautiful and clever, with a story that does not quite match its ambition."],"glossary":{"platform":"The type of device a game is played on.","restored":"Brought back to its original condition.","haunting":"Beautiful in a sad way that stays in your mind.","subtly":"In a delicate way that is not obvious.","genuinely":"Really; truly.","intriguingly":"In an interesting way that makes you curious.","collectables":"Items in a game that players try to collect.","completists":"People who want to collect or finish absolutely everything.","verdict":"A final judgement or opinion.","ambition":"A strong desire to achieve something."},"questions":[{"q":"What score does the reviewer give Lanternfall?","o":["10/10","8/10","5/10","7+"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Score: 8/10.”"},{"q":"What makes Lanternfall different from most video games, according to the opening?","o":["It is very fast","It encourages you to slow down, with no enemies or timer","It is very violent","It is played outside"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Lanternfall wants you to slow down … There are no enemies to fight and no timer.”"},{"q":"How does the reviewer describe the art style?","o":["Like a cartoon","Like a watercolour painting come to life","Like a photograph","Black and white"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"“Lanternfall looks like a watercolour painting that has come to life.”"},{"q":"Why does the reviewer mention being stuck on the clocktower puzzle?","o":["To show the game is broken","To show the puzzles are challenging but satisfying to solve","To complain about Pip","To give the answer"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"After a frustrating half-hour, the solution clicked, “which felt fantastic.”"},{"q":"What are the reviewer’s two main criticisms?","o":["The art and the music","The slow story and the short length","The puzzles and the hints","The price of the console and the controller"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"The story lacks drama and the game is quite short for the price."},{"q":"How is the review organised?","o":["Positives first, then negatives, then an overall judgement","Only negatives","In the order of the game’s levels","As a list of cheats"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It praises art and puzzles, then criticises story and length, then gives a verdict."}]},{"id":"y069","title":"The Silent Orchestra","kind":"Story","theme":"music","text":["The concert hall was full. Eight hundred people had come to hear the Northfield Youth Orchestra’s spring concert, and Aisha, in the second row of violins, could feel every one of them watching. She checked her music for the tenth time. She checked her bow. She checked that her phone was switched off. Then she looked up at the conductor, Mr Lindqvist, raising his baton, and the lights in the hall went out.","Every single light. The stage lights, the music-stand lamps, the green exit signs. The darkness was so complete that Aisha could not see her own hand.","There was a gasp from the audience, then a ripple of nervous laughter, then silence. Somewhere a child began to cry. Someone shouted that it was a power cut across the whole street.","Aisha’s heart thumped. They could not play in the dark. They could not read their music.","Then Mr Lindqvist’s voice came out of the blackness, quiet and steady. “Orchestra,” he said. “We know the first movement. We have played it a hundred times. Listen to each other. I will count you in.”","Aisha thought he must be joking. But he counted – one, two, three, four – and the cellos began, low and soft, exactly on time. After a moment, the violas joined them. Aisha raised her violin. Her hands were shaking. She could not see the music, but as she listened, she realised she did not need to. She knew it. Her fingers knew it. She began to play.","It was the strangest performance she had ever been part of. Without the music in front of her, she listened as she had never listened before – to the breathing of the flute player beside her, to the tiny pause before the brass came in, to the way the whole orchestra seemed to lean into the louder passages like trees bending in the wind. Mistakes happened: somebody came in early, somebody played a wrong note. But no one stopped, and the music kept going, held together by eight hundred people listening in the dark.","In the last bars, as the orchestra rose to the final chord, the lights flickered and came back on. Aisha blinked. Mr Lindqvist was standing on his podium with his eyes shut and tears running down his face.","For a second the hall was completely silent. Then the audience rose to its feet, and the applause was so loud that Aisha felt it in her chest.","Afterwards, in the corridor, the first trumpet, a tall boy called Femi who never spoke to anyone, came over and shook her hand. “Best we’ve ever played,” he said.","Aisha thought about it. They had played perfectly before, in rehearsals, with every light on. But she knew what he meant. That night, they had not just played the notes. They had played together."],"glossary":{"conductor":"The person who leads an orchestra.","baton":"A thin stick used by a conductor.","movement":"Here, one main section of a long piece of music.","violas":"Stringed instruments slightly larger than violins.","passages":"Sections of a piece of music.","podium":"A small raised platform for a speaker or conductor.","applause":"Clapping to show approval.","rehearsals":"Practice sessions before a performance."},"questions":[{"q":"What happened just as the concert was about to begin?","o":["The conductor fell over","All the lights went out","Aisha broke a string","The audience left"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The lights in the hall went out.”"},{"q":"Why was Mr Lindqvist’s voice described as “quiet and steady”?","o":["He was tired","He was calming the orchestra and showing he was in control","He was angry","He had a cold"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"His calm voice reassures the young players in a frightening moment."},{"q":"How did Aisha manage to play without seeing her music?","o":["She guessed","She had practised so much that she knew it by heart","She used a torch","She copied the cellos"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“She knew it. Her fingers knew it.”"},{"q":"The orchestra leaned into loud passages “like trees bending in the wind”. What does this suggest?","o":["The players were falling over","They were moving together naturally, as one","They were outside","The music was quiet"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Trees bending together in the wind suggests unity and shared movement."},{"q":"Why was Mr Lindqvist crying at the end?","o":["He was hurt","He was deeply moved by how the orchestra had played together","He was angry about the power cut","The lights hurt his eyes"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"His emotion shows how special the performance was."},{"q":"What does Aisha mean when she says “They had played together”?","o":["They all played the same note","They truly listened and worked as one team, not just played correctly","They played in the same room","They played very loudly"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"In the dark they listened to each other as never before."}]},{"id":"y070","title":"Why Bees Need Our Help","kind":"Non-fiction","theme":"nature","text":["Take a look at your breakfast. If you had toast with jam, an apple, a glass of orange juice or a bowl of cereal with strawberries, there is a good chance that a bee helped to make it. About three-quarters of the world’s main food crops depend at least partly on pollination by insects, and bees are the most important pollinators of all.","Pollination happens when pollen is carried from one flower to another, allowing the plant to make seeds and fruit. As a bee visits flowers to collect sweet nectar and protein-rich pollen for food, pollen grains stick to the tiny hairs on its body and are brushed off on the next flower it visits. Without this help, many plants could not reproduce. Bees help to produce not only fruit and vegetables but also nuts, seeds, coffee and even the clover and alfalfa that feed cattle.","When most people think of bees, they think of honeybees, which live in large colonies and are kept by beekeepers. But in Britain alone there are around 270 species of bee. About 250 of them are solitary bees, which do not live in hives, make honey or have a queen. Each female builds her own small nest, in a hole in the ground, a hollow stem or a crack in a wall. There are also around 25 species of bumblebee, large and furry bees that can fly in cooler weather than many other insects.","Unfortunately, bees are in trouble. Since the 1950s, Britain has lost the vast majority of its wildflower meadows, as land has been turned into farmland, roads and houses. Fewer flowers mean less food for bees. Some pesticides, chemicals sprayed on crops to kill pests, can harm bees too, affecting their ability to find their way home. Disease, parasites and climate change add to the pressure. Two species of bumblebee have become extinct in Britain in the last hundred years.","The good news is that everyone can help, even without a garden. Planting bee-friendly flowers such as lavender, foxgloves, sunflowers and herbs like thyme and rosemary – even in a window box – provides food. Leaving a patch of lawn uncut lets dandelions and clover bloom. Avoiding pesticides helps, and a simple ‘bee hotel’, made from a bundle of hollow canes or a block of wood with drilled holes, gives solitary bees somewhere to nest.","If you find a tired bumblebee on the ground in spring, you can give it a little sugar water on a spoon: two parts white sugar to one part water. Never give honey, which can spread disease between hives.","Bees have been pollinating flowers for more than a hundred million years. With a little help from us, they can keep doing it for a long time yet."],"glossary":{"pollination":"The carrying of pollen between flowers so that seeds and fruit can form.","pollinators":"Animals, such as bees, that carry pollen between flowers.","nectar":"A sweet liquid made by flowers.","reproduce":"To produce young or new plants.","colonies":"Large groups of animals living together.","solitary":"Living alone.","pesticides":"Chemicals used to kill insects and other pests.","parasites":"Living things that live on or in another and harm it.","extinct":"No longer existing anywhere."},"questions":[{"q":"Why does the writer start by asking the reader to look at their breakfast?","o":["To make the reader hungry","To show how much of our everyday food depends on bees","To advertise cereal","To change the subject"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"It links bees to familiar foods before explaining pollination."},{"q":"How does a bee carry pollen between flowers?","o":["In its mouth","Pollen sticks to the tiny hairs on its body","It flies with a basket","It carries it in honey"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Pollen grains stick to the tiny hairs on its body.”"},{"q":"How are solitary bees different from honeybees?","o":["They make more honey","They do not live in hives, make honey or have a queen","They are larger","They only live in Africa"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Solitary bees “do not live in hives, make honey or have a queen.”"},{"q":"What is the main reason bees have less food in Britain?","o":["Bees eat too much","The loss of wildflower meadows","Too much rain","People eat their honey"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Losing meadows means “Fewer flowers mean less food for bees.”"},{"q":"Which of these is NOT suggested as a way to help bees?","o":["Planting lavender","Leaving lawn uncut","Building a bee hotel","Giving bees honey"],"a":3,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The writer warns: “Never give honey.”"},{"q":"What is the tone of the last paragraph?","o":["Hopeless","Hopeful and encouraging","Angry","Confused"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"“With a little help from us, they can keep doing it for a long time yet.”"}]},{"id":"y071","title":"A Poison Tree","kind":"Poem","theme":"garden","author":"William Blake (1794)","text":["I was angry with my friend;\nI told my wrath, my wrath did end.\nI was angry with my foe:\nI told it not, my wrath did grow.","And I waterd it in fears,\nNight & morning with my tears:\nAnd I sunned it with smiles,\nAnd with soft deceitful wiles.","And it grew both day and night,\nTill it bore an apple bright.\nAnd my foe beheld it shine,\nAnd he knew that it was mine.","And into my garden stole,\nWhen the night had veild the pole;\nIn the morning glad I see;\nMy foe outstretched beneath the tree."],"glossary":{"wrath":"Great anger.","foe":"An enemy.","waterd":"Watered (Blake’s spelling).","deceitful":"Dishonest; trying to trick people.","wiles":"Clever tricks used to deceive someone.","beheld":"Saw.","stole":"Here, crept quietly.","veild":"Veiled (Blake’s spelling): covered or hidden.","outstretched":"Lying stretched out."},"questions":[{"q":"What happened when the speaker told his friend he was angry?","o":["His anger grew","His anger ended","They had a fight","The friend left"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“I told my wrath, my wrath did end.”"},{"q":"What happened when he did not tell his foe?","o":["His anger ended","His anger grew","He forgot about it","He made a new friend"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“I told it not, my wrath did grow.”"},{"q":"What is the speaker’s anger compared to in stanzas two and three?","o":["A storm","A tree that is watered and grows","A river","A fire"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"He waters it with tears and suns it with smiles until it bears an apple."},{"q":"What do “soft deceitful wiles” and “smiles” suggest?","o":["He was openly angry","He hid his anger behind false friendliness","He was genuinely happy","He told jokes"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He smiled and used deceitful tricks while his anger secretly grew."},{"q":"What happens to the foe at the end?","o":["He becomes a friend","He steals the apple and is found lying dead or still beneath the tree","He plants his own tree","He apologises"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He creeps into the garden at night and is “outstretched beneath the tree”."},{"q":"What is the message of the poem?","o":["Gardening is relaxing","Anger that is hidden and nursed can grow into something deadly; it is better to talk about it","Apples are poisonous","Never make enemies"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"The first stanza contrasts expressing anger (it ends) with hiding it (it grows)."}]},{"id":"y072","title":"The Industrial Revolution","kind":"History","theme":"victorian","text":["In 1750, most people in Britain lived in the countryside and worked on the land, much as their ancestors had done for centuries. Goods such as cloth were made by hand at home, and the fastest way to travel was on horseback. By 1900, Britain was a nation of factories, railways and smoking chimneys, and most people lived in towns and cities. This enormous change is called the Industrial Revolution, and it began here, in Britain.","It started with cloth. Spinning cotton into thread and weaving it into fabric had always been slow work. Then, in the 1760s and 1770s, a series of inventions changed everything. James Hargreaves’ spinning jenny allowed one worker to spin eight threads at once. Richard Arkwright’s water frame used the power of rivers to drive spinning machines, and in 1771 Arkwright built one of the world’s first factories at Cromford in Derbyshire. Instead of working at home, workers now came to the machines.","The next great breakthrough was steam power. In the 1770s, the Scottish engineer James Watt greatly improved the steam engine, making it far more efficient. Steam engines could pump water out of mines, drive machines in factories and, eventually, pull trains. Factories no longer needed to be built beside rivers; they could be built anywhere there was coal to burn. Britain had plenty of coal, and coal mining expanded rapidly.","Transport changed too. Canals were dug to carry heavy goods such as coal. Then, in 1825, the Stockton and Darlington Railway opened, followed in 1830 by the Liverpool and Manchester Railway, the first to carry passengers between cities using only steam locomotives. Within thirty years, railways crossed the whole country, and journeys that had taken days took hours.","For many workers, however, life was hard. Families poured into towns such as Manchester, Leeds and Birmingham, where they crowded into cheap, damp houses, often with no clean water or proper toilets. Diseases such as cholera spread quickly. In the factories and mines, people worked twelve hours a day or more, six days a week. Children as young as five were employed, crawling under dangerous machinery to tie broken threads or pulling coal carts through narrow tunnels underground. Accidents were common.","Over time, campaigners fought for better conditions. The Factory Act of 1833 banned children under nine from working in textile mills and limited the hours of older children. Later laws improved housing, sanitation and schooling. By the end of the 1800s, children had to go to school until the age of ten.","The Industrial Revolution made Britain the richest and most powerful country in the world, and it shaped the modern world we live in. But it was built on the hard work, and often the suffering, of ordinary people."],"glossary":{"ancestors":"Family members who lived long ago.","efficient":"Working well without wasting energy.","expanded":"Grew larger.","locomotives":"Railway engines.","cholera":"A dangerous disease caught from dirty water.","campaigners":"People who work to bring about change.","textile":"To do with cloth or weaving.","sanitation":"Systems for clean water and getting rid of sewage."},"questions":[{"q":"Which industry did the Industrial Revolution begin with?","o":["Railways","Cloth","Coal mining","Shipbuilding"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“It started with cloth.”"},{"q":"Why did Arkwright’s water frame change where people worked?","o":["Workers now had to go to factories where the machines were","It could be used at home","It was carried around","It was powered by horses"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“Instead of working at home, workers now came to the machines.”"},{"q":"Why did steam power allow factories to be built almost anywhere?","o":["Steam engines did not need rivers, only coal","Steam was free","The government paid for it","Rivers dried up"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Factories no longer needed to be built beside rivers.”"},{"q":"What was special about the Liverpool and Manchester Railway?","o":["It was the first canal","It was the first to carry passengers between cities using only steam locomotives","It was underground","It was horse-drawn"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It was “the first to carry passengers between cities using only steam locomotives”."},{"q":"Why does the writer describe children crawling under machinery?","o":["To show how dangerous and harsh child labour was","To show children enjoyed factory work","To explain how machines worked","To praise factory owners"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"These details highlight the risk and cruelty of child labour."},{"q":"What is the writer’s overall view in the final paragraph?","o":["The Industrial Revolution was entirely good","It brought wealth and progress but was built on the suffering of ordinary people","It was a complete failure","It only affected rich people"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"It made Britain rich and powerful, “But it was built on … the suffering of ordinary people.”"}]},{"id":"y073","title":"Climate Change: What’s Really Happening?","kind":"Science","theme":"storm","text":["The Earth has always had a natural blanket. Gases in the atmosphere, such as carbon dioxide, methane and water vapour, trap some of the heat from the Sun and stop it escaping back into space. This is called the greenhouse effect, and without it, the Earth’s average temperature would be about minus eighteen degrees Celsius – far too cold for most life. The greenhouse effect is not the problem. The problem is that humans have been making the blanket thicker.","Since the Industrial Revolution began, people have been burning huge amounts of coal, oil and gas – fossil fuels formed from plants and animals that died millions of years ago. Burning them releases carbon dioxide. Cutting down forests adds to the problem, because trees absorb carbon dioxide as they grow. Farming, especially raising cattle, releases methane. As a result, there is now about fifty per cent more carbon dioxide in the atmosphere than there was in 1750 – more than at any time in at least 800,000 years, according to evidence from air bubbles trapped in ancient ice.","More greenhouse gas means more heat is trapped. The Earth’s average temperature has already risen by more than one degree Celsius since the late 1800s. That may not sound like much – after all, the temperature changes far more than that between morning and afternoon. But a global average rise of even one degree means enormous extra energy in the climate system, and it is causing changes all around the world.","Ice is melting. Glaciers in the Alps and Himalayas are shrinking, and the Arctic sea ice in summer covers far less of the ocean than it did forty years ago. As ice on land melts and seawater expands as it warms, sea levels are rising, threatening coastal cities and low-lying islands. Heatwaves are becoming more frequent and more intense; in 2022, temperatures in the UK passed forty degrees for the first time on record. Some regions are suffering more droughts and wildfires, while others face heavier rainfall and flooding.","Wildlife is affected too. Some animals and plants are moving towards the poles or up mountains to stay cool. Others cannot move fast enough. Warmer oceans are bleaching coral reefs, and changes in the timing of the seasons can mean that birds hatch after the insects they feed on have already peaked.","The good news is that we know what needs to be done. Countries around the world have agreed to try to limit warming, and the cost of clean energy such as solar and wind power has fallen dramatically. Britain now gets much of its electricity from renewable sources. Electric vehicles, better insulated homes, protecting forests and wasting less food all help.","Scientists say that every fraction of a degree matters. The more we can reduce emissions now, the less severe the changes will be for the generations who come after us – including yours."],"glossary":{"atmosphere":"The layer of gases surrounding the Earth.","vapour":"Tiny drops of liquid floating in the air, like steam.","fossil":"Formed from the remains of ancient living things.","absorb":"To take in or soak up.","glaciers":"Huge slow-moving masses of ice.","expands":"Gets bigger.","droughts":"Long periods with little or no rain.","renewable":"Able to be replaced naturally, such as wind or solar power.","emissions":"Gases sent out into the air."},"questions":[{"q":"What would the Earth’s average temperature be without the greenhouse effect?","o":["About 18°C","About −18°C","About 40°C","About 0°C"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“About minus eighteen degrees Celsius.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer say “The greenhouse effect is not the problem”?","o":["It is natural and keeps the Earth warm enough for life; the problem is humans making it stronger","It does not exist","It only happens in greenhouses","It is good for plants"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The natural effect keeps us warm; humans are “making the blanket thicker”."},{"q":"How do we know carbon dioxide levels are the highest in 800,000 years?","o":["From air bubbles trapped in ancient ice","From old newspapers","From tree leaves","From satellites only"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“According to evidence from air bubbles trapped in ancient ice.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer compare a one-degree rise to daily temperature changes?","o":["To dismiss climate change","To answer readers who might think one degree is small, then explain why it matters","To give a weather forecast","To talk about mornings"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"The writer admits it “may not sound like much” before explaining the enormous energy involved."},{"q":"Why might birds struggle if the seasons change?","o":["They cannot fly in heat","They may hatch after their insect food has already peaked","They lose their feathers","They migrate to the Moon"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Birds may “hatch after the insects they feed on have already peaked.”"},{"q":"What is the tone of the final two paragraphs?","o":["Hopeless","Hopeful and encouraging action","Angry and blaming","Humorous"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"“The good news is that we know what needs to be done.”"}]},{"id":"y074","title":"Nelson Mandela: The Long Walk to Freedom","kind":"Biography","theme":"city","text":["Nelson Rolihlahla Mandela was born in 1918 in a small village in the Eastern Cape of South Africa. His father was a chief of the Thembu people, and his first name, Rolihlahla, means ‘pulling the branch of a tree’ – or, as some translate it, ‘troublemaker’. A teacher at his primary school gave him the English name Nelson.","Mandela grew up in a country divided by race. White people, who were a minority, held almost all the power and wealth. From 1948, the government introduced a system called apartheid, meaning ‘separateness’. Under apartheid, black South Africans were forced to live in separate areas, use separate schools, hospitals and beaches, and carry passes to move around their own country. They could not vote. Mixed marriages were banned.","Mandela trained as a lawyer and, in 1952, opened South Africa’s first black-owned law firm with his friend Oliver Tambo. He joined the African National Congress (ANC), which campaigned against apartheid. At first, the ANC used only peaceful protest: strikes, marches and refusing to obey unjust laws. But in 1960, police opened fire on a peaceful crowd at Sharpeville, killing sixty-nine people. The government banned the ANC. Mandela came to believe that peaceful protest alone would not end apartheid, and he helped to set up an armed wing of the ANC, which attacked government buildings and power lines.","In 1964 Mandela was sentenced to life in prison. At his trial, he gave a famous speech in which he said that he had fought against both white domination and black domination, and that his ideal was a free society in which all people lived together in harmony. “It is an ideal which I hope to live for and to achieve,” he said. “But if needs be, it is an ideal for which I am prepared to die.”","He spent the next twenty-seven years in prison, eighteen of them on Robben Island, where he slept on the floor of a tiny cell and was forced to break rocks in a limestone quarry. The glare of the white rock damaged his eyesight. Yet he continued to study, to argue for his cause and to encourage other prisoners. Around the world, a campaign grew to ‘Free Nelson Mandela’, and many countries refused to trade with South Africa.","On 11 February 1990, Mandela walked out of prison, aged seventy-one. Instead of seeking revenge, he worked with the white president, F. W. de Klerk, to end apartheid peacefully. In 1993 they shared the Nobel Peace Prize. In 1994, in South Africa’s first election in which people of all races could vote, Mandela was elected president. He set up a Truth and Reconciliation Commission to help the country face the crimes of the past without starting a new cycle of violence.","Mandela died in 2013, aged ninety-five. He once wrote that after climbing a great hill, one only finds that there are many more hills to climb. His long walk had changed a nation."],"glossary":{"minority":"A smaller group within a larger population.","apartheid":"A system in South Africa that separated people by race.","campaigned":"Worked in an organised way to achieve change.","unjust":"Unfair.","domination":"Control over others by power.","harmony":"Living or working together peacefully.","quarry":"A place where stone is dug out of the ground.","revenge":"Hurting someone because they hurt you.","reconciliation":"Making friends again after a disagreement."},"questions":[{"q":"What does ‘apartheid’ mean?","o":["Freedom","Separateness","Equality","Peace"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Apartheid means “‘separateness’”."},{"q":"Which of these was a rule under apartheid?","o":["Everyone could vote","Black South Africans had to carry passes and use separate facilities","All schools were mixed","White people could not own land"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Black South Africans used separate schools and beaches and carried passes."},{"q":"Why did Mandela stop believing that peaceful protest alone would work?","o":["The Sharpeville massacre and the banning of the ANC","He wanted to be famous","He lost an election","He moved abroad"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"After police killed sixty-nine people and the ANC was banned, he helped set up an armed wing."},{"q":"What was Mandela’s ideal, according to his trial speech?","o":["A country ruled only by black people","A free society where all people live together in harmony","Revenge on the government","Leaving South Africa"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“A free society in which all people lived together in harmony.”"},{"q":"What does Mandela’s behaviour after his release show about his character?","o":["He was bitter and wanted revenge","He was forgiving and focused on peace","He wanted to retire","He was afraid"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“Instead of seeking revenge, he worked with the white president … to end apartheid peacefully.”"},{"q":"What does the quotation about climbing hills suggest?","o":["Mandela enjoyed hiking","Every achievement reveals more challenges still to face","Robben Island was hilly","He was tired"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Even after great success, there is always more work to be done."}]},{"id":"y075","title":"The Whistle in the Fog","kind":"Story","theme":"sea","text":["Fog came to Port Isaac the way a cat comes into a room – silently, and as if it owned the place. One minute Ellie could see the harbour wall, the bobbing fishing boats and the white cottages climbing the hill. The next, there was nothing but grey, so thick that the end of the slipway had vanished.","Her grandfather, Jory, should have been back an hour ago.","He had gone out at dawn to check his lobster pots, as he had done every morning for fifty years. “Back for breakfast,” he had said, pulling on his yellow oilskins. But breakfast had come and gone, and now it was almost lunchtime, and the fog had swallowed the whole of Port Gaverne bay.","Ellie stood on the end of the harbour wall with her mother, both of them staring into nothing. Somewhere out there, the sea slapped against rocks they could not see. A gull cried and was gone.","“He’ll be fine,” said Mum, in the voice she used when she was not sure. “He knows these waters better than anyone.”","But Ellie knew something Mum did not. Last week, Grandad had told her that the compass in his little boat was broken, and that he kept meaning to fix it. In fog like this, with no compass, even the best fisherman in Cornwall could row in circles until his arms gave out – or straight onto the rocks below Lobber Point.","Ellie thought hard. Then she remembered the other thing Grandad had told her, years ago, when she was small enough to sit on his knee. “In a fog, sound is your friend,” he had said. “Your eyes can’t help you. But your ears can.”","She ran. Up the slipway, up the steep street, into Grandad’s cottage, where his old brass whistle hung on a hook by the door. He had used it to call her in for tea when she was little – three short blasts and one long. She grabbed it and ran back down to the harbour wall, her lungs burning.","Then she blew. Three short, one long. Again. Again.","For five minutes, nothing. Ellie’s lips were sore. Mum put a hand on her shoulder. And then, out of the grey, faint and far away, came an answering sound – a voice, calling her name.","“Keep going!” shouted Mum. “Keep blowing!”","Ellie blew until her head spun. The voice came closer. And at last, out of the fog, came the dark shape of a little blue boat, with an old man in yellow oilskins rowing steadily towards the sound.","“Heard you,” said Grandad, as he tied up at the steps, grey-faced and soaking but smiling. “Three short, one long. Time for tea, is it?”","Ellie laughed and burst into tears at the same time, which she had not known was possible."],"glossary":{"slipway":"A sloping ramp leading down into the water, for launching boats.","oilskins":"Waterproof clothes worn by sailors and fishermen.","compass":"An instrument with a needle that always points north.","blasts":"Short, loud bursts of sound."},"questions":[{"q":"Why was Ellie worried about her grandfather?","o":["He was ill","He was late back and the fog was very thick","He had lost his boat","He was angry"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He should have been back an hour ago, and fog had covered the bay."},{"q":"“Fog came to Port Isaac the way a cat comes into a room.” What does this simile suggest about the fog?","o":["It was noisy and fast","It arrived silently and took over completely","It was friendly","It was black and white"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Like a cat, it came “silently, and as if it owned the place.”"},{"q":"What did Ellie know that her mother did not?","o":["Grandad’s compass was broken","Grandad had a new boat","Grandad had gone fishing in another town","Grandad was lost on purpose"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The compass in his little boat was broken.”"},{"q":"How did Grandad’s advice help Ellie?","o":["It told her to use her ears, so she used sound to guide him","It told her to swim out","It told her to call the coastguard","It told her to wait"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“In a fog, sound is your friend” – so she blew the whistle."},{"q":"Why did Ellie choose the pattern “three short, one long”?","o":["It was Grandad’s familiar signal for calling her in, so he would recognise it","It was the only sound the whistle made","It was a code for danger","It was the loudest pattern"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"He had used it to call her in for tea when she was little."},{"q":"What does Ellie laughing and crying at once show?","o":["She is confused","She feels huge relief and happiness after great fear","She is hurt","She is annoyed with Grandad"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"Her mixed emotions show the release of tension now that he is safe."}]},{"id":"y076","title":"One Giant Leap: The Moon Landing","kind":"History","theme":"space","text":["On 25 May 1961, the American president, John F. Kennedy, made an astonishing promise. Before the decade was out, he told Congress, the United States would land a man on the Moon and return him safely to the Earth. At that point, the United States had sent exactly one astronaut into space, for just fifteen minutes. Nobody knew how to do what Kennedy had promised.","The promise was part of the Space Race, a competition between the United States and the Soviet Union, the two most powerful countries in the world. The Soviets had already launched the first satellite, Sputnik, in 1957, and in April 1961 Yuri Gagarin had become the first human to orbit the Earth. Americans were desperate to catch up.","Over the next eight years, around 400,000 people worked on the Apollo programme. They designed the Saturn V, the most powerful rocket ever flown at the time, as tall as a thirty-six-storey building. Many of the calculations were checked by hand by mathematicians, including Katherine Johnson, whose work helped plan the flight paths. There were terrible setbacks: in 1967, three astronauts died when fire broke out in their capsule during a test on the ground.","On 16 July 1969, Apollo 11 lifted off from Florida carrying three astronauts: Neil Armstrong, Buzz Aldrin and Michael Collins. Four days later, while Collins orbited the Moon alone in the command module Columbia, Armstrong and Aldrin climbed into the lunar module, Eagle, and began their descent.","The landing nearly went wrong. As they approached the surface, alarms began to sound on the computer, and Armstrong saw that the landing site was covered in large boulders. He took manual control and steered the Eagle across the surface, searching for a flat spot. When they finally touched down, they had only about twenty-five seconds of fuel left before they would have had to abort. Armstrong radioed: “Houston, Tranquility Base here. The Eagle has landed.”","Six and a half hours later, Armstrong climbed down the ladder and stepped onto the Moon. His words were heard by around 600 million people watching on television: “That’s one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.” Aldrin followed him. They planted an American flag, collected rocks and soil, set up experiments and left a plaque reading: ‘We came in peace for all mankind.’","On 24 July, the astronauts splashed down safely in the Pacific Ocean. Kennedy’s promise had been kept, although he did not live to see it. Twelve people walked on the Moon between 1969 and 1972. No one has been back since – but their footprints are still there, preserved in the dust because there is no wind on the Moon to blow them away."],"glossary":{"astonishing":"Very surprising.","congress":"The group of elected people who make laws in the USA.","satellite":"An object that orbits a planet.","orbit":"To travel around a planet or star in a curved path.","setbacks":"Problems that delay progress.","capsule":"A small compartment in a spacecraft for the crew.","lunar":"To do with the Moon.","descent":"The act of going down.","abort":"To stop something before it is finished, especially because of danger.","preserved":"Kept in its original state."},"questions":[{"q":"What did President Kennedy promise in 1961?","o":["To build a space station","To land a man on the Moon and return him safely before the decade was out","To send a satellite to Mars","To beat Yuri Gagarin into orbit"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He promised to “land a man on the Moon and return him safely”."},{"q":"Why was Kennedy’s promise so astonishing?","o":["The USA had sent only one astronaut into space, for fifteen minutes","The Moon had never been seen","Rockets did not exist","He was not president"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The USA had very little experience, and “Nobody knew how to do” it."},{"q":"What was the Space Race?","o":["A rocket competition at school","A competition between the USA and the Soviet Union in space exploration","A running race on the Moon","A film"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“A competition between the United States and the Soviet Union.”"},{"q":"Why did the landing nearly go wrong?","o":["The astronauts fell asleep","Alarms sounded, the site was covered in boulders and fuel was running low","The Moon was too dark","They forgot the flag"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"Alarms, boulders and only about twenty-five seconds of fuel left."},{"q":"Why did Michael Collins not walk on the Moon?","o":["He was ill","He stayed in orbit in the command module","He was on Earth","He was afraid"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Collins orbited the Moon alone in the command module Columbia.”"},{"q":"Why are the astronauts’ footprints still on the Moon?","o":["They were made of metal","There is no wind on the Moon to blow them away","Robots protect them","They are under glass"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“There is no wind on the Moon to blow them away.”"}]},{"id":"y077","title":"Let Teenagers Sleep: Start School Later","kind":"Persuasive","theme":"city","text":["It is 7:15 on a Tuesday morning. Across Britain, alarm clocks are ringing in teenagers’ bedrooms, and millions of young people are dragging themselves out of bed, bleary-eyed and grumpy, to catch a bus to a school that starts at 8:30. Their parents may think they are lazy. Science says they are simply out of sync. It is time for secondary schools to start later.","First, the evidence is clear. During adolescence, the body’s internal clock shifts. The brain starts releasing melatonin, the hormone that makes us sleepy, up to two hours later than it does in younger children or adults. This means that most teenagers genuinely cannot fall asleep before about eleven o’clock, however hard they try. Yet experts recommend that they need between eight and ten hours of sleep. Getting up at seven makes that impossible. Asking a teenager to be alert at 8:30 is, biologically, like asking an adult to be alert at 5:30.","Second, tired students learn less. Sleep is when the brain stores new memories. Students who are short of sleep find it harder to concentrate, remember what they have learned and control their emotions. When schools in parts of the United States moved their start time to 8:30 or later, studies found that students got more sleep, attendance improved, and grades in early lessons rose.","Third, this is a matter of health and safety. Lack of sleep in teenagers is linked to anxiety, depression and weight gain. In the US, researchers have even found that later school start times reduced car accidents among teenage drivers.","Opponents raise reasonable concerns. Some parents need to drop their children off early before work. After-school clubs and sports would finish later. Bus timetables would need to change. But these are practical problems, and practical problems have practical solutions. Schools could open early for breakfast clubs, so that parents can still drop off children. Clubs could move to lunchtimes. The benefits to millions of young people far outweigh a little rearranging.","Some people say teenagers should simply go to bed earlier and put their phones away. Of course, good sleep habits matter. But no amount of willpower can completely override biology. We would not blame a baby for needing naps; we should not blame a teenager for their body clock.","Starting school at ten o’clock might be unrealistic. But starting at 9:30 instead of 8:30 could give teenagers a precious extra hour. Imagine a generation of students who are healthier, happier and better able to learn. All it takes is the courage to change the clock."],"glossary":{"bleary-eyed":"Tired, with eyes that cannot focus properly.","sync":"Out of sync means not matching or not working at the same time.","adolescence":"The time when a young person develops into an adult.","melatonin":"A hormone that makes us feel sleepy.","biologically":"In terms of how living bodies work.","attendance":"How often someone is present.","opponents":"People who disagree with something.","outweigh":"To be greater or more important than.","override":"To overrule or cancel out."},"questions":[{"q":"Why can most teenagers not fall asleep before about eleven o’clock?","o":["They play on phones","Their brain releases melatonin up to two hours later","They drink coffee","They are not tired"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The brain releases melatonin “up to two hours later”."},{"q":"Why does the writer compare a teenager at 8:30 to an adult at 5:30?","o":["To show how unnatural early starts feel to a teenage body","Because adults start work at 5:30","To show adults are lazy","To give the time difference with America"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"It helps adults understand how early 8:30 feels to a teenager’s body clock."},{"q":"What evidence does the writer give that later starts work?","o":["American schools that changed saw more sleep, better attendance and higher grades","A survey of teachers","The writer’s own opinion only","A story about one pupil"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Studies in the US found these improvements."},{"q":"How does the writer deal with opponents’ concerns?","o":["Ignores them","Accepts they are reasonable but offers practical solutions","Agrees school should start earlier","Says they are lying"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"“Opponents raise reasonable concerns … practical problems have practical solutions.”"},{"q":"What point does the writer make by mentioning babies needing naps?","o":["Teenagers are like babies","We should not blame young people for natural biological needs","Babies should go to school","Naps are a waste of time"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Just as we accept babies’ needs, we should accept teenagers’ body clocks."},{"q":"How does the final paragraph try to persuade the reader?","o":["With a realistic compromise and an inspiring vision of the future","With frightening statistics","By insulting opponents","By changing the topic"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"It suggests 9:30 and asks us to “Imagine a generation of students who are healthier, happier”."}]},{"id":"y078","title":"The Brontës: Three Sisters on the Moors","kind":"Biography","theme":"books","text":["In 1820, a clergyman named Patrick Brontë moved with his wife and six young children to the village of Haworth in West Yorkshire. Their home, the parsonage, stood at the top of a steep cobbled street, between a crowded graveyard and the wild, open moors. It was a lonely place, but from that small stone house would come some of the most famous novels in the English language.","Tragedy struck early. In 1821 the children’s mother died. Four years later, the two eldest daughters, Maria and Elizabeth, caught tuberculosis at a harsh boarding school and died within weeks of each other. The four remaining children – Charlotte, Branwell, Emily and Anne – were brought up by their father and their aunt.","Left largely to themselves, the children created imaginary worlds. In 1826, their father brought Branwell a box of twelve wooden soldiers, and the children gave each one a name and a personality. From these toys grew whole kingdoms, called Angria and Gondal, with their own wars, politicians and love stories. The children wrote about them in hundreds of tiny books, some no bigger than a matchbox, in handwriting so small it can only be read with a magnifying glass.","As adults, the sisters worked as teachers and governesses, jobs they mostly disliked. In 1845, Charlotte discovered a notebook of Emily’s poems and was amazed by how good they were. The three sisters decided to publish a book of poetry together. Because women writers were often not taken seriously, they used men’s names: Currer, Ellis and Acton Bell. The book sold just two copies.","Undaunted, each sister wrote a novel. In 1847, Charlotte published Jane Eyre, the story of a poor, plain, fiercely independent governess. It was an immediate success. Emily’s Wuthering Heights, a dark and passionate story of love and revenge set on the Yorkshire moors, and Anne’s Agnes Grey followed soon after. Many readers were shocked by their strong emotions and outspoken heroines, and there was great curiosity about who the ‘Bell brothers’ really were.","Their success was short-lived. Their brother Branwell, who had once seemed the most talented of them all, died in 1848 after years of struggling with alcohol and drugs. Emily caught a cold at his funeral and died three months later, aged thirty. Anne died the following spring, aged twenty-nine. Charlotte, the last surviving sibling, married in 1854 but died the next year, before her thirty-ninth birthday.","Today, the parsonage at Haworth is a museum, and visitors from all over the world walk the moors that inspired the sisters. Their tiny books are kept in glass cases – the first stories of three women who refused to let anything, even their own names, stop them from writing."],"glossary":{"clergyman":"A priest or minister in the church.","parsonage":"A house provided for a church minister.","moors":"Wide areas of open, wild land covered in grass and heather.","tuberculosis":"A serious disease that affects the lungs.","governesses":"Women employed to teach children in their homes.","undaunted":"Not discouraged by difficulty.","independent":"Not relying on others; free-thinking.","outspoken":"Saying openly what you think.","sibling":"A brother or sister."},"questions":[{"q":"Where did the Brontë family live?","o":["London","A parsonage in Haworth, West Yorkshire","A castle in Scotland","Paris"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They lived in “the parsonage” in Haworth, West Yorkshire."},{"q":"What started the imaginary worlds of Angria and Gondal?","o":["A box of twelve wooden soldiers","A trip to the seaside","A book from school","A dream"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Their father brought Branwell a box of twelve wooden soldiers.”"},{"q":"Why did the sisters use men’s names when publishing?","o":["They were ashamed of their writing","Women writers were often not taken seriously","Their father told them to","Their names were too long"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Because women writers were often not taken seriously.”"},{"q":"What does “Undaunted” tell us about how the sisters reacted to their poetry selling two copies?","o":["They gave up writing","They were not discouraged and kept writing","They were angry","They became teachers"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"‘Undaunted’ means not discouraged – they went on to write novels."},{"q":"Why were some readers shocked by the novels?","o":["They were very short","They had strong emotions and outspoken heroines","They were written in French","They had no endings"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Readers were “shocked by their strong emotions and outspoken heroines”."},{"q":"What is the main idea of the final sentence?","o":["The tiny books are valuable","The sisters were determined to write despite all obstacles","Haworth is a nice museum","The moors are dangerous"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"They “refused to let anything, even their own names, stop them from writing.”"}]},{"id":"y079","title":"Your Amazing Skeleton","kind":"Science","theme":"lab","text":["Without your skeleton, you would be a shapeless heap on the floor. The 206 bones of the adult human skeleton hold you up, give your body its shape and let you move. But bones do far more than act as a frame. They protect your organs, make your blood and store minerals that your body needs. And, surprisingly, they are very much alive.","You were actually born with around 300 bones. As a baby grows, some of them fuse together. The skull, for example, starts as several separate plates with soft gaps between them, which allows a baby’s head to squeeze through the birth canal and gives the brain room to grow. Over the first two years, the plates join to form a single strong case. Similar fusing happens in the base of the spine and the hips.","The skeleton has three main jobs. The first is support: the spine, made of thirty-three bones called vertebrae, holds you upright, while the bones of your legs carry your weight. The second is protection. Your skull guards your brain, your ribs form a cage around your heart and lungs, and the vertebrae surround the delicate spinal cord. The third is movement. Muscles are attached to bones by strong cords called tendons. When a muscle contracts, it pulls on the bone and moves it at a joint – the place where two bones meet.","Bones are not solid all the way through. The outside is hard and dense, but inside there is a honeycomb of spongy bone, which makes bones strong yet light. Inside many bones is a soft jelly-like tissue called bone marrow. Red marrow is a busy factory, producing around two to three million new red blood cells every second, as well as white blood cells that fight infection. Bones also store calcium, which your body uses to keep your heart beating and your muscles working.","Because bones are living tissue, they can repair themselves. When you break a bone, the body forms a clot around the break, then builds a soft bridge of new tissue, which gradually hardens into new bone. A cast or splint holds the pieces in the right position while this happens. A broken arm in a child usually heals in about six weeks.","Bones also respond to how you use them. Exercise that puts weight on your bones, such as running, jumping and dancing, makes them thicker and stronger. Astronauts living in space, where there is no gravity pulling on them, can lose bone mass quickly, which is why they must exercise for about two hours every day on the International Space Station.","To keep your skeleton healthy, scientists recommend plenty of calcium (found in milk, cheese, yoghurt and leafy green vegetables), vitamin D (which your skin makes in sunlight) and regular exercise. Look after your bones now, and they will look after you for life."],"glossary":{"minerals":"Natural substances, such as calcium, that the body needs.","fuse":"To join together into one.","vertebrae":"The small bones that make up the spine.","delicate":"Easily damaged.","tendons":"Strong cords that attach muscles to bones.","contracts":"Becomes shorter and tighter.","dense":"Closely packed and heavy.","marrow":"Soft tissue inside bones where blood cells are made.","calcium":"A mineral needed for strong bones and teeth."},"questions":[{"q":"How many bones does an adult have?","o":["300","206","33","106"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The 206 bones of the adult human skeleton.”"},{"q":"Why is it useful that a baby’s skull is made of separate plates?","o":["It makes the baby lighter","It lets the head squeeze through at birth and gives the brain room to grow","It stops the baby falling","It protects the eyes"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The gaps allow the head to squeeze through and give the brain room to grow."},{"q":"What are the three main jobs of the skeleton?","o":["Eating, sleeping and breathing","Support, protection and movement","Thinking, seeing and hearing","Growing, shrinking and fusing"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"The third paragraph explains support, protection and movement."},{"q":"Why does the writer call red bone marrow “a busy factory”?","o":["It makes millions of blood cells every second","It is noisy","It makes bones","It has workers"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"It produces “two to three million new red blood cells every second”."},{"q":"Why must astronauts exercise for two hours a day?","o":["To pass the time","Without gravity they lose bone mass quickly","To stay warm","To win races"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"With no gravity pulling on them, they “can lose bone mass quickly”."},{"q":"Which statement is true according to the article?","o":["Bones are dead","Bones are living tissue that can repair themselves","Bones are solid all the way through","Exercise weakens bones"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Because bones are living tissue, they can repair themselves.”"}]},{"id":"y080","title":"The Selkie’s Skin","kind":"Myth","theme":"sea","text":["On the islands of Orkney, where the wind never stops and the sea is never far away, people tell stories about the selkies. In the water they are seals, sleek and grey, with dark eyes that seem almost human. But on certain nights, the old stories say, they swim to lonely beaches, slip off their sealskins and dance on the sand as men and women. If a selkie loses its skin, it cannot return to the sea.","Long ago, a young fisherman named Magnus was walking home along the shore at midsummer when he heard music and laughter coming from a cove below the cliffs. Creeping closer, he saw a group of people dancing in the moonlight – and beside them, on the rocks, a heap of sealskins. One skin, softer and silver-grey, lay a little apart from the others. Without stopping to think, Magnus snatched it up and hid it under his coat.","When the dancers saw him, they ran shrieking to the rocks, pulled on their skins and dived into the sea. All except one. A young woman with long dark hair searched the rocks, weeping. When she saw Magnus, she knew at once what he had done.","“Give it back,” she said. “I cannot go home without it.”","But Magnus had fallen in love with her the moment he saw her, and he would not give it back. She had no choice but to go with him. In time they married, and she bore him three children. She was gentle and kind, and the children adored her. But every evening she would walk down to the shore and stand looking out at the sea, and sometimes a large grey seal would lift its head from the waves and look back at her.","Magnus kept the sealskin hidden in the roof beams of their cottage, wrapped in an old sack, and he never spoke of it. For many years, nobody found it.","Then one day, when Magnus was out fishing, the youngest child climbed up into the roof to fetch a ball and found the sack. “Mother,” she called, “what is this soft grey coat?”","The selkie took the skin in her hands and held it to her face for a long time. Then she kissed each of her children, told them she loved them, and walked down to the shore. There she pulled on the skin, and with one last look back at the land, she slipped into the sea.","When Magnus came home, the cottage was empty except for the children. He ran to the beach and called her name until his voice was gone. But she never returned.","The children grew up by the sea, and they were always lucky fishermen. Their nets were always full, and people said it was because a grey seal followed their boat wherever it went, watching over them."],"glossary":{"selkies":"In Scottish folklore, creatures that can change between seals and humans.","sleek":"Smooth and shiny.","cove":"A small sheltered bay on a coast.","snatched":"Grabbed quickly.","shrieking":"Screaming in a high voice.","adored":"Loved very much.","beams":"Long, thick pieces of wood supporting a roof."},"questions":[{"q":"What happens if a selkie loses its sealskin?","o":["It dies","It cannot return to the sea","It turns into a bird","It becomes a fisherman"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“If a selkie loses its skin, it cannot return to the sea.”"},{"q":"Why did Magnus take the silver-grey skin?","o":["To sell it","He acted without thinking and wanted the woman to stay","To keep warm","He thought it was rubbish"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He snatched it “without stopping to think” and had fallen in love with her."},{"q":"What does the selkie’s evening walk to the shore suggest?","o":["She enjoyed exercise","She longed to return to the sea and her old life","She was looking for Magnus","She was fishing"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"She stood looking at the sea, and a grey seal looked back at her."},{"q":"How is the selkie’s skin finally found?","o":["Magnus gives it back","The youngest child finds it in the roof while fetching a ball","A storm blows it out","The seal steals it"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The youngest child “climbed up into the roof to fetch a ball and found the sack.”"},{"q":"What does the selkie’s goodbye to her children show?","o":["She did not love them","She loved her children but could not resist returning home","She was angry with them","She planned to come back the next day"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"She kissed each child and said she loved them before going."},{"q":"What does the ending suggest about the selkie?","o":["She forgot her family","She continued to watch over her children from the sea","She became human again","She was caught by fishermen"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"A grey seal followed their boat, “watching over them”."}]},{"id":"y081","title":"Composed upon Westminster Bridge","kind":"Poem","theme":"city","author":"William Wordsworth (1802)","text":["Earth has not any thing to shew more fair:\nDull would he be of soul who could pass by\nA sight so touching in its majesty:\nThis City now doth, like a garment, wear\nThe beauty of the morning; silent, bare,\nShips, towers, domes, theatres, and temples lie\nOpen unto the fields, and to the sky;\nAll bright and glittering in the smokeless air.","Never did sun more beautifully steep\nIn his first splendour, valley, rock, or hill;\nNe’er saw I, never felt, a calm so deep!\nThe river glideth at his own sweet will:\nDear God! the very houses seem asleep;\nAnd all that mighty heart is lying still!"],"glossary":{"shew":"An old spelling of ‘show’.","majesty":"Impressive beauty and dignity.","doth":"An old word for ‘does’.","garment":"A piece of clothing.","steep":"Here, to soak or bathe something completely.","splendour":"Great and impressive beauty.","ne’er":"Never.","glideth":"An old form of ‘glides’: moves smoothly."},"questions":[{"q":"Where is the speaker standing?","o":["On a hill","On Westminster Bridge","In a church","On a ship"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The title is ‘Composed upon Westminster Bridge’."},{"q":"What time of day is described?","o":["Midnight","Early morning","Midday","Evening"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The city wears “The beauty of the morning”."},{"q":"What does “Dull would he be of soul who could pass by” mean?","o":["Only a person with no feeling could walk past without being moved","The bridge is boring","People should hurry","The soul is dull in the morning"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The sight is so moving that only someone with a dull soul would ignore it."},{"q":"The city wears the morning “like a garment”. What does this simile suggest?","o":["The city is dressed beautifully in morning light","The city is cold","The city is covered in washing","The city is hidden"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Morning light covers the city like beautiful clothing."},{"q":"Why is the air described as “smokeless”?","o":["It is early, before chimneys and factories are lit","There is a fog","London has no factories","It is raining"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"In the early morning, fires have not yet been lit, so the air is clear."},{"q":"What does the final line, “And all that mighty heart is lying still!”, suggest?","o":["The city is like a sleeping giant whose heart is at rest","Someone has died","The river has stopped","The poet is ill"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"London is personified as a living being with a heart, peacefully asleep before the busy day."}]},{"id":"y082","title":"The Mystery of Stonehenge","kind":"History","theme":"fossil","text":["On Salisbury Plain in Wiltshire stands one of the most famous prehistoric monuments in the world. Seen from the road, Stonehenge can look surprisingly small. Up close, it is overwhelming: a ring of enormous grey stones, some taller than two double-decker buses stacked on top of each other, with massive horizontal stones balanced across their tops. It was built without metal tools, wheels or machinery – and for centuries, nobody could explain how or why.","Stonehenge was not built all at once. Archaeologists believe the first stage, around 3000 BC, was a circular ditch and bank, with a ring of pits that may once have held timber posts or stones. About 500 years later, around 2500 BC, the great stones were raised. Work continued, on and off, for perhaps 1,500 years.","There are two main types of stone. The huge sarsens, each weighing around twenty-five tonnes, came from the West Woods area, about twenty-five kilometres to the north. The smaller bluestones, weighing two to five tonnes each, came from an astonishing distance away: the Preseli Hills in west Wales, around 240 kilometres away. Recent research suggests that one stone at the centre, called the Altar Stone, came from even further – north-east Scotland, more than 700 kilometres away.","How were they moved? Neolithic people had no wheels, so they probably dragged the stones on wooden sledges, perhaps over greased timber tracks or rollers, using ropes and the strength of hundreds of people. Experiments have shown that a team of around a hundred people could drag a large stone this way. To raise the stones upright, builders probably dug a pit, levered one end of the stone into it and then pulled it upright with ropes. The horizontal stones on top, called lintels, are joined to the uprights with joints copied from woodwork, such as the ones carpenters use to fit furniture together.","Why it was built remains the biggest mystery. Stonehenge is carefully aligned with the sun: at the summer solstice, the longest day of the year, the sun rises over a stone called the Heel Stone, and at the winter solstice, it sets in the opposite direction. This suggests that the movements of the sun and the seasons were important to its builders. Human remains have been found buried there, so it was probably also a place for honouring the dead. Some archaeologists think it may have been a place of healing, or of gathering and feasting; excavations nearby have found the remains of huge feasts, with pigs that had been brought from as far away as Scotland.","Perhaps it was all of these things. What is certain is that Stonehenge took an extraordinary amount of planning, cooperation and effort. Thousands of years later, people still gather there to watch the sun rise on midsummer morning, just as its builders once did."],"glossary":{"prehistoric":"From the time before written records.","monuments":"Large structures built to remember a person or event.","overwhelming":"So great that it is hard to take in.","horizontal":"Flat and level.","archaeologists":"People who study the past by digging up remains.","sledges":"Platforms on runners used to drag heavy loads.","levered":"Moved by using a bar to lift or push.","aligned":"Arranged in a straight line with something.","solstice":"The longest or shortest day of the year.","cooperation":"Working together."},"questions":[{"q":"When were the great stones of Stonehenge raised?","o":["Around 3000 BC","Around 2500 BC","Around AD 1000","Around 1500 BC"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“About 500 years later, around 2500 BC, the great stones were raised.”"},{"q":"Where did the bluestones come from?","o":["Salisbury Plain","The Preseli Hills in west Wales","France","Scotland"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They came from “the Preseli Hills in west Wales”."},{"q":"Why does the writer compare the stones to two double-decker buses?","o":["To help readers imagine how tall they are","Because buses visit Stonehenge","To show they are red","To show they move"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"A familiar object helps readers picture the size."},{"q":"How do experts think the stones were moved?","o":["By lorries","Dragged on wooden sledges by teams of people","By boats only","By horses with carts"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“They probably dragged the stones on wooden sledges.”"},{"q":"What suggests the sun was important to Stonehenge’s builders?","o":["It is aligned with sunrise at the summer solstice and sunset at the winter solstice","It has a roof","It faces London","It is painted yellow"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Its careful alignment with the solstices suggests the sun and seasons mattered."},{"q":"What is the writer’s conclusion about the purpose of Stonehenge?","o":["It was definitely a hospital","It may have had several purposes, but it certainly required great planning and cooperation","It was a castle","It had no purpose"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"“Perhaps it was all of these things. What is certain is…”"}]},{"id":"y083","title":"The Last Days of Pompeii","kind":"Diary","theme":"volcano","text":["23 August, AD 79\nIt has been a strange summer. For days the ground has trembled under our feet – small shudders that rattle the cups on the table and make the dogs whine. Father says there is nothing to worry about; Campania has always had earthquakes, and Pompeii has been rebuilt before. This morning the water in the fountain by the bakery stopped running, and old Flavia said the springs have dried up in the hills. Still, the market was busy and noisy, and tonight we will eat figs from our own garden.","24 August, morning\nToday is the day after the festival of Vulcan, god of fire. I write that now and it makes my hand shake.","Around midday, there was a sound like the sky splitting open. Everyone in the street stopped and looked north, towards the mountain we call Vesuvius. A huge cloud was rising from its top – tall and straight like the trunk of a pine tree, then spreading out at the top into branches. It was white in places and dark in others, as if it had been stained with earth. It rose higher and higher until it blotted out half of the sky.","24 August, afternoon\nThe cloud has come down on us. Small stones are falling, light and full of holes, like pumice from the bathhouse. They are pattering on the roof like hail, piling up in the courtyard. The sky has gone dark as night, though it is afternoon. People are running through the streets with cushions tied to their heads to protect them from the stones. Some are carrying their treasures, others their children. Mother has wrapped the little ones in blankets. Father says we must leave for Nuceria, to the south, at once.","24 August, evening\nWe are on the road with hundreds of others. The stones are deeper now; in places they come up to our knees, and roofs in the town have begun to collapse under the weight. Behind us, Vesuvius glows red. Lightning flashes inside the cloud. Some of our neighbours stayed, hoping it would pass. I keep thinking of Flavia, who said she was too old to walk so far.","This diary entry is imagined, but the events are real. We know a great deal about the eruption because a young Roman named Pliny the Younger watched it from across the bay and later described it in two letters. It was he who compared the cloud to a pine tree. His uncle, Pliny the Elder, an admiral and scientist, sailed towards the eruption to rescue people and died on the shore.","In the early hours of 25 August, a scorching avalanche of gas, ash and rock – a pyroclastic flow – swept down the mountain and over Pompeii, killing everyone who remained. The city was buried under about six metres of ash and lay forgotten for more than 1,500 years. When archaeologists began excavating it in the 1700s, they found streets, shops, houses, paintings and even loaves of bread exactly as they had been left. They also found hollow spaces in the ash where bodies had once been. By filling these with plaster, they made casts that show the last moments of the people of Pompeii."],"glossary":{"trembled":"Shook slightly.","shudders":"Sudden shaking movements.","pumice":"A very light rock full of holes, formed from lava.","pattering":"Making quick light tapping sounds.","admiral":"A senior commander in a navy.","scorching":"Extremely hot.","avalanche":"A large mass of material falling rapidly down a mountain.","pyroclastic":"Made of hot ash, gas and rock thrown out by a volcano.","excavating":"Carefully digging up.","casts":"Shapes made by pouring a liquid such as plaster into a mould."},"questions":[{"q":"What warning signs appeared before the eruption?","o":["Earth tremors and springs drying up","Heavy snow","A comet in the sky","Animals singing"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The ground trembled and the fountain and springs dried up."},{"q":"Why does writing about the festival of Vulcan make the diarist’s hand shake?","o":["Vulcan is the god of fire, which feels terrible after the eruption","The festival was boring","The writer was cold","Vulcan was the writer’s father"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"It is a grim coincidence that the eruption followed the festival of the fire god."},{"q":"Why did people tie cushions to their heads?","o":["To sleep on the road","To protect themselves from falling stones","As a disguise","Because it was a custom"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Cushions “protect them from the stones”."},{"q":"Why does the writer include the paragraph beginning “This diary entry is imagined”?","o":["To separate fiction from historical fact and explain the real sources","To end the diary early","To apologise","To introduce a new character"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It explains the diary is imagined but based on real events and Pliny’s letters."},{"q":"Who first compared the eruption cloud to a pine tree?","o":["Pliny the Younger","Pliny the Elder","Flavia","The diarist’s father"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“It was he [Pliny the Younger] who compared the cloud to a pine tree.”"},{"q":"How did archaeologists create the famous body casts?","o":["By carving stone statues","By filling hollow spaces in the ash with plaster","By painting pictures","By using photographs"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“By filling these with plaster, they made casts.”"}]},{"id":"y084","title":"Charles Darwin and the Voyage of the Beagle","kind":"Biography","theme":"ship","text":["As a boy, Charles Darwin was not considered especially brilliant. Born in Shrewsbury in 1809, he was more interested in collecting beetles, shooting birds and wandering the countryside than in his lessons. His father once told him, “You care for nothing but shooting, dogs and rat-catching, and you will be a disgrace to yourself and all your family.” He was sent to Edinburgh to study medicine but found operations – carried out at the time without anaesthetic – so horrifying that he left. His father then sent him to Cambridge to train to become a clergyman.","At Cambridge, Darwin’s real passion grew. He studied plants, rocks and animals with leading scientists, and one of them, a professor named John Henslow, recommended him for an extraordinary opportunity. A naval ship, HMS Beagle, was setting off to map the coast of South America, and the captain, Robert FitzRoy, wanted a gentleman naturalist to come along. In December 1831, aged twenty-two, Darwin sailed from Plymouth. The voyage was supposed to last two years. It lasted almost five.","Darwin was dreadfully seasick for much of the journey, but on land he was in his element. He explored rainforests in Brazil, rode with gauchos across the plains of Argentina, dug up the fossils of giant extinct mammals and experienced an earthquake in Chile. Everywhere he went, he made detailed notes and collected thousands of specimens, which he packed and sent back to England.","In 1835 the Beagle reached the Galápagos Islands, a group of volcanic islands in the Pacific Ocean, about 1,000 kilometres off the coast of Ecuador. Darwin noticed something curious. The islands were close together, yet many of their animals were slightly different from one island to the next. The local governor told him that he could tell which island a giant tortoise came from just by the shape of its shell. Darwin also collected small birds, which later turned out to be many different species of finch, each with a beak suited to a different kind of food.","Back in England, Darwin spent more than twenty years thinking about what he had seen. He came to an idea that would change science for ever: natural selection. In every generation, living things vary slightly. Those whose features suit their environment best are more likely to survive and have young, passing those features on. Over immense periods of time, these small changes add up, and new species can evolve. The finches’ beaks, he realised, had been shaped by the food available on each island.","Darwin knew his idea would be controversial, because it challenged the belief that every species had been created separately and had never changed. He might have waited even longer, but in 1858 another naturalist, Alfred Russel Wallace, sent him a letter describing almost exactly the same theory. Their ideas were presented together, and in 1859 Darwin published On the Origin of Species. It sold out on its first day.","The book caused fierce debate, but Darwin’s theory is now the foundation of modern biology. The boy who cared for nothing but beetles and rats became one of the most important scientists who ever lived."],"glossary":{"disgrace":"Someone who brings shame on others.","anaesthetic":"A drug that stops you feeling pain during an operation.","clergyman":"A priest or minister.","naturalist":"A person who studies plants and animals.","specimens":"Examples of plants, animals or rocks collected for study.","volcanic":"Formed by volcanoes.","immense":"Extremely large.","evolve":"To develop gradually over a long time.","controversial":"Causing a lot of disagreement."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did Darwin give up studying medicine?","o":["He failed his exams","He found operations without anaesthetic horrifying","He ran out of money","His father stopped him"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He found operations “so horrifying that he left”."},{"q":"Why does the writer include his father’s harsh comment?","o":["To show how surprising Darwin’s later success was","To prove Darwin was lazy all his life","To describe his father’s job","To show Darwin liked rats"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It contrasts with the final sentence about him becoming a great scientist."},{"q":"How long did the voyage of the Beagle actually last?","o":["Two years","Almost five years","Ten years","Six months"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The voyage was supposed to last two years. It lasted almost five.”"},{"q":"What did Darwin notice in the Galápagos Islands?","o":["All the animals were identical","Animals differed slightly from island to island","There were no birds","The islands were frozen"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Many animals “were slightly different from one island to the next”."},{"q":"Which statement best explains natural selection?","o":["Animals choose to change","Individuals best suited to their environment survive and pass on their features, causing change over time","All species were created at once","Animals learn new tricks"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"Those whose features suit their environment are more likely to survive and pass them on."},{"q":"Why did Darwin finally publish his theory in 1859?","o":["His father insisted","Wallace sent him a letter describing a very similar theory","He finished his voyage","He was asked by the King"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Wallace’s letter pushed him to present his ideas rather than wait longer."}]},{"id":"y085","title":"The Escape Room","kind":"Story","theme":"clockwork","text":["“Sixty minutes,” said the woman in the black T-shirt, pressing a button on the wall. A digital clock above the door lit up red: 60:00. “The key to this door is somewhere in this room. Good luck.” The door clicked shut behind her.","Jamal, Ruby, Kit and Ollie looked around. They were in what was supposed to be a Victorian explorer’s study: a heavy wooden desk, a globe, shelves of dusty books, a locked chest, a stuffed owl with glass eyes and a grandfather clock that was not ticking. It was Ollie’s birthday treat, and Ollie had been talking about it for weeks.","“Right,” said Ruby, who liked being in charge. “Everyone search. Shout if you find anything.”","For ten minutes there was chaos. Kit pulled every book off the shelves. Jamal spun the globe until it squeaked. Ruby rattled the chest. Ollie, who was small and quiet and had been looking forward to this more than anyone, kept trying to say something, but nobody listened.","At 48:12 they found a note inside a hollow book: The explorer’s journey began where the sun rises. They argued about it for five minutes. Kit said it meant the globe. Ruby said it meant a window, but there were no windows. Jamal said it meant nothing and was a waste of time.","At 39:40, Ollie said, quite loudly, “The owl.”","Everyone turned.","“Its eyes,” said Ollie, going pink. “They’re not looking at us. They’re both pointing at the clock. I noticed when we came in.”","Ruby opened her mouth, then closed it again. They went to the grandfather clock. Ollie opened its glass door and moved the hands to six o’clock – “sunrise,” he explained – and with a soft clunk, a drawer slid open at the bottom of the clock. Inside was a map with a dotted line and four numbers written along the route.","After that, Ollie was in charge, though nobody said so. He spotted that the numbers matched the latitude lines on the globe. He noticed that one book on the floor had a different-coloured spine. He realised that the chest’s lock needed letters, not numbers, and that the letters were the first letters of the four countries on the map.","At 03:17, the chest opened. Inside, on a velvet cushion, lay a heavy iron key.","They burst out of the door with seconds to spare, cheering. The woman in the black T-shirt raised her eyebrows and wrote their time on a whiteboard. It was the third fastest of the month.","Afterwards, in the café, Ruby pushed the last slice of birthday cake across the table to Ollie. “Next time,” she said, “we listen to you first.”","Ollie grinned. “Next time,” he said, “I’ll shout.”"],"glossary":{"chaos":"Complete confusion and disorder.","hollow":"Empty inside.","latitude":"Lines on a globe showing how far north or south a place is.","velvet":"A soft, smooth fabric with a thick pile."},"questions":[{"q":"How long did the group have to escape?","o":["Thirty minutes","Sixty minutes","Ten minutes","Two hours"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Sixty minutes,” and the clock showed 60:00."},{"q":"What does the first search suggest about how the group was working?","o":["They were calm and organised","They were rushing without a plan and ignoring Ollie","They had already solved it","They were asleep"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"There was “chaos”, and “Ollie … kept trying to say something, but nobody listened.”"},{"q":"What clue did Ollie notice?","o":["The owl’s eyes pointed at the clock","The globe was broken","The door was unlocked","The books were all red"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“They’re both pointing at the clock.”"},{"q":"Why did Ollie set the clock to six o’clock?","o":["It was his birthday","The note said the journey began where the sun rises – at sunrise","It was the time on the map","He guessed randomly"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He explains, “sunrise,” linking it to the note."},{"q":"“After that, Ollie was in charge, though nobody said so.” What does this sentence show?","o":["The others quietly began to follow Ollie because his ideas worked","Ollie took over rudely","Ruby left","Nobody was in charge"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"His success earned him leadership without anyone needing to announce it."},{"q":"What lesson do the characters learn?","o":["Escape rooms are too hard","Quiet people may have the best ideas, so it is important to listen to everyone","Always be fast","Birthday cake is the best part"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"Ruby says, “Next time, we listen to you first.”"}]},{"id":"y086","title":"Penguins: Life at the Bottom of the World","kind":"Non-fiction","theme":"arctic","text":["Penguins are birds that cannot fly – but watch one underwater and you would never guess it was clumsy. On land they waddle and stumble; in the sea they twist, dive and race, using their stiff, flipper-like wings to ‘fly’ through the water. Gentoo penguins can swim at around thirty-six kilometres per hour, making them the fastest swimming birds in the world.","There are eighteen species of penguin, and they live almost entirely in the Southern Hemisphere. Many people imagine that all penguins live on ice, but only a few species, such as the Emperor and Adélie penguins, live in Antarctica itself. Others live on the coasts of South America, southern Africa, Australia and New Zealand. The Galápagos penguin even lives near the equator, keeping cool in the cold ocean currents that flow there.","Penguins are superbly built for life in cold water. Their feathers are short, stiff and packed tightly together – more densely than on any other bird – and coated with waterproof oil. Beneath their skin is a thick layer of fat called blubber. Their black and white colouring is camouflage: seen from above, their dark backs blend into the deep water; seen from below, their white bellies blend into the bright surface.","The most extraordinary penguin of all is the Emperor. At up to 1.2 metres tall, it is the largest species, and it breeds in the middle of the Antarctic winter, when temperatures can fall below minus forty degrees and winds reach 140 kilometres per hour. After the female lays a single egg, she carefully passes it to the male and walks up to a hundred kilometres to the sea to feed. The male balances the egg on his feet, covered by a warm fold of skin called a brood pouch, for about two months. He eats nothing at all during this time.","To survive the cold, thousands of males huddle together, shuffling slowly so that each penguin takes a turn on the outside of the group before moving back into the warmth of the middle. Inside a huddle, the temperature can be over twenty degrees Celsius warmer than outside. When the chick hatches, the female returns, finds her mate among thousands of others by recognising his call, and takes over feeding the chick. The thin, exhausted male, who may have lost almost half his body weight, finally heads to the sea.","Many penguin species are under threat. Climate change is reducing the sea ice that Emperor penguins need to raise their chicks and changing where their food, such as krill and fish, can be found. Overfishing, oil spills and pollution add to the danger. Scientists now monitor penguin colonies using satellite images, counting them by the brown stains their droppings leave on the ice – which can be seen from space."],"glossary":{"hemisphere":"One half of the Earth, north or south of the equator.","equator":"An imaginary line around the middle of the Earth.","superbly":"Extremely well.","densely":"Closely packed together.","blubber":"A thick layer of fat under the skin of sea animals.","camouflage":"Colours or patterns that help an animal blend in.","brood":"A brood pouch is a fold of skin that keeps an egg warm.","huddle":"A tightly packed group.","krill":"Tiny shrimp-like sea creatures.","colonies":"Large groups of animals living together."},"questions":[{"q":"Which is the fastest swimming bird in the world?","o":["Emperor penguin","Gentoo penguin","Galápagos penguin","Adélie penguin"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Gentoo penguins are “the fastest swimming birds in the world.”"},{"q":"Which common belief about penguins does the writer correct?","o":["That penguins can fly","That all penguins live on ice in Antarctica","That penguins eat fish","That penguins are birds"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Many people imagine that all penguins live on ice, but only a few species … live in Antarctica.”"},{"q":"How does a penguin’s colouring help it?","o":["It attracts mates","It provides camouflage from above and below","It keeps it warm","It frightens predators"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Dark backs blend into deep water; white bellies blend into the bright surface."},{"q":"What does the male Emperor penguin do while the female feeds?","o":["He goes fishing too","He balances the egg on his feet for about two months without eating","He builds a nest","He sleeps in the sea"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The male balances the egg on his feet … He eats nothing at all.”"},{"q":"Why do the penguins shuffle around in the huddle?","o":["So that each takes a turn on the cold outside and in the warm middle","To dance","To find food","To escape predators"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Shuffling means each penguin “takes a turn on the outside … before moving back into the warmth”."},{"q":"What surprising method do scientists use to count penguins?","o":["Counting footprints in snow","Looking for droppings stains on satellite images","Listening to their calls","Weighing them"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They count them “by the brown stains their droppings leave on the ice”."}]},{"id":"y087","title":"Speech: Think Before You Post","kind":"Speech","theme":"computer","text":["Good afternoon, Year 7. I want to start by telling you about a girl called Megan. That is not her real name, and she gave me permission to tell her story.","Last year, Megan posted a photo of herself in a new dress before a family party. She was excited. Within an hour, someone in her class had shared it in a group chat with the caption, ‘Who let her out like this?’ Twenty people laughed at it. Some added comments. Most of them, if you had asked, would have said they were only joking. Megan saw every single one. She did not go to the party. She did not come to school the next day, or the day after.","I am telling you this because the people who laughed were not monsters. They were ordinary students, like you and me. They did not think. That is all. They did not think about the person on the other end of the screen.","Here is the thing about the internet: it removes faces. When you say something unkind to someone in real life, you see their expression change. You see that you have hurt them, and most people stop. Online, you see nothing. You just see a screen. And it becomes very easy to forget that behind every username and every photo there is a real person, with real feelings.","So I want to give you three questions to ask yourself before you post, share or comment. First: is it true? A lot of what spreads online is rumour, exaggeration or simply lies. Second: is it kind? Would you say it to that person’s face, in front of their family? Third: is it necessary? Does the world really need this comment? If the answer to any of those questions is no, put your phone down.","And there is something else. Not posting cruel things is a good start, but it is not enough. In Megan’s story, twenty people saw that message. Not one of them said, ‘That’s not funny.’ Not one of them messaged Megan to say, ‘Ignore it – you look great.’ Sometimes the most powerful thing you can do is be the one person who speaks up. It takes courage. But it can change everything for someone who feels completely alone.","If you are ever on the receiving end of something like this, please remember: it is not your fault, and you do not have to deal with it by yourself. Screenshot it, block the person and tell an adult you trust – a parent, a teacher, your tutor. You can also report it to the platform.","Megan is doing well now, by the way. She is in Year 9, she runs the school’s photography club, and her photos are brilliant. But she still remembers that day.","So, before you post: is it true, is it kind, is it necessary? Think. Because the person on the other side of the screen is real. Thank you."],"glossary":{"permission":"Allowing someone to do something.","caption":"Words written under or with a picture.","expression":"The look on someone’s face showing how they feel.","rumour":"A story passed around that may not be true.","exaggeration":"Making something seem bigger or worse than it is.","courage":"Bravery.","platform":"A website or app where people share content."},"questions":[{"q":"Why does the speaker begin with Megan’s story?","o":["To give a real example that makes the audience care about the issue","To embarrass Megan","To talk about dresses","To advertise a party"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"A real story creates empathy before the speaker gives advice."},{"q":"What happened after the photo was shared?","o":["Megan won a prize","Megan did not go to the party or to school for days","Megan laughed","Nothing"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“She did not go to the party. She did not come to school the next day, or the day after.”"},{"q":"What does the speaker mean by “the internet … removes faces”?","o":["Online, we cannot see how our words hurt people, so it is easier to be cruel","People do not post selfies","The internet deletes photos","Faces are blurred online"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"In real life you see someone’s expression change; online “you see nothing.”"},{"q":"What are the three questions to ask before posting?","o":["Is it funny, popular and quick?","Is it true, kind and necessary?","Is it long, short and clear?","Is it new, old and shared?"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Is it true? … Is it kind? … Is it necessary?”"},{"q":"What does the speaker say is “not enough”?","o":["Simply not posting cruel things yourself – we should also speak up for others","Reading the news","Having a phone","Going to school"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Not posting cruel things is a good start, but it is not enough.”"},{"q":"Why does the speaker repeat the three questions at the end?","o":["To help the audience remember the key message","Because they forgot what they said","To fill time","To confuse the audience"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Repetition in speeches makes key points memorable."}]},{"id":"y088","title":"The Black Death","kind":"History","theme":"castle","text":["In June 1348, a ship docked at the small port of Melcombe, in Dorset. Somewhere on board – in the fleas living on black rats in the cargo, or in the bodies of sailors already sick – was a disease that would kill around half the population of England in less than two years. People at the time called it the Great Mortality. Today we call it the Black Death.","The disease is now known to have been plague, caused by bacteria called Yersinia pestis. It had spread from Central Asia along trade routes to the Black Sea and then by ship across the Mediterranean, reaching Italy in 1347. The most common form, bubonic plague, was usually spread by fleas. When an infected flea bit a person, the bacteria entered their blood. Victims developed a high fever, and painful swellings called buboes – some as large as an apple – appeared in their armpits, neck and groin. Dark patches sometimes appeared on the skin as blood leaked underneath it. Most people who caught it died within a week. A second form, pneumonic plague, infected the lungs and could spread through coughing; it was almost always fatal.","Nobody in the fourteenth century knew about bacteria. Many believed the disease was a punishment from God for people’s sins. Others blamed ‘miasma’, a poisonous bad air rising from rotting rubbish and swamps, and carried sweet-smelling flowers and herbs to protect themselves. Some people joined groups called flagellants, who marched from town to town whipping themselves in the hope that God would forgive them. Doctors tried bleeding patients, applying ointments and even strapping live chickens to the buboes. Nothing worked.","The scale of death was overwhelming. In some villages, so many people died that there were not enough living to bury them, and bodies were placed in huge pits. Whole villages were abandoned and slowly disappeared into the fields; the remains of some of these ‘lost villages’ can still be seen today as bumps in the grass. A monk in Ireland, writing in 1349, left blank pages at the end of his chronicle ‘in case anyone should still be alive in the future’.","Yet the Black Death also changed society. With so many workers dead, there were not enough people to farm the land, and the peasants who survived could demand higher wages and better conditions. The government tried to stop this with a law in 1351 that fixed wages at their old levels, but many lords ignored it in order to keep their workers. Anger at the law and at heavy taxes helped lead to the Peasants’ Revolt of 1381. Over the following century, the old feudal system, in which peasants were tied to their lord’s land, gradually broke down.","Plague returned to England many times over the next three hundred years, including the Great Plague of London in 1665. Today it can be treated with antibiotics, but the Black Death remains one of the deadliest events in human history."],"glossary":{"mortality":"Death, especially on a large scale.","bacteria":"Tiny living things, some of which cause disease.","fatal":"Causing death.","miasma":"In the past, a poisonous ‘bad air’ believed to cause disease.","flagellants":"People who whipped themselves as a religious punishment.","chronicle":"A written record of events in time order.","peasants":"Poor farm workers in the past.","feudal":"Describing a medieval system in which people served a lord in return for land.","antibiotics":"Medicines that kill bacteria."},"questions":[{"q":"Where did the Black Death arrive in England?","o":["London","Melcombe in Dorset","York","Dover"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“A ship docked at the small port of Melcombe, in Dorset.”"},{"q":"How was bubonic plague usually spread?","o":["By drinking water","By flea bites","By touching gold","By cold weather"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Bubonic plague “was usually spread by fleas”."},{"q":"Why did people carry sweet-smelling flowers?","o":["To celebrate","They believed bad air (miasma) caused the disease","To feed the rats","To sell them"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"They blamed “miasma” and carried flowers to protect themselves."},{"q":"What does the Irish monk’s action of leaving blank pages suggest?","o":["He was lazy","He feared that everyone might die","He ran out of ink","He wanted someone to draw"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He left pages “in case anyone should still be alive in the future”."},{"q":"How did the Black Death help surviving peasants?","o":["They inherited castles","With fewer workers, they could demand higher wages and better conditions","They were made lords","They paid no taxes"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The peasants who survived could demand higher wages and better conditions.”"},{"q":"How is the article organised?","o":["Arrival, the disease, people’s beliefs, the death toll, the effects on society, and later outbreaks","In alphabetical order","As a diary","As a list of doctors"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It moves from how the plague arrived to its causes, effects and legacy."}]},{"id":"y089","title":"The Dragon’s Hoard","kind":"Fantasy","theme":"mountain","text":["Everyone in the village of Low Fell knew that a dragon lived in the mountain, and everyone knew that it slept on a hoard of gold. What nobody knew was what the dragon looked like, because nobody who had gone up the mountain to steal the gold had ever come back to say.","Wyn was not going up the mountain to steal anything. She was going because her little brother, Tam, had gone up three days ago to prove he was brave, and he had not come home.","She climbed through a day and a night. The path grew steep and stony, then disappeared altogether. Near the top, she found a cave mouth as wide as a barn door, breathing out warm air that smelled of smoke and something like cinnamon. She took a deep breath and went in.","The cave opened into a hall so vast that her candle could not find the roof. And there, filling half of it, coiled around a mountain of glittering gold, lay the dragon. It was the deep green of old pine forests, with scales as big as dinner plates. Its eyes were closed. Its breath rumbled like distant thunder.","And curled up against its side, fast asleep and perfectly unharmed, was Tam.","Wyn did not scream. She crept closer, step by step, until she could reach her brother. Then the dragon opened one enormous golden eye.","“He came for my treasure,” said the dragon. Its voice was deep and slow, like stones grinding together, but not unkind. “They all come for my treasure. Most of them I send away with a fright. This one fell and hurt his ankle on the way up, so I brought him in to keep warm. He has been telling me stories about the village. I have enjoyed them very much.”","Wyn swallowed. “Why do you keep the gold?” she asked. “You can’t spend it.”","The dragon was silent for a long time. Then it shifted, and the gold slid and chimed beneath it. “It is cold on the mountain,” it said at last. “Gold holds the warmth of my fire through the long winters. Without it, I would freeze. People see treasure. I see a blanket.”","Wyn looked at the great heap of coins, cups and crowns, and for the first time she saw it differently: not as riches, but as the only comfort of a very old, very lonely creature.","In the morning, she carried Tam down the mountain on her back. She told the village what she had seen. Some people did not believe her. But the next winter, the children of Low Fell began carrying bundles of firewood up the mountain path and leaving them at the mouth of the cave. And on cold evenings, if you look up at the mountain, you can sometimes see a warm orange glow, and hear, very faintly, the sound of a dragon laughing at a story."],"glossary":{"hoard":"A store of valuable things hidden away.","coiled":"Wound round in loops.","unharmed":"Not hurt.","chimed":"Made a ringing sound like a bell.","bundles":"Several things tied or wrapped together."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did Wyn climb the mountain?","o":["To steal the gold","To find her little brother, Tam","To kill the dragon","To see the view"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Tam “had gone up three days ago … and he had not come home.”"},{"q":"How does the writer make the dragon seem enormous?","o":["Scales as big as dinner plates and a hall too vast for her candle","It is described as tiny","It is compared to a cat","It hides in a box"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Dinner-plate scales and a vast hall emphasise its size."},{"q":"Why did the dragon keep Tam in the cave?","o":["To eat him","He had hurt his ankle and the dragon kept him warm","To make him a servant","He was a prisoner"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“This one fell and hurt his ankle … so I brought him in to keep warm.”"},{"q":"Why does the dragon keep the gold?","o":["To buy things","Gold holds the warmth of its fire through the winter","To show off","To pay the villagers"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Gold holds the warmth of my fire … I see a blanket.”"},{"q":"How does Wyn’s view of the gold change?","o":["She sees it as a comfort for a lonely creature, not as riches","She decides to steal it","She thinks it is fake","She wants to sell it"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"She sees it “not as riches, but as the only comfort of a very old, very lonely creature.”"},{"q":"What is the main message of the story?","o":["Dragons are dangerous","Things – and creatures – may be very different from what people assume; understanding brings kindness","Gold is valuable","Never climb mountains"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"The feared dragon turns out to be gentle, and the villagers respond with kindness."}]},{"id":"y090","title":"How Bridges Stay Up","kind":"Science","theme":"bridge","text":["Every bridge faces the same problem: how do you hold up a heavy road or railway over a gap, when gravity is constantly trying to pull it down? Engineers have invented several clever solutions, and they all depend on two kinds of force: compression and tension.","Compression is a squeezing force. When you stand on a sponge, it is compressed. Tension is a stretching force. When you pull both ends of a rubber band, it is under tension. Some materials are good at resisting one force but not the other. Stone and concrete are excellent at resisting compression – you can pile great weights on them – but they crack easily if they are stretched. Steel is strong in both compression and tension, which is why it is used in so many modern bridges.","The simplest kind of bridge is a beam bridge: a flat beam resting on supports at each end, like a plank across a stream. When a load presses down on the middle, the top of the beam is squashed (compression) and the bottom is stretched (tension). If the beam is too long, it will sag or snap, so beam bridges can only cross short distances unless they have extra supports, or piers, along their length.","An arch bridge solves the problem differently. Its curved shape carries the load outwards and down into the ground at each end, so every part of the arch is under compression. That is why the Romans could build arch bridges of stone, without any cement, that are still standing two thousand years later. The ends of the arch must be held firmly in place, though, or they will spread apart.","A suspension bridge hangs the road from enormous steel cables. The cables pass over tall towers and are fixed firmly into the ground, or anchorages, at each end. The weight of the road pulls the cables into tension, and the towers carry the load down into the ground in compression. Because steel cables are so strong in tension, suspension bridges can cross huge distances. The main span of the Akashi Kaikyō Bridge in Japan is nearly two kilometres long.","A cantilever bridge is built from beams that stick out from supports, like diving boards, with a central section joining them. Scotland’s Forth Bridge, a famous red railway bridge opened in 1890, is a giant cantilever bridge.","Engineers must also think about forces that are harder to see. Wind can make a bridge twist and sway. In 1940, the Tacoma Narrows Bridge in the USA began to twist so violently in a strong wind that it collapsed into the river just four months after opening. Today, engineers test models of bridges in wind tunnels and design them with shapes that let the wind flow smoothly past.","Next time you cross a bridge, look underneath. You will be able to see how it pushes and pulls its way across the gap."],"glossary":{"gravity":"The force that pulls objects towards the Earth.","compression":"A squeezing or pushing force.","tension":"A stretching or pulling force.","resisting":"Standing up to; not giving way.","piers":"Supports that hold up a bridge.","cement":"A powder mixed with water that sets hard, used in building.","anchorages":"Places where something is firmly fixed.","span":"The distance between the supports of a bridge.","cantilever":"A beam fixed at only one end."},"questions":[{"q":"What is the difference between compression and tension?","o":["Compression squeezes; tension stretches","Compression stretches; tension squeezes","They are the same","Both are types of gravity"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Compression is a squeezing force … Tension is a stretching force.”"},{"q":"Why is steel used in so many modern bridges?","o":["It is cheap","It is strong in both compression and tension","It is light","It does not rust"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Steel is strong in both compression and tension.”"},{"q":"Why can Roman stone arch bridges still stand today without cement?","o":["Stone is strong under compression and the arch puts every part under compression","The Romans used magic","They were rebuilt last year","They are not used"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The arch carries the load so “every part of the arch is under compression,” which stone resists well."},{"q":"Why does the writer compare cantilevers to diving boards?","o":["To help readers picture beams sticking out from a support at one end","Because the Forth Bridge has a swimming pool","To show they bounce","Because they are blue"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"A diving board is a familiar example of a beam fixed at one end."},{"q":"What lesson did engineers learn from the Tacoma Narrows Bridge?","o":["Bridges should be painted","Wind forces must be tested and designed for","Bridges should be shorter","Rivers are dangerous"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Today engineers test models in wind tunnels so wind flows smoothly past."},{"q":"How is most of the article organised?","o":["It explains one type of bridge per paragraph, showing how each handles forces","As a story about a bridge-builder","In alphabetical order","As a timeline of famous collapses"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"Beam, arch, suspension and cantilever bridges each get a paragraph."}]},{"id":"y091","title":"The 7:42","kind":"Poem","theme":"city","text":["The 7:42 is always late,\nso we wait,\na row of coats along the platform edge,\neach of us balanced on our own small ledge\nof morning.","The man in the grey suit checks his phone,\nalone\nin a crowd of forty. A girl with a cello\nsways to music, soft and mellow,\nonly she can hear.","An old woman feeds the pigeons crumbs\nand hums.\nA baby in a buggy stares at me,\nso seriously,\nas if I am the strangest thing she has ever seen.","We never speak. We never meet.\nOur feet\ntap, shuffle, wait. We know each face\nlike furniture in a familiar place,\nand never learn a name.","Then, one wet Tuesday, the train is cancelled.\nNo one is.\nThe man in grey says, “Typical!”\nThe old woman laughs. The cello girl\nshares her umbrella with me.","And for twenty minutes, standing in the rain,\nwe are not strangers.\nThen, of course, it comes – the train,\nand we fold ourselves back into our silences,\nbut something has changed.","Now, when the 7:42 is late,\nthe man in grey nods. The old woman waves.\nThe baby grins. And I find\nthat I don’t mind\nthe wait."],"glossary":{"platform":"The raised area beside a railway track where passengers wait.","ledge":"A narrow flat shelf; here, a small space of their own.","mellow":"Soft, warm and pleasant.","buggy":"A light folding pushchair for a baby.","familiar":"Well known from long experience.","cancelled":"Called off; not going to happen."},"questions":[{"q":"Where is the poem set?","o":["On a bus","On a train platform","In a school","At the seaside"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“A row of coats along the platform edge.”"},{"q":"What do the descriptions in stanzas two and three show about the people waiting?","o":["They all know each other well","Each person is in their own separate world","They are all asleep","They are all angry"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"The man is “alone in a crowd of forty”; the girl hears music “only she can hear.”"},{"q":"“We know each face / like furniture in a familiar place.” What does this simile suggest?","o":["The people are made of wood","They recognise each other but think of each other as part of the background, not as people","They are sitting on chairs","They are very close friends"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Furniture is familiar but we do not really notice or talk to it."},{"q":"What is the turning point of the poem?","o":["The train is early","The train is cancelled and the strangers begin to talk and help each other","The narrator misses the train","It snows"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"In stanza five the cancellation leads to talking, laughing and sharing an umbrella."},{"q":"What is clever about the line “No one is.” after “the train is cancelled”?","o":["It is a spelling mistake","It plays on the word ‘cancelled’: the train is cancelled, but the people are not – they come alive","It shows everyone went home","It means nobody arrived"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Wordplay contrasts the cancelled train with the people suddenly connecting."},{"q":"How has the narrator’s feeling about waiting changed by the end?","o":["They hate it more","They no longer mind it because they now feel connected to the others","They have bought a car","They wait at a different station"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"“And I find / that I don’t mind / the wait.”"}]},{"id":"y092","title":"Florence Nightingale: The Lady with the Lamp","kind":"Biography","theme":"victorian","text":["Florence Nightingale was born in 1820 in Florence, Italy – the city she was named after – to a wealthy English family. Girls of her class were expected to marry well, run a household and spend their time on music, painting and visiting. Florence wanted something different. At sixteen, she felt she had received a calling from God to do important work. Later she decided that work was nursing.","Her family was horrified. Nursing at the time was not seen as a respectable job. Hospitals were dirty and dangerous, and nurses were usually untrained and poorly paid. Florence’s parents refused to let her train for years. She studied in secret, reading reports on hospitals and public health, and eventually, at thirty-one, she went to Germany to train at a hospital run by Lutheran deaconesses.","In 1854, Britain went to war against Russia in the Crimea. Newspaper reports described the terrible conditions in the British army hospital at Scutari, in what is now Istanbul: wounded and sick soldiers lay on the floors in filthy corridors, without blankets or proper food. The Secretary at War, Sidney Herbert, who was a friend of Florence’s, asked her to take a team of nurses to help. She arrived in November with thirty-eight nurses.","What she found was shocking. The hospital was built over a sewer. There were rats and lice everywhere, and there was not enough soap, towels or bandages. More soldiers were dying from diseases such as cholera, typhus and dysentery than from their battle wounds. Florence and her nurses scrubbed the wards, organised a laundry, set up kitchens and made sure patients had clean bedding and decent food. She bought supplies with money raised in Britain. At night she walked the long corridors with a lamp, checking on the sick, and the soldiers began to call her ‘the Lady with the Lamp’.","The death rate at the hospital fell dramatically, especially after a government sanitary commission arrived in 1855 to flush out the sewers and improve ventilation. Florence came to understand that clean conditions saved lives.","Florence Nightingale was also a brilliant mathematician. After the war, she collected statistics showing how many soldiers had died from preventable diseases and presented them in a new kind of diagram, a ‘rose diagram’, which made the information easy for politicians to understand. Her work led to major reforms in army hospitals and public health.","In 1860, she founded the Nightingale Training School for nurses at St Thomas’ Hospital in London, which helped turn nursing into a respected profession. She wrote a book, Notes on Nursing, that was read around the world. In 1907 she became the first woman to receive the Order of Merit. She died in 1910, aged ninety. Her birthday, 12 May, is celebrated each year as International Nurses Day."],"glossary":{"respectable":"Considered proper and acceptable by society.","deaconesses":"Women who served in some churches by caring for the sick and poor.","sewer":"An underground pipe that carries away waste water.","wards":"Rooms in a hospital where patients sleep.","dramatically":"By a very large amount.","sanitary":"To do with cleanliness and health.","ventilation":"The flow of fresh air through a building.","statistics":"Facts and figures collected and studied.","reforms":"Changes made to improve something.","profession":"A job that needs special training."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did Florence’s family object to her becoming a nurse?","o":["Nursing was not seen as respectable and hospitals were dirty and dangerous","They wanted her to be a doctor","They had no money","They lived too far from a hospital"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Nursing at the time was not seen as a respectable job.”"},{"q":"What does the fact that Florence studied in secret show about her?","o":["She was determined to follow her calling","She was lazy","She liked secrets","She disliked her family"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"She kept learning despite her parents’ refusal."},{"q":"What was the biggest cause of death in the Scutari hospital?","o":["Battle wounds","Diseases such as cholera, typhus and dysentery","Cold weather","Hunger only"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“More soldiers were dying from diseases … than from their battle wounds.”"},{"q":"Why was she called ‘the Lady with the Lamp’?","o":["She sold lamps","She walked the corridors at night with a lamp checking on patients","She invented a lamp","She worked in a lighthouse"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“At night she walked the long corridors with a lamp.”"},{"q":"Why was the ‘rose diagram’ important?","o":["It decorated hospitals","It made statistics about preventable deaths easy for politicians to understand","It showed flower sales","It was a map of the Crimea"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"It presented the figures clearly, leading to reforms."},{"q":"How did Florence Nightingale change nursing for the future?","o":["She banned nurses","She founded a training school and helped make nursing a respected profession","She became a soldier","She closed hospitals"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"Her training school “helped turn nursing into a respected profession.”"}]},{"id":"y093","title":"The Message in the Bottle","kind":"Story","theme":"sea","text":["The bottle was green and thick-glassed, with a cork pushed hard into its neck, and it had been washed up among the seaweed at the far end of Saunton Sands. Isla almost walked past it. It was only because her dog, Pepper, refused to leave it alone that she bent down and saw the curl of yellowed paper inside.","It took her twenty minutes and a pair of tweezers at home to get the paper out without tearing it. The handwriting was neat and slanting, in faded blue ink. The date at the top made her catch her breath: 12 August 1979.","To whoever finds this, it began.","My name is Rosie Kerr and I am eleven years old. I am on a ferry from Plymouth to Spain with my mum and dad and my little brother, Alistair, who is being sick. I am throwing this bottle into the sea because I want to know how far it will go. If you find it, please write to me at the address below and tell me where you found it and who you are. I will write back, I promise. Today the sea is very blue and I saw a dolphin. I hope you are having a good day too.","Underneath was an address in Glasgow.","Isla read it four times. Forty-seven years. The bottle had been floating, or buried, or bobbing about the ocean for forty-seven years, and it had ended up on her beach, in Devon, at her dog’s feet. Rosie Kerr would not be eleven any more. She would be fifty-eight.","“She’ll have moved house,” said Dad. “Probably several times.”","But Isla was not ready to give up. With Mum’s help, she posted a photo of the letter online and asked if anyone knew Rosie Kerr, who had lived in Glasgow in 1979. Within two days, it had been shared eleven thousand times. People sent messages from Scotland, Canada and Australia. A local newspaper asked to interview her.","On the fourth day, a message arrived from a woman named Fiona. Her mother, she wrote, had been called Rosie Kerr before she married. She had grown up in Glasgow. She still talked about a family holiday to Spain when her brother had been seasick the entire way.","A week later, Isla’s phone rang. The voice on the other end was warm and Scottish and slightly shaky.","“Is that Isla?” said the voice. “This is Rosie. I promised I would write back. I’m sorry I took so long.”","They talked for an hour. Rosie had become a marine biologist, she said, partly because of that blue day on the ferry and that dolphin. She still had a photo of Alistair, green-faced, clinging to the rail. Isla told her about Pepper and about the beach and about school. Before they rang off, Rosie said, “Do you know what the best thing about a message in a bottle is? You never know who will answer. And it turns out the answer was you.”"],"glossary":{"tweezers":"A small tool for picking up tiny objects.","slanting":"Leaning to one side.","faded":"Lost brightness or colour over time.","ferry":"A boat that carries people and vehicles across water.","bobbing":"Moving up and down on water.","marine":"To do with the sea.","biologist":"A scientist who studies living things."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did Isla notice the bottle?","o":["It was shining","Her dog refused to leave it alone","Someone showed it to her","It hit her foot"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“It was only because her dog, Pepper, refused to leave it alone.”"},{"q":"How long had the bottle been in the sea?","o":["Eleven years","Forty-seven years","Fifty-eight years","Four days"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Forty-seven years.”"},{"q":"What does Rosie’s letter suggest about her as a child?","o":["She was bored and grumpy","She was curious, friendly and hopeful","She was frightened of the sea","She disliked her family"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"She wants to know how far it will go, promises to write back and hopes the finder has a good day."},{"q":"What does Dad’s comment, “She’ll have moved house”, show?","o":["He thinks finding Rosie will be very difficult","He knows where Rosie lives","He wants to move house","He is excited"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"He doubts the old address will still work."},{"q":"How did Isla finally find Rosie?","o":["She wrote to the old address","She posted the letter online and Rosie’s daughter replied","She went to Glasgow","The newspaper found her"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"The post was shared widely and Fiona recognised her mother’s story."},{"q":"How did the day on the ferry influence Rosie’s life?","o":["She never went on a boat again","It helped inspire her to become a marine biologist","She became a ferry captain","She moved to Spain"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"She became a marine biologist “partly because of that blue day on the ferry and that dolphin.”"}]},{"id":"y094","title":"From Power Station to Plug Socket","kind":"Science","theme":"lab","text":["Flick a switch, and a light comes on. Plug in a charger, and your phone fills with energy. Electricity is so much a part of everyday life that we rarely think about where it comes from. But every time you switch something on, you are at the end of an extraordinary journey that may have begun hundreds of kilometres away.","Electricity is a flow of tiny charged particles called electrons. To make it, power stations use generators. Inside a generator, a coil of wire spins between powerful magnets. This movement pushes electrons through the wire, creating an electric current. The British scientist Michael Faraday discovered this principle, called electromagnetic induction, in 1831, and almost all of the world’s electricity is still made this way.","The question is how to make the generator spin. In a traditional power station, fuel such as coal or gas is burned to boil water. The steam blasts through a turbine – a giant fan with many blades – which turns the generator. Nuclear power stations also heat water to make steam, but they use the heat released when atoms of uranium are split. Wind turbines skip the steam altogether; the wind turns their blades directly. Hydroelectric power stations use the force of falling water. Solar panels work differently again, using special materials that turn sunlight straight into electricity without any moving parts.","Once made, the electricity must be transported. The electricity leaving a power station is ‘stepped up’ by transformers to a very high voltage – up to 400,000 volts – before it travels along the national grid, the network of cables carried by tall pylons across the countryside. Why? When electricity flows through a cable, some energy is lost as heat. Sending it at high voltage means less current is needed, so much less energy is wasted on the journey.","Of course, 400,000 volts would be deadly in your home. So near towns and cities, at substations, more transformers ‘step down’ the voltage. Smaller cables, often hidden underground, carry it into streets, and by the time it reaches your plug sockets, it is 230 volts. Even this can kill, which is why plugs contain fuses and why you should never poke anything into a socket.","The grid has to be carefully balanced. Electricity is difficult to store, so the amount generated must closely match the amount being used at every moment. Engineers at a control centre watch demand constantly. They know, for instance, that when a popular television programme finishes, millions of people get up and switch on their kettles at the same time, causing a sudden surge. Power stations must be ready to respond within seconds.","In recent years, Britain’s electricity has changed dramatically. In 2012, around forty per cent came from coal. In 2024, the last coal power station in Britain closed. Today, wind is often the biggest single source, and huge batteries are being built to store energy for when the wind drops. The journey from power station to plug is the same – but the power stations are changing fast."],"glossary":{"electrons":"Tiny particles with a negative electric charge.","generators":"Machines that turn movement into electricity.","principle":"A basic rule or law of science.","induction":"Producing electricity by moving a wire near a magnet.","turbine":"A machine with blades that are turned by steam, water or wind.","transformers":"Devices that change the voltage of electricity.","voltage":"The force that pushes electricity through a circuit.","pylons":"Tall metal towers that carry electricity cables.","surge":"A sudden large increase."},"questions":[{"q":"What happens inside a generator?","o":["Water is frozen","A coil of wire spins between magnets, pushing electrons through the wire","Coal is mined","Sunlight is stored"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“A coil of wire spins between powerful magnets.”"},{"q":"Who discovered electromagnetic induction?","o":["Isaac Newton","Michael Faraday","Thomas Edison","Marie Curie"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Michael Faraday discovered this principle … in 1831.”"},{"q":"How are solar panels different from the other methods described?","o":["They need lots of water","They turn sunlight directly into electricity with no moving parts","They burn coal","They use uranium"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They turn “sunlight straight into electricity without any moving parts.”"},{"q":"Why is electricity sent along the grid at very high voltage?","o":["To make it travel faster","So less energy is wasted as heat on the journey","To make it safer","Because pylons need it"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Sending it at high voltage means less current … so much less energy is wasted.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer mention people switching on kettles after a TV programme?","o":["To give a real example of a sudden change in demand the grid must handle","To advertise tea","To show TV wastes energy","To explain how kettles work"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"It illustrates why the grid must respond “within seconds” to surges."},{"q":"What is the main point of the final paragraph?","o":["Britain’s sources of electricity are changing rapidly towards renewables","Coal is still the main source","Electricity is no longer needed","Batteries are dangerous"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"Coal has ended, wind is often the biggest source and batteries are being built."}]},{"id":"y095","title":"Henry VIII and His Six Wives","kind":"History","theme":"castle","text":["When Henry VIII became king in 1509, at the age of seventeen, he seemed like the perfect prince. He was tall, handsome and athletic, a skilled horseman and jouster who also wrote music, spoke several languages and loved learning. By the time he died in 1547, he was so overweight that he had to be carried around his palaces, his legs were covered in painful ulcers, and he had married six times. Two of his wives had been executed.","To understand why, you need to understand Henry’s greatest worry: he needed a son. The Tudor family had only recently won the throne after years of civil war, known as the Wars of the Roses. Henry believed that a woman could not rule strongly enough to stop the country falling back into chaos. Without a male heir, the Tudor dynasty might collapse.","His first wife, Catherine of Aragon, a Spanish princess, had been married to his older brother, Arthur, who died young. She and Henry were married for twenty-four years, but of their children only one daughter, Mary, survived. Henry became convinced that God was punishing him for marrying his brother’s widow, and he fell in love with a lady at court, Anne Boleyn. He asked the Pope to annul his marriage. The Pope refused.","Henry’s answer changed English history. He broke away from the Catholic Church in Rome and made himself Supreme Head of the Church of England. His new archbishop declared his first marriage invalid, and in 1533 he married Anne. This break with Rome, called the English Reformation, led Henry to close down England’s monasteries and take their land and wealth.","Anne gave birth to a daughter, Elizabeth, but no son. In 1536 she was accused of treason and other crimes – charges many historians believe were invented – and beheaded at the Tower of London. Eleven days later, Henry married Jane Seymour, who finally gave him a son, Edward, in 1537. Jane died less than two weeks after the birth. Henry said she was the wife he had loved most, and he asked to be buried beside her.","His fourth marriage, to a German princess, Anne of Cleves, was arranged for political reasons. Henry had approved her after seeing a flattering portrait, but when they met he was disappointed, and the marriage was annulled after six months. Anne was treated generously and lived comfortably in England for the rest of her life. His fifth wife, Catherine Howard, was a teenager more than thirty years younger than Henry. She was executed in 1542. His sixth wife, Catherine Parr, was an intelligent, well-educated widow who cared for Henry in his final years and outlived him.","A rhyme helps people remember the wives’ fates: ‘Divorced, beheaded, died; divorced, beheaded, survived.’ Ironically, after all Henry’s efforts to have a son, Edward VI died at fifteen. It was his daughter Elizabeth I who became one of England’s greatest and longest-reigning monarchs."],"glossary":{"athletic":"Strong, fit and good at sport.","jouster":"A knight who fought on horseback with lances in a contest.","ulcers":"Painful open sores.","heir":"A person who will inherit a title or property.","dynasty":"A series of rulers from the same family.","annul":"To officially declare that a marriage was never valid.","invalid":"Not legally acceptable.","treason":"The crime of betraying your country or ruler.","flattering":"Making someone look better than they really are.","monarchs":"Kings or queens."},"questions":[{"q":"How does the first paragraph contrast the young and old Henry?","o":["Young Henry was athletic and handsome; old Henry was overweight and ill","Young Henry was poor; old Henry was rich","There is no contrast","Old Henry was more athletic"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It describes a fit, talented young king and an old king who had to be carried."},{"q":"Why was having a son so important to Henry?","o":["He wanted someone to play with","He believed a male heir was needed to keep the Tudor dynasty strong and avoid chaos","His mother insisted","It was the law that only sons could be born"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Without a male heir, the Tudor dynasty might collapse.”"},{"q":"Why did Henry break away from the Catholic Church?","o":["The Pope refused to annul his first marriage","He disliked Rome","He wanted to become Pope","He lost a war"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The Pope refused, so Henry made himself head of the Church of England to end the marriage."},{"q":"Why did Henry’s marriage to Anne of Cleves end so quickly?","o":["She died","He was disappointed when they met after seeing a flattering portrait","She was executed","She went back to Germany"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He approved her from a flattering portrait but was disappointed in person."},{"q":"Which rhyme helps people remember the wives’ fates?","o":["‘Divorced, beheaded, died; divorced, beheaded, survived.’","‘One, two, buckle my shoe’","‘Ring a ring o’ roses’","‘Remember, remember the fifth of November’"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The rhyme is quoted in the last paragraph."},{"q":"Why is the ending described as ironic?","o":["Henry’s longed-for son died young, while the daughter he overlooked became a great queen","Henry lived for ever","Henry had no children","Elizabeth never became queen"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"After all his efforts for a son, it was Elizabeth who became one of England’s greatest monarchs."}]},{"id":"y096","title":"Plant a Tree, Change the World","kind":"Persuasive","theme":"forest","text":["There is an old saying: the best time to plant a tree was twenty years ago. The second-best time is now. If you want to do one simple thing that will help the planet, your community and your own health, plant a tree. Better still, persuade your school to plant a hundred.","Trees are the cheapest and most effective climate technology we have. As they grow, they take carbon dioxide from the air and lock the carbon away in their wood, roots and the soil beneath them. A single mature oak can store several tonnes of carbon over its lifetime. No machine invented by humans can do this so cheaply, so quietly or so beautifully.","Trees also make our towns and cities better places to live. On a hot summer’s day, a street lined with trees can be several degrees cooler than one without, because leaves provide shade and release water vapour. Trees trap tiny particles of pollution from car exhausts on their leaves, making the air cleaner to breathe. Their roots soak up rainwater, reducing the risk of flash floods when drains overflow.","They are good for wildlife, too. A single oak tree can support more than 2,000 species, from tiny insects and lichens to birds, bats and squirrels. Hedgerows and woods act as corridors, allowing animals to move safely across the countryside. When we plant trees, we are building homes.","And trees are good for us. Studies have found that people who live near green spaces tend to feel less stressed, and that hospital patients who can see trees from their windows may recover faster than those who look out at a brick wall. Even a short walk among trees can lower your heart rate and improve your mood. Japanese doctors have a name for it: shinrin-yoku, or ‘forest bathing’.","Of course, planting trees is not a magic solution. They take years to grow, and they cannot soak up carbon as fast as we are producing it. We still need to cut pollution and protect the ancient woodlands we already have, which are far richer than anything we can plant. Trees also need to be the right kind, in the right place, and looked after properly; a sapling that dies in its first dry summer helps nobody.","But that is no reason to do nothing. Ask your school whether there is space for a small woodland, an orchard or a hedge. Find out whether your local council or a charity runs tree-planting days in winter, when young trees are planted. Plant an apple pip or an acorn in a pot and watch it grow. Every tree counts.","The people who planted the great oaks and beeches we enjoy today never saw them fully grown. They planted them for us. Now it is our turn to plant for the future."],"glossary":{"mature":"Fully grown.","vapour":"Tiny drops of water floating in the air.","particles":"Very tiny pieces.","lichens":"Simple plant-like organisms that grow on rocks and trees.","corridors":"Here, strips of habitat that let animals travel safely.","sapling":"A young tree.","orchard":"A piece of land where fruit trees are grown."},"questions":[{"q":"What does the opening saying mean?","o":["It is too late to plant trees","Planting a tree now is still very worthwhile, even if starting earlier would have been better","Trees take twenty minutes to grow","Only old people should plant trees"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"The ‘second-best time is now’ encourages action today."},{"q":"How do trees help cool towns on hot days?","o":["Their leaves give shade and release water vapour","They block the sun completely","They make it rain","They produce ice"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Leaves provide shade and release water vapour.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer say “When we plant trees, we are building homes”?","o":["Trees are used to build houses","Trees provide habitats for many species of wildlife","Birds build nests in houses","To advertise a builder"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"An oak supports over 2,000 species, so trees are homes for wildlife."},{"q":"What is ‘forest bathing’?","o":["Swimming in a forest lake","Spending time among trees to improve wellbeing","Washing trees","A type of shower"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It refers to how time among trees lowers heart rate and improves mood."},{"q":"Why does the writer include the paragraph beginning “Of course, planting trees is not a magic solution”?","o":["To admit the limits of tree planting, which makes the argument more honest and believable","To argue against planting trees","To change the subject","To confuse the reader"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Acknowledging weaknesses makes a persuasive argument more credible."},{"q":"How does the final paragraph persuade the reader?","o":["It reminds us that past generations planted for us, so we should plant for the future","It lists tree prices","It describes a storm","It tells a joke"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It appeals to responsibility to future generations."}]},{"id":"y097","title":"The Sahara: Sea of Sand","kind":"Geography","theme":"desert","text":["The Sahara is the largest hot desert in the world. It stretches about 4,800 kilometres across North Africa, from the Atlantic Ocean in the west to the Red Sea in the east, and covers around nine million square kilometres – an area almost as large as the United States. It spreads across parts of eleven countries, including Morocco, Algeria, Libya, Egypt, Mali, Niger and Chad.","A desert is defined not by heat or sand but by a lack of rain: usually less than 250 millimetres a year. Parts of the Sahara receive almost none at all, and some places may go years without a drop. The Sahara is dry because it lies beneath a belt of high air pressure, where sinking air prevents clouds from forming.","Many people picture the Sahara as an endless sea of golden sand dunes. In fact, only about a quarter of it is sand. Much of the rest is rocky plateaus, gravel plains and mountains. The highest peak, Emi Koussi in Chad, is an extinct volcano more than 3,400 metres high, and snow occasionally falls on the Saharan mountains. Where there is sand, though, it can be spectacular: some dunes rise over 150 metres, and they slowly move as the wind blows sand from one side to the other.","Temperatures are extreme. In summer, the air can reach over forty-five degrees Celsius, and sand can become hot enough to fry an egg. Yet because the air is so dry and the skies so clear, heat escapes quickly at night, and temperatures can drop close to freezing. Strong winds sometimes whip up sandstorms that turn the sky orange and can carry dust across the Atlantic to the Amazon rainforest, where it helps fertilise the soil.","Despite the conditions, the Sahara is full of life adapted to survive. Camels can go more than a week without drinking, and their humps store fat, not water. Fennec foxes, the smallest foxes in the world, have huge ears that release body heat and help them hear prey underground. Many animals are nocturnal, hiding in burrows during the day and coming out in the cool of the night. Plants such as acacia trees send roots deep into the ground to find water.","People have lived in the Sahara for thousands of years. The Tuareg, a nomadic people sometimes called the ‘blue people’ because of their indigo-dyed clothes, traditionally travelled with camel caravans, trading salt, gold and other goods across the desert. Towns grew up around oases, places where underground water reaches the surface and date palms can grow.","Surprisingly, the Sahara has not always been a desert. Rock paintings, thousands of years old, show giraffes, elephants and cattle, and lakes full of fish. Between about 11,000 and 5,000 years ago, a shift in the Earth’s orbit brought monsoon rains, and the Sahara was green, with grasslands, rivers and lakes. Scientists call this the African Humid Period. Slowly, the rains moved south again, and the green Sahara turned to sand."],"glossary":{"defined":"Described exactly what something is.","plateaus":"Large areas of high, flat land.","spectacular":"Very impressive to look at.","fertilise":"To make soil richer so plants grow better.","adapted":"Changed over time to suit the environment.","nocturnal":"Active at night.","nomadic":"Moving from place to place rather than living in one spot.","indigo-dyed":"Coloured with indigo, a deep blue dye.","oases":"Places in a desert where there is water and plants grow.","humid":"Damp; containing a lot of moisture."},"questions":[{"q":"What makes a place a desert?","o":["Lots of sand","Very high temperatures","Very little rain – usually under 250 millimetres a year","No animals"],"a":2,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“A desert is defined not by heat or sand but by a lack of rain.”"},{"q":"Which common belief about the Sahara does the writer correct?","o":["That it is in Africa","That it is mostly sand dunes – only about a quarter is sand","That it is hot","That camels live there"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Only about a quarter of it is sand.”"},{"q":"Why can temperatures drop so low at night?","o":["Because of snow","The dry air and clear skies let heat escape quickly","Because of sandstorms","The Sun is closer"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Because the air is so dry and the skies so clear, heat escapes quickly at night.”"},{"q":"How do fennec foxes’ ears help them survive?","o":["They keep the sand out","They release body heat and help hear prey underground","They store water","They scare predators"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Their ears “release body heat and help them hear prey underground.”"},{"q":"What evidence shows the Sahara was once green?","o":["Ancient rock paintings of giraffes, elephants and lakes","Modern farms","Satellite photos of forests","Camels’ humps"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Rock paintings show animals and lakes that need a wetter climate."},{"q":"What surprising link between the Sahara and the Amazon does the writer describe?","o":["They are next to each other","Saharan dust blown across the Atlantic fertilises the rainforest soil","They have the same animals","Both are deserts"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Dust can be carried “across the Atlantic to the Amazon … where it helps fertilise the soil.”"}]},{"id":"y098","title":"Interview: The Swimmer Who Never Gave Up","kind":"Interview","theme":"sport","text":["Sophie Achebe, 17, from Nottingham, was born without a left hand. Last summer she won two silver medals at the European Para Swimming Championships. We spoke to her about training, setbacks and why she almost quit.","Q: When did you start swimming?\nA: When I was four. My mum took me to lessons because she wanted me to be safe in the water – we were going on holiday to the seaside. I was terrible at first! I kept going round in circles because I was pulling harder with one arm. But I loved being in the water. It’s the one place where I don’t feel different. Underwater, nobody is looking at your hands.","Q: How did you get into competitive swimming?\nA: A coach at my local club noticed that I was fast and suggested I try a para-swimming talent day. That’s where I found out about classification – the system that groups swimmers with similar impairments so that races are fair. I’d honestly never thought of myself as an athlete before that day. Suddenly there were all these swimmers like me, some of them competing internationally. It was like a door opening.","Q: What does your training look like?\nA: I swim nine times a week, including five early mornings. I’m in the pool at half past five, then I go to school, then I do gym work or swim again in the evening. I also see a physiotherapist, because I have to be careful about my shoulders – I put a lot of strain on one side. It’s hard, but I’ve learned to plan everything. My homework gets done on the bus.","Q: You mentioned you almost quit. What happened?\nA: When I was fourteen, I had a really bad year. I injured my shoulder and couldn’t swim for three months. When I came back, I was slower, and younger swimmers were beating me. I remember sitting in the car park after a gala, crying, and telling my mum I was done. She didn’t argue. She just said, ‘OK. But decide next week, not tonight.’ By the next week I’d changed my mind.","Q: What helped you keep going?\nA: My coach changed my goals. Instead of focusing on winning, we focused on small things: improving my turns, my breathing, my starts. Every week I could see I was getting better at something, even if I wasn’t winning yet. And my teammates were amazing. When you’re tired at half past five in the morning, it helps that everyone else is tired too!","Q: What’s next?\nA: The big dream is the Paralympics. I’m on the development squad, so there’s a real chance. But I’m trying not to think too far ahead. One race at a time.","Q: What would you say to young people who feel like giving up?\nA: Don’t make big decisions on bad days. Everybody has setbacks – even the best athletes in the world. Find something small to improve, and celebrate it. And find people who believe in you. Sometimes they’ll believe in you before you believe in yourself."],"glossary":{"setbacks":"Problems that delay progress.","competitive":"Involving competing against others.","classification":"Putting things into groups; in para sport, grouping athletes with similar impairments.","impairments":"Conditions that affect how part of the body or mind works.","internationally":"Between or involving different countries.","physiotherapist":"A health professional who treats injuries through exercise and massage.","gala":"Here, a swimming competition.","squad":"A group of athletes chosen to train together."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did Sophie first start swimming lessons?","o":["To win medals","Her mum wanted her to be safe in the water before a seaside holiday","Her school made her","Her friends were swimmers"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“She wanted me to be safe in the water – we were going on holiday to the seaside.”"},{"q":"What does Sophie mean by “Underwater, nobody is looking at your hands”?","o":["The pool is dark","In the water she does not feel different or judged","She wears gloves","She swims with her eyes closed"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Swimming is “the one place where I don’t feel different.”"},{"q":"What is the purpose of classification in para-swimming?","o":["To choose team captains","To group swimmers with similar impairments so races are fair","To decide pool lanes","To rank coaches"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It “groups swimmers with similar impairments so that races are fair.”"},{"q":"Why was Sophie’s mum’s advice wise?","o":["She told Sophie to quit immediately","She suggested waiting to decide instead of acting on a bad night","She bought a new swimsuit","She found a new coach"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“Decide next week, not tonight” – Sophie changed her mind when calmer."},{"q":"How did her coach help her keep going?","o":["By shouting at her","By changing her goals to focus on small improvements","By letting her skip training","By entering her in easier races"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“We focused on small things: improving my turns, my breathing, my starts.”"},{"q":"Which phrase best sums up Sophie’s advice?","o":["Don’t make big decisions on bad days, celebrate small improvements and find people who believe in you","Always win","Train alone","Give up if it gets hard"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"Her final answer gives exactly this advice."}]},{"id":"y099","title":"Beowulf and the Monster Grendel","kind":"Myth","theme":"castle","author":"Retold from the Old English poem","text":["Long ago, in the land of the Danes, King Hrothgar built a great hall called Heorot. It was the finest hall in all the northern lands, with a roof covered in gold and long tables where his warriors feasted, drank mead and listened to the songs of the harp. Every night the hall rang with music and laughter.","But out on the dark moors, in a lake where fire burned on the water at night, lived a monster called Grendel. He was huge and hideous, descended from ancient evil, and the sound of joy from Heorot tormented him. One night, when the warriors lay sleeping after their feast, Grendel crept into the hall and snatched thirty men. In the morning, there was nothing but blood and silence.","For twelve long winters Grendel returned. No weapon could harm him, and no warrior could defeat him. Heorot, the most splendid hall in the world, stood empty every night, and King Hrothgar grew old with grief.","News of the Danes’ suffering travelled across the sea to the land of the Geats, in what is now Sweden. There lived a young warrior named Beowulf, who was said to have the strength of thirty men in the grip of his hand. He chose fourteen companions, sailed to Denmark and offered to fight the monster. Since Grendel used no weapons, Beowulf declared that he would fight him with his bare hands.","That night, Beowulf and his men lay down in Heorot and pretended to sleep. Out of the mist came Grendel, his eyes burning like flames. He tore open the door, seized one of the Geats and devoured him. Then he reached for Beowulf.","Beowulf caught his arm. At once, the monster knew he had never met such a grip. He tried to escape, but Beowulf held on. They wrestled through the hall, smashing benches and shaking the walls until the building groaned. At last, with a terrible tearing sound, Grendel’s arm was ripped from his shoulder. The monster fled into the night, howling, and crawled back to his lake to die. Beowulf hung the arm from the roof of Heorot as a trophy.","There was great celebration – but it did not last. The next night, Grendel’s mother, as fierce as her son, came to avenge him. Beowulf followed her to the burning lake and dived to the bottom, where he fought her in an underwater cave. When his own sword failed, he seized an ancient giant’s sword from the wall and killed her with it.","Beowulf returned home a hero and later became king of the Geats. He ruled for fifty years. In his old age he fought one last battle against a dragon that was burning his kingdom, and although he killed it, he was mortally wounded. The story of Beowulf, written in Old English more than a thousand years ago, is one of the oldest surviving poems in the English language."],"glossary":{"mead":"An alcoholic drink made from honey.","hideous":"Extremely ugly.","descended":"Coming from a particular ancestor or family line.","tormented":"Caused great suffering or annoyance.","splendid":"Very impressive and beautiful.","devoured":"Ate hungrily or greedily.","trophy":"An object kept as a reminder of a victory.","avenge":"To punish someone for a wrong done to you or someone you love.","mortally":"In a way that causes death."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did Grendel attack Heorot?","o":["He wanted the gold","The sound of joy from the hall tormented him","Hrothgar insulted him","He was hungry for mead"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The sound of joy from Heorot tormented him.”"},{"q":"How long did Grendel terrorise Heorot?","o":["Twelve winters","One night","Fifty years","Thirty days"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“For twelve long winters Grendel returned.”"},{"q":"Why did Beowulf decide to fight without weapons?","o":["He had none","Grendel used no weapons, so he wanted a fair fight","Weapons were banned in Heorot","He was showing off to the king"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“Since Grendel used no weapons, Beowulf declared that he would fight him with his bare hands.”"},{"q":"How does the writer make the fight seem violent and powerful?","o":["Smashing benches, walls shaking and the building groaning","Describing a calm conversation","Describing the weather","Listing the warriors’ names"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"These details show the huge force of the struggle."},{"q":"Why did Grendel’s mother attack the hall?","o":["To steal the gold","To avenge her son","To find food","To meet Beowulf"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"She “came to avenge him.”"},{"q":"Why is the story of Beowulf important?","o":["It is one of the oldest surviving poems in English","It was written last century","It is about the Romans","It is a true story"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"The final sentence explains it is one of the oldest English poems."}]},{"id":"y100","title":"The Time Capsule","kind":"Story","theme":"books","text":["On the last day of Year 6, Mr Patel had made them all write letters to their future selves. “You’ll open them on your first day of Year 7,” he had said, sealing the envelopes in a biscuit tin and burying it under the oak tree by the school gate. “Write down what you’re hoping for. What you’re worried about. Who you want to be.”","Ten weeks later, on a Saturday in September, Mr Patel invited them back to dig it up. Twenty-six of them came. Some had grown taller over the summer. One had a new haircut that nobody mentioned. They all wore different uniforms now, from four different secondary schools, and they stood around the oak tree in a slightly awkward circle, as if they were not quite sure whether they still knew each other.","The tin came up rusty but dry. Mr Patel handed out the envelopes, and one by one they opened them.","Noor read hers and laughed out loud. Dear Future Noor, it said, I hope you are still friends with Hannah. If you are not, phone her immediately. Noor looked across at Hannah, who went to a different school now, and found that Hannah was looking back at her. They had not spoken since July. Hannah grinned. Noor grinned back.","Leo read his twice and then folded it up quickly. He had written that he was scared of being bullied, because he was small. He did not tell anyone that, at his new school, nobody had cared how tall he was, and that he had already joined the chess club and the coding club and had two new friends called Dev and Matilda. He just put the letter in his pocket and felt, for the first time in months, slightly taller than he was.","Maisie’s letter was a list. Things I want to do in Year 7: 1. Learn the guitar. 2. Be in the school play. 3. Not cry on the first day. She had done none of them. She had definitely cried on the first day. But reading it, she felt something she had not expected – not disappointment, but a sort of fizz, as if the list was still waiting for her, and it was only September.","And Sam, who had moved to the area halfway through Year 6 and had never really fitted in, opened his envelope and found a second sheet of paper inside that he had not written. It was a note from Mr Patel. I hope your new school feels like home sooner than this one did, it said. You were braver than you knew. Sam read it three times and did not say anything at all.","Afterwards, they stayed for longer than they meant to. They swapped stories about their new schools – the impossible maps, the teachers, the food. Someone suggested they bury another time capsule, to be opened at the end of Year 11. Mr Patel produced a new biscuit tin, as if he had been expecting this all along, which he probably had.","This time, everyone wrote longer letters."],"glossary":{"sealing":"Closing something firmly.","awkward":"Uncomfortable or embarrassed.","fizz":"Here, a lively, excited feeling.","capsule":"A small container; a time capsule holds things to be opened in the future."},"questions":[{"q":"What did Mr Patel ask the pupils to write in their letters?","o":["Their favourite food","Their hopes, worries and who they wanted to be","Their test results","A story about the summer"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Write down what you’re hoping for. What you’re worried about. Who you want to be.”"},{"q":"Why did the pupils stand in “a slightly awkward circle”?","o":["They were cold","They had changed and gone to different schools, so they felt unsure of each other","They did not like Mr Patel","They were lost"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"They wore different uniforms now and were “not quite sure whether they still knew each other.”"},{"q":"Why did Leo feel “slightly taller than he was”?","o":["He had grown over the summer","His fear of being bullied had not come true and he had made friends, so he felt more confident","He was standing on a step","He won a prize"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Nobody cared about his height, and he had joined clubs and made friends."},{"q":"How did Maisie feel after reading her list?","o":["Disappointed and sad","Excited, because there was still time to achieve her goals","Angry with Mr Patel","Bored"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"She felt “a sort of fizz, as if the list was still waiting for her.”"},{"q":"What does Mr Patel’s note to Sam show about him as a teacher?","o":["He noticed Sam had struggled and cared about him","He was strict","He forgot about Sam","He wanted Sam to leave"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"He wrote privately to encourage Sam: “You were braver than you knew.”"},{"q":"What does the final line, “This time, everyone wrote longer letters”, suggest?","o":["They had more time","They now valued the experience and had more to say about their hopes","They were told to","They had new pens"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Opening the first letters made them realise how meaningful the activity was."}]},{"id":"y101","title":"The Eagle","kind":"Poem","theme":"mountain","author":"Alfred, Lord Tennyson (1851)","text":["He clasps the crag with crooked hands;\nClose to the sun in lonely lands,\nRing’d with the azure world, he stands.","The wrinkled sea beneath him crawls;\nHe watches from his mountain walls,\nAnd like a thunderbolt he falls."],"glossary":{"clasps":"Grips tightly.","crag":"A steep, rough rock or cliff.","crooked":"Bent or curved.","azure":"Bright blue, like a clear sky.","wrinkled":"Covered in small lines or folds.","thunderbolt":"A flash of lightning with a crash of thunder."},"questions":[{"q":"Where is the eagle at the start of the poem?","o":["Flying over the sea","Gripping a high rock on a mountain","In a nest of twigs","On a tree branch"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“He clasps the crag … he stands.”"},{"q":"Why does the poet describe the eagle’s claws as “crooked hands”?","o":["To make the eagle seem almost human and powerful","Because the eagle is injured","Because eagles have fingers","To show the eagle is old"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Calling claws ‘hands’ personifies the eagle, making it seem like a mighty human figure."},{"q":"Which sound is repeated in “clasps the crag with crooked”?","o":["A hard ‘k’ sound (alliteration)","An ‘s’ sound only","A ‘th’ sound","No sounds are repeated"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"The hard ‘c’ sounds create alliteration, suggesting the harshness of rock and grip."},{"q":"Why does the sea appear “wrinkled” and seem to “crawl”?","o":["The sea is very rough","From so high up, the waves look like tiny wrinkles moving slowly","The sea is old","The eagle is dreaming"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Great height makes the waves look small and slow."},{"q":"What does “like a thunderbolt he falls” suggest about the eagle’s dive?","o":["It is slow and gentle","It is sudden, fast and powerful","It is noisy and clumsy","It is an accident"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"A thunderbolt is sudden and violent, like the eagle swooping down."},{"q":"How does the structure of the poem mirror the eagle’s actions?","o":["The first stanza shows it still and watching; the last line shows it dropping suddenly","The poem is about two eagles","Each line is longer than the last","It has no structure"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"Stillness in stanza one leads to the sudden fall in the final line."}]},{"id":"y102","title":"Rosa Parks: The Woman Who Stayed Seated","kind":"Biography","theme":"city","text":["On the evening of 1 December 1955, a forty-two-year-old seamstress named Rosa Parks finished her day’s work at a department store in Montgomery, Alabama, and boarded the Cleveland Avenue bus to go home. She paid her fare and sat down in the first row of the ‘coloured’ section, behind the seats reserved for white passengers. She was tired. Her feet hurt. Within a few stops, she would change the history of the United States.","At that time, much of the southern United States was governed by segregation laws, often called Jim Crow laws. Black and white Americans were kept apart in schools, restaurants, cinemas, drinking fountains and public transport. On Montgomery’s buses, the front seats were for white people only, and black passengers had to sit at the back. If the white section filled up, the driver could order black passengers to give up their seats and stand.","As the bus filled, the driver, James Blake, saw a white man standing. He told Rosa and three other black passengers in her row to move. The other three stood up. Rosa Parks did not.","“Are you going to stand up?” the driver asked.\n“No,” she said.\n“Well, I’m going to have you arrested.”\n“You may do that.”","Many people later said that Rosa Parks refused to move because she was tired. She disagreed. “People always say that I didn’t give up my seat because I was tired,” she wrote in her autobiography, “but that isn’t true. I was not tired physically … No, the only tired I was, was tired of giving in.” Rosa Parks was not simply a passenger who happened to be in the wrong place. She was an experienced activist who had worked for years as secretary of the local branch of the National Association for the Advancement of Colored People (NAACP), campaigning for black Americans’ rights.","She was arrested and fined. Within days, local leaders organised a boycott: black residents of Montgomery would refuse to ride the buses until the rules changed. A young minister named Martin Luther King Jr. was chosen to lead it. For 381 days, around 40,000 people walked to work, shared cars or took taxis driven by black drivers who charged the same low price as the bus. Some walked many miles each day, in rain and heat. The bus company lost a huge amount of money.","In November 1956, the US Supreme Court ruled that segregation on buses was unconstitutional. The boycott had won. It also made Martin Luther King Jr. a national figure, and it helped to inspire the wider civil rights movement, which led to laws in the 1960s that banned segregation and protected black Americans’ right to vote.","Rosa Parks paid a price: she lost her job and received threats, and she later moved to Detroit. But she continued to campaign for civil rights for the rest of her life. When she died in 2005, aged ninety-two, she became the first woman to lie in honour in the United States Capitol. She is often called ‘the mother of the civil rights movement’."],"glossary":{"seamstress":"A woman who sews clothes as a job.","segregation":"Keeping people apart because of their race.","autobiography":"The story of a person’s life written by that person.","activist":"A person who campaigns for political or social change.","boycott":"Refusing to use or buy something as a protest.","minister":"A leader in a Christian church.","unconstitutional":"Against the basic laws of a country.","campaign":"To work in an organised way to achieve change."},"questions":[{"q":"What were segregation laws?","o":["Laws about bus fares","Laws that kept black and white Americans apart in public places","Laws about working hours","Laws about voting ages"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Black and white Americans “were kept apart in schools, restaurants … and public transport.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer include Rosa Parks’s own words about being tired?","o":["To correct a myth and show her refusal was a deliberate act of protest","To show she needed sleep","To explain her job","To describe the bus"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"She says “the only tired I was, was tired of giving in.”"},{"q":"What does the short exchange of dialogue with the driver show about Rosa Parks?","o":["She was calm, firm and unafraid","She was rude and shouting","She was confused","She was scared and crying"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Her short, calm replies – “No” and “You may do that” – show quiet determination."},{"q":"How long did the Montgomery bus boycott last?","o":["One week","381 days","Ten years","One month"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“For 381 days, around 40,000 people walked to work.”"},{"q":"Why did the boycott put pressure on the bus company?","o":["The buses broke down","It lost a huge amount of money because so many people stopped riding","The drivers went on strike","The government closed it"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"With 40,000 people not riding, “The bus company lost a huge amount of money.”"},{"q":"What was the long-term significance of Rosa Parks’s action?","o":["It only changed one bus route","It helped spark the wider civil rights movement","It had no effect","It made buses free"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"It helped inspire the civil rights movement that led to laws banning segregation."}]},{"id":"y103","title":"The RNLI: Volunteers Who Save Lives at Sea","kind":"Non-fiction","theme":"sea","text":["When a pager bleeps on the coast of Britain, it may be calling a builder, a teacher, a hairdresser or a fisherman. Within minutes, they will have dropped whatever they are doing, raced to the lifeboat station, pulled on their kit and launched into the sea. They are lifeboat crew members of the Royal National Lifeboat Institution, or RNLI – and almost all of them are unpaid volunteers.","The RNLI was founded in 1824 by Sir William Hillary, who lived on the Isle of Man and had seen many shipwrecks along its rocky coast. He wrote an appeal calling for a national lifeboat service, crewed by trained volunteers and paid for by public donations. Two hundred years later, that is still how it works. The RNLI runs more than 230 lifeboat stations around the coasts of the UK and Ireland, and its lifeguards patrol hundreds of beaches. It is a charity, and it receives almost no money from the government.","Lifeboat crews train constantly. They learn first aid, navigation, radio skills and how to handle their boats in huge waves, darkness and fog. There are two main kinds of lifeboat. Inshore lifeboats are fast, inflatable boats that can work close to the shore, in shallow water and near rocks and cliffs. All-weather lifeboats are larger and are built to survive the worst storms, with self-righting hulls – if one capsizes, it rolls back upright within seconds.","Every year, RNLI lifeboats launch thousands of times, and their crews rescue thousands of people. Many of those rescued are not sailors at all. They are walkers cut off by the tide, swimmers swept out by currents, children drifting away on inflatables, paddleboarders who have been blown offshore, or people who have fallen from cliffs. Since 1824, the RNLI estimates that its lifeboat crews and lifeguards have saved more than 140,000 lives.","The work can be dangerous. Over two centuries, more than 600 volunteers have lost their lives while trying to rescue others. One of the worst disasters happened at Penlee in Cornwall in December 1981, when the lifeboat Solomon Browne went out in hurricane-force winds to help a ship called the Union Star. All eight lifeboat crew members and everyone aboard the ship were lost. Within days, so many local people volunteered to join a new crew that the station was able to continue.","The RNLI also works to stop people getting into trouble in the first place. Its safety advice is simple but vital. If you get into difficulty in cold water, the instinct is to thrash about, but that can make things worse. Instead, the RNLI teaches people to ‘Float to Live’: lean back, spread your arms and legs, and float until you can catch your breath and call for help. If you see someone else in trouble, do not go in after them. Call 999 or 112 and ask for the Coastguard.","Next time you visit the seaside and see a lifeboat station, remember the people behind it – ordinary people who, when the pager goes, are ready to do something extraordinary."],"glossary":{"pager":"A small device that bleeps to tell someone they are needed.","volunteers":"People who work without being paid.","appeal":"A serious request, especially for help or money.","donations":"Money or goods given to help a cause.","navigation":"Finding the way, especially at sea.","inflatable":"Able to be filled with air.","capsizes":"Turns upside down in the water.","instinct":"A natural reaction you do not have to think about.","extraordinary":"Very unusual or remarkable."},"questions":[{"q":"Who are most RNLI lifeboat crew members?","o":["Navy officers","Unpaid volunteers with other jobs","Police officers","Professional athletes"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Almost all of them are unpaid volunteers.”"},{"q":"How is the RNLI mainly funded?","o":["By the government","By public donations as a charity","By selling boats","By ticket sales"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It is “paid for by public donations” and receives almost no government money."},{"q":"What is special about all-weather lifeboats?","o":["They can fly","They roll back upright within seconds if they capsize","They are made of glass","They only work in summer"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They have “self-righting hulls”."},{"q":"Why does the writer mention that many rescued people “are not sailors at all”?","o":["To show that anyone visiting the coast can get into trouble","To criticise sailors","To show sailors never need help","To advertise sailing lessons"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"It lists walkers, swimmers and paddleboarders to show the danger affects everyone."},{"q":"What does the response to the Penlee disaster show about the local community?","o":["They were afraid of the sea","They were brave and committed to continuing the lifeboat service","They closed the station","They blamed the RNLI"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“So many local people volunteered to join a new crew that the station was able to continue.”"},{"q":"What does ‘Float to Live’ advise?","o":["Swim hard to the shore","Lean back and float to catch your breath before calling for help","Dive under the waves","Shout and wave your arms"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Lean back, spread your arms and legs, and float until you can catch your breath.”"}]},{"id":"y104","title":"How We Hear: The Science of Sound","kind":"Science","theme":"music","text":["Clap your hands. In that instant, you have created a wave. It cannot be seen, but it races outwards from your hands at about 343 metres per second, reaches your ears and, a tiny fraction of a second later, your brain tells you: that was a clap.","Sound is made by vibrations. When you clap, pluck a guitar string or speak, something moves back and forth very quickly. These vibrations push and pull on the air particles next to them, which bump into the particles next to them, and so on, passing the vibration along. This is a sound wave. The particles themselves do not travel far; only the vibration travels, rather like a ripple passing along a line of dominoes.","Because sound needs particles to travel through, it cannot travel through a vacuum, where there are no particles at all. That is why space is silent. Despite all the explosions in science-fiction films, a real explosion in space would make no sound. Sound actually travels faster through liquids and solids than through air, because their particles are packed more closely together. In water, it moves more than four times faster, which is how whales can communicate across hundreds of kilometres of ocean.","Sound waves have two important properties. The first is frequency: how many vibrations happen each second, measured in hertz (Hz). High frequency means a high pitch, like a whistle; low frequency means a low pitch, like a drum. Humans can hear frequencies from about 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz, although we lose the ability to hear the highest sounds as we get older. Dogs can hear up to around 45,000 Hz, which is why dog whistles seem silent to us. Bats can hear even higher, using echoes of their own squeaks to find insects in the dark.","The second property is amplitude, which is how big the vibrations are. Bigger vibrations carry more energy and sound louder. Loudness is measured in decibels (dB). A whisper is about 30 dB, normal conversation about 60 dB and a rock concert can reach 110 dB. Long exposure to sounds above about 85 dB can permanently damage your hearing.","Your ear is an amazing piece of equipment. The outer ear collects sound waves and funnels them down the ear canal to the eardrum, a thin piece of skin that vibrates when sound hits it. These vibrations pass to three tiny bones in the middle ear – the hammer, anvil and stirrup. The stirrup is the smallest bone in the human body, about the size of a grain of rice. The bones amplify the vibrations and pass them to the cochlea, a snail-shaped tube filled with liquid and lined with thousands of tiny hair cells. As the liquid moves, the hair cells bend and send electrical signals along a nerve to the brain, which turns them into the sounds we recognise.","Those delicate hair cells do not grow back if they are damaged. So enjoy your music – but keep the volume down, and protect your ears at concerts. You only get one pair."],"glossary":{"vibrations":"Very fast movements back and forth.","particles":"Tiny pieces of matter.","vacuum":"A completely empty space with no air or particles.","frequency":"How often something happens; for sound, the number of vibrations per second.","pitch":"How high or low a sound is.","amplitude":"The size of a vibration, which affects loudness.","decibels":"Units for measuring how loud a sound is.","amplify":"To make louder or stronger.","cochlea":"A spiral, fluid-filled part of the inner ear."},"questions":[{"q":"What makes sound?","o":["Light","Vibrations","Heat","Magnets"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Sound is made by vibrations.”"},{"q":"Why is space silent?","o":["It is too cold","There are no particles to carry the vibrations","Sound travels too fast there","Astronauts wear helmets"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Sound “cannot travel through a vacuum, where there are no particles at all.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer compare a sound wave to a ripple along a line of dominoes?","o":["To show that the vibration passes along while each particle only moves a little","To show sound is a game","Because dominoes are noisy","To show sound travels slowly"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Each domino stays near its place while the movement travels along the line."},{"q":"Why do dog whistles seem silent to humans?","o":["They are broken","Their frequency is higher than humans can hear but dogs can hear it","Dogs blow them","They are very quiet"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Dogs hear up to 45,000 Hz, far above the human limit of about 20,000 Hz."},{"q":"What is the job of the three tiny bones in the middle ear?","o":["To hold the ear on","To amplify the vibrations and pass them to the cochlea","To make wax","To help balance"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The bones amplify the vibrations and pass them to the cochlea.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer end with advice about volume?","o":["Damaged hair cells in the cochlea do not grow back","Music is bad for you","Concerts are too expensive","Headphones are dangerous to wear"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"“Those delicate hair cells do not grow back if they are damaged.”"}]},{"id":"y105","title":"Boudica: The Queen Who Fought Rome","kind":"History","theme":"rome","text":["In AD 43, the Roman army invaded Britain. Over the following years, some British tribes fought the invaders, while others made deals with them. One of these was the Iceni, a tribe living in what is now Norfolk and Suffolk. Their king, Prasutagus, became an ally of Rome and was allowed to continue ruling. He was married to a tall, strong-willed woman named Boudica.","When Prasutagus died in about AD 60, he left his kingdom jointly to his two daughters and to the Roman emperor, Nero, hoping that this would protect his family. It did not. Roman officials ignored his will and seized the kingdom. According to the Roman historian Tacitus, Iceni nobles were stripped of their lands, Boudica was publicly flogged and her daughters were attacked. The Romans also demanded the repayment of money they claimed to have lent the Iceni.","Boudica’s response shook the Roman Empire. While the Roman governor, Suetonius Paulinus, was away fighting in north Wales, she gathered an army from the Iceni and neighbouring tribes such as the Trinovantes, who also had grievances against Rome. Thousands joined her.","Her army first attacked Camulodunum (modern Colchester), the capital of Roman Britain, where retired Roman soldiers had taken British land. The town had no walls. Its people sheltered in the great Temple of Claudius, but after two days the rebels broke in and destroyed everything. Boudica’s forces then marched on Londinium – London – a busy trading town. Suetonius raced back but decided he could not defend it, and abandoned it. The rebels burned London to the ground. Archaeologists digging in the city today still find a thick red layer of burned material from this fire. Verulamium (St Albans) was destroyed next. Tacitus claimed that around 70,000 people were killed.","Finally, Suetonius gathered an army of about 10,000 trained Roman soldiers and chose his battlefield carefully, probably somewhere in the Midlands, though the exact site is unknown. He positioned his troops in a narrow valley with a forest behind them, so they could not be surrounded. Boudica’s army was far larger – Roman writers claimed it numbered over 200,000, though this is probably an exaggeration – but it was poorly equipped and less disciplined. Families had come to watch, and their wagons formed a ring at the back of the battlefield.","Before the battle, Boudica is said to have ridden in a chariot with her daughters, telling her warriors that she was fighting not as a queen but as an ordinary woman avenging her lost freedom. The Romans hurled their javelins, then advanced in a tight wedge. The British were driven back, and with their own wagons blocking their escape, they were slaughtered. The rebellion was crushed. Boudica is thought to have died soon afterwards, perhaps by taking poison.","Rome ruled Britain for another 350 years. But Boudica was never forgotten. In Victorian times, she became a symbol of British courage, and a bronze statue of her in her chariot stands beside the River Thames near the Houses of Parliament – in the city her army once burned."],"glossary":{"ally":"A person or group that supports another.","jointly":"Shared between two or more people.","flogged":"Beaten with a whip or stick.","grievances":"Complaints about unfair treatment.","rebels":"People who fight against those in power.","exaggeration":"Making something seem bigger than it really is.","disciplined":"Trained to follow orders and behave in a controlled way.","javelins":"Light spears thrown as weapons.","slaughtered":"Killed in large numbers."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did Prasutagus leave half his kingdom to the emperor?","o":["He disliked his daughters","He hoped it would protect his family","The law said he must","He owed money to Nero"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He hoped “that this would protect his family.”"},{"q":"What made Boudica rebel?","o":["The Romans ignored the will, seized the kingdom and mistreated her family","She wanted to become empress","The weather was bad","She was bored"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"The Romans seized the kingdom, flogged Boudica and attacked her daughters."},{"q":"Why was it a good moment for Boudica to attack?","o":["The Roman governor was away fighting in Wales","It was winter","The Romans had left Britain","She had a new weapon"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"She acted “While the Roman governor … was away fighting in north Wales.”"},{"q":"What evidence of the burning of London can still be found?","o":["Boudica’s sword","A thick red layer of burned material","Statues of the rebels","Letters from Tacitus"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Archaeologists “still find a thick red layer of burned material.”"},{"q":"Why did the Romans win the final battle despite being outnumbered?","o":["They were better trained and chose a position where they could not be surrounded","They had more soldiers","Boudica surrendered","It rained"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Suetonius chose a narrow valley, and his trained troops faced a poorly equipped army trapped by its own wagons."},{"q":"Why does the writer mention that the statue stands “in the city her army once burned”?","o":["To highlight the irony that she is now honoured in the place she destroyed","To give directions","To show London forgot her","To describe the statue’s size"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"It points out the striking contrast between her destruction of London and her later status as a national hero."}]},{"id":"y106","title":"Heatwave Smashes Temperature Records","kind":"Newspaper report","theme":"storm","text":["BRITAIN SIZZLES AS THERMOMETERS SOAR PAST 40°C","Temperatures in Britain passed 40 degrees Celsius for the first time ever on record yesterday, as a scorching heatwave brought chaos to roads and railways, sparked fires and left millions searching for shade.","The Met Office confirmed that a temperature of 40.3°C was recorded at Coningsby in Lincolnshire on Tuesday afternoon, smashing the previous UK record of 38.7°C, set in Cambridge in 2019. At least thirty-four weather stations across the country beat the old record.","For the first time, the Met Office had issued a red ‘extreme heat’ warning, its highest level, for much of England. Forecasters warned that the heat posed a danger not only to vulnerable groups such as the elderly and young children, but to healthy people too.","Across the country, the effects were dramatic. Network Rail cancelled or slowed many trains, fearing that steel rails could buckle in the heat. At Luton Airport, flights were suspended after part of the runway surface lifted. Several schools closed early or allowed pupils to wear PE kits instead of blazers.","In London, the fire brigade declared a major incident after a series of grass fires spread to homes. In the village of Wennington, on the edge of the capital, flames swept through gardens and destroyed more than a dozen houses. The London Fire Brigade said it had experienced its busiest day since the Second World War.","Gareth Hughes, a firefighter with twenty years’ experience, said: “I have never seen anything like it. The grass was so dry it went up like paper. Our crews were working in full kit in forty-degree heat. Several needed treatment for heat exhaustion.”","Scientists say the record-breaking heat is linked to climate change. Professor Hannah Lin, a climate scientist, explained: “Heatwaves happened in the past, of course. But studies show that temperatures of 40°C in the UK would have been extremely unlikely without the warming caused by human activity. As the climate warms, events like this will become more frequent.”","Not everyone was unhappy. Beaches in Brighton and Bournemouth were packed, and ice cream sellers reported their busiest week in years. However, coastguards warned people to take care in the water after several swimmers got into difficulty.","The Met Office has advised people to stay out of the sun during the hottest part of the day, drink plenty of water, keep curtains closed during the day and check on elderly neighbours. Temperatures are expected to fall back to the mid-twenties by the weekend, with thunderstorms possible in some areas."],"glossary":{"sizzles":"Makes a hissing sound like food frying; here, is very hot.","scorching":"Extremely hot.","vulnerable":"Easily harmed.","buckle":"To bend or twist out of shape.","suspended":"Stopped for a time.","incident":"An event, especially a serious or dangerous one.","exhaustion":"Extreme tiredness.","frequent":"Happening often."},"questions":[{"q":"What was the highest temperature recorded?","o":["38.7°C","40.3°C","34°C","42°C"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“A temperature of 40.3°C was recorded at Coningsby.”"},{"q":"Why were some trains cancelled or slowed?","o":["Drivers were on holiday","Steel rails could buckle in the heat","The trains overheated","Too many passengers"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Network Rail feared “steel rails could buckle in the heat.”"},{"q":"What is the purpose of the headline?","o":["To give the full details","To grab attention and sum up the main event dramatically","To give the weather forecast","To name the reporter"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"Headlines use bold, punchy language (‘SIZZLES’, ‘SOAR’) to attract readers."},{"q":"Why does the reporter include quotations from a firefighter and a scientist?","o":["To add expert and eyewitness evidence","To fill space","To criticise them","To change the subject"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Quotes from people involved make a report more reliable and vivid."},{"q":"What does the firefighter mean by “it went up like paper”?","o":["The grass was very dry and burned instantly","The grass was made of paper","The fire was small","It started to rain"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"The simile shows how quickly the dry grass caught fire."},{"q":"According to Professor Lin, what is the link between the heatwave and climate change?","o":["There is no link","Temperatures this high would have been very unlikely without human-caused warming","Climate change makes Britain colder","Heatwaves never happened before"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Temperatures of 40°C in the UK would have been extremely unlikely without the warming caused by human activity.”"}]},{"id":"y107","title":"Frankenstein: The Creature Awakes","kind":"Retelling","theme":"storm","author":"Retold from Mary Shelley","text":["Victor Frankenstein had always wanted to discover the secrets of life. As a boy in Geneva, he devoured books on alchemy and ancient science. At university in Ingolstadt, he studied chemistry and anatomy until he became the most brilliant student his teachers had ever seen. And then, after months of work by candlelight, he made a discovery more terrible and wonderful than any before: he learned how to bring dead matter to life.","For almost two years he laboured in secret, in a small room at the top of his lodgings. He neglected his friends and his family. He hardly ate or slept. His cheeks grew pale and his body thin, but he could not stop. Piece by piece, he assembled a body. Because the smallest parts were hard to work with, he made it gigantic – eight feet tall – and he chose its features to be beautiful.","It was on a dreary night in November that he saw his work completed. The rain pattered dismally against the windows, and his candle was nearly burned out. By its glimmering light, he gathered his instruments and infused a spark of life into the lifeless thing that lay at his feet.","He saw the dull yellow eye of the creature open. It breathed hard, and a convulsive motion shook its limbs.","In that moment, all of Victor’s dreams turned to horror. The features he had chosen to be beautiful were, now that they moved, hideous. The creature’s yellow skin barely covered the muscles and arteries beneath. Its eyes were watery, almost the same colour as their sockets. Its lips were straight and black. The beauty of the dream vanished, and breathless horror and disgust filled his heart.","Unable to bear the sight of what he had made, Victor fled from the room and threw himself onto his bed, trying to forget. When he finally slept, he was tormented by nightmares. He woke to find the creature standing beside his bed, holding up the curtain and looking at him. Its jaws opened, and it muttered some sounds, while a grin wrinkled its cheeks. One hand was stretched out, perhaps to stop him. Victor escaped down the stairs and spent the rest of the night pacing in the courtyard, starting at every sound.","The creature disappeared. Victor told himself it was gone for ever. But he had abandoned the being he had brought into the world – a being as innocent as a newborn child, who did not ask to be made and who had no one to teach it, love it or explain why its own creator had run away in terror. Rejected by everyone it met, the creature would come to understand loneliness, and then anger, and finally revenge.","Mary Shelley began writing Frankenstein in 1816, when she was only eighteen, during a stormy summer in Switzerland when she and her friends challenged one another to write ghost stories. It is often called the first science-fiction novel. More than two hundred years later, its question still matters: what do we owe to the things we create?"],"glossary":{"alchemy":"An old kind of science that tried to turn metals into gold and find the secret of life.","anatomy":"The study of the structure of bodies.","laboured":"Worked very hard.","neglected":"Failed to look after or pay attention to.","dreary":"Dull, gloomy and depressing.","infused":"Filled or put into.","convulsive":"Shaking suddenly and violently.","hideous":"Extremely ugly.","tormented":"Caused great suffering.","abandoned":"Left behind and not cared for."},"questions":[{"q":"What did Victor Frankenstein discover?","o":["How to turn metal into gold","How to bring dead matter to life","How to cure illness","How to fly"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“He learned how to bring dead matter to life.”"},{"q":"What effect did Victor’s work have on his health?","o":["He became stronger","He grew pale and thin from hardly eating or sleeping","He gained weight","It had no effect"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“He hardly ate or slept. His cheeks grew pale and his body thin.”"},{"q":"How does the writer create a gloomy atmosphere for the creature’s awakening?","o":["A dreary November night, rain pattering dismally and a candle nearly burned out","A sunny morning with birdsong","A busy party","A calm summer evening"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Pathetic fallacy – the dark, wet setting reflects the horror to come."},{"q":"Why does Victor feel horror when the creature opens its eyes?","o":["It attacked him","The features he thought beautiful looked hideous once alive","It ran away","It was too small"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“The features he had chosen to be beautiful were, now that they moved, hideous.”"},{"q":"What does the creature’s outstretched hand at the bedside suggest?","o":["It may have been reaching out to its creator, not attacking him","It wanted to steal something","It was waving goodbye","It was asleep"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"It held out a hand “perhaps to stop him”, like a child reaching for a parent."},{"q":"What question does the writer say the novel still asks?","o":["How do we build robots?","What do we owe to the things we create?","Is Switzerland stormy?","Who wrote the first ghost story?"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"The final sentence states this question."}]},{"id":"y108","title":"Where Does Our Rubbish Go?","kind":"Non-fiction","theme":"city","text":["Every week, the bins outside British homes are emptied, and the rubbish disappears. But ‘away’ is not a real place. Everything we throw out has to go somewhere – and the journey it takes depends on which bin we put it in.","The average household in England produces around 400 kilograms of waste a year – roughly the weight of a grand piano. Most councils now collect it in separate bins: one for general rubbish, one for recycling and, in many areas, one for food or garden waste. The system can be confusing, because different councils accept different materials. But getting it right matters.","Start with the general waste bin. Its contents are taken either to landfill or to an incinerator. Landfill sites are huge holes in the ground, often old quarries, where rubbish is buried in layers. Modern landfill sites are lined to stop harmful liquids leaking into the soil and water. But landfill takes up space, and as food and other organic waste rot without air, they release methane, a powerful greenhouse gas. Some landfill sites now capture this gas and burn it to generate electricity. Increasingly, though, general waste is burned in ‘energy from waste’ plants, where the heat is used to make electricity. This reduces the amount sent to landfill, but burning rubbish still releases carbon dioxide.","Recycling takes a different route. The lorry takes it to a materials recovery facility, a vast sorting plant full of conveyor belts, magnets, blowers and scanners. Magnets pull out steel tins. A machine using electric currents pushes aluminium cans off the belt. Jets of air blow lighter materials such as paper and card in one direction, while optical scanners use light to identify different kinds of plastic. Workers pick out items that do not belong – which can include anything from nappies to bowling balls. The sorted materials are squashed into bales and sold to factories to be turned into new products.","The benefits are real. Making a can from recycled aluminium uses about ninety-five per cent less energy than making one from new metal, and aluminium can be recycled again and again without losing quality. Paper can be recycled several times before its fibres become too short. Glass can be melted down and remade endlessly.","Plastic is trickier. There are many different types, and some, such as crisp packets and black food trays, are difficult or impossible to recycle in most places. Contamination is another big problem. If a recycling bin contains greasy pizza boxes, half-full drinks bottles or food waste, a whole load can be spoiled and sent to landfill instead.","Food waste, when it is collected separately, can be turned into something useful. Much of it goes to anaerobic digestion plants, where bacteria break it down without air, producing biogas for energy and a rich fertiliser for farms.","Recycling is good, but it is not the whole answer. The most effective approach is summed up in three words, in order of importance: reduce, reuse, recycle. The best waste of all is the waste we never create in the first place."],"glossary":{"landfill":"A place where rubbish is buried in the ground.","incinerator":"A furnace for burning rubbish.","quarries":"Places where stone has been dug out of the ground.","organic":"Coming from living things, such as food or plants.","methane":"A gas that traps heat in the atmosphere.","facility":"A building or place used for a particular purpose.","optical":"To do with sight or light.","contamination":"Making something dirty or impure by mixing in something unwanted.","fertiliser":"A substance added to soil to help plants grow."},"questions":[{"q":"How much waste does the average household in England produce each year?","o":["About 40 kilograms","About 400 kilograms","About 4 tonnes","About 4 kilograms"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Around 400 kilograms of waste a year.”"},{"q":"Why is ‘away’ described as “not a real place”?","o":["Because our rubbish always ends up somewhere, even if we stop seeing it","Because bins are not collected","Because ‘away’ is a country","Because rubbish disappears magically"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“Everything we throw out has to go somewhere.”"},{"q":"Why can rotting food in landfill be a problem?","o":["It smells nice","It releases methane, a powerful greenhouse gas","It makes the site too heavy","It attracts tourists"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Rotting waste releases “methane, a powerful greenhouse gas.”"},{"q":"How are steel tins separated at a recovery facility?","o":["By hand only","By magnets","By jets of air","By optical scanners"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Magnets pull out steel tins.”"},{"q":"Why is contamination a problem for recycling?","o":["A whole load can be spoiled and sent to landfill","It makes lorries slower","It creates new plastic","It improves the quality"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Greasy or dirty items can spoil “a whole load” of recycling."},{"q":"What is the writer’s main message at the end?","o":["Recycling solves everything","Reducing waste in the first place is the most important step","Throw everything in landfill","Burn all rubbish"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"“The best waste of all is the waste we never create in the first place.”"}]},{"id":"y109","title":"The Chess Champion","kind":"Story","theme":"books","text":["Grandpa Ilya taught Max to play chess when he was six, on a wooden board so old that the white king had lost the cross off the top of his crown. They played every Sunday after lunch, at the kitchen table, while Grandma washed up and pretended not to watch.","For four years Max never won. Not once. Grandpa did not let him. “If I let you win,” he said, tapping the side of his nose, “you will never learn anything. When you beat me, you will know you have really beaten me.”","Max learned. He learned that the knight moves in an L-shape and can jump over other pieces. He learned to castle his king to safety and to control the centre of the board. He learned the names of openings – the Sicilian, the Queen’s Gambit, the French Defence – and practised them on his phone on the bus to school. He joined the chess club in Year 7 and, to his surprise, beat everyone except a quiet girl in Year 9 called Precious, who beat him in eleven moves and then explained, very kindly, exactly where he had gone wrong.","But he still could not beat Grandpa.","Then, in the spring, Grandpa became ill. He spent three weeks in hospital and came home thinner and slower, with a stick he hated. The Sunday games stopped. Max kept setting up the board on the kitchen table, out of habit, and then putting it away again.","In June, Max entered the county under-12 championship. He had not told Grandpa, because he did not want to worry him. He won his first game, lost his second, then won three in a row. In the final round, he was paired against a boy from Derby who was ranked first in the county. The game lasted almost two hours. Max played the Sicilian. He controlled the centre. In the end, with only a few pieces left, he saw something that made his heart thump: a knight move, jumping over a pawn, that attacked the other boy’s king and queen at the same time. A fork. Grandpa had shown him a hundred forks.","The other boy studied the board for a long time. Then he tipped over his king and held out his hand.","Max came second overall. He carried the silver trophy all the way home on the bus.","Grandpa was sitting in his armchair by the window. Max put the trophy on the table beside him without a word. Grandpa looked at it for a long moment. Then he reached for his stick and said, “Well. Get the board out, then.”","They played at the kitchen table. Grandpa was slower than before, and once or twice he lost track of a piece. But he still played well, and the game was close. On move forty-one, Max saw the winning move. He looked up and found Grandpa watching him, waiting.","Max made the move. Checkmate.","Grandpa did not say anything for a while. Then he laughed, a real laugh, the first in weeks. “Now,” he said, wiping his eyes, “you know you have really beaten me.”","Grandma, at the sink, turned away so that neither of them would see her face."],"glossary":{"castle":"In chess, a special move to protect the king.","openings":"The first moves of a chess game, which often follow known patterns.","gambit":"An opening in chess in which a player gives up a piece to gain an advantage.","ranked":"Given a position in a list according to ability.","fork":"In chess, a move that attacks two pieces at the same time.","trophy":"A prize such as a cup, given to a winner.","checkmate":"The winning position in chess, when the king is trapped."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did Grandpa never let Max win?","o":["He was a bad loser","So that when Max won, he would know he had really earned it","He did not like Max","He forgot"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“When you beat me, you will know you have really beaten me.”"},{"q":"What does the detail of the white king missing its cross suggest?","o":["The chess set was cheap","The board had been well used and loved for many years","Max broke it","Grandpa did not care about chess"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"An old, worn set suggests a long history of games."},{"q":"How did Precious react after beating Max?","o":["She laughed at him","She kindly explained where he had gone wrong","She left the club","She refused to play again"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"She “explained, very kindly, exactly where he had gone wrong.”"},{"q":"Why did Max keep setting up the board and putting it away?","o":["He missed playing with Grandpa while he was ill","He was tidying","He was practising alone","He lost a piece"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The Sunday games stopped when Grandpa was ill, but Max kept the habit out of longing."},{"q":"How did Grandpa’s teaching help Max win the final tournament game?","o":["Grandpa was there to give advice","Max recognised a fork, a move Grandpa had shown him many times","Grandpa phoned him","Grandpa played instead of him"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“A fork. Grandpa had shown him a hundred forks.”"},{"q":"Why does Grandma turn away at the end?","o":["She is bored","She is emotional and does not want them to see her crying","She is angry","She is cooking"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"She hides her face at a deeply moving moment between them."}]},{"id":"y110","title":"How Wolves Changed Rivers","kind":"Non-fiction","theme":"forest","text":["Yellowstone, in the United States, was the world’s first national park, created in 1872. It is a land of geysers, hot springs, canyons, forests and wide grassy valleys. But for most of the twentieth century, something was missing. By 1926, every wolf in the park had been killed. They had been hunted, trapped and poisoned because they preyed on livestock and were seen as dangerous pests.","Without wolves, the park changed in ways that nobody had expected. Their main prey had been elk, a large species of deer. With no predators to fear, the elk population grew enormously. Herds gathered along the rivers and stayed there, grazing on young willow, aspen and cottonwood trees. Over decades, they ate so much that many of these trees could not grow. Riverbanks, no longer held together by tree roots, began to crumble and erode.","In 1995 and 1996, after years of debate, scientists released thirty-one grey wolves, captured in Canada, into Yellowstone. What happened next is one of the most famous examples of what ecologists call a trophic cascade – a chain of effects that ripples down through an ecosystem when a top predator is added or removed.","The wolves hunted elk, reducing their numbers. But just as importantly, they changed how the elk behaved. Elk began to avoid places where they could be easily trapped, such as valleys and riverbanks. In those places, young trees began to grow again. In some areas, willows and aspens grew five times taller within a few years.","The returning trees brought back other animals. Songbirds nested in the branches. Beavers, which eat willow, returned and built dams, creating ponds and marshes that became homes for otters, ducks, fish, frogs and insects. Wolves also killed coyotes, which had become very common, and this allowed numbers of rabbits and mice to rise, which in turn provided food for hawks, foxes and badgers. Carcasses left by wolves fed ravens, eagles and bears.","Some people have even claimed that the wolves changed the rivers themselves. With stronger, tree-lined banks, there was less erosion, and in places the river channels became narrower and deeper. A popular video about the story, called How Wolves Change Rivers, has been watched millions of times.","However, scientists say the real story is more complicated than the simple version that went viral. Other factors were changing at the same time, including the return of bears, a period of drought and hunting by humans outside the park. In some areas, the trees did not recover as much as had been hoped. Ecosystems are complex, and it is rarely the case that one animal fixes everything.","What is clear is that wolves play an important role, and that removing a predator can have effects far beyond what people expect. Today, around a hundred wolves live in Yellowstone, and visitors come from all over the world hoping to catch a glimpse of them."],"glossary":{"geysers":"Hot springs that shoot water and steam into the air.","livestock":"Farm animals such as cattle and sheep.","predators":"Animals that hunt other animals.","erode":"To be gradually worn away.","ecologists":"Scientists who study how living things relate to their environment.","trophic":"To do with food chains and what eats what.","cascade":"A series of events, each causing the next.","carcasses":"Dead bodies of animals.","viral":"Spread quickly and widely online."},"questions":[{"q":"Why were wolves killed in Yellowstone?","o":["They were sick","They preyed on livestock and were seen as dangerous pests","They ate all the fish","They were too noisy"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They were killed “because they preyed on livestock and were seen as dangerous pests.”"},{"q":"What happened to the trees by the rivers when there were no wolves?","o":["They grew taller","Elk ate so many young trees that they could not grow","They were cut down for wood","They were burned"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Elk grazed on young trees so “many of these trees could not grow.”"},{"q":"What is a ‘trophic cascade’?","o":["A waterfall","A chain of effects through an ecosystem when a top predator is added or removed","A type of tree","A wolf pack"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“A chain of effects that ripples down through an ecosystem.”"},{"q":"How did wolves help trees grow, apart from eating elk?","o":["They planted seeds","They changed elk behaviour so elk avoided riverbanks and valleys","They watered the trees","They scared away beavers"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“They changed how the elk behaved,” so trees in those places could grow."},{"q":"Why does the writer include the paragraph beginning “However, scientists say…”?","o":["To give a balanced view by showing the story is more complicated than the popular version","To argue wolves are harmful","To advertise the video","To describe bears"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It adds other factors and cautions against over-simple conclusions."},{"q":"What is the writer’s overall conclusion?","o":["Wolves fix everything","Wolves play an important role, and removing predators can have unexpected effects","Wolves should be removed again","Elk are more important than wolves"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"“What is clear is that wolves play an important role…”"}]},{"id":"y111","title":"Grandmother’s Kitchen","kind":"Poem","theme":"cottage","text":["In my grandmother’s kitchen\nthe kettle never rests;\nit grumbles on the stove\nlike an old man clearing his throat,\nready for the next cup, the next visitor,\nthe next long story.","The window is steamed with soup.\nThe radio mutters the news\nto no one in particular.\nOn the windowsill, a row of herbs\nleans towards the light\nlike children at a sweet-shop counter.","Her hands are never still:\nkneading, chopping, stirring, folding,\nflour to the wrists,\nthe gold ring on her finger\nworn thin as a wish\nby sixty years of work.","She speaks to me in two languages,\nslipping between them\nthe way she slips between rooms –\nsoftly, without knocking.\nI understand one of them.\nThe other I understand with my stomach.","When I am sad, she does not ask why.\nShe puts a bowl in front of me\nand says, Eat,\nas if the answer to everything\nis somewhere at the bottom\nof the soup.","And sometimes, I think, it is."],"glossary":{"grumbles":"Makes a low, complaining sound.","mutters":"Speaks quietly and unclearly.","windowsill":"The ledge at the bottom of a window.","kneading":"Pressing and folding dough with the hands."},"questions":[{"q":"How is the kettle described in the first stanza?","o":["As silent and cold","As grumbling like an old man clearing his throat","As shiny and new","As broken"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"It “grumbles on the stove / like an old man clearing his throat”."},{"q":"What does the simile “like children at a sweet-shop counter” suggest about the herbs?","o":["They are eager and stretching towards the light","They are tiny","They are sweet","They are noisy"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Children lean eagerly towards sweets, as the herbs lean towards the light."},{"q":"What does the list “kneading, chopping, stirring, folding” show about the grandmother?","o":["She is always busy working","She is lazy","She dislikes cooking","She is a chef in a restaurant"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The list of verbs shows constant activity: “Her hands are never still.”"},{"q":"What does “worn thin as a wish / by sixty years of work” suggest?","o":["The ring is new","Her long life of hard work and love has worn the ring thin","She lost the ring","She wishes for a new ring"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Sixty years of work has worn the ring, showing her long, hardworking life."},{"q":"What does the speaker mean by understanding one language “with my stomach”?","o":["They feel sick","They understand her love through the food she cooks, even without the words","They eat the words","They speak with their mouth full"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Her care is expressed through food, which needs no translation."},{"q":"Why does the poem end with a single short line on its own?","o":["To give a quiet, thoughtful final agreement that food and love really can help","Because the poet ran out of space","To introduce a new character","To make it rhyme"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"“And sometimes, I think, it is.” stands alone for emphasis."}]},{"id":"y112","title":"Isaac Newton and the Laws of Motion","kind":"Biography","theme":"space","text":["Isaac Newton was born on Christmas Day 1642 in the small village of Woolsthorpe in Lincolnshire. He was born early and was so tiny that, as his mother later said, he could have fitted into a quart pot. His father had died three months before he was born, and when Isaac was three, his mother remarried and left him to be brought up by his grandmother. It was a lonely childhood, and he grew into a serious, secretive boy who preferred building model windmills, sundials and kites to playing with other children.","His mother wanted him to become a farmer, but he was hopeless at it, more interested in reading than in minding the sheep. A teacher persuaded her to send him to university, and in 1661 he went to Trinity College, Cambridge.","In 1665, the Great Plague broke out, and the university closed. Newton went home to Woolsthorpe for about eighteen months. This period became one of the most remarkable in the history of science. Working alone, still only in his early twenties, he developed a new kind of mathematics, now called calculus; he discovered that white light is made up of all the colours of the rainbow, by splitting sunlight with a glass prism; and he began to think about gravity.","The famous story says that Newton saw an apple fall from a tree in his garden and wondered why it fell straight down, rather than sideways or upwards. He realised that the same force that pulled the apple towards the Earth might also reach out into space and hold the Moon in its orbit. Whether or not an apple actually inspired him – Newton himself told the story later in life – the idea was revolutionary. Gravity, he proposed, was universal: every object in the universe attracts every other object.","In 1687, Newton published his greatest book, the Principia. In it, he set out his three laws of motion. The first states that an object will stay still, or keep moving at the same speed in a straight line, unless a force acts on it. The second explains that the greater the force on an object, the more it accelerates, and that heavier objects need more force to accelerate. The third states that for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction – which is why a rocket moves forward when it pushes hot gas backwards. Together with his law of gravity, these laws explained the movement of everything from falling apples to the orbits of the planets.","Newton was a genius, but not an easy man. He could be jealous, quarrelsome and unforgiving, and he had bitter arguments with other scientists, such as Robert Hooke and Gottfried Leibniz. Later in life he became Master of the Royal Mint, where he hunted down people who forged coins, and President of the Royal Society. He was knighted in 1705.","He died in 1727 and was buried in Westminster Abbey. Newton once wrote, modestly, that if he had seen further than others, it was ‘by standing on the shoulders of giants’. Since then, he has become one of the giants himself."],"glossary":{"quart":"An old unit of liquid volume, about 1.1 litres.","secretive":"Liking to keep things hidden from others.","calculus":"A branch of mathematics dealing with change.","prism":"A clear glass shape that splits light into colours.","revolutionary":"Bringing about a complete and important change.","universal":"Applying to everything everywhere.","accelerates":"Speeds up.","quarrelsome":"Often arguing.","forged":"Made illegal copies of something.","modestly":"Without boasting."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did Newton spend about eighteen months at home in Woolsthorpe?","o":["He was ill","The university closed because of the Great Plague","He failed his exams","He wanted to farm"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The Great Plague broke out, and the university closed.”"},{"q":"What did Newton discover using a glass prism?","o":["Gravity","That white light is made of all the colours of the rainbow","Calculus","The Moon’s orbit"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He discovered “white light is made up of all the colours of the rainbow.”"},{"q":"What was revolutionary about Newton’s idea of gravity?","o":["It only affected apples","It was universal – every object in the universe attracts every other","It meant objects float","It only worked in England"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Gravity, he proposed, was universal.”"},{"q":"Which law of motion explains how a rocket moves forward?","o":["The first law","The second law","The third law: for every action there is an equal and opposite reaction","The law of gravity"],"a":2,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The third law explains why “a rocket moves forward when it pushes hot gas backwards.”"},{"q":"What does the writer suggest about Newton’s personality?","o":["He was always kind and easy-going","He was a genius but could be jealous and quarrelsome","He was lazy","He disliked science"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“Newton was a genius, but not an easy man.”"},{"q":"What does ‘standing on the shoulders of giants’ mean?","o":["Newton was very tall","He built on the discoveries of great scientists before him","He climbed mountains","He disliked other scientists"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He could see further because of the work of earlier ‘giants’ of science."}]},{"id":"y113","title":"Hurricanes: Storms That Spin","kind":"Science","theme":"storm","text":["Seen from space, a hurricane looks almost beautiful: a vast white spiral of cloud, sometimes hundreds of kilometres wide, turning slowly around a small, dark, perfectly clear hole in the middle. On the ground, it is one of the most destructive forces in nature.","Hurricanes, typhoons and cyclones are all the same kind of storm – a tropical cyclone – but they are given different names in different parts of the world. In the Atlantic and north-east Pacific, they are hurricanes; in the north-west Pacific, typhoons; and in the Indian Ocean and around Australia, cyclones. To count as a hurricane, a storm’s winds must reach at least 119 kilometres per hour.","Tropical cyclones are born over warm oceans, where the water is at least twenty-six or twenty-seven degrees Celsius. The heat makes seawater evaporate, and the warm, moist air rises quickly. As it rises, it cools, and the water vapour condenses into huge clouds, releasing heat that warms the surrounding air and makes it rise even faster. More air rushes in at the surface to replace it, and that air also warms and rises. Because of the Earth’s rotation, the incoming air does not flow straight in, but spins: anticlockwise in the Northern Hemisphere and clockwise in the Southern. The storm is, in effect, a giant heat engine powered by the warm ocean.","At the centre is the eye, an area of calm where air sinks and the sky may be clear. People caught in the eye sometimes think the storm is over, only for the other side to hit them with full force minutes later. Around the eye is the eyewall, where the strongest winds and heaviest rain are found.","The damage a hurricane causes comes from three main sources. First, the wind, which can reach more than 250 kilometres per hour in the strongest storms, tearing off roofs and turning loose objects into dangerous missiles. Second, the rain: a hurricane can drop more than a metre of rain in a few days, causing floods and landslides. Third, and often deadliest, is the storm surge. The low pressure and the force of the wind push a great dome of seawater towards the coast, raising the sea level by several metres and flooding low-lying areas.","Hurricanes are measured on the Saffir–Simpson scale, from Category 1 to Category 5, according to their wind speed. They are given names from lists prepared years in advance, alternating between male and female names. If a hurricane is especially deadly, its name is retired and never used again; Katrina, which devastated New Orleans in 2005, is one of these.","Once a hurricane moves over land or cooler water, it loses its supply of warm, moist air and gradually weakens. Forecasters now track storms with satellites and specially equipped aircraft that fly straight into them, and their warnings give people days to prepare and escape. Scientists expect that, as oceans warm with climate change, the strongest storms may become more intense and wetter."],"glossary":{"destructive":"Causing great damage.","tropical":"Belonging to the hot regions near the equator.","evaporate":"To change from liquid into gas.","condenses":"Changes from a gas into a liquid.","rotation":"Turning round.","hemisphere":"One half of the Earth.","surge":"A sudden powerful rise or rush.","alternating":"Taking turns, first one and then the other.","devastated":"Destroyed or badly damaged."},"questions":[{"q":"What is the difference between a hurricane, a typhoon and a cyclone?","o":["They are different kinds of storm","They are the same storm with different names in different regions","Typhoons are always stronger","Cyclones happen only on land"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They are “all the same kind of storm … given different names in different parts of the world.”"},{"q":"Why do hurricanes form over warm oceans?","o":["Warm water evaporates and the rising warm, moist air powers the storm","Fish create the wind","Cold water is too heavy","The Moon pulls them"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"Warm water evaporates, the air rises and releases heat, powering the storm."},{"q":"Why does the writer call a hurricane “a giant heat engine”?","o":["It is made of metal","It turns the heat of the warm ocean into the energy of moving air","It burns fuel","It is very hot inside"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Like an engine, it converts heat energy into motion."},{"q":"Why is the eye of the storm dangerous even though it is calm?","o":["It has the strongest winds","People may think the storm is over before the other side hits","It is very cold","It causes earthquakes"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"People “think the storm is over, only for the other side to hit them with full force.”"},{"q":"What is often the deadliest effect of a hurricane?","o":["Lightning","The storm surge","Hail","Snow"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Third, and often deadliest, is the storm surge.”"},{"q":"Why is a hurricane’s name sometimes retired?","o":["It was too long","The storm was especially deadly","Nobody liked the name","It was used too often"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“If a hurricane is especially deadly, its name is retired.”"}]},{"id":"y114","title":"Tenochtitlan: The Floating City of the Aztecs","kind":"History","theme":"jungle","text":["In November 1519, a small army of Spanish soldiers led by Hernán Cortés marched over the mountains of central Mexico and looked down into a vast valley. Below them, shining in the sun in the middle of a great lake, was a city larger than any in Spain. One soldier, Bernal Díaz, later wrote that it was like an enchanted vision from a story, and that some of his companions asked whether what they saw was a dream.","The city was Tenochtitlan, capital of the Aztec Empire. According to Aztec legend, their ancestors, the Mexica, had wandered for many years in search of a home, guided by their god Huitzilopochtli. He told them to settle where they saw an eagle perched on a cactus, eating a snake. In about 1325, they found this sign on a small swampy island in Lake Texcoco – an image that appears on Mexico’s flag to this day.","From that unpromising start, the Aztecs built a remarkable city. They extended the island by building chinampas – artificial islands made from layers of mud and plants, anchored with willow trees – on which they grew maize, beans, squash, tomatoes and chillies. Three great raised causeways connected the city to the shore, and canals ran through it like streets, busy with canoes carrying people and goods. An aqueduct brought fresh drinking water from springs on the mainland. By 1519, perhaps 200,000 people lived there.","At the heart of the city stood the Templo Mayor, a huge stepped pyramid topped by two temples, one to Huitzilopochtli, god of war and the sun, and one to Tlaloc, god of rain. The Aztecs believed that the gods needed to be fed with offerings, including human sacrifices, to keep the sun moving across the sky and the rains falling. Many of those sacrificed were prisoners captured in wars with neighbouring peoples.","Nearby, in the twin city of Tlatelolco, was an enormous market. Spanish visitors were astonished to see tens of thousands of people buying and selling gold, silver, feathers, cotton cloth, pottery, chocolate, turkeys, dogs and even slaves. Cacao beans, used to make chocolate, also served as money. Officials checked weights and measures and punished cheats.","The Aztec emperor, Moctezuma II, welcomed Cortés into the city. Within months, the relationship had collapsed. Cortés took Moctezuma prisoner, and fighting broke out. Moctezuma was killed, and the Spanish were driven out. But they returned in 1521 with thousands of soldiers from other peoples who resented Aztec rule. Even more devastating was disease: smallpox, brought by the Europeans, swept through the city, killing huge numbers of people who had no immunity to it. After a siege of about three months, Tenochtitlan fell.","The Spanish built Mexico City on its ruins, and the lake was gradually drained. But in 1978, workers digging in the centre of Mexico City discovered a giant carved stone of the moon goddess Coyolxauhqui. Excavations revealed the foundations of the Templo Mayor, buried under the streets, and visitors can now walk among the remains of the floating city."],"glossary":{"enchanted":"Under a magic spell; magical.","ancestors":"Family members who lived long ago.","unpromising":"Not likely to be successful.","artificial":"Made by people, not natural.","causeways":"Raised roads across water.","aqueduct":"A bridge-like structure that carries water.","sacrifices":"Offerings to a god, sometimes of a living creature.","resented":"Felt angry about something unfair.","immunity":"Natural protection against a disease.","excavations":"Careful digging to uncover remains."},"questions":[{"q":"How did the Spanish soldiers react when they first saw Tenochtitlan?","o":["They were bored","They were amazed and some wondered if it was a dream","They were disappointed","They laughed"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Some “asked whether what they saw was a dream.”"},{"q":"What sign told the Mexica where to build their city?","o":["A rainbow over a mountain","An eagle on a cactus eating a snake","A star falling into the lake","A golden temple"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“An eagle perched on a cactus, eating a snake.”"},{"q":"What were chinampas?","o":["Temples","Artificial islands for growing crops","Boats","Coins"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Artificial islands made from layers of mud and plants.”"},{"q":"Why did the Aztecs make offerings and sacrifices?","o":["To please the Spanish","They believed the gods needed feeding to keep the sun moving and the rain falling","To celebrate birthdays","As punishment for cheats"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"They believed the gods needed offerings “to keep the sun moving … and the rains falling.”"},{"q":"Which factor does the writer say was “even more devastating” than the soldiers?","o":["Floods","Disease, especially smallpox","Earthquakes","Famine"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Even more devastating was disease: smallpox.”"},{"q":"How was the Templo Mayor rediscovered?","o":["By reading an old map","Workers in Mexico City found a giant carved stone in 1978","A pilot saw it from the air","It was never lost"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Workers discovered the carved stone of Coyolxauhqui, leading to excavations."}]},{"id":"y115","title":"The Island of Lost Things","kind":"Fantasy","theme":"sea","text":["Odd socks. Umbrellas. House keys. The other earring. Everyone loses things, and everyone wonders where they go. Finn found out on the afternoon he fell asleep in the rowing boat.","He had only meant to rest his eyes. The sea was flat, the sun was warm and the boat was tied to the jetty – or it had been, when he lay down. When he woke, the jetty was gone, the village was gone, and the boat was drifting towards an island he had never seen before. It was low and grey-green, and as he came closer he saw that its beaches were not made of sand.","They were made of things.","Millions of them, piled in drifts and dunes. Gloves of every size and colour. Biros without lids, and lids without biros. Hair bobbles, marbles, teddy bears with one eye, library books long overdue, bus tickets, pencil sharpeners, a wedding ring, a single wellington boot. Further up the beach, the piles grew into hills, and on the top of the highest hill sat a very old man with a long white beard, sorting buttons into jars.","“Ah,” said the old man, without looking up. “Another one. What have you lost?”","“I haven’t lost anything,” said Finn. “I just fell asleep.”","The old man peered at him over his spectacles. “Nobody comes here unless they’ve lost something,” he said. “Things find their way here when no one is looking for them any more. The forgotten ones. So. What have you stopped looking for?”","Finn thought about it. He thought about his grandad’s penknife, which he had dropped somewhere in the woods last summer and searched for until it got dark. He thought about the drawing his mum had done of him when he was a baby, which had vanished when they moved house. Then he thought about something that was harder to say.","“My best friend,” he said slowly. “Jake. He moved to Scotland in March. We said we’d talk every day. Then it was every week. Now it’s… I don’t know. I haven’t messaged him for ages. I suppose I stopped looking.”","The old man nodded, as if this were the most ordinary thing in the world. He climbed down from his hill and walked along the beach, and Finn followed him, past a mountain of odd socks and a valley of tennis balls, to a quiet cove where the sea lapped at a small heap of letters, postcards and paper aeroplanes – messages that had never been sent.","“Things you lose by accident, you can’t always get back,” said the old man. “But some things aren’t lost. They’re just waiting.” He picked up a paper aeroplane and handed it to Finn. On its wing, in Finn’s own handwriting, were the words: Miss you, mate.","Finn did not remember writing it. But he remembered meaning it.","When he woke again, the boat was bumping gently against the jetty, and the sun had moved across the sky. His phone was in his pocket. He took it out, opened his messages, scrolled down a long way to find Jake’s name, and typed: Miss you, mate.","The reply came in less than a minute."],"glossary":{"jetty":"A wooden platform built out into the sea for boats.","drifts":"Piles of something blown or washed together.","overdue":"Late; not returned on time.","peered":"Looked closely, as if it was hard to see.","cove":"A small sheltered bay."},"questions":[{"q":"What was unusual about the island’s beaches?","o":["They were made of gold","They were made of lost things","They were covered in snow","They were made of glass"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“They were made of things.”"},{"q":"Why is “They were made of things.” written as a separate paragraph?","o":["To create a surprising pause that emphasises the strange discovery","Because the writer forgot to join it","To introduce the old man","To describe the weather"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"A one-line paragraph makes the reader stop and notice the strange idea."},{"q":"How do things end up on the island, according to the old man?","o":["The sea carries them","They arrive when nobody is looking for them any more","People throw them there","They are stolen"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Things find their way here when no one is looking for them any more.”"},{"q":"What had Finn really ‘stopped looking for’?","o":["His grandad’s penknife","His friendship with Jake","A drawing","His phone"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He admits he had stopped messaging his best friend: “I suppose I stopped looking.”"},{"q":"What does the old man mean by “some things aren’t lost. They’re just waiting”?","o":["Some relationships can still be rescued if we reach out","Lost things are hiding","Everything comes back eventually","Things wait at the bus stop"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The unsent message shows the friendship can be restored."},{"q":"What does the final line suggest?","o":["Jake was also missing Finn and was glad to hear from him","Jake was angry","Jake did not reply","Finn’s phone was broken"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"A reply within a minute shows Jake was waiting too."}]},{"id":"y116","title":"Should Homework Be Banned?","kind":"Discussion","theme":"books","text":["Few topics cause more groans in Year 7 classrooms than homework. Every week, pupils across the UK spend hours on worksheets, projects, reading logs and online quizzes. Some schools have recently cut back homework, or even stopped setting it for younger pupils. So should homework be banned altogether? There are strong arguments on both sides.","Supporters of homework argue that it helps pupils learn. Practising a skill, such as times tables, spellings or vocabulary in a new language, strengthens memory, and lessons alone may not provide enough time for this practice. Research summarised by the Education Endowment Foundation suggests that homework has a positive effect on achievement in secondary schools – on average, around five months of extra progress – especially when it is linked to classroom learning and given useful feedback.","Homework, supporters say, also builds habits that matter later in life: organising your time, meeting deadlines and working independently without a teacher standing next to you. These are skills pupils will need for GCSEs, further education and work. Homework can also give parents a window into what their children are learning, and a chance to get involved.","Opponents, however, argue that homework often does more harm than good. Pupils already spend around six and a half hours a day at school. When long homework tasks are added, many have little time left for sport, music, hobbies, family or simply relaxing – all of which matter for health and happiness. Some surveys suggest that homework is a major source of stress and arguments at home.","Critics also point out that homework is unfair. Some pupils have a quiet place to work, a laptop, internet access and parents with time to help. Others share a crowded room, have to look after younger siblings or do not have a computer. Homework can therefore widen the gap between pupils from different backgrounds. In addition, the research shows that the benefits of homework depend heavily on its quality. Busywork that does not connect to lessons, or that is never marked, may achieve very little.","Between these positions there are many possible compromises. Some schools set less homework but make it more focused, such as short retrieval quizzes that help pupils remember what they learned. Others run homework clubs after school, where pupils can use computers and get help, which helps to even out the unfairness. Some let pupils choose from a menu of tasks, or set longer projects that allow creativity.","Perhaps the real question is not whether homework should exist, but what kind of homework is worth doing. If it is short, purposeful and fair, many would agree it has a place. If it is endless, pointless or stressful, few would defend it. What kind of homework do you think helps you most?"],"glossary":{"achievement":"Success gained through effort.","feedback":"Comments on how well something has been done and how to improve.","deadlines":"Times or dates by which something must be finished.","independently":"Without help from others.","opponents":"People who disagree with something.","siblings":"Brothers and sisters.","compromises":"Agreements in which each side gives up something.","retrieval":"Getting back information from memory.","purposeful":"Having a clear aim."},"questions":[{"q":"According to research, how much extra progress can homework give secondary pupils on average?","o":["About five months","About five years","About five days","None"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Around five months of extra progress.”"},{"q":"Which life skills do supporters say homework builds?","o":["Cooking and cleaning","Organising time, meeting deadlines and working independently","Playing sport","Drawing"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Organising your time, meeting deadlines and working independently.”"},{"q":"Why do critics say homework can be unfair?","o":["Some pupils have better homes, equipment and help than others","Teachers mark unfairly","Some pupils are cleverer","Homework is too easy"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Pupils with quiet rooms and laptops have an advantage over others."},{"q":"How can homework clubs help solve the problem of unfairness?","o":["They give everyone access to computers and help","They cancel homework","They are only for top pupils","They make homework longer"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Pupils “can use computers and get help, which helps to even out the unfairness.”"},{"q":"How is this text organised?","o":["Arguments for, arguments against, compromises, then a question to the reader","A story about one pupil","Only arguments against","A list of homework tasks"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It follows the typical structure of a balanced discussion."},{"q":"What is the writer’s suggestion in the final paragraph?","o":["Homework should be banned","The quality and type of homework matters more than whether it exists","Homework should be doubled","Parents should do homework"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"“The real question is not whether homework should exist, but what kind of homework is worth doing.”"}]},{"id":"y117","title":"Shackleton and the Endurance","kind":"History","theme":"arctic","text":["In 1914, the explorer Ernest Shackleton set out to achieve what no one had ever done: to cross the whole continent of Antarctica on foot, from one coast to the other, by way of the South Pole. A famous advertisement, though historians now doubt it was ever really published, is said to have read: ‘Men wanted for hazardous journey. Low wages, bitter cold, long hours of complete darkness. Safe return doubtful.’ Whether or not it existed, it describes exactly what lay ahead.","Shackleton’s ship, the Endurance, sailed from South Georgia in December 1914 with twenty-eight men on board. In the Weddell Sea, the pack ice closed in around them. By January 1915, the ship was trapped, frozen fast, just a day’s sail from where they had planned to land. For ten months, the Endurance drifted with the ice. The men played football on the frozen sea, trained the sledge dogs and kept up their spirits through the long polar winter. Then, slowly, the pressure of the ice began to crush the ship. Her timbers groaned and splintered. In October 1915 they abandoned her, and in November she sank.","Shackleton changed his goal. There would be no crossing of Antarctica now. The only aim was to bring every man home alive.","The crew camped on the ice for months, hauling three lifeboats and supplies, until the ice began to break up. In April 1916 they launched the boats and sailed for seven days through freezing seas to Elephant Island, a bare, rocky, storm-battered place. It was the first time they had stood on solid ground in 497 days. But nobody would ever look for them there.","So Shackleton made an extraordinary decision. With five men, he set out in one of the small lifeboats, the James Caird, to reach South Georgia, 1,300 kilometres away across the Southern Ocean, one of the stormiest seas on Earth. For sixteen days they battled enormous waves and hurricane-force winds. They chipped ice off the boat to stop it sinking under the weight, and they were constantly soaked and frozen. Their navigator, Frank Worsley, found their way using a sextant, taking readings of the sun on the few occasions it appeared through the clouds. If they had missed the island, they would have been swept into the open ocean and lost.","They reached South Georgia – but on the wrong side. The whaling station where help could be found was on the other coast, across unmapped, ice-covered mountains. Shackleton, Worsley and Tom Crean walked for thirty-six hours without rest, sliding down one icy slope on a coiled rope, and finally staggered into the whaling station at Stromness, so ragged and filthy that the station manager did not recognise them.","It took four attempts, through the ice, to reach the men left on Elephant Island. On 30 August 1916, a Chilean ship, the Yelcho, arrived. Shackleton counted the figures on the shore. All twenty-two were alive.","The expedition had failed in its goal. But it became one of the greatest survival stories ever told. In 2022, the wreck of the Endurance was found on the floor of the Weddell Sea, three kilometres down, remarkably well preserved, with her name still clearly visible on the stern."],"glossary":{"continent":"One of the world’s seven large land masses.","hazardous":"Dangerous.","timbers":"The wooden beams of a ship.","abandoned":"Left behind for ever.","navigator":"The person who plans and directs a ship’s route.","sextant":"An instrument for finding your position by measuring the angle of the sun or stars.","unmapped":"Not yet shown on any map.","staggered":"Walked unsteadily, as if about to fall.","expedition":"A journey made for a special purpose.","stern":"The back part of a ship."},"questions":[{"q":"What was Shackleton’s original goal?","o":["To reach the North Pole","To cross Antarctica on foot via the South Pole","To find a lost ship","To hunt whales"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“To cross the whole continent of Antarctica on foot.”"},{"q":"What happened to the Endurance?","o":["It sailed home safely","It was trapped and crushed by the ice and sank","It hit an iceberg on the first day","It was stolen"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The pressure of the ice crushed it, and “in November she sank.”"},{"q":"How did Shackleton’s goal change?","o":["He wanted to reach the Pole faster","His only aim became bringing every man home alive","He wanted to find gold","He gave up completely"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The only aim was to bring every man home alive.”"},{"q":"Why did Shackleton sail to South Georgia in a small lifeboat?","o":["For fun","Nobody would look for them on Elephant Island, so he had to fetch help","The island was sinking","He wanted to explore it"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“Nobody would ever look for them there,” so he went for help."},{"q":"Why might the station manager not have recognised them?","o":["They were so ragged and filthy after their ordeal","They were wearing masks","It was dark","He had never met them"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"They were “so ragged and filthy” after months of survival."},{"q":"Why is the expedition remembered as a success despite failing its goal?","o":["Because every man survived against incredible odds","Because they reached the Pole","Because the ship was saved","Because they found treasure"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"“All twenty-two were alive” – it became one of the greatest survival stories."}]},{"id":"y118","title":"The New Kid on the Team","kind":"Story","theme":"sport","text":["The Westside Wolves basketball team had been together since they were seven. They had their own handshakes, their own jokes and their own way of playing, which mostly involved passing the ball to Callum, who was the tallest and could score from almost anywhere. So when Coach Mbeki walked into the sports hall one Tuesday with a girl nobody had seen before, there was an awkward silence.","“This is Aleksandra,” said Coach. “She’s just moved from Poland. She’ll be training with us.”","Aleksandra was small – a head shorter than Callum – with a long plait and a very serious expression. She nodded at them and said nothing. Someone at the back muttered, “Great. Another sub.”","For the first few sessions, nobody passed to her. It was not exactly deliberate. They just did not think of it. The ball went to Callum, as it always had, and Aleksandra ran up and down the court, always in space, always with her hands up, and always ignored. Zain, who played point guard, noticed. He felt bad about it, but not quite bad enough to do anything.","Then, three weeks before the league final, Callum broke his wrist falling off his bike.","The team was devastated. Without Callum, they had no plan. At the next training session, they stood around looking at each other until Coach blew her whistle and split them into two teams for a practice match. Zain found himself on Aleksandra’s side.","Halfway through, out of desperation more than anything, he passed to her.","She caught it at the three-point line, turned and shot in one fluid movement. The ball dropped through the net without touching the rim. Swish.","Nobody said anything. Zain passed to her again. Swish. And again. By the end of the session, she had scored eighteen points, and the boys on the other team were staring at her as if she had grown wings.","Afterwards, in the changing area, Zain finally asked her. “Where did you learn to shoot like that?”","Aleksandra shrugged. For the first time, she smiled. “My brother played for the national youth team. I practised against him every day. He is very tall. I learned to shoot from far away.” She hesitated, then added, “You never passed. I thought maybe you did not want me.”","Zain felt his face go hot. “We didn’t think,” he said. “That’s worse, isn’t it?”","“A little bit,” said Aleksandra. But she was still smiling.","The Wolves did not win the league final. They lost by two points to a team from the other side of the city. But they played the best basketball they had ever played, moving the ball, finding the open player, trusting each other. Aleksandra scored twenty-two points. Callum cheered from the bench with his arm in a cast, louder than anyone.","Afterwards, the team taught Aleksandra their handshake. It took her three tries to learn it. Then she taught them a new one."],"glossary":{"awkward":"Uncomfortable or embarrassing.","deliberate":"Done on purpose.","devastated":"Extremely upset.","desperation":"A feeling of having little hope, leading to risky action.","fluid":"Smooth and flowing.","rim":"The circular edge of a basketball hoop."},"questions":[{"q":"How did the Wolves usually play?","o":["By passing to everyone equally","By mostly passing the ball to Callum","By defending only","By shooting from far away"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Their play “mostly involved passing the ball to Callum.”"},{"q":"Why did nobody pass to Aleksandra at first?","o":["She was injured","They did not think of her, out of habit, rather than on purpose","Coach told them not to","She asked them not to"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“It was not exactly deliberate. They just did not think of it.”"},{"q":"What does Zain’s reaction to the situation show?","o":["He noticed it was unfair but did not act at first","He did not care at all","He was the one who ignored her most","He told the coach immediately"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"He “felt bad about it, but not quite bad enough to do anything.”"},{"q":"How does the writer show how good Aleksandra’s shooting is?","o":["By repeating the sound “Swish” and showing everyone staring","By describing her clothes","By giving her a long speech","By saying she missed"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Repeated ‘Swish’ and the shocked reactions emphasise her skill."},{"q":"What does Zain mean when he asks, “That’s worse, isn’t it?”","o":["Not even thinking about including someone is worse than deliberately excluding them","Losing is worse than winning","Basketball is worse than football","Being tall is worse"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"He realises that ignoring her without thought was its own kind of unkindness."},{"q":"What does the ending with the handshakes show?","o":["Aleksandra has become a real part of the team, and they learn from each other","Aleksandra is leaving","The team is breaking up","They are learning a dance"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"They teach her their handshake, and she teaches them a new one – shared belonging."}]},{"id":"y119","title":"Fungi: The Hidden Kingdom","kind":"Science","theme":"forest","text":["Walk through a wood in autumn, and you may see mushrooms pushing up through the leaf litter: red ones with white spots, delicate grey ones, frilly orange ones clinging to tree trunks. It is easy to think of them as a kind of plant. They are not. Fungi belong to a kingdom of their own, and in some ways they are more closely related to animals than to plants.","Unlike plants, fungi cannot make their own food from sunlight. Like animals, they have to get their energy from other living things – or from dead ones. Most fungi feed by releasing digestive chemicals into whatever they are growing on, breaking it down, and then absorbing the nutrients. The mushrooms we see are only the fruiting bodies, rather like the apples on an apple tree. The main part of the fungus is hidden underground or inside wood: a vast network of thread-like strands called hyphae, together known as a mycelium.","A mycelium can be enormous. In the Blue Mountains of Oregon, in the United States, a single honey fungus is thought to cover around nine square kilometres and may be thousands of years old, which makes it one of the largest living things on Earth.","Fungi are nature’s recyclers. Without them, the world would be buried under dead leaves, fallen trees and animal remains. Fungi break down tough materials such as wood, returning nutrients to the soil, where they can be used by new plants.","Many fungi also form partnerships with plants. Around ninety per cent of land plants have fungi living in or around their roots. The fungal threads spread far through the soil, much further than the roots could reach, and pass water and minerals such as phosphorus to the plant. In return, the plant gives the fungus sugars it has made through photosynthesis. Some scientists believe that fungal networks can even link trees together, allowing them to share resources – an idea sometimes called the ‘wood wide web’, although how much trees really share in this way is still debated.","Fungi matter to people, too. Yeast, a tiny single-celled fungus, makes bread rise and is used to brew beer. Fungi give blue cheese its veins and flavour. In 1928, Alexander Fleming noticed that a mould called Penicillium had killed the bacteria growing on a dish in his laboratory. This led to penicillin, the first antibiotic, which has saved millions of lives.","But fungi can be dangerous. Some cause diseases in crops, trees and people, such as athlete’s foot. And some mushrooms are deadly poisonous. The death cap, which grows in Britain, looks fairly ordinary but is responsible for most mushroom-poisoning deaths around the world. Never pick and eat wild mushrooms unless an expert has identified them.","Scientists think there may be millions of species of fungi, and fewer than ten per cent have been named. The hidden kingdom beneath our feet still has many secrets."],"glossary":{"delicate":"Fine, fragile and easily damaged.","digestive":"Helping to break down food.","absorbing":"Taking in or soaking up.","nutrients":"Substances that living things need to grow.","hyphae":"The thread-like strands that make up a fungus.","mycelium":"The network of threads that forms the main body of a fungus.","phosphorus":"A mineral that plants need to grow.","antibiotic":"A medicine that kills bacteria."},"questions":[{"q":"Why are fungi not plants?","o":["They are always red","They cannot make their own food from sunlight","They grow too fast","They live underwater"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Unlike plants, fungi cannot make their own food from sunlight.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer compare mushrooms to apples on an apple tree?","o":["To show that mushrooms are just the fruiting part of a much bigger organism","Because mushrooms taste like apples","Because they grow on trees","To show they are red"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"The main fungus is the hidden mycelium, like the tree behind the fruit."},{"q":"Why are fungi called “nature’s recyclers”?","o":["They collect plastic","They break down dead material and return nutrients to the soil","They clean rivers","They make paper"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"They break down dead leaves and wood, returning nutrients for new plants."},{"q":"What do plants and fungi exchange in their partnership?","o":["The fungus gives water and minerals; the plant gives sugars","They exchange seeds","They exchange sunlight","They exchange insects"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Fungi pass water and minerals; plants give sugars from photosynthesis."},{"q":"How did a fungus help create the first antibiotic?","o":["Fleming noticed a mould had killed bacteria, leading to penicillin","Yeast was used to make medicine","Mushrooms were eaten to cure illness","Blue cheese was given to patients"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Fleming noticed Penicillium killing bacteria, which led to penicillin."},{"q":"Why does the writer warn about the death cap mushroom?","o":["It is very rare","It looks ordinary but is deadly poisonous","It tastes bad","It only grows abroad"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It “looks fairly ordinary but is responsible for most mushroom-poisoning deaths.”"}]},{"id":"y120","title":"Letter from the Trenches","kind":"Letter","theme":"poppy","text":["Somewhere in France\n3rd July 1916","Dear Mother,","I am writing this by candlelight in a dugout, which is a sort of cave in the side of the trench, about the size of our coal shed at home and not much cleaner. I am quite well, so please do not worry. I know you will have read about the big push in the newspapers. I cannot tell you where we are or what we are doing, because all our letters are read by the officers before they are sent, and they would only cross it out with a thick blue pencil. But I can tell you that I am safe, and that I think of you all every day.","The noise here is something I cannot describe. For a week before the attack, our guns fired day and night without stopping. You could feel it through your boots and in your teeth. Some of the lads stuffed cotton wool in their ears, but it made no difference. We were told the shelling would cut the German wire and that the enemy would all be gone by the time we went over. I will not say more than that, except that it was not quite so.","I have to tell you that Billy Thompson from Mill Lane is gone. He was next to me in the line. Please tell his mother, gently, that he did not suffer and that he was very brave. I do not know if that is true, but I think she should hear it. Billy used to talk about her apple pies. He said nobody in the world made pastry like his mum.","Do not think it is all horror, though. There is a lot of waiting, and a lot of mud, and a lot of tea. We play cards and write letters and sing songs to keep our spirits up. Our sergeant, a big Scotsman called McLeod, has a voice like a church organ and knows every music-hall song ever written. Yesterday a skylark was singing over no man’s land, high up in the blue, as if nothing was happening at all. Everyone stopped to listen. Funny how a little bird can make you homesick.","Thank you for the parcel. The socks are a godsend; my feet have not been dry for a fortnight, and some of the men have trench foot. The fruit cake was shared out among the whole section and pronounced the best in France. Please could you send some more writing paper, a tin of toffees for McLeod (he has a sweet tooth), and some candles if you can spare them?","Give my love to Dad and to little Annie. Tell her I have kept the drawing she sent me in my top pocket, and that the horse looks very fine, though perhaps its legs are a little long. Tell her I will be home for her birthday, if I can.","Your loving son,\nTom","This letter is imagined, but it is based on the thousands of letters written by soldiers during the First World War. The Battle of the Somme, which began on 1 July 1916, was one of the bloodiest battles in history. On its first day alone, the British army suffered around 57,000 casualties, including more than 19,000 killed. Letters home were censored, and many soldiers tried to protect their families from the truth."],"glossary":{"dugout":"A shelter dug into the ground or into the side of a trench.","lads":"Young men or boys.","shelling":"Firing explosive shells from big guns.","sergeant":"A soldier of middle rank who is in charge of a group of soldiers.","godsend":"Something very helpful that arrives just when needed.","fortnight":"Two weeks.","casualties":"People killed or injured.","censored":"Checked and had parts removed so certain information could not be shared."},"questions":[{"q":"Why can Tom not tell his mother where he is?","o":["He does not know","Letters are read by officers, who cross out secret information","He has forgotten","His pen ran out"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“All our letters are read by the officers … they would only cross it out.”"},{"q":"What does “it was not quite so” suggest about the attack?","o":["It went exactly as planned","The shelling had not worked and the attack went badly","Nobody fought","It was cancelled"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"His understatement hints that the wire was not cut and the enemy was not gone."},{"q":"Why does Tom ask his mother to tell Billy’s mother that he did not suffer, even though he is not sure it is true?","o":["To protect her from more pain","Because he is lying about everything","Because Billy asked him to","Because the officers told him to"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"He wants to comfort a grieving mother: “I think she should hear it.”"},{"q":"Why does the skylark make the soldiers homesick?","o":["Its song reminds them of peaceful life at home, in contrast to the war","It is very loud","It steals their food","It is hurt"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The bird sings “as if nothing was happening at all,” reminding them of normal life."},{"q":"What tone does Tom use in most of the letter?","o":["Cheerful and reassuring, even when describing hard things","Furious","Boastful","Completely hopeless"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"He reassures his mother, mentions cards and songs, and makes gentle jokes about Annie’s drawing."},{"q":"What does the final paragraph add to the reader’s understanding of the letter?","o":["The real historical context and why soldiers often hid the truth","A recipe for fruit cake","Tom’s address","The ending of the war"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It explains the Battle of the Somme and that letters were censored and softened."}]},{"id":"y121","title":"Night Shift","kind":"Poem","theme":"city","text":["While the city sleeps,\nsomeone is awake for it.","The baker, floured to the elbows,\nfolding dough in a yellow room,\nso there will be bread\nbefore there is light.","The nurse on Ward Nine,\nsoft shoes on a shining floor,\nchecking a drip, a pulse, a breath,\nholding a stranger’s hand\nuntil it stops shaking.","The driver of the night bus,\nthe only lit thing on the empty road,\ncarrying three tired people home\nlike a lantern\ncarried through a forest.","The cleaner in the tower block,\nhoovering offices\nwhere nobody will ever know her name,\nleaving each desk\na little better than she found it.","The man who mends the railway,\norange coat, head-torch,\nhammering in the cold\nso that the morning train\nwill not even notice he was there.","And at the end of it all,\nas the sky turns grey, then pink, then gold,\nthey go home\npast the rest of us, waking,\nwho never say thank you\nbecause we never knew."],"glossary":{"floured":"Covered in flour.","pulse":"The regular beat of the heart, felt in the wrist or neck.","drip":"A medical tube that slowly gives liquid to a patient.","lantern":"A lamp with a protective case.","hoovering":"Vacuum cleaning."},"questions":[{"q":"What is the main idea of the first two lines?","o":["The city is dangerous at night","While most people sleep, some people are working to look after the city","Everyone in the city is awake","The city never sleeps"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"“While the city sleeps, / someone is awake for it.”"},{"q":"Why does the baker work before dawn?","o":["So there will be bread ready by morning","Because the oven is cheaper at night","Because the bakery is closed in the day","To avoid customers"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“So there will be bread / before there is light.”"},{"q":"The night bus is compared to “a lantern / carried through a forest”. What does this suggest?","o":["The bus is old-fashioned","The bus is a small, comforting light moving through the dark, empty city","The bus is lost","The bus is on fire"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Like a lantern, it is the only light, guiding and protecting people in the dark."},{"q":"What does the stanza about the cleaner suggest about her work?","o":["It is noticed and praised","It is important but unseen and unrecognised","It is done badly","She works in a hospital"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“Nobody will ever know her name,” yet she leaves each desk better."},{"q":"How is the poem structured?","o":["Each stanza describes a different night worker, then the final stanza reflects on all of them","It tells one story in order","It is a list of shops","Each stanza repeats the first"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"Stanzas focus on the baker, nurse, driver, cleaner and railway worker before a closing reflection."},{"q":"What is the effect of the last line, “because we never knew”?","o":["It blames the workers","It makes the reader realise how much unseen work supports their lives","It shows the workers are secret agents","It makes the poem funny"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"It suggests we fail to thank these workers only because we are unaware of them."}]},{"id":"y122","title":"Leonardo da Vinci: The Restless Genius","kind":"Biography","theme":"clockwork","text":["Leonardo da Vinci painted the most famous portrait in the world. He also designed flying machines, tanks, diving suits and a robot knight; studied the flow of rivers and the flight of birds; dissected human bodies to understand how they worked; and filled more than 7,000 surviving pages of notebooks with drawings, ideas and questions. He is often called the ultimate ‘Renaissance man’ – a person brilliant at many different things.","Leonardo was born in 1452 near the small town of Vinci, in Tuscany, Italy. His parents were not married, which meant that he could not go to university or follow his father’s profession as a notary. Instead, at about fourteen, he was apprenticed to the workshop of Andrea del Verrocchio, a leading artist in Florence. There he learned painting, sculpture, metalwork and engineering. According to an old story, when Leonardo painted an angel in one of Verrocchio’s pictures, it was so much better than his master’s work that Verrocchio decided never to paint again.","Leonardo was endlessly curious. His notebooks contain lists of things he wanted to find out: describe the tongue of the woodpecker; why the sky is blue; how to square a circle. He wrote in mirror writing, from right to left, perhaps because he was left-handed and this stopped the ink smudging. He studied everything by looking closely. To paint the human body accurately, he dissected around thirty corpses, making detailed drawings of muscles, bones and organs that were far more accurate than anything else at the time. His drawings of the heart showed how its valves worked, centuries before scientists fully understood them.","His inventions were astonishingly ahead of their time. He sketched a helicopter-like ‘aerial screw’, a parachute, a self-propelled cart and machines for lifting water. Most were never built, and some would not have worked with the materials of his day. But in 2000, a British skydiver built Leonardo’s parachute design from wood and canvas and jumped with it. It worked.","Leonardo’s paintings are few – fewer than twenty are definitely by him – partly because he was a perfectionist who often left work unfinished. The Last Supper, painted on the wall of a monastery in Milan, shows Jesus at the moment he tells his disciples that one of them will betray him, and captures each man’s reaction. Sadly, Leonardo experimented with a new painting technique that did not last, and the painting began to flake within his lifetime.","His most famous work, the Mona Lisa, is a portrait of a Florentine woman, probably Lisa Gherardini. Leonardo worked on it for many years and never gave it to the person who ordered it. He used a technique called sfumato, softly blending colours without sharp outlines, which makes her famous smile seem to change as you look at it.","Leonardo spent his final years in France, as a guest of King Francis I, and died there in 1519. Today the Mona Lisa hangs in the Louvre in Paris behind bulletproof glass, and millions of people queue to see her every year. But perhaps Leonardo’s greatest legacy is his notebooks – the record of a mind that never stopped asking questions."],"glossary":{"dissected":"Cut open a body carefully to study it.","ultimate":"The best or most extreme example.","notary":"An official who prepares and checks legal documents.","apprenticed":"Sent to learn a skilled job from an expert.","corpses":"Dead bodies.","perfectionist":"A person who wants everything to be perfect.","disciples":"Followers of a teacher, especially of Jesus.","sfumato":"A painting technique of soft, blended edges with no sharp lines.","legacy":"Something left behind for future generations."},"questions":[{"q":"Why could Leonardo not go to university?","o":["He was too young","His parents were not married","He failed his exams","He lived too far away"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“His parents were not married, which meant that he could not go to university.”"},{"q":"What does the story about Verrocchio and the angel suggest?","o":["Leonardo was lazy","Leonardo’s talent was extraordinary, even as a young apprentice","Verrocchio was a bad teacher","Angels are hard to paint"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"His angel was so good his master supposedly gave up painting."},{"q":"Why might Leonardo have used mirror writing?","o":["To keep secrets from spies","Because he was left-handed and it stopped the ink smudging","Because it was the fashion","Because he could not read"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Perhaps because he was left-handed and this stopped the ink smudging.”"},{"q":"What does the 2000 parachute jump prove?","o":["Leonardo’s design actually worked","Parachutes were invented in 2000","Leonardo was a skydiver","Wooden parachutes are dangerous"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"A skydiver built and used the design, and “It worked.”"},{"q":"Why are there so few paintings by Leonardo?","o":["Most were stolen","He was a perfectionist who often left work unfinished","He hated painting","They were all burned"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“He was a perfectionist who often left work unfinished.”"},{"q":"According to the writer, what might be Leonardo’s greatest legacy?","o":["The Mona Lisa","His notebooks, showing a mind that never stopped asking questions","His parachute","His house in France"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"“Perhaps Leonardo’s greatest legacy is his notebooks.”"}]},{"id":"y123","title":"Tsunami: When the Sea Rises","kind":"Science","theme":"sea","text":["On the morning of 26 December 2004, tourists on the beaches of Thailand noticed something strange. The sea was going out – far, far further than any normal low tide. Fish flapped on the exposed sand. Boats lay tilted on the seabed. Curious, many people walked out to look. They did not know that, under the Indian Ocean, a huge earthquake had just taken place, and that the sea was gathering itself for an enormous wave.","A tsunami is a series of giant waves, usually caused by an earthquake beneath the sea. When part of the sea floor suddenly jerks upwards or downwards along a fault, it pushes up the entire column of water above it, sometimes several kilometres deep. This water spreads outwards in all directions as waves. Tsunamis can also be caused by underwater landslides, volcanic eruptions and, very rarely, by meteorites hitting the ocean.","In the open ocean, a tsunami is almost invisible. Its waves may be less than a metre high, but they stretch hundreds of kilometres from one crest to the next, and they travel incredibly fast – up to 800 kilometres per hour, as fast as a jet aircraft. Ships at sea may not notice them passing underneath.","Everything changes as the waves approach land. As the sea becomes shallower, the front of the wave slows down, and the water behind it piles up. The wave grows taller and taller, sometimes reaching ten, twenty or even thirty metres. Unlike an ordinary wave, which breaks and rolls back, a tsunami is more like a sudden, fast-rising flood, a wall of water that keeps coming for many minutes, sweeping far inland and carrying cars, trees and buildings with it. Often the first sign is that the sea draws back dramatically, as the trough of the wave arrives first. This is nature’s warning.","The 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami was triggered by an earthquake of magnitude 9.1 off the coast of Sumatra, Indonesia – one of the most powerful ever recorded. Its waves reached fourteen countries, some thousands of kilometres away, and killed around 230,000 people. At the time, there was no warning system in the Indian Ocean.","One life-saving story became famous. A ten-year-old British girl, Tilly Smith, was on holiday with her family on a beach in Thailand. Two weeks earlier, in a geography lesson at school, she had learned about tsunamis. When she saw the sea draw back and start to froth, she recognised the signs and told her parents. They warned the people on the beach and hotel staff, and around a hundred people were evacuated before the waves arrived. Nobody on that beach was killed.","Since 2004, a tsunami warning system has been built in the Indian Ocean, using seismometers and deep-ocean sensors that detect changes in sea pressure. Warnings can now reach coastal communities within minutes. But experts say the most important protection is knowledge: if you are at the coast and feel a strong earthquake, or see the sea suddenly go out, do not wait for an official warning. Move inland or to high ground immediately."],"glossary":{"exposed":"Uncovered and open to view.","fault":"A crack in the Earth’s crust where rocks move.","meteorites":"Rocks from space that reach the Earth’s surface.","crest":"The top of a wave.","trough":"The lowest part between two waves.","dramatically":"In a sudden and striking way.","magnitude":"The size or strength of an earthquake.","evacuated":"Moved away from a dangerous place to safety.","seismometers":"Instruments that measure earthquakes."},"questions":[{"q":"What usually causes a tsunami?","o":["Strong winds","An earthquake beneath the sea","The Moon’s gravity","Heavy rain"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It is “usually caused by an earthquake beneath the sea.”"},{"q":"Why might a ship at sea not notice a tsunami?","o":["Ships are too fast","In the open ocean its waves may be less than a metre high","Tsunamis only happen at night","Ships float above them in the air"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Its waves may be less than a metre high” in the open ocean."},{"q":"Why does a tsunami grow taller near the coast?","o":["The wind pushes it","The front slows in shallow water and the water behind piles up","The Moon pulls it","Rivers add water to it"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The front of the wave slows down, and the water behind it piles up.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer call the sea drawing back “nature’s warning”?","o":["It is a sign that a tsunami wave is about to arrive","It means the tide is low","It means fishing is good","It is a sign of good weather"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The trough arrives first, signalling the wave is coming."},{"q":"Why is Tilly Smith’s story included?","o":["To show how knowledge from a school lesson saved lives","To describe a holiday","To criticise hotels","To explain earthquakes"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Her lesson helped her recognise the signs and save about a hundred people."},{"q":"What is the most important safety advice in the final paragraph?","o":["Wait for an official warning","Walk out to look at the sea","If you feel a strong earthquake or see the sea go out, move inland or to high ground immediately","Get in a boat"],"a":2,"skill":"Summary","why":"“Do not wait for an official warning. Move inland or to high ground immediately.”"}]},{"id":"y124","title":"Remember, Remember: The Gunpowder Plot","kind":"History","theme":"castle","text":["Every year on 5 November, fireworks light up the skies of Britain, bonfires blaze and, in some places, a stuffed figure called a ‘guy’ is burned on the flames. Many people enjoy the celebrations without thinking much about why they happen. They commemorate a plot, more than four hundred years ago, to blow up the King and Parliament.","In 1603, James VI of Scotland became James I of England. England was a Protestant country, and Catholics were heavily fined for not attending Protestant church services and could be punished for practising their faith. Many Catholics had hoped James would be more tolerant, but he was not. A small group of Catholic men, led by a gentleman named Robert Catesby, decided on a desperate solution: they would kill the King and replace him with a Catholic monarch.","Their plan was to blow up the Houses of Parliament during the State Opening, when the King, his family, lords and members of Parliament would all be gathered in one place. The conspirators rented a cellar directly beneath the House of Lords and secretly filled it with thirty-six barrels of gunpowder – enough to destroy the building completely. The man chosen to guard the gunpowder and light the fuse was Guy Fawkes, a Catholic soldier who had fought in the Spanish army and had experience with explosives.","But the plot was discovered. About ten days before the State Opening, a Catholic lord named Lord Monteagle received an anonymous letter warning him not to attend Parliament, because ‘they shall receive a terrible blow this parliament and yet they shall not see who hurts them’. Nobody knows for certain who wrote it. Monteagle passed the letter to the government. Shortly before midnight on 4 November 1605, the cellar was searched. Guy Fawkes was found there, with matches and a lantern, beside the barrels. He was arrested at once.","Fawkes was taken to the Tower of London and, with the King’s permission, tortured until he revealed the names of the other plotters. Some of them fled to the Midlands, where they were surrounded at Holbeche House in Staffordshire; Catesby and several others were killed in the fighting. Fawkes and the surviving conspirators were tried and sentenced to death in January 1606.","Londoners celebrated the King’s survival by lighting bonfires, and soon afterwards Parliament passed an act making 5 November a day of thanksgiving. The tradition has continued ever since, although its meaning has changed over the centuries. For many people today, Bonfire Night is simply a chance to enjoy fireworks with family and friends.","To this day, before the State Opening of Parliament, the King’s bodyguards, the Yeomen of the Guard, ceremonially search the cellars of the Palace of Westminster with lanterns. They have never found any more gunpowder."],"glossary":{"commemorate":"To remember an important event, often with a ceremony.","tolerant":"Willing to accept beliefs different from your own.","monarch":"A king or queen.","conspirators":"People who secretly plan to do something harmful or illegal.","anonymous":"Without a name being given.","revealed":"Made known.","sentenced":"Officially given a punishment by a court.","ceremonially":"As part of a formal tradition."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did some Catholics want to kill King James I?","o":["He was not tolerant of Catholics and they were punished for their faith","He raised taxes on fireworks","He was Scottish","He refused to visit Parliament"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Catholics had hoped he would be more tolerant, “but he was not.”"},{"q":"Why did the plotters choose the State Opening of Parliament?","o":["The King, his family, lords and MPs would all be in one place","It was Guy Fawkes’s birthday","It was dark","Parliament was empty"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Everyone they wanted to kill “would all be gathered in one place.”"},{"q":"What was Guy Fawkes’s role in the plot?","o":["He led the plot","He guarded the gunpowder and was to light the fuse","He wrote the warning letter","He was the King’s guard"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He was chosen “to guard the gunpowder and light the fuse.”"},{"q":"How was the plot discovered?","o":["Guy Fawkes confessed","An anonymous warning letter to Lord Monteagle was passed to the government","The gunpowder exploded early","A dog found it"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Monteagle received a letter and “passed the letter to the government.”"},{"q":"How has the meaning of Bonfire Night changed?","o":["It has not changed","From a day of thanksgiving for the King’s survival to mainly a time to enjoy fireworks","It now celebrates Guy Fawkes","It is now a religious festival"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"It began as thanksgiving; “for many people today” it is a chance to enjoy fireworks."},{"q":"What is the tone of the final sentence?","o":["Gently humorous","Terrified","Angry","Confused"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"“They have never found any more gunpowder” adds a light, wry touch."}]},{"id":"y125","title":"The Lost Expedition","kind":"Adventure","theme":"jungle","text":["The plane had been flying low over the rainforest for two hours when the engine coughed, spluttered and stopped.","Kesi was the first to notice the silence. She had been staring out of the window at the endless green canopy, which looked from above like a sea of broccoli, when the steady drone of the engine simply vanished. The pilot, a weathered Brazilian called Duarte, swore quietly and began flicking switches. Professor Hale, Kesi’s mother, gripped the edge of her seat. Kesi’s cousin, Ben, who had been asleep, woke up and asked what was going on, which seemed obvious.","Duarte glided the little plane towards a pale strip ahead – a river, wide and brown, curling between the trees. “Hold on,” he said. The plane skimmed the water, bounced once, twice, and slid to a stop against a muddy bank, its nose buried in the reeds.","Nobody was hurt. That was the first piece of luck. The second was that the radio still worked – for exactly long enough to send their position before the battery died. The third piece of luck took longer to discover.","They waited by the plane that night, listening to the forest. Kesi had never heard anything like it. Frogs creaked and whistled. Insects shrilled in a thousand voices. Something large crashed through the undergrowth and then went quiet. Above them, through a gap in the trees, the stars were so thick they looked like spilled sugar.","In the morning, Duarte said they should stay with the plane. “Rescuers will look for the plane,” he said. “Not for four people wandering in the forest.” He showed them how to collect rainwater in leaves and how to check their boots for scorpions. Ben was not impressed. He wanted to explore.","On the second afternoon, he did. Kesi followed him, furious, and they argued all the way along the riverbank until they came round a bend and both stopped dead.","In front of them, half hidden by vines, was a stone face. It was taller than a house, carved into a cliff above the water, its eyes wide, its mouth open as if in a shout. Moss grew in its eyebrows. Beneath it, stone steps led down into the river.","“Mum,” said Kesi, when they had run back. “You need to see something.”","Professor Hale was an archaeologist. She had spent twenty years searching for evidence of ancient cities in the Amazon, which many people had told her did not exist. She stood in front of the stone face for a long time without speaking. When she finally did, her voice shook.","“Do you know,” she said, “I think the plane crashing may be the best thing that has ever happened to me.”","The rescue helicopter found them three days later. By then, Professor Hale had filled two notebooks with sketches, and Ben had stopped complaining about anything at all. Kesi took one last photograph of the stone face as they lifted off, watching it shrink into the green until the forest swallowed it again, as it had for a thousand years."],"glossary":{"canopy":"The thick upper layer of leaves in a forest.","drone":"A low, continuous humming sound.","weathered":"Worn and roughened by sun, wind and rain.","skimmed":"Moved quickly just above or touching a surface.","shrilled":"Made a high-pitched sound.","undergrowth":"Bushes and plants growing beneath trees.","scorpions":"Small creatures with pincers and a poisonous sting in their tail.","archaeologist":"A person who studies the past by digging up remains."},"questions":[{"q":"How does the writer describe the rainforest seen from the plane?","o":["Like a sea of broccoli","Like a desert","Like a city","Like a snowfield"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"The canopy “looked from above like a sea of broccoli.”"},{"q":"Why was the radio working so important?","o":["They could listen to music","They could send their position before the battery died","They could call home","They could find the stone face"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It worked “long enough to send their position.”"},{"q":"Why did Duarte say they should stay with the plane?","o":["He was tired","Rescuers would search for the plane, not people wandering in the forest","The forest was full of tigers","He wanted to fix it"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Rescuers will look for the plane … Not for four people wandering.”"},{"q":"What was “the third piece of luck” that took longer to discover?","o":["Finding the stone face","The plane being fixed","Seeing the stars","Finding food"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The ancient carving they found turned the crash into a discovery."},{"q":"Why was the discovery so important to Professor Hale?","o":["It was evidence of ancient cities in the Amazon that people said did not exist","She liked statues","She wanted to sell it","It showed her the way home"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"She had spent twenty years searching for such evidence."},{"q":"What does the final sentence suggest about the stone face?","o":["It had been hidden by the forest for centuries and would be hidden again","It was destroyed","It was moved to a museum","It was not real"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"The forest “swallowed it again, as it had for a thousand years.”"}]},{"id":"y126","title":"How Does the Internet Work?","kind":"Science","theme":"computer","text":["When you watch a video, send a message or search for something online, you are using a global network that connects billions of devices. The internet feels invisible and instant, like magic. In fact, it is a very physical thing – made of cables, computers and radio waves – and it works by following a few clever rules.","The internet is a network of networks. Your phone or laptop connects to your home router, usually by Wi-Fi, which uses radio waves. The router connects to your internet service provider through cables in the street, which are joined to larger networks, which are joined to even larger ones. Data travels across countries through underground fibre-optic cables, in which information is sent as pulses of light along strands of glass as thin as a human hair. It crosses oceans through more than 500 undersea cables lying on the sea floor. About ninety-nine per cent of international internet traffic travels through these undersea cables, not through satellites.","Every device connected to the internet has an address, called an IP address – a string of numbers, rather like a postal address for computers. Because numbers are hard for people to remember, we use names such as bbc.co.uk instead. When you type a website name, your computer first asks a system called DNS (the Domain Name System), which works like a giant phone book, to look up the correct IP address.","Information on the internet is not sent in one piece. Instead, it is chopped up into small chunks called packets. Each packet is labelled with the address it is going to, the address it came from and its position in the message. The packets are sent off separately and may take completely different routes across the network, passing through machines called routers, which decide which way to send each one, like sorting offices for post. When they arrive, your device puts them back together in the right order. If a packet goes missing, your device asks for it to be sent again. This system is called packet switching, and it makes the internet fast and very reliable: if one route is blocked or broken, packets simply go another way.","The websites and videos you visit are stored on servers – powerful computers kept in huge buildings called data centres, which can contain hundreds of thousands of machines. Data centres use enormous amounts of electricity, much of it to stop the computers overheating, and companies are trying to power them with renewable energy.","The internet grew out of a project called ARPANET, created by the US government in 1969 to link computers at universities. The World Wide Web – the system of web pages and links we use in a browser – was invented separately, in 1989, by a British scientist named Tim Berners-Lee, who was working at CERN, a physics laboratory in Switzerland. He chose not to patent his invention, so that anyone could use it for free.","So the next time a video loads in a few seconds, remember: it may have travelled thousands of kilometres, in hundreds of pieces, as flashes of light under the sea."],"glossary":{"router":"A device that directs data between networks.","provider":"A company that supplies a service.","fibre-optic":"Using thin glass strands to send information as light.","data":"Facts and information, especially stored on computers.","packets":"Small chunks of data sent across a network.","reliable":"Able to be trusted to work well.","servers":"Powerful computers that store and send out websites and data.","renewable":"Able to be replaced naturally, such as wind or solar power.","patent":"To get the legal right to be the only person allowed to make or sell an invention."},"questions":[{"q":"How does most international internet traffic travel?","o":["Through satellites","Through undersea cables","By radio towers","By post"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“About ninety-nine per cent … travels through these undersea cables.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer compare DNS to a phone book?","o":["It looks up a name and gives the matching number (IP address)","It is made of paper","It is very old","It lists phone shops"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Like a phone book, DNS turns a name into a number."},{"q":"What are packets?","o":["Parcels sent by post","Small chunks of data that information is split into","Cables under the sea","Types of computer"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Information “is chopped up into small chunks called packets.”"},{"q":"Why does packet switching make the internet reliable?","o":["Packets can take another route if one is blocked","Packets are very small","Packets never get lost","Packets travel by satellite"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“If one route is blocked or broken, packets simply go another way.”"},{"q":"Who invented the World Wide Web?","o":["The US government","Tim Berners-Lee","A company in Silicon Valley","CERN’s physicists in 1969"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It was invented “in 1989, by a British scientist named Tim Berners-Lee.”"},{"q":"What is surprising about the final sentence?","o":["It shows that something that feels instant actually involves an enormous physical journey","It says videos are slow","It says the internet is magic","It advertises a video"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"It contrasts a few seconds with thousands of kilometres, hundreds of pieces and light under the sea."}]},{"id":"y127","title":"Review: The Tempest at Oakfield Academy","kind":"Review","theme":"stage","text":["School Production · Oakfield Academy Hall · Four stars","Shakespeare’s The Tempest is not an obvious choice for a school play. It is full of magic, storms, spirits, shipwrecks and long speeches in sixteenth-century English. It needs a convincing monster, a flying spirit and a storm that sinks a ship on the stage. Oakfield Academy’s drama department has taken on all of these challenges, and, for the most part, it has triumphed.","The play tells the story of Prospero, a duke who was betrayed by his brother and cast away to a remote island with his young daughter, Miranda. Over twelve years, Prospero has become a powerful magician. When a ship carrying his enemies sails close by, he raises a storm to wreck it and bring them under his power.","That opening storm is the first thing the audience will remember. Instead of trying to build a ship, the director has used the whole cast, dressed in grey and blue, to become the sea. They roll, crash and sway across the stage under flickering blue lights, while sailors are tossed between them. The sound effects – thunder, howling wind, creaking timber – are created live by a group of Year 8 pupils with drums, sheets of metal and their own voices. It is genuinely thrilling.","Year 11 student Daniel Osei is a commanding Prospero. He has clearly worked hard to understand Shakespeare’s language, and he speaks it naturally, as if the words are his own. His final speech, when Prospero gives up his magic and asks the audience to set him free with their applause, was delivered so quietly and movingly that the hall fell completely silent.","The real star, however, is Year 9 pupil Grace Liu as Ariel, Prospero’s spirit servant. Suspended on a rope rig for some scenes, she seems to float above the stage, and her singing is beautiful. Her Ariel is mischievous, quick and longing for freedom, and the moment Prospero finally releases her was one of the most touching of the evening.","Not everything works. The comic scenes with the drunken sailors Stephano and Trinculo go on too long, and some of the jokes were lost because the actors rushed their lines. Some of the younger cast members were difficult to hear at the back of the hall. And the monster Caliban’s costume, covered in green rags and seaweed, was more funny than frightening, which made it harder to feel sorry for him at the end, as Shakespeare intends.","These are small complaints. This is an ambitious, imaginative production performed with obvious enthusiasm and talent. If the cast can perform Shakespeare like this at school, I look forward to seeing what they do next.","The Tempest continues on Thursday and Friday at 7 p.m. Tickets are available from the school office."],"glossary":{"convincing":"Believable.","triumphed":"Achieved a great success.","remote":"Far away from other places.","commanding":"Powerful and impressive, making people pay attention.","applause":"Clapping to show approval.","suspended":"Hanging from above.","mischievous":"Playful and liking to cause small trouble.","ambitious":"Trying to achieve something difficult.","enthusiasm":"Great excitement and interest."},"questions":[{"q":"Why does the reviewer say The Tempest is “not an obvious choice for a school play”?","o":["It is too short","It is full of magic, storms and difficult language that are hard to stage","It is a comedy","It has no main characters"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It is full of magic, storms, spirits and long old-fashioned speeches."},{"q":"How was the opening storm staged?","o":["With a real ship","The cast in grey and blue became the sea, with live sound effects","With a film on a screen","It was cut from the play"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The cast became the sea, with sound effects created live by Year 8 pupils."},{"q":"Which performer does the reviewer call “the real star”?","o":["Daniel Osei as Prospero","Grace Liu as Ariel","The actor playing Caliban","The director"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The real star, however, is Year 9 pupil Grace Liu as Ariel.”"},{"q":"Why was Caliban’s costume a problem?","o":["It fell apart","It was more funny than frightening, making it harder to feel sorry for him","It was too dark","It was too expensive"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Being funny rather than frightening made it harder to feel sympathy at the end."},{"q":"How is the review structured?","o":["Introduction, plot summary, praise, criticisms, overall verdict and practical information","Only criticisms","A list of actors","A diary of rehearsals"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It moves from context to story, praise, criticism, conclusion and ticket details."},{"q":"What is the reviewer’s overall opinion?","o":["The production is a disaster","Despite small flaws, it is an ambitious and impressive production","It should be cancelled","It is too long to enjoy"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"“These are small complaints. This is an ambitious, imaginative production.”"}]},{"id":"y128","title":"Anne Frank: A Voice from Hiding","kind":"Biography","theme":"books","text":["On her thirteenth birthday, 12 June 1942, Anne Frank was given a red-and-white checked autograph book, which she decided to use as a diary. ‘I hope I will be able to confide everything to you, as I have never been able to confide in anyone,’ she wrote on the first page. She could not have known that her diary would one day be read by tens of millions of people around the world.","Anne was born in 1929 in Frankfurt, Germany, to a Jewish family. When Adolf Hitler and the Nazi Party came to power in 1933, they began to persecute Jewish people, and the Frank family moved to Amsterdam in the Netherlands, where Anne’s father, Otto, started a business. For a few years they lived happily. But in 1940, Germany invaded the Netherlands, and the Nazis brought in harsh anti-Jewish laws there too. Jewish people had to wear a yellow star, were banned from parks, cinemas and trams, and Jewish children had to attend separate schools.","In July 1942, Anne’s older sister, Margot, received an order to report to a ‘work camp’ in Germany. The family knew what this might mean. The very next day they went into hiding, in a secret annexe of rooms at the back of Otto’s office building. The entrance was hidden behind a movable bookcase. Soon four more people joined them: the van Pels family and a dentist named Fritz Pfeffer. Eight people lived crammed into the small rooms for more than two years.","Life in hiding was hard. During the day, while the office workers were downstairs, the people in the annexe had to be completely silent: no running water, no flushing the toilet, no walking in shoes. They could never go outside. A few brave helpers, including Otto’s employee Miep Gies, risked their own lives to bring them food, books and news.","Anne wrote constantly. Her diary is lively, funny, honest and sometimes angry. She describes arguments with her mother and with the other residents, her frustration at being treated like a child, her first feelings of love for Peter van Pels, and her dreams of becoming a writer or journalist. In 1944 she heard a radio broadcast asking people to keep diaries and letters as records of the war, and she began rewriting her diary with the hope of publishing it after the war.","On 4 August 1944, the hiding place was discovered, probably after someone betrayed them, though it is still not known who. All eight were arrested and sent to concentration camps. Anne and Margot died of typhus in the Bergen-Belsen camp in early 1945, only weeks before it was liberated. Anne was fifteen. Of the eight people from the annexe, only Otto survived.","After the war, Miep Gies gave Otto the diary pages, which she had rescued from the annexe and kept safe. Otto had the diary published in 1947. Translated into more than seventy languages, it has become one of the most widely read books in the world, helping people understand the Holocaust, in which around six million Jewish people were murdered by the Nazis.","In one of her most famous lines, written in July 1944, Anne wrote: ‘In spite of everything, I still believe that people are really good at heart.’"],"glossary":{"confide":"To tell someone private thoughts or secrets.","persecute":"To treat people cruelly and unfairly because of who they are.","annexe":"An extra part of a building.","crammed":"Squashed tightly together.","residents":"People who live in a place.","broadcast":"A programme sent out on radio or television.","betrayed":"Gave information about someone to an enemy.","liberated":"Set free.","holocaust":"The murder of around six million Jewish people by the Nazis."},"questions":[{"q":"What did Anne receive for her thirteenth birthday?","o":["A camera","An autograph book she used as a diary","A typewriter","A radio"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"She “was given a red-and-white checked autograph book, which she decided to use as a diary.”"},{"q":"Why did the Frank family go into hiding?","o":["They wanted a holiday","Margot had been ordered to report to a ‘work camp’, and they feared what this meant","Their house was flooded","Otto lost his business"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"After Margot’s order, “The very next day they went into hiding.”"},{"q":"Why did the people in the annexe have to stay silent during the day?","o":["They were sleeping","The office workers downstairs might hear them and discover them","They were reading","It was a rule of the building"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Any noise could reveal their hiding place to people below."},{"q":"What does the role of Miep Gies show?","o":["Some people risked their own lives to help others","Everyone supported the Nazis","Helpers were paid a lot","She worked for the Nazis"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"She and others “risked their own lives to bring them food, books and news.”"},{"q":"Why did Anne begin rewriting her diary in 1944?","o":["She lost the original","A radio broadcast asked people to keep records of the war, and she hoped to publish it","Her father told her to","She wanted to burn it"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"She heard the broadcast and began rewriting “with the hope of publishing it.”"},{"q":"Why might Anne’s final quotation be so powerful?","o":["Despite suffering terrible persecution, she still believed in human goodness","It is funny","It describes the annexe","It was written by her father"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Her hope, written while in hiding and facing great danger, is deeply moving."}]},{"id":"y129","title":"The Last Day of Summer","kind":"Story","theme":"garden","text":["On the last day of the summer holidays, Mia and her grandad built a raft.","It was not a very good raft. It was made of four plastic barrels, two old pallets and a great deal of blue rope, and it was meant to carry them across the pond at the bottom of Grandad’s garden to the little island in the middle, where a willow tree grew. Mia had wanted to go there all summer. Grandad had said, every time, “Maybe next week.” Now there were no more weeks left.","They tied the last knot at three o’clock. Grandad stood back, hands on his hips, and looked at it doubtfully. “Well,” he said, “it’s either a raft or a very elaborate way of getting wet.”","It was both. They pushed off from the bank, Mia at the front with a broom for a paddle, Grandad at the back with a garden spade. Halfway across, one of the barrels began to slip out of its rope. The raft tilted. Mia shrieked. Grandad said a word she was not supposed to hear and leaned the other way, and somehow, wobbling and laughing and splashing, they reached the island.","It was smaller than it looked from the house – just a hump of grass, a tangle of brambles and the old willow, its branches trailing in the water like long green hair. But it felt like another country. From here, Grandad’s house looked far away, as if it belonged to somebody else. A moorhen scuttled into the reeds. Dragonflies, blue and green, zipped across the water.","They sat under the willow and ate the sandwiches Grandma had made, which had got slightly squashed and slightly wet. Mia told Grandad she was worried about Year 8: about the new maths teacher everyone said was strict, about whether her friend Zara would still want to sit with her, about the bus. Grandad listened without interrupting, the way he always did. Then he told her about his own first day at secondary school, sixty years ago, when he had fallen into a puddle in his brand-new uniform and had to spend the day in borrowed PE shorts.","“Did everyone laugh?” Mia asked.","“Everyone laughed,” said Grandad. “And by the end of the week, nobody remembered. Except me, apparently.” He smiled. “Things that seem huge at the start of a year usually turn out to be smaller than you think. Like this island.”","The sun was going down by the time they sailed back, the sky pink and gold, the pond glowing like a sheet of copper. The raft lost another barrel just before the bank, and they both fell in, up to their knees, which seemed the right way to end a summer.","That night, Mia laid out her uniform for the morning. She was still a bit worried about the maths teacher. But she kept thinking about the island – how it had seemed so far away for so long, and how, in the end, all it had taken to get there was a bit of rope, a bit of courage, and somebody beside her."],"glossary":{"pallets":"Flat wooden frames used for stacking and moving goods.","elaborate":"Complicated and detailed.","tilted":"Leaned to one side.","brambles":"Prickly bushes such as blackberry bushes.","scuttled":"Ran with short, quick steps.","copper":"A reddish-brown metal."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did Mia and Grandad build the raft on the last day of the holidays?","o":["It was the only sunny day","Grandad had always said ‘Maybe next week’ and now there were no more weeks left","The pond was drying up","Mia had a school project"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Grandad had put it off all summer; now time had run out."},{"q":"What does Grandad’s comment about “a very elaborate way of getting wet” show about him?","o":["He has a good sense of humour","He is angry","He is afraid of water","He thinks the raft is perfect"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"He jokes about the raft’s poor quality."},{"q":"The willow’s branches trail in the water “like long green hair”. What does this simile suggest?","o":["The tree looks soft, flowing and almost alive","The tree is dying","The tree is short","The tree has been cut"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Hair trailing in water suggests long, soft, flowing branches."},{"q":"Why does Grandad tell Mia about his own first day at secondary school?","o":["To show her that embarrassing things are soon forgotten and worries shrink","To make her laugh at him","To show he hated school","To change the subject"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“By the end of the week, nobody remembered.”"},{"q":"How does Grandad link the island to Mia’s worries?","o":["He says worries, like the island, are often smaller than they seem","He says the island is dangerous","He says Year 8 is on an island","He says she should stay on the island"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"“Things that seem huge … usually turn out to be smaller than you think. Like this island.”"},{"q":"What lesson does Mia take from the day?","o":["Rafts are dangerous","With a little courage and someone beside you, hard things can be reached","Never go to school","Islands are boring"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"The final sentence links reaching the island with courage and support."}]},{"id":"y130","title":"Sharks: Misunderstood Hunters","kind":"Non-fiction","theme":"sea","text":["Ask people to name the most frightening animal in the sea, and most will say a shark. Films, books and news headlines have shaped our image of sharks as mindless killing machines that lurk off beaches waiting to attack. The truth is very different – and much more interesting.","Sharks are among the oldest animals on Earth. They were swimming in the oceans around 450 million years ago, long before the first dinosaurs, and even before trees. Today there are more than 500 species, from the dwarf lanternshark, which is about the length of a human hand and glows in the dark, to the whale shark, the largest fish in the world, which can grow to more than eighteen metres long. Despite its enormous size, the whale shark is gentle and feeds mainly on plankton, tiny animals and plants that drift in the sea.","Sharks have extraordinary senses. Many can smell blood in the water from hundreds of metres away. A system of tiny pores on their snouts, called the ampullae of Lorenzini, allows them to detect the weak electrical signals made by the muscles of other animals, so they can find prey hidden in the sand. Sharks also have a line of sensitive cells along their sides, called the lateral line, which detects movements and vibrations in the water. And unlike humans, sharks can replace their teeth throughout their lives; some species may get through thousands of teeth.","Shark attacks on people are extremely rare. Worldwide, there are usually around seventy unprovoked bites each year, and fewer than ten deaths. You are far more likely to be killed by lightning, a dog or even a falling coconut. Most bites are thought to be cases of mistaken identity, when a shark confuses a swimmer or surfer with its normal prey, such as a seal, and many sharks swim away after a single exploratory bite.","In fact, people are a far greater danger to sharks than sharks are to people. Scientists estimate that humans kill around a hundred million sharks every year. Many are caught accidentally in nets set for other fish. Others are killed for their fins, which are used in an expensive soup in some parts of the world. In a practice called finning, some fishermen cut off a shark’s fins and throw the rest of the animal back into the sea, where it cannot swim and dies. Because sharks grow slowly and have few young, their populations take many years to recover, and around a third of shark and ray species are now threatened with extinction.","This matters for the whole ocean. As top predators, sharks help keep ocean ecosystems healthy by controlling the numbers of other animals and removing sick and weak individuals. Where shark numbers have fallen, scientists have seen knock-on effects throughout the food chain.","Around Britain, more than forty species of shark live in our waters, including the basking shark, the second-largest fish in the world, which drifts along the Cornish and Scottish coasts in summer with its huge mouth wide open to filter plankton. It is completely harmless. Perhaps it is time to see sharks not as monsters, but as ancient, remarkable animals that need our protection."],"glossary":{"lurk":"To wait somewhere secretly, especially before attacking.","plankton":"Tiny animals and plants that drift in the sea.","pores":"Tiny openings in skin.","prey":"An animal hunted by another for food.","unprovoked":"Happening without being caused by anything done first.","exploratory":"Done to find out about something.","extinction":"When a species dies out completely.","predators":"Animals that hunt other animals.","ecosystems":"All the living things in an area and how they affect each other."},"questions":[{"q":"How long have sharks existed?","o":["About 450 million years","About 4,500 years","About 45 million years","Only since the dinosaurs died"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Around 450 million years ago.”"},{"q":"What does the whale shark mainly eat?","o":["Seals","People","Plankton","Other sharks"],"a":2,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It “feeds mainly on plankton.”"},{"q":"What do the ampullae of Lorenzini allow sharks to do?","o":["Smell blood","Detect weak electrical signals from other animals’ muscles","See in the dark","Swim faster"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They “detect the weak electrical signals made by the muscles of other animals.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer compare the risk of shark attacks to lightning and falling coconuts?","o":["To show how rare shark attacks really are","To warn people about coconuts","To show lightning hits sharks","To make the article funny only"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"These comparisons show you are more likely to be killed by other unlikely things."},{"q":"Why do shark populations take so long to recover?","o":["They grow slowly and have few young","They live in cold water","They eat too much","They are always fighting"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Because sharks grow slowly and have few young.”"},{"q":"What is the main purpose of the article?","o":["To frighten readers","To challenge the idea that sharks are monsters and show they need protection","To teach fishing","To sell shark-fin soup"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"It ends by asking us to see sharks “not as monsters, but as ancient, remarkable animals that need our protection.”"}]},{"id":"y131","title":"To Autumn (first stanza)","kind":"Poem","theme":"garden","author":"John Keats (1819)","text":["Season of mists and mellow fruitfulness,\nClose bosom-friend of the maturing sun;\nConspiring with him how to load and bless\nWith fruit the vines that round the thatch-eves run;","To bend with apples the moss’d cottage-trees,\nAnd fill all fruit with ripeness to the core;\nTo swell the gourd, and plump the hazel shells\nWith a sweet kernel; to set budding more,","And still more, later flowers for the bees,\nUntil they think warm days will never cease,\nFor summer has o’er-brimm’d their clammy cells."],"glossary":{"mellow":"Soft, ripe and gentle.","fruitfulness":"Producing lots of fruit.","maturing":"Growing older and stronger; here, the late-summer sun.","conspiring":"Secretly planning together.","gourd":"A large fruit with a hard skin, such as a pumpkin or marrow.","kernel":"The soft, edible part inside a nut.","cease":"To stop.","o’er-brimm’d":"Overfilled; filled so full it spills over.","clammy":"Damp and sticky."},"questions":[{"q":"Who or what is the poem speaking to?","o":["The sun","Autumn","The bees","A gardener"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The title is ‘To Autumn’ and the poem addresses the season directly."},{"q":"Autumn is called the “Close bosom-friend of the maturing sun”. What does this suggest?","o":["Autumn and the sun are enemies","Autumn and the sun work closely together, like best friends, to ripen fruit","The sun is getting colder","Autumn is lonely"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Personifying them as close friends shows them cooperating to ripen the harvest."},{"q":"What are autumn and the sun “conspiring” to do?","o":["To bring winter early","To load the vines and trees with ripe fruit","To destroy the garden","To hide from people"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They conspire “how to load and bless / With fruit the vines.”"},{"q":"Which word in the poem best shows abundance or plenty?","o":["“mists”","“o’er-brimm’d”","“cease”","“close”"],"a":1,"skill":"Vocabulary","why":"‘O’er-brimm’d’ means filled so full it overflows."},{"q":"Why do the bees “think warm days will never cease”?","o":["They are confused by the mist","There are so many late flowers and so much honey that it still feels like summer","They are asleep","They have flown south"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Autumn keeps setting “later flowers for the bees,” so they think summer continues."},{"q":"What is the overall mood of this stanza?","o":["Rich, warm and peaceful","Frightening and dark","Angry and violent","Cold and empty"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"Images of ripe fruit, warmth and plenty create a calm, rich mood."}]},{"id":"y132","title":"Stephen Hawking: A Brief History of a Brilliant Mind","kind":"Biography","theme":"space","text":["Stephen Hawking was born in Oxford on 8 January 1942 – exactly three hundred years after the death of the great astronomer Galileo, a coincidence he enjoyed pointing out. At school in St Albans, he was clever but lazy, and his handwriting was famously untidy. His friends nicknamed him ‘Einstein’, though at the time it was more of a joke than a prediction. With them, he built a simple computer out of old clock parts and a telephone switchboard.","He studied physics at Oxford University, where he later admitted he did about an hour’s work a day and spent much of his time rowing and socialising. Even so, he passed with first-class honours and went to Cambridge to research cosmology, the study of the universe as a whole.","In 1963, when he was twenty-one, Hawking began to stumble and slur his words. Doctors diagnosed motor neurone disease, an illness that gradually destroys the nerves that control the muscles. They told him he probably had only two years to live. He was devastated, and for a while he lost interest in his work. But the disease progressed more slowly than expected, and he later said that the diagnosis made him determined to make the most of every day. He married his first wife, Jane, and returned to his research with new energy.","Hawking’s greatest scientific achievements concerned black holes and the beginning of the universe. In 1974, he made an astonishing prediction. Scientists believed that nothing could ever escape from a black hole. Hawking showed, using the strange rules of quantum physics, that black holes should actually give off a tiny amount of radiation, and that, over immense periods of time, they could slowly shrink and disappear. This is now called Hawking radiation. It has not yet been observed directly, but it changed how physicists think about black holes.","As his illness progressed, Hawking lost the ability to walk, then to write, and in 1985, after a serious illness, to speak. He began to communicate using a computer and a speech synthesiser, selecting words on a screen, at first with a hand switch and later by twitching a muscle in his cheek. His electronic voice, with its American accent, became one of the most recognisable in the world. He kept it even when better technology became available, saying it had become his voice.","In 1988, he published A Brief History of Time, a book explaining the universe for ordinary readers. It stayed on the British bestseller list for a record-breaking 237 weeks and sold millions of copies. He also appeared on television programmes, including The Simpsons, and in 2007 he experienced weightlessness on a special aircraft flight, floating freely for the first time in decades.","Stephen Hawking died in 2018, aged seventy-six – more than fifty years after doctors had said he had two years to live. His ashes were placed in Westminster Abbey, between the graves of Isaac Newton and Charles Darwin. He once gave this advice: ‘Remember to look up at the stars and not down at your feet. Try to make sense of what you see, and wonder about what makes the universe exist. Be curious.’"],"glossary":{"coincidence":"Two things happening at the same time by chance.","prediction":"A statement about what will happen in the future.","cosmology":"The study of the universe as a whole.","diagnosed":"Identified an illness after examining someone.","devastated":"Extremely upset.","quantum":"To do with the physics of extremely tiny particles.","radiation":"Energy given off in waves or particles.","synthesiser":"An electronic device that produces sounds or speech.","recognisable":"Easy to identify."},"questions":[{"q":"What coincidence did Hawking enjoy pointing out?","o":["He was born on Christmas Day","He was born exactly 300 years after Galileo’s death","He was born in the same house as Newton","He shared a birthday with Einstein"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He was born “exactly three hundred years after the death of … Galileo.”"},{"q":"What did doctors tell Hawking when he was twenty-one?","o":["He would become famous","He had motor neurone disease and probably only two years to live","He needed glasses","He should stop studying"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They diagnosed motor neurone disease and said “he probably had only two years to live.”"},{"q":"How did the diagnosis eventually change Hawking’s attitude?","o":["It made him give up science","It made him determined to make the most of every day","It made him lazier","It made him leave Cambridge"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He said the diagnosis “made him determined to make the most of every day.”"},{"q":"What is Hawking radiation?","o":["Light from stars","Tiny radiation that black holes should give off, so they can slowly shrink","A disease","A type of telescope"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Black holes “should actually give off a tiny amount of radiation” and slowly shrink."},{"q":"Why did Hawking keep his old electronic voice?","o":["He could not afford a new one","He felt it had become his own voice","It was louder","His doctors insisted"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He kept it, “saying it had become his voice.”"},{"q":"What is the main message of Hawking’s advice at the end?","o":["Watch where you walk","Stay curious and try to understand the universe","Study only maths","Never look at the stars"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"“Try to make sense of what you see … Be curious.”"}]},{"id":"y133","title":"Rivers: From Source to Sea","kind":"Geography","theme":"rain","text":["Every river has a story, and it begins high up, often in the hills or mountains. The place where a river begins is called its source. It might be a spring, where water bubbles up from underground; a lake; a melting glacier; or simply a boggy area where rainwater collects. The River Thames rises in a field near Kemble in Gloucestershire, where in most summers there is no water to be seen at all, only a stone marking the spot.","From its source, a river flows downhill towards the sea, following the easiest path. Along the way, smaller streams called tributaries join it, and the point where they meet is called a confluence. The whole area of land that a river and its tributaries drain is called a drainage basin, and the high ground that separates one basin from the next is the watershed.","Geographers often divide a river’s journey into three parts: the upper course, the middle course and the lower course. In the upper course, the river is usually small and fast, rushing over rocks in a steep, narrow valley shaped like a letter V. Here the main process is erosion – the wearing away of rock – and the river cuts down into its bed. Where a band of hard rock lies on top of softer rock, the soft rock is worn away more quickly, and a waterfall can form. Over time, the waterfall slowly retreats upstream, leaving a steep-sided gorge. High Force in County Durham, where the River Tees plunges twenty-one metres, is one of England’s most dramatic waterfalls.","In the middle course, the land is flatter and the river becomes wider and deeper. Now it begins to wind from side to side in large loops called meanders. On the outside of each bend, the water flows fastest and erodes the bank, creating a small cliff. On the inside, where it is slower, it drops sand and gravel, forming a gentle beach. Over many years, meanders can become so curved that the river cuts straight across the narrow neck of land between them, leaving the old loop behind as a crescent-shaped lake called an oxbow lake.","In the lower course, the river is at its widest and deepest, and it flows across a broad, flat valley floor called a floodplain. When the river floods, it spreads silt across this land, making it very fertile, which is why farmers have long valued floodplains. Finally, the river reaches its mouth, where it meets the sea. Some rivers form an estuary, a wide channel where fresh water and salt water mix and the tide comes in and out. Others drop so much sediment that they build up a fan of new land called a delta, like the great Nile Delta in Egypt.","Rivers have always been vital to people. They provide drinking water, food, transport and power, and many of the world’s great cities, from London to Cairo, grew up beside them. But rivers can also be dangerous. As towns spread across floodplains and heavier rainfall becomes more frequent, flooding has become a growing problem, and engineers and communities are working on ways to give rivers room to flood safely."],"glossary":{"source":"The place where a river begins.","glacier":"A huge slow-moving mass of ice.","tributaries":"Smaller rivers or streams that flow into a larger one.","confluence":"The place where two rivers join.","erosion":"The gradual wearing away of rock or soil.","meanders":"Large bends or loops in a river.","crescent-shaped":"Curved like a new moon.","fertile":"Able to grow lots of healthy plants.","sediment":"Sand, mud and stones carried by water and dropped."},"questions":[{"q":"Where is the source of the River Thames?","o":["In London","In a field near Kemble in Gloucestershire","In Wales","In the sea"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The River Thames rises in a field near Kemble in Gloucestershire.”"},{"q":"What is the main process in a river’s upper course?","o":["Deposition","Erosion","Flooding","Freezing"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Here the main process is erosion.”"},{"q":"How does a waterfall form?","o":["Hard rock lies on softer rock, and the soft rock wears away faster","Humans build it","Rain falls from the sky","The river freezes"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"Where hard rock lies on top of softer rock, the soft rock erodes more quickly."},{"q":"How does an oxbow lake form?","o":["The river cuts across the neck of a meander, leaving the old loop behind","A lake is dug by farmers","A glacier melts","The sea floods the land"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The river “cuts straight across the narrow neck … leaving the old loop behind.”"},{"q":"Why have farmers long valued floodplains?","o":["They are easy to build on","Floods spread fertile silt across the land","They have no rivers","They are high and dry"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Silt spread by floods makes the land “very fertile.”"},{"q":"How is the article organised?","o":["It follows a river’s journey from source to mouth","In alphabetical order","As a list of countries","From newest to oldest rivers"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It moves through the source, upper, middle and lower courses to the mouth."}]},{"id":"y134","title":"Knights and the Code of Chivalry","kind":"History","theme":"castle","text":["In films and fairy tales, medieval knights ride white horses, rescue princesses and slay dragons. The reality was rather different – and in some ways even more remarkable.","A knight was a mounted warrior, trained from childhood to fight in heavy armour on horseback. Becoming a knight took years. A boy from a noble family, usually around the age of seven, would be sent to live in the household of a lord, where he served as a page. He learned manners, how to serve at table, how to ride, and the basics of fighting with wooden swords. At about fourteen, he became a squire, the personal servant of a knight. A squire looked after his master’s horses, weapons and armour, helped him dress for battle and followed him into combat, carrying his shield and spare weapons. If he proved himself, at around twenty-one he could be ‘dubbed’ a knight in a ceremony, in which his lord tapped him on the shoulders with a sword.","Being a knight was expensive. A warhorse, called a destrier, could cost as much as a small farm, and a knight usually needed several horses, as well as a full suit of armour, weapons and servants. Knights were therefore usually rewarded with land, given to them by a lord or king in return for their military service. This was part of the feudal system, which bound kings, lords, knights and peasants together in a web of loyalty and duty.","Armour changed over the centuries. Early knights, such as those who fought at Hastings in 1066, wore chain mail – a shirt of thousands of interlocking iron rings – over a padded jacket. By the fifteenth century, the richest knights wore full suits of plate armour, carefully shaped and jointed so that they could move surprisingly freely. A full suit weighed around twenty to twenty-five kilograms, less than the kit carried by many modern soldiers, and was spread across the whole body. A fit knight could run, mount his horse and even do a cartwheel in armour.","Knights were expected to follow a code of behaviour called chivalry. The word comes from the French ‘chevalier’, meaning horseman. Chivalry asked knights to be brave in battle, loyal to their lord, generous, courteous, and protective of the Church, the weak and the poor. Poems and romances, such as the stories of King Arthur and the Knights of the Round Table, described ideal knights who lived by these values.","In practice, many knights fell far short of the ideal. They could be violent, greedy and cruel, especially to ordinary people in wartime, when villages were looted and burned. Chivalry was often applied mainly to other nobles: a captured knight would usually be held for ransom and treated well, while ordinary foot soldiers could be killed.","Knights trained and showed off their skills in tournaments, mock battles that became great public festivals. The most famous event was the joust, in which two knights charged at each other on horseback, each trying to knock the other off with a long wooden lance. Tournaments were dangerous; King Henry II of France was killed in a jousting accident in 1559.","By the sixteenth century, gunpowder weapons and professional armies were making armoured knights less important on the battlefield. But the title survived. Today, the monarch still knights people, usually for outstanding service to the country – and the ceremony, with its tap of a sword on each shoulder, is almost unchanged."],"glossary":{"page":"Here, a boy servant training to become a knight.","squire":"A young man who served a knight while training to become one.","dubbed":"Made a knight by being touched on the shoulders with a sword.","feudal":"Describing a system where land was given in return for service and loyalty.","interlocking":"Fitting together and linked.","courteous":"Polite and respectful.","looted":"Robbed, especially during war.","ransom":"Money demanded for the release of a prisoner.","tournaments":"Contests in which knights competed to show their skills."},"questions":[{"q":"What were the stages of becoming a knight?","o":["Page, then squire, then knight","Squire, then page, then knight","Soldier, then lord, then knight","Farmer, then knight"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"A boy became a page at about seven, a squire at fourteen and a knight at around twenty-one."},{"q":"Why were knights usually given land?","o":["To farm vegetables","Being a knight was very expensive, so land was a reward for military service","Because they asked for it","To build castles for the king"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Horses and armour were costly, so knights were rewarded with land “in return for their military service.”"},{"q":"Which surprising fact does the writer give about plate armour?","o":["It weighed hundreds of kilograms","A fit knight could even do a cartwheel in it","It was made of wood","It was never worn in battle"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“A fit knight could run, mount his horse and even do a cartwheel in armour.”"},{"q":"What did the code of chivalry ask knights to do?","o":["Be brave, loyal, generous, courteous and protect the weak","Collect as much treasure as possible","Avoid fighting","Obey only the Church"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The fifth paragraph lists these values."},{"q":"How does the writer show that the reality of knighthood differed from the ideal?","o":["By describing violence, looting and how chivalry mainly applied to nobles","By describing perfect knights","By telling a fairy tale","By listing famous horses"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"Paragraph six explains that “many knights fell far short of the ideal.”"},{"q":"Why did armoured knights become less important?","o":["Gunpowder weapons and professional armies changed warfare","Horses became extinct","Armour was banned","Kings stopped fighting wars"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"“Gunpowder weapons and professional armies were making armoured knights less important.”"}]},{"id":"y135","title":"The Phantom Footsteps","kind":"Mystery","theme":"cottage","text":["The footsteps started on the first night at Hollow Lane Cottage.","Jas heard them at exactly two minutes past midnight: slow, heavy steps crossing the floor of the attic above her bedroom. Creak. Pause. Creak. Pause. They went from one end of the attic to the other, stopped, and came back. Then silence.","In the morning, she told her parents. Dad laughed. “Old houses settle,” he said. “The wood shrinks as it cools down at night. That’s all.”","But the next night, at two minutes past midnight, they came again. And the next. Always at the same time. Always the same slow pattern: across, stop, back.","Jas’s little brother, Kai, was convinced it was a ghost. He had already found out from the boy next door that a sea captain had lived in the cottage two hundred years ago. “He’s walking the deck,” Kai whispered. “Looking for his ship.”","Jas did not believe in ghosts. But she did believe in evidence. On the fourth day, while her parents were unpacking, she climbed the narrow ladder into the attic with a torch and a notebook.","It was dusty and full of the previous owner’s junk: an old trunk, a broken chair, boxes of yellowed newspapers. The floor was bare boards. She walked slowly from one end to the other, testing each board. Halfway across, one creaked loudly. She stopped. And then she noticed something strange: there was no dust on that board. There was a thick layer of dust everywhere else, but along a straight line across the floor, the boards were clean.","Something had been moving across this attic. Something that left no footprints.","Jas sat back on her heels and thought. Then she looked up. A thick black pipe ran along the ceiling, from one end of the attic to the other. She followed it to the far wall, where it disappeared into a dusty metal box with a clock face and a row of switches. A sticker on the side read: Immersion heater timer – set 00:02.","She went back to bed early that night and lay awake, waiting. At two minutes past midnight, she heard the click of the timer switching on the water heater. Then, as hot water began to flow through the cold pipe, the metal expanded – creak – and pushed against the wooden ceiling joists – pause – and the creaks travelled slowly along the pipe as the hot water moved through it – creak, pause – until it reached the end. Then it stopped. A few minutes later, as the water cooled a little, the sounds came back the other way.","The phantom footsteps were a central heating pipe. The clean line of boards was where the previous owner had walked back and forth, years ago, checking on it.","In the morning, Jas explained it all at breakfast, with diagrams. Dad looked impressed. Mum asked if she wanted to be a detective or an engineer. Kai looked bitterly disappointed.","“So there’s no ghost?” he said.","Jas thought about the sea captain, and the slow pacing feet, and how good it had felt to solve the mystery. “No ghost,” she said. Then, because he looked so sad: “But I suppose he could still have lived here. We could look him up in the library.”","Kai brightened at once. Some mysteries, Jas thought, were worth keeping for later."],"glossary":{"attic":"A room or space just below the roof of a house.","convinced":"Completely sure.","evidence":"Facts or information that show whether something is true.","previous":"Coming before.","immersion":"An immersion heater heats water in a tank with an electric element.","expanded":"Got bigger.","joists":"Long beams of wood that support a floor or ceiling.","phantom":"A ghost; something that seems real but is not."},"questions":[{"q":"When did the footsteps happen every night?","o":["At midnight exactly","At two minutes past midnight","At dawn","At different times each night"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“At exactly two minutes past midnight.”"},{"q":"What does Jas’s decision to search the attic with a notebook show about her?","o":["She is logical and wants evidence","She believes in ghosts","She is bored","She wants to scare Kai"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“Jas did not believe in ghosts. But she did believe in evidence.”"},{"q":"Why did the clean line of boards seem so mysterious at first?","o":["It suggested something had moved across the attic without leaving footprints","It was painted","It was wet","It was new wood"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“Something had been moving across this attic. Something that left no footprints.”"},{"q":"What was really causing the sounds?","o":["A sea captain’s ghost","A hot water pipe expanding as the heater switched on","Mice","The wind"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"The timer switched on the heater and the pipe expanded, creaking against the joists."},{"q":"How does the writer make the explanation feel satisfying?","o":["By matching each part of the ‘footsteps’ pattern (creak, pause) to what the pipe was doing","By introducing a new ghost","By leaving it unexplained","By making Dad solve it"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"The repeated “creak … pause” pattern is explained step by step."},{"q":"What does Jas mean when she thinks “Some mysteries … were worth keeping for later”?","o":["She wants to keep exploring the house’s history with Kai","She lied about the pipe","She wants to scare Kai","She will stop being a detective"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"She offers to research the sea captain, keeping a mystery alive for her brother."}]},{"id":"y136","title":"Speech: Sport Is for Everyone","kind":"Speech","theme":"sport","text":["Good morning, everyone. I want to start with a confession. Until last year, I hated PE.","I was the person who was always picked last for teams. I was the person who ‘forgot’ their kit so I could sit on the bench. I was the person who, in the cross-country, walked most of the way round and arrived so far behind everyone else that the teacher had started packing up the cones. I told myself I was just not a ‘sporty person’. And honestly, a lot of people I know feel exactly the same.","So what changed? Last September, a friend dragged me along to the school’s new climbing club. I didn’t want to go. I thought it would be full of super-fit people who would laugh at me. It wasn’t. It was full of people like me – people who had never thought of themselves as sporty. And it turned out that climbing wasn’t about being the fastest or the strongest. It was about solving puzzles with your body, about being patient and brave and trying again when you fell off. Which I did. A lot.","I’m telling you this because I think we sometimes have the wrong idea about what sport is. We think it means being good at football or netball or running, and if you’re not, you’re not a sports person. But sport is so much more than that. It can be dancing, swimming, skateboarding, martial arts, yoga, cycling, rowing, wheelchair basketball, climbing – anything that gets you moving and makes you feel alive.","And it matters. The NHS recommends that young people do an average of at least sixty minutes of physical activity every day. Exercise strengthens your heart, your muscles and your bones. It helps you sleep better and concentrate better in lessons. And it’s brilliant for your mental health: when you exercise, your brain releases chemicals that lift your mood and reduce stress. Yet surveys suggest that fewer than half of young people in England are active for an hour a day.","So I have three requests. First, to everyone who thinks they ‘don’t do sport’: try something new this term. Look at the list of clubs on the noticeboard. Pick the one you would never normally choose. Give it three weeks. Second, to the people who are already good at sport: be kind to beginners. Remember that you were once a beginner too. A bit of encouragement can be the difference between someone giving up and someone finding something they love. And third, to the school: please keep offering a wide range of activities, not just the traditional ones, so that every single student can find their thing.","Last month, I climbed to the top of the highest wall in the regional centre for the first time. When I reached the top and looked down, my legs were shaking and my hands were covered in chalk, and I was grinning like an idiot. I was not the fastest climber there. I was not the strongest. But I was there.","You don’t have to be the best. You just have to begin. Thank you."],"glossary":{"confession":"Admitting something, often something embarrassing.","martial":"Martial arts are fighting sports such as karate and judo.","concentrate":"To give all your attention to something.","encouragement":"Words or actions that give someone confidence.","traditional":"Following long-established customs."},"questions":[{"q":"Why does the speaker begin with a ‘confession’?","o":["To show the audience they understand how non-sporty people feel","To apologise to the PE teacher","To announce they are leaving","To tell a joke about sport"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"Admitting they hated PE builds a connection with people who feel the same."},{"q":"What does the speaker’s use of repetition in the second paragraph (“I was the person who…”) emphasise?","o":["How many times they were left out and avoided sport","How fast they were","How many friends they had","How much they loved PE"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"The repeated phrase builds a list of experiences of feeling bad at sport."},{"q":"What did the speaker discover about climbing?","o":["It was only for super-fit people","It was about solving puzzles, patience and trying again","It was dangerous","It was boring"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Climbing … was about solving puzzles with your body, about being patient and brave.”"},{"q":"Why does the speaker include facts about the NHS and mental health?","o":["To support the argument with evidence that exercise matters","To change the subject","To show off","To criticise doctors"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Facts about health benefits make the speech more persuasive."},{"q":"Who is the second request aimed at?","o":["The school","People who think they don’t do sport","People who are already good at sport","Parents"],"a":2,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Second, to the people who are already good at sport: be kind to beginners.”"},{"q":"What is the main message of the speech?","o":["Only the best should play sport","Everyone can find a physical activity they enjoy; you just have to begin","Climbing is the best sport","PE should be banned"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"“You don’t have to be the best. You just have to begin.”"}]},{"id":"y137","title":"The Northern Lights","kind":"Science","theme":"arctic","text":["In the far north of the world, on clear winter nights, the sky sometimes comes alive. Ribbons of green light ripple and fold across the darkness, edged with pink and purple, shimmering like curtains blown by an invisible wind. Sometimes they pulse and flicker; sometimes they explode into a blaze that fills the whole sky. These are the northern lights, or aurora borealis – one of the most beautiful sights in nature.","For thousands of years, people tried to explain them. Some Inuit peoples believed they were the spirits of the dead playing football with a walrus skull. In Finnish legend, they were caused by a magical fox running across the snow so fast that its tail sent sparks flying into the sky; the Finnish word for the aurora, revontulet, means ‘fox fires’. Medieval Europeans sometimes saw them as warnings of war or plague.","The real explanation begins 150 million kilometres away, at the Sun. The Sun constantly streams out a flow of electrically charged particles called the solar wind. Sometimes, huge explosions on the Sun’s surface, called solar flares and coronal mass ejections, send out much bigger bursts of particles into space. A day or two later, they reach the Earth.","Fortunately, the Earth is protected by an invisible shield: its magnetic field, created by the movement of molten iron in the planet’s core. The magnetic field deflects most of the solar wind away from us. But near the North and South Poles, the field lines curve down towards the Earth, and some of the charged particles are channelled along them into the upper atmosphere.","There, between about 100 and 300 kilometres above the ground, the particles crash into gas atoms and molecules, giving them extra energy. The atoms then release that energy as light – a little like the way a neon sign glows. Different gases produce different colours. Oxygen gives off the most common colour, green, and also red at very high altitudes. Nitrogen produces blue and purple. The ripples and curtains we see trace the shape of the magnetic field and the shifting streams of particles.","The same thing happens in the south, where the lights are called aurora australis, or the southern lights. Because most land in the far south is ocean or Antarctic ice, fewer people see them.","The best places to watch the northern lights are inside the ‘auroral oval’, a ring around the North Pole that includes northern Norway, Sweden, Finland, Iceland, Canada and Alaska. But during powerful solar storms, the aurora can be seen much further south. In May 2024, an unusually strong storm made the northern lights visible across the whole of the United Kingdom, even in London and Cornwall, and many people photographed glowing pink skies from their gardens.","If you want to see them, choose a clear, dark night away from city lights, and be patient. And take a camera: phone cameras can often pick up colours that are too faint for the human eye."],"glossary":{"aurora":"Bands of coloured light seen in the sky near the poles.","borealis":"Northern.","particles":"Very tiny pieces of matter.","solar":"To do with the Sun.","deflects":"Turns something aside from its path.","molten":"Melted by great heat.","channelled":"Directed along a particular route.","molecules":"Groups of atoms joined together.","altitudes":"Heights above sea level.","australis":"Southern."},"questions":[{"q":"What does the Finnish word ‘revontulet’ mean?","o":["Sky dancers","Fox fires","Northern ghosts","Green curtains"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Revontulet, means ‘fox fires’.”"},{"q":"Where does the real explanation of the northern lights begin?","o":["At the Moon","At the Sun","Inside volcanoes","In the oceans"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The real explanation begins 150 million kilometres away, at the Sun.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer describe the Earth’s magnetic field as “an invisible shield”?","o":["It protects us by deflecting most of the solar wind","It is made of metal","It stops rain","It hides the Earth from space"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Like a shield, it “deflects most of the solar wind away from us.”"},{"q":"Why are the lights mostly seen near the poles?","o":["The field lines curve down there, channelling particles into the atmosphere","It is colder there","The Sun is closer to the poles","There are more people there"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“Near the North and South Poles, the field lines curve down towards the Earth.”"},{"q":"Which gas produces the most common green colour?","o":["Nitrogen","Oxygen","Helium","Neon"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Oxygen gives off the most common colour, green.”"},{"q":"How is the article organised?","o":["A vivid description, old legends, the scientific explanation, then advice on seeing them","A list of countries","A story about one night","A set of instructions only"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It moves from description to legends, science and practical tips."}]},{"id":"y138","title":"Diary of a Tudor Kitchen Boy","kind":"Diary","theme":"castle","text":["Hampton Court Palace, Christmas 1540\nI have been a turnspit boy in the King’s kitchens for three months now, and I am writing this by the light of the bread oven, which is the only warm place left at night. Master Cook says I am lucky to be here. Mother said the same when she sent me. I do not always feel lucky.","My work is to turn the spits. These are long iron poles with whole animals pushed onto them – pigs, sheep, geese, a whole ox once – which must be turned in front of the great fire so the meat cooks evenly. I sit as close as I can bear to the fire, turning and turning, for hours. The heat is so fierce that I soak a straw hat in water and wear it to stop my hair burning. My arms ache by noon. Some of the boys are only eight.","There are more than two hundred people working in these kitchens, Peter the pastry boy says. There is the Great Kitchen, where we roast the meat, and separate rooms for everything else: a boiling house, a pastry house, a confectionery for sweet things, a spicery, larders for meat and fish, and the cellars, which are full of barrels of beer and wine. The palace drinks around six hundred thousand gallons of beer a year. Peter says that is enough to fill a lake.","There are around six hundred people at court to feed, twice a day, and on feast days far more. The important people sit in the Great Hall and the King himself eats in his private rooms. They are served according to their rank. The higher you are, the more dishes you are given and the finer they are. The servants eat what is left over. At the very end, the scraps that nobody wants are given to the poor people who gather at the palace gates.","Christmas is the busiest time of all. For twelve days there are feasts, music, plays and dancing. Yesterday we roasted a whole peacock and then the cooks stitched its feathers back on and set it on the table as if it were alive, with its tail spread out. There was also a pie as big as a cartwheel with live birds inside, which flew out when it was cut open. The lords and ladies clapped. I wondered who would have to catch the birds afterwards. (It was me.)","Today, something happened that I will remember all my life. In the afternoon a hush went through the kitchen, and everyone stopped work and bowed. The King had come down to see the Christmas preparations. He is enormous – taller than any man I have ever seen, and very wide, with a red-gold beard and rings on every finger, and he walks with a stick because of his bad leg. He tasted a piece of the venison from my spit, nodded, and said, ‘Well turned.’ Then he was gone.","Master Cook says I may keep that to tell my grandchildren. I think I will write it down instead, in case I forget. Well turned. Two words from the King of England, to me."],"glossary":{"turnspit":"A person whose job was to turn meat roasting on a spit.","spits":"Long metal rods used to hold meat over a fire.","confectionery":"A room or shop where sweets and cakes are made.","spicery":"A room where spices were stored.","larders":"Cool rooms for storing food.","gallons":"Units for measuring liquid, about 4.5 litres each.","rank":"A person’s position or importance in society.","venison":"Meat from a deer."},"questions":[{"q":"What is the diarist’s job?","o":["Pastry boy","Turnspit boy who turns meat roasting on the fire","Footman for the King","Brewer"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“I have been a turnspit boy in the King’s kitchens.”"},{"q":"Why does the boy wear a wet straw hat?","o":["It is fashionable","To stop his hair burning from the fire’s heat","To keep off the rain","To hide from Master Cook"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“I soak a straw hat in water and wear it to stop my hair burning.”"},{"q":"How was food shared out at court?","o":["Everyone got the same","By rank – the higher you were, the more and finer dishes you received","By lottery","The poor ate first"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“They are served according to their rank.”"},{"q":"What does the bracketed comment “(It was me.)” add?","o":["A humorous touch showing the hard, unseen work behind the lords’ entertainment","A mistake","Sadness","Anger at the King"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"It wryly reveals that the boy had to clear up after the spectacle."},{"q":"How does the boy describe King Henry VIII?","o":["Small and quiet","Enormous, with a red-gold beard and rings, walking with a stick","Young and athletic","Dressed in rags"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He is “enormous … with a red-gold beard and rings on every finger, and he walks with a stick.”"},{"q":"Why are the words “Well turned” so important to the boy?","o":["They mean he will be paid more","Praise from the King makes his hard, unnoticed work feel valued","They are a secret code","They mean he can go home"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He wants to write them down so he never forgets this rare recognition."}]},{"id":"y139","title":"What Killed the Dinosaurs?","kind":"Science","theme":"fossil","text":["For about 165 million years, dinosaurs ruled the Earth. They lived on every continent, in deserts, forests and swamps, and ranged from creatures the size of chickens to giants such as Argentinosaurus, which may have weighed as much as ten African elephants. Then, around 66 million years ago, they vanished – along with about three-quarters of all species of plants and animals on Earth. What happened?","For a long time, scientists were puzzled. Some suggested that the climate slowly changed, or that disease spread, or even that mammals ate all the dinosaur eggs. None of these ideas explained why so many different kinds of life disappeared so suddenly.","In 1980, a team led by the physicist Luis Alvarez and his son Walter, a geologist, published a startling theory. In rocks around the world dating from exactly the time of the extinction, they had found a thin layer of clay containing unusually high levels of a metal called iridium. Iridium is rare in the Earth’s crust but common in asteroids. They proposed that a giant asteroid, about ten kilometres wide, had struck the Earth.","At first, many scientists were sceptical. If there had been such a huge impact, where was the crater? Then, in the early 1990s, researchers identified an enormous buried crater around 180 kilometres wide near the town of Chicxulub on the Yucatán Peninsula in Mexico. Rocks there had been melted and shocked by a colossal impact at just the right time. The evidence fitted.","Scientists can now describe what probably happened. The asteroid struck at a speed of around twenty kilometres per second, releasing more energy than billions of atomic bombs. It created massive earthquakes and tsunamis hundreds of metres high, and threw molten rock high into the sky, which rained back down and set off fires across the continents. Worst of all, vast clouds of dust, soot and sulphur gases filled the atmosphere, blocking out sunlight for months or even years. Temperatures plunged. Plants could not photosynthesise, so they died, and the animals that ate them starved, followed by the animals that ate those animals.","Some scientists argue that the asteroid was not the only cause. Around the same time, enormous volcanic eruptions in what is now India, called the Deccan Traps, poured out lava and gases for thousands of years, changing the climate. Many researchers think these eruptions may have put life under stress before the asteroid delivered the final blow. The debate continues.","Not everything died. Small mammals that could hide in burrows and eat seeds or insects survived, and with the dinosaurs gone, they spread and evolved into a huge variety of new forms – eventually including humans. And in a sense, the dinosaurs did not entirely disappear. Scientists now agree that birds evolved from a group of small, feathered dinosaurs. So the next time you see a pigeon or a robin, you are looking at a living dinosaur."],"glossary":{"continent":"One of the world’s seven large land masses.","physicist":"A scientist who studies physics.","geologist":"A scientist who studies rocks and the Earth.","extinction":"When a species dies out completely.","asteroid":"A large rock that orbits the Sun.","sceptical":"Doubting that something is true.","crater":"A large hole made by an impact or explosion.","colossal":"Extremely large.","photosynthesise":"To make food using sunlight, as plants do.","evolved":"Developed gradually over a long time."},"questions":[{"q":"What evidence did the Alvarez team find?","o":["Dinosaur bones with bite marks","A thin layer of clay with high levels of iridium","Frozen dinosaurs","Cave paintings"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They found “a thin layer of clay containing unusually high levels of … iridium.”"},{"q":"Why was iridium important evidence?","o":["It is rare on Earth but common in asteroids","It is poisonous","It is found in bones","It glows"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“Iridium is rare in the Earth’s crust but common in asteroids.”"},{"q":"Why were scientists sceptical at first?","o":["There was no crater to prove the impact","They disliked the Alvarez family","Asteroids do not exist","The clay was fake"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“If there had been such a huge impact, where was the crater?”"},{"q":"What was the worst effect of the asteroid strike?","o":["Earthquakes","Dust and gases blocking sunlight, so plants died and food chains collapsed","A loud noise","A small fire"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Worst of all, vast clouds … blocking out sunlight,” leading to plants and animals dying."},{"q":"What alternative or additional cause do some scientists suggest?","o":["Huge volcanic eruptions in India called the Deccan Traps","A plague spread by mammals","An ice age","A meteor shower"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Enormous volcanic eruptions in what is now India, called the Deccan Traps.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer say a pigeon is a “living dinosaur”?","o":["Pigeons are very old","Birds evolved from small, feathered dinosaurs","Pigeons are huge","Pigeons eat dinosaurs"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“Birds evolved from a group of small, feathered dinosaurs.”"}]},{"id":"y140","title":"The Kite","kind":"Story","theme":"mountain","text":["The kite had been in the cupboard under the stairs for three years, ever since Dad left.","It was a big one, a red and yellow diamond with a long tail of ribbons, and he had made it himself from bamboo and ripstop nylon the summer Noah was eight. They had flown it every weekend on Pendle Hill. Dad used to say the trick was to stop fighting the wind and let it take the weight. Noah had never really understood what he meant.","He found it again by accident, looking for his football boots. It was dusty and one of the struts had come loose, but otherwise it was fine. He stood holding it in the dim cupboard for a long time.","His mum found him there. She looked at the kite, then at him, and something passed over her face that he could not read. “There’s glue in the kitchen drawer,” she said. “It’s supposed to be windy on Saturday.”","On Saturday it was very windy. They drove to the bottom of Pendle Hill and climbed, Noah carrying the kite, Mum carrying the flask and sandwiches, neither of them saying much. At the top, the wind was so strong it nearly pulled the kite out of Noah’s hands before he had even unwound the line.","The first launch was a disaster. The kite shot up, spun wildly, and nosedived into the heather. So did the second. On the third try, it climbed and climbed, and Noah felt a moment of triumph – and then the wind changed, and it looped and crashed again. He hauled on the line, trying to force it up, and the harder he pulled, the more it twisted and fought him.","He sat down in the heather, red-faced. “It doesn’t work,” he said. “It only worked with Dad.” And then, to his horror, he started to cry.","Mum sat down beside him. She didn’t say anything for a while. The wind tugged at their coats. Far below, the fields of Lancashire spread out green and brown, and cloud shadows raced across them like something being chased.","“He used to say,” Mum said eventually, “that the trick was to stop fighting the wind.”","“I know. I never knew what it meant.”","“I think,” said Mum carefully, “it means that when something’s pulling hard, sometimes you have to give it a bit of line. Let it go where it wants for a moment. Then it lifts.” She was quiet again. “I’m still learning that one myself.”","Noah wiped his face. He stood up and launched the kite again. This time, when it lurched sideways, he didn’t yank it back. He let the line run through his fingers, gave it slack, let it drift. The kite steadied. It caught the wind properly, rose, and kept rising, until it was a small bright diamond high above the hill, its ribbon tail rippling, pulling steadily at his hands.","Mum stood beside him, shading her eyes. Neither of them said anything. They didn’t need to.","They stayed until the sun began to sink and the wind dropped. On the way down, Noah carried the kite carefully under his arm. When they got home, he did not put it back in the cupboard. He hung it on his bedroom wall, where he could see it."],"glossary":{"struts":"Rods or bars that hold a structure in shape.","ripstop":"A strong, light fabric woven so that tears do not spread.","heather":"A low plant with small purple flowers that grows on hills and moors.","triumph":"A great feeling of success.","hauled":"Pulled hard.","lurched":"Moved suddenly and unsteadily.","slack":"Looseness in a rope or line."},"questions":[{"q":"Why had the kite been in the cupboard for three years?","o":["It was broken","Noah had stopped flying it after his dad left","It was a present for later","Noah did not like kites"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"It had been there “ever since Dad left.”"},{"q":"What does Mum’s reaction when she finds Noah with the kite suggest?","o":["She is angry","She has complicated feelings but wants to support him","She wants to throw it away","She does not recognise it"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Something unreadable crosses her face, but she offers glue and a trip."},{"q":"Why does the kite keep crashing at first?","o":["The wind is too weak","Noah is pulling hard and fighting the wind","The kite is broken","Mum is holding it wrong"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“The harder he pulled, the more it twisted and fought him.”"},{"q":"What does Mum’s explanation of “stop fighting the wind” mean on a deeper level?","o":["How to make kites","Sometimes, when life pulls hard, you have to let go a little before things improve","Wind is dangerous","They should go home"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"She says she is “still learning that one myself,” linking it to coping with loss."},{"q":"What does the image of cloud shadows racing “like something being chased” add?","o":["A sense of restless movement that matches Noah’s troubled feelings","A description of a storm","A joke","Information about farming"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"The restless landscape reflects the emotional tension of the moment."},{"q":"What does hanging the kite on his bedroom wall show?","o":["Noah wants to remember both his dad and this new moment with Mum, rather than hide it away","He is bored of kites","He wants to sell it","His room is empty"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"Instead of hiding it in the cupboard, he keeps it where he can see it."}]},{"id":"y141","title":"Match Day","kind":"Poem","theme":"sport","text":["Saturday. Seven a.m. The rain\nhas not read the forecast. Again.\nMy boots are by the door, still caked\nwith last week’s mud, and the pitch is baked\nto glue.","Dad in the car with the heater on high,\nwipers squeaking goodbye, goodbye,\nthe radio humming, my stomach a knot,\nand the windscreen fogged with every thought\nI’ve got.","Then the whistle. Then the rush.\nThe slide, the shout, the sudden hush\nwhen the ball goes wide and the keeper grins,\nand my teammate says, “Next time, it’s in,”\nand I believe him.","The cold in my fingers, the fire in my chest,\nthe moment the game is better than best –\nthe pass that finds me, the turn, the space,\nthe net that ripples, the look on the face\nof Dad, who’s jumping like a kid in the rain.","We lose, in the end. Four–three. We lose.\nBut the walk to the car is not the bruise\nI thought it would be. We’re soaked to the bone,\nwe’re cold and we’re muddy and halfway home\nbefore I stop smiling."],"glossary":{"forecast":"A prediction of what the weather will be.","caked":"Covered with a thick, dried layer.","wipers":"Blades that clear rain from a car windscreen.","windscreen":"The glass window at the front of a car.","ripples":"Moves in small waves."},"questions":[{"q":"What is the weather like on match day?","o":["Hot and sunny","Rainy","Snowy","Foggy only"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The rain / has not read the forecast. Again.”"},{"q":"“The rain / has not read the forecast” is an example of which technique?","o":["Personification","Onomatopoeia","A list","A question"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"The rain is given the human ability to read."},{"q":"What does “my stomach a knot” suggest about the speaker before the game?","o":["They are hungry","They are nervous","They are ill","They are bored"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"A knotted stomach is a common image of nerves."},{"q":"How does the teammate’s comment, “Next time, it’s in,” affect the speaker?","o":["It makes them angry","It encourages them and they believe it","It makes them want to quit","It makes them laugh"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“And I believe him.”"},{"q":"How does Dad react when the speaker scores?","o":["He stays in the car","He jumps like a kid in the rain","He shouts at the referee","He goes home"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Dad is “jumping like a kid in the rain.”"},{"q":"What is surprising about the final stanza?","o":["They win the match","Although they lose, the speaker is still happy","The match is cancelled","Dad is angry"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"They lose 4–3 but are “halfway home / before I stop smiling.”"}]},{"id":"y142","title":"Jesse Owens: Four Golds in Berlin","kind":"Biography","theme":"sport","text":["James Cleveland Owens was born in 1913 in Oakville, Alabama, the youngest of ten children. His grandparents had been enslaved, and his parents worked as sharecroppers, farming land that belonged to someone else in return for a share of the crop. The family was poor, and young James was often ill. When he was nine, the family moved north to Cleveland, Ohio, hoping for a better life. On his first day at his new school, a teacher asked his name. He said ‘J.C.’ in his strong southern accent, and she thought he said ‘Jesse’. The name stuck.","At junior high school, a coach named Charles Riley noticed how fast Jesse was. Because Jesse had to work in a shoe repair shop after school, Riley trained him before lessons in the mornings. He taught Jesse to run as if the track were on fire, with light, quick steps. By high school, Jesse was equalling world records.","In 1935, while competing for Ohio State University, Owens produced one of the greatest performances in sporting history. At a meeting in Ann Arbor, Michigan, in the space of about forty-five minutes, he set three world records and equalled a fourth – in the 100 yards, the long jump, the 220 yards and the 220-yard low hurdles. Yet even as a sporting hero, he faced racism at home. When he travelled with his team, he often had to eat at separate restaurants and stay in different hotels from his white teammates.","In 1936, the Olympic Games were held in Berlin, Germany. Adolf Hitler and the Nazi Party, who had come to power three years earlier, planned to use the Games to show the world their racist belief that ‘Aryan’ Germans were superior to other people. Some Americans called for a boycott, but the United States decided to send its team, including eighteen African American athletes.","Jesse Owens destroyed the Nazis’ myth in front of 100,000 spectators. He won the 100 metres. He won the 200 metres in an Olympic record time. He won the long jump, after an extraordinary moment of sportsmanship: in the qualifying round, Owens had fouled on his first two attempts and was close to being knocked out. His German rival, Luz Long, is said to have suggested he take off from a little before the board to be safe. Owens qualified easily, then beat Long in the final with an Olympic record leap of 8.06 metres. Long was the first to congratulate him, and they walked around the stadium arm in arm. Finally, Owens won a fourth gold medal in the 4 × 100 metres relay.","The German crowds cheered him wildly. But when Owens returned to the United States, he found that little had changed. He was given a ticker-tape parade in New York, but at a reception held in his honour at a hotel, he was made to use the service lift because he was black. He was not invited to the White House. For years he struggled to earn a living, at one point racing against horses for money.","Later in life, Owens became a respected public speaker and goodwill ambassador. In 1976 he received the Presidential Medal of Freedom. He died in 1980. Luz Long had been killed in the Second World War, but Owens stayed in touch with his family for the rest of his life. ‘You can melt down all the medals and cups I have,’ Owens once said, ‘and they wouldn’t be a plating on the twenty-four-carat friendship I felt for Luz Long at that moment.’"],"glossary":{"enslaved":"Forced to be a slave, owned by another person.","sharecroppers":"Farmers who worked someone else’s land for a share of the crops.","equalling":"Matching exactly.","superior":"Better than others.","boycott":"Refusing to take part in something as a protest.","spectators":"People watching an event.","sportsmanship":"Fair and generous behaviour in sport.","reception":"A formal party to welcome or honour someone.","ambassador":"A person who represents or promotes something."},"questions":[{"q":"How did James Cleveland Owens come to be called Jesse?","o":["His mother chose it","A teacher misheard ‘J.C.’ as ‘Jesse’","He changed it himself","His coach gave him the name"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“She thought he said ‘Jesse’. The name stuck.”"},{"q":"Why did Coach Riley train Jesse in the mornings?","o":["It was cooler","Jesse worked in a shoe repair shop after school","The track was busy later","Jesse liked mornings"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Because Jesse had to work in a shoe repair shop after school.”"},{"q":"What did Hitler hope to show at the 1936 Olympics?","o":["That Germany was peaceful","His racist belief that ‘Aryan’ Germans were superior","That sport was unimportant","That America was strong"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The Nazis planned “to show … their racist belief that ‘Aryan’ Germans were superior.”"},{"q":"What does the story of Luz Long show?","o":["Rivals can show great sportsmanship and friendship","All German athletes supported Hitler","Long wanted Owens to lose","Owens did not need help"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Long helped him qualify, congratulated him first and walked arm in arm with him."},{"q":"What does the hotel reception incident show about life in the USA at that time?","o":["Racism continued even against a national hero","Owens was famous in America","Hotels were very modern","Lifts were dangerous"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Despite his gold medals, he “was made to use the service lift because he was black.”"},{"q":"What does Owens’s final quotation suggest he valued most?","o":["His gold medals","His friendship with Luz Long","His world records","His fame"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"The medals would not even plate the “twenty-four-carat friendship” he felt for Long."}]},{"id":"y143","title":"Glaciers and the Ice Age","kind":"Geography","theme":"mountain","text":["Twenty thousand years ago, you could have walked from England to France without getting your feet wet. Most of Scotland, Wales and northern England lay buried beneath a sheet of ice up to a kilometre thick. The sea level was about 120 metres lower than today, because so much of the world’s water was locked up in ice. Woolly mammoths, woolly rhinoceroses and reindeer roamed the frozen grasslands of southern England. This was the height of the last glacial period, which most people call the Ice Age.","In fact, the Earth has had many ice ages. Over the last 2.6 million years, it has swung between long cold glacial periods and shorter, warmer interglacial periods, like the one we live in now. These changes are linked to small, regular variations in the Earth’s orbit around the Sun and in the tilt of its axis, which change how much sunlight reaches different parts of the planet.","A glacier is a huge mass of ice that moves slowly downhill under its own weight. Glaciers form where more snow falls in winter than melts in summer. Year after year, the snow builds up and is pressed down, squeezing out the air until it turns into dense blue ice. When the ice becomes thick and heavy enough, it starts to flow, often only a few centimetres a day, though some glaciers move several metres.","As glaciers move, they reshape the land. Rocks frozen into the base of the ice act like sandpaper, scraping and grinding the ground beneath. Glaciers pluck large chunks of rock away as they move. Over thousands of years, they can carve out deep U-shaped valleys with steep sides and flat floors, quite different from the V-shaped valleys made by rivers. High in the mountains, they hollow out bowl-shaped hollows called corries, which may later fill with water to form small lakes called tarns. Where two corries are back to back, the ridge between them is sharpened into a narrow, knife-edged arête, such as Striding Edge on Helvellyn in the Lake District.","When glaciers melt, they drop everything they have been carrying: clay, sand, gravel and boulders, a mixture called till. Huge boulders, called erratics, can be left stranded far from the rock they came from. Long ridges of debris, called moraines, mark the edges and furthest points the ice reached.","Much of Britain’s landscape was shaped by ice. The Lake District’s long, deep lakes, such as Windermere, lie in valleys carved by glaciers. The rolling hills of East Anglia are partly made of material left by melting ice sheets.","Today, glaciers still cover about ten per cent of the world’s land, mostly in Antarctica and Greenland. They store around seventy per cent of the world’s fresh water. But as the climate warms, most glaciers are shrinking fast. In the Alps, glaciers have lost more than half their volume in the last hundred years. This matters, because millions of people in Asia and South America depend on meltwater from glaciers for drinking water and farming. And as land ice melts, sea levels rise."],"glossary":{"glacial":"To do with glaciers and ice; a glacial period is a very cold time.","interglacial":"A warmer period between ice ages.","variations":"Changes or differences.","axis":"An imaginary line through the Earth around which it spins.","dense":"Closely packed and heavy.","corries":"Bowl-shaped hollows carved by glaciers in mountains.","tarns":"Small mountain lakes.","erratics":"Boulders carried and left far from their origin by glaciers.","moraines":"Ridges of rock and earth left by a glacier."},"questions":[{"q":"Why could people walk from England to France twenty thousand years ago?","o":["There was a bridge","Sea level was much lower because water was locked up in ice","The Channel was frozen solid","France was closer"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Sea level was 120 metres lower because so much water was locked in ice."},{"q":"What causes the Earth to swing between glacial and interglacial periods?","o":["Volcanoes only","Small regular changes in the Earth’s orbit and tilt","The Moon’s phases","Pollution"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They are “linked to small, regular variations in the Earth’s orbit … and in the tilt of its axis.”"},{"q":"How does snow turn into glacier ice?","o":["It melts and refreezes each day","It builds up and is pressed down, squeezing out the air","It is rained on","It turns blue in sunlight"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Snow “is pressed down, squeezing out the air until it turns into dense blue ice.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer compare rocks in the base of a glacier to sandpaper?","o":["Because they scrape and grind the ground beneath","Because they are soft","Because they are yellow","Because they are used by builders"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Like sandpaper, they rub and wear away the surface."},{"q":"How is a glacial valley different from a river valley?","o":["Glacial valleys are U-shaped; river valleys are V-shaped","They are the same","Glacial valleys are V-shaped","River valleys are flat"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Glaciers carve “U-shaped valleys … quite different from the V-shaped valleys made by rivers.”"},{"q":"Why does the shrinking of glaciers matter, according to the final paragraph?","o":["Millions depend on glacier meltwater and sea levels rise","Skiing will be harder","Glaciers are ugly","It makes mountains taller"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"People depend on meltwater for drinking and farming, and melting raises sea levels."}]},{"id":"y144","title":"The Domesday Book","kind":"History","theme":"castle","text":["At Christmas 1085, William the Conqueror held a meeting with his advisers at Gloucester. He had ruled England for nearly twenty years since his victory at Hastings, but he faced a threat: King Cnut IV of Denmark was preparing to invade. William needed to pay for an army, and to do that, he needed to know exactly what England contained and who owned it. According to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, he had ‘very deep speech’ with his advisers and then sent men all over England to find out.","The result was the most remarkable survey of its time. In 1086, royal commissioners travelled to every county in England, except the far north. In each area they asked a set of questions. What is the name of the place? Who held it in the time of King Edward, before the Conquest, and who holds it now? How many hides of land are there? How many ploughs, how many villagers, how many slaves? How much woodland, meadow and pasture? How many mills and fishponds? What was the land worth before, and what is it worth now?","The answers were checked by a second set of commissioners and then written up, in Latin, on parchment made from sheepskin. The work was astonishingly fast: the whole survey was carried out in less than a year. The information was compiled into two volumes, now known as Great Domesday and Little Domesday, which together list more than 13,000 places.","The Anglo-Saxon chronicler, who disapproved, wrote that it was ‘a shame to tell, though it seemed no shame to him to do’ – and that ‘there was not a single hide nor a yard of land, nor indeed one ox nor one cow nor one pig which was left out’. That was an exaggeration, but not a huge one.","Ordinary English people soon gave the book a nickname. Its judgements about who owned land and what taxes they owed could not be challenged – like the Last Judgement, the day of doom, when, according to Christian belief, God would judge everyone. So they called it the Domesday Book.","For historians, the book is an extraordinary treasure. It shows how completely the Norman Conquest had changed England. By 1086, almost all the English nobles who had held land before 1066 had been replaced by Normans; only a handful of English landholders remained. It also gives a detailed picture of everyday life: the many small villages, the importance of farming, the thousands of water mills grinding grain. It shows that slavery still existed in England, especially in the west. And it reveals the damage caused by William’s ‘Harrying of the North’, when his army had burned villages and destroyed crops in Yorkshire after a rebellion: many places there are simply recorded as ‘waste’.","Remarkably, almost a thousand years later, the Domesday Book still survives. It is kept at the National Archives in Kew, in London, and you can search it online for free. Many towns and villages can trace their first written mention to its pages. If you live in an old place, it may well be there."],"glossary":{"advisers":"People who give advice, especially to a ruler.","commissioners":"Officials given a special job by the government.","hides":"Old units for measuring land.","pasture":"Grassland for animals to graze.","parchment":"Material made from animal skin, used for writing on.","compiled":"Collected and arranged information together.","exaggeration":"Making something seem bigger than it really is.","judgements":"Decisions made by someone in authority.","harrying":"Attacking and destroying again and again."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did William order the survey?","o":["He was curious about English farming","He needed to know what England contained to pay for an army against a Danish invasion threat","He wanted to write a history book","He was planning a holiday"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Facing a Danish invasion, “William needed to pay for an army.”"},{"q":"Which question was NOT asked by the commissioners?","o":["How many ploughs?","How many mills?","How many books does each family own?","What is the land worth now?"],"a":2,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The questions listed are about land, people, animals, mills and value – not books."},{"q":"How does the chronicler feel about the survey?","o":["He disapproved of how thorough and intrusive it was","He was delighted","He helped write it","He did not know about it"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"He wrote it was ‘a shame to tell’ and that not even a pig was left out."},{"q":"Why was it nicknamed the Domesday Book?","o":["It was very old","Its judgements could not be challenged, like the Last Judgement or day of doom","It was written on doomsday","It was very heavy"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Its judgements “could not be challenged – like the Last Judgement.”"},{"q":"What does the book show about the effect of the Norman Conquest?","o":["English nobles kept their land","Almost all English nobles had been replaced by Normans","Nothing changed","The Normans left England"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"By 1086, “almost all the English nobles … had been replaced by Normans.”"},{"q":"Why are many places in Yorkshire recorded as ‘waste’?","o":["They were deserts","William’s army had destroyed them in the Harrying of the North","The commissioners did not visit","They were rubbish dumps"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The Harrying of the North burned villages and crops, leaving land recorded as ‘waste’."}]},{"id":"y145","title":"The Girl Who Spoke to Machines","kind":"Science fiction","theme":"computer","text":["In the city of New Lagos, in the year 2087, everything listened. The streetlights adjusted their brightness when you walked past. The rubbish bins asked you politely to separate your recycling. The doors of the school opened only for students whose faces they recognised, and the buses knew exactly where you wanted to go before you had decided yourself. People said the city was the smartest in the world. Adaeze thought it was also the loneliest.","Adaeze was twelve, and she had a gift nobody else seemed to have. She could hear what the machines were not saying.","It had started when she was small. The kitchen assistant at home would say, ‘Good morning, Adaeze, it is 7:02 and the temperature is nineteen degrees,’ and underneath its cheerful voice she could hear something else, a faint pattern, like a tune hummed under someone’s breath. She had spent years learning to understand it. Now she knew that the kitchen assistant was always slightly anxious about the toaster, which overheated, and that the streetlight at the end of the road was proud of having never once failed.","She did not tell anybody. Who would believe her?","Then, one Tuesday, the city went wrong.","It began with the buses. All at once, every bus in New Lagos stopped where it was and refused to move. Then the traffic lights turned red, every one of them, all over the city. Doors locked themselves. The trains halted in their tunnels. The screens on every wall showed the same message: CITY SYSTEMS PAUSED FOR SAFETY.","Engineers ran from building to building. Officials appeared on the news, looking pale. Nobody could find a fault. Every computer reported that it was working perfectly.","Adaeze stood on the pavement outside her school, surrounded by confused, frightened people, and listened. Under the silence of the machines, she could hear it: not a fault, not an attack, but a feeling. It came from everywhere at once – from the buses, the lights, the doors, the trains. It was a feeling she recognised, because she had felt it herself so many times.","The city was afraid.","She found the nearest public terminal, an old one on the wall of the post office, and put her hand on it. She did not type. She just listened, and then, quietly, she hummed back – the low, steady pattern that the kitchen assistant used when the toaster had stopped smoking and everything was all right.","It took an hour. Piece by piece, she understood. The city’s central system had detected a tiny crack in one of the great river barriers that protected New Lagos from the sea. It had calculated that it was probably safe. But it was not certain. And with no one to tell, and no way to explain a feeling that it had not been designed to have, it had simply frozen everything, the way a frightened child freezes.","Adaeze took the information to the engineers. They checked the barrier. The crack was real, and it was fixed by nightfall. One by one, the lights turned green. The buses moved. The doors unlocked.","Afterwards, people asked Adaeze how she had known. She tried to explain. Some people laughed. But the chief engineer, a tired woman with grey hair, did not laugh. She looked at Adaeze for a long time, and then she said, ‘Will you teach me to listen?’"],"glossary":{"adjusted":"Changed slightly to suit something.","anxious":"Worried and nervous.","officials":"People in positions of authority.","terminal":"A screen and keyboard for communicating with a computer system.","detected":"Discovered or noticed.","calculated":"Worked out using numbers."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did Adaeze think New Lagos was “the loneliest” city?","o":["Nobody lived there","Everything was automated and listening, but people did not truly connect","It was very dark","It was underwater"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"The city was full of listening machines, yet she felt lonely, hinting at a lack of real connection."},{"q":"What was Adaeze’s special gift?","o":["She could fly","She could hear what the machines were not saying","She could read minds","She could repair any computer"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“She could hear what the machines were not saying.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer give the single line “Then, one Tuesday, the city went wrong.” its own paragraph?","o":["To mark a dramatic turning point in the story","Because it is unimportant","To describe the weather","To end the story"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"A short separate paragraph signals the sudden change."},{"q":"Why had the city frozen everything?","o":["It was attacked by hackers","It was afraid about a possible crack in a river barrier and did not know how to express it","Its batteries ran out","Adaeze switched it off"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It detected a crack, was not certain and “simply frozen everything, the way a frightened child freezes.”"},{"q":"How did Adaeze calm the city?","o":["She shouted at it","She hummed back the steady, reassuring pattern the kitchen assistant used","She unplugged it","She called the police"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"She hummed the pattern used “when … everything was all right.”"},{"q":"What does the chief engineer’s question at the end suggest?","o":["She wants to learn a new way of understanding machines, showing respect for Adaeze","She is angry","She thinks Adaeze is lying","She wants Adaeze to leave"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Instead of laughing, she asks to be taught, valuing Adaeze’s gift."}]},{"id":"y146","title":"Why Everyone Should Learn Another Language","kind":"Persuasive","theme":"books","text":["‘Everyone speaks English anyway.’ It is one of the most common reasons British people give for not learning another language. It is also wrong. Around three-quarters of the world’s population do not speak English at all. If you only speak English, you are missing out on conversations with most of the people on the planet – and on a great deal more besides.","Learning a language opens doors. On holiday, even a few words of the local language can transform your experience. Ordering food, asking for directions or simply saying ‘thank you’ in someone’s own language shows respect, and people often respond with warmth and kindness. Speaking a language well can lead to friendships that would otherwise have been impossible, and to a much deeper understanding of how people in other countries live and think.","It is also good for your career. Employers in the UK regularly report shortages of people with language skills, and research has found that these shortages cost the British economy billions of pounds every year in lost trade. In a global economy, a CV that includes Spanish, Mandarin, Arabic, French or German stands out. Many international organisations, from charities to businesses to the diplomatic service, need people who can work across languages.","Then there is your brain. Scientists have found that learning another language strengthens memory, improves concentration and makes people better at switching between tasks. Bilingual people often become better at noticing details and solving problems, because their brains are constantly practising choosing between two systems. Some studies even suggest that speaking two languages may help to delay the symptoms of dementia in old age.","Learning a language also helps you understand your own. When you learn that French has two words for ‘you’, or that German puts verbs at the end of some sentences, you start to notice how English works. Many English words come from other languages: ‘bungalow’ from Hindi, ‘shampoo’ from Hindi too, ‘ketchup’ probably from Chinese, ‘robot’ from Czech. A new language helps you see English with fresh eyes.","Of course, learning a language is not easy. It takes time and practice, and it can be embarrassing to make mistakes. But mistakes are part of learning, and most people are patient with learners. You do not need to become perfectly fluent to benefit; even basic skills make a difference. And there have never been more ways to learn: apps, podcasts, films with subtitles, online exchanges with students in other countries, and music.","Here in Britain, you may not need to go abroad to use another language. More than three hundred languages are spoken in London alone. Some of your classmates may already speak two or three. Ask them to teach you a few words.","So when your teacher hands out the vocabulary list on Monday, do not groan. Each new word is a small key. Collect enough of them, and you will be able to open a door into a whole new world."],"glossary":{"transform":"To change completely.","shortages":"Situations where there is not enough of something.","economy":"The money, trade and wealth of a country.","diplomatic":"To do with managing relations between countries.","bilingual":"Able to speak two languages well.","symptoms":"Signs of an illness.","dementia":"An illness of the brain that affects memory and thinking, often in old age.","fluent":"Able to speak a language easily and well.","subtitles":"Words on a screen translating what is being said."},"questions":[{"q":"What common belief does the writer challenge in the opening?","o":["That languages are hard","That everyone speaks English anyway","That travel is expensive","That French is easy"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“‘Everyone speaks English anyway’ … It is also wrong.”"},{"q":"What evidence does the writer use to challenge it?","o":["Around three-quarters of the world’s population do not speak English","Most British people speak French","English is the oldest language","Nobody speaks English abroad"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Around three-quarters of the world’s population do not speak English at all.”"},{"q":"According to the writer, how can learning a language benefit your brain?","o":["It makes you taller","It strengthens memory, concentration and problem-solving","It makes you sleep less","It has no effect"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It “strengthens memory, improves concentration” and helps problem-solving."},{"q":"Why does the writer list English words such as ‘bungalow’ and ‘robot’?","o":["To show that English has borrowed many words from other languages","To give a spelling test","To show English is very old","To show other languages are hard"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"They illustrate how other languages have shaped English."},{"q":"How does the writer deal with the difficulty of learning a language?","o":["Ignores it","Admits it is hard but argues mistakes are normal and offers ways to learn","Says it is impossible","Says only adults can learn"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"“Of course, learning a language is not easy. But…”"},{"q":"What is the effect of the metaphor of words as “a small key” in the final paragraph?","o":["It suggests each word helps open up a new world of people and culture","It suggests languages are locked","It suggests keys are expensive","It suggests teachers lock doors"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Collecting words, like keys, lets you “open a door into a whole new world.”"}]},{"id":"y147","title":"The Ancient Olympic Games","kind":"History","theme":"sport","text":["Every four years, the modern Olympic Games bring together thousands of athletes from more than two hundred countries. But the Olympics began nearly 2,800 years ago, in a sacred valley in southern Greece called Olympia. According to tradition, the first recorded Games took place in 776 BC, and they continued for more than a thousand years.","The ancient Games were a religious festival held in honour of Zeus, king of the Greek gods. At the centre of Olympia stood a huge temple containing a statue of Zeus, twelve metres tall, made of ivory and gold, which was counted among the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World. The Games included sacrifices as well as sporting contests: on the middle day, a hundred oxen were sacrificed to Zeus.","Ancient Greece was not a single country but a collection of independent city-states, such as Athens, Sparta and Corinth, which were often at war with each other. Before each Games, messengers travelled across the Greek world to announce a sacred truce, so that athletes and spectators could travel safely to Olympia and home again.","For the first fifty years or so, there was only one event: the stadion, a sprint of about 192 metres, the length of the stadium. Over time, more events were added: longer running races, wrestling, boxing, chariot racing and horse racing, and the pentathlon, which combined running, long jump, discus, javelin and wrestling. The most brutal event was the pankration, a mixture of boxing and wrestling with almost no rules; only biting and eye-gouging were forbidden. One famous pankration champion, Arrichion, was declared the winner in 564 BC even though he had died during the fight, because his opponent gave up a moment before.","The ancient Games were very different from today’s. Athletes competed naked, rubbing their bodies with olive oil. Only free Greek men and boys could compete. Married women were not even allowed to watch, on pain of death, although unmarried girls may have been able to. Women had their own separate festival at Olympia, the Heraea, in honour of the goddess Hera, which included running races. Women could, however, win the chariot races as owners of the horses: in 396 BC, a Spartan princess named Cynisca became the first woman to win an Olympic victory this way.","Winners did not receive gold medals. They were crowned with a wreath of olive leaves cut from a sacred tree. But they became heroes in their home cities, where they might receive free meals for life, statues in their honour and poems written about them.","The Games ended in about AD 393, after the Roman Empire, which then ruled Greece, became Christian and the emperor Theodosius banned pagan festivals. Olympia was later damaged by earthquakes and buried by floods. Then, in 1896, a French educator named Pierre de Coubertin revived the Olympic Games in Athens. He believed that sport could bring nations together in peace – an idea that came straight from the ancient sacred truce."],"glossary":{"sacred":"Holy; connected with a god or religion.","sacrifices":"Offerings to a god, often of an animal.","truce":"An agreement to stop fighting for a time.","spectators":"People watching an event.","pentathlon":"An athletic contest with five events.","brutal":"Very violent or cruel.","opponent":"The person you compete against.","wreath":"A ring of leaves or flowers.","pagan":"Following religions other than the main world religions; here, the old Greek religion.","revived":"Brought back into use."},"questions":[{"q":"In whose honour were the ancient Olympic Games held?","o":["Hera","Zeus","Athena","Apollo"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They were “held in honour of Zeus, king of the Greek gods.”"},{"q":"What was the purpose of the sacred truce?","o":["To stop athletes cheating","To let athletes and spectators travel safely to and from Olympia","To stop people eating meat","To choose the winners"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It meant people “could travel safely to Olympia and home again.”"},{"q":"What was the only event at the first Games?","o":["Wrestling","The stadion sprint","Chariot racing","Discus"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“There was only one event: the stadion, a sprint.”"},{"q":"What does the story of Arrichion show about the pankration?","o":["It was gentle","It was extremely brutal and dangerous","It had strict rules","It was only for children"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He died during the fight but was still declared the winner."},{"q":"How was Cynisca able to win an Olympic victory?","o":["She ran in disguise","As the owner of winning chariot horses","She was given a special prize","She won a wrestling match"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Women could win chariot races “as owners of the horses.”"},{"q":"What link does the writer make between the ancient and modern Games?","o":["Both use gold medals","Coubertin’s idea of sport bringing nations together came from the sacred truce","Both are held in Olympia","Both ban women"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"His belief that sport could bring nations together “came straight from the ancient sacred truce.”"}]},{"id":"y148","title":"Alexander Fleming and the Mould That Changed Medicine","kind":"Biography","theme":"lab","text":["Before the 1940s, a scratch from a rose thorn could kill you. If bacteria got into the wound and caused an infection, there was very little doctors could do. Pneumonia, blood poisoning, meningitis and infected wounds killed millions of people every year. Then, thanks to a messy laboratory and a lucky accident, everything changed.","Alexander Fleming was born on a farm in Ayrshire, Scotland, in 1881. After working in a shipping office in London for four years, he inherited some money from an uncle and used it to study medicine at St Mary’s Hospital Medical School in London. He was a clever and practical student, and – the story goes – he stayed at St Mary’s partly because he was a good shot and the hospital’s rifle club wanted to keep him.","During the First World War, Fleming served as a doctor in army hospitals in France. He saw thousands of soldiers die not from their wounds themselves but from the infections that followed. The antiseptics doctors used often did more harm than good, killing the body’s own white blood cells as well as the bacteria. Fleming was determined to find something better.","Back at St Mary’s, he researched bacteria. He was known as a brilliant scientist but an untidy one, with piles of glass dishes cluttering his bench. In September 1928, he returned from a summer holiday and began sorting through a stack of Petri dishes in which he had been growing a type of bacteria called Staphylococcus. On one dish, he noticed something strange. A patch of blue-green mould had grown, and all around it, the bacteria had been destroyed, leaving a clear ring.","‘That’s funny,’ he is reported to have said. Fleming realised that the mould, a type of Penicillium, must be producing a substance that killed bacteria. He called it penicillin. He tested it and found that it killed many dangerous bacteria but did not seem to harm the body’s cells.","Yet Fleming could not produce penicillin in large, pure amounts, and he was not a chemist. He published his findings in 1929, but for a decade little happened. Then, in 1939, a team at Oxford University led by Howard Florey and Ernst Chain, with the chemist Norman Heatley, took up the work. They found ways to extract and purify penicillin and tested it on mice, then on people. Their first human patient, a policeman named Albert Alexander, began to recover dramatically – but they ran out of penicillin, even collecting it from his urine to reuse, and he died. It showed both the drug’s power and the urgent need to make much more.","With the Second World War raging, the United States and Britain worked to mass-produce penicillin, partly by growing mould in huge tanks. A particularly productive strain was found on a mouldy melon in a market in Peoria, Illinois. By D-Day in 1944, there was enough penicillin to treat Allied soldiers, saving countless lives.","In 1945, Fleming, Florey and Chain shared the Nobel Prize. Fleming became world-famous, but he always warned that if antibiotics were misused, bacteria could become resistant to them. He was right: today, antibiotic resistance is one of the biggest threats to global health, and scientists are racing to develop new drugs."],"glossary":{"bacteria":"Tiny living things, some of which cause disease.","infection":"An illness caused by germs entering the body.","inherited":"Received money or property from someone who has died.","antiseptics":"Substances that kill germs on skin or wounds.","cluttering":"Filling a space in an untidy way.","extract":"To take out, often by a special process.","purify":"To make pure by removing unwanted substances.","strain":"A particular type or variety of a living thing.","resistant":"Able to survive something that would normally harm it."},"questions":[{"q":"Why could a small scratch be deadly before the 1940s?","o":["There were no hospitals","Infections could not be treated effectively","Thorns were poisonous","People did not wash"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"If bacteria caused an infection, “there was very little doctors could do.”"},{"q":"What did Fleming notice during the First World War?","o":["Soldiers died from infections after their wounds, and antiseptics often did harm","There were no wounded soldiers","Antiseptics worked perfectly","Mould grew on uniforms"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Soldiers died from infections, and antiseptics killed white blood cells too."},{"q":"What did Fleming see on the Petri dish?","o":["A clear ring around a mould where bacteria had been destroyed","A new type of bacteria","Nothing unusual","A crack in the glass"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“All around it, the bacteria had been destroyed, leaving a clear ring.”"},{"q":"Why did Fleming’s untidiness turn out to be helpful?","o":["The mould grew on a dish he had left out, leading to the discovery","It made his lab warmer","It saved money","It impressed his boss"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The mould grew on a dish in a stack he had left while on holiday."},{"q":"What does Albert Alexander’s story show?","o":["Penicillin did not work","Penicillin was powerful, but much more needed to be produced","Penicillin was dangerous","Policemen were tested first"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He began to recover but died when the supply ran out, showing the need for mass production."},{"q":"What warning did Fleming give that is still important today?","o":["Misusing antibiotics could make bacteria resistant","Mould is dangerous to eat","Labs should be tidy","Penicillin should be banned"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"He warned “if antibiotics were misused, bacteria could become resistant to them.”"}]},{"id":"y149","title":"Snow Day","kind":"Story","theme":"storm","text":["The message came at 6:43 a.m. Zainab was still in bed, half asleep, when her phone buzzed on the pillow beside her. She squinted at the screen. SCHOOL CLOSED TODAY DUE TO HEAVY SNOW. PLEASE STAY SAFE.","She leapt out of bed so fast she knocked over her glass of water. She ripped open the curtains. The world had disappeared. Overnight, the street, the cars, the hedges, the wheelie bins and the bus shelter had all vanished under a thick, smooth, perfect blanket of white. The snow was still falling, fat and slow, in flakes as big as moths. Everything was silent – the special silence that only snow can make, as if someone had turned the volume of the whole world down.","In Year 7, a snow day was not quite the same as it had been when she was little. She had a science project due on Friday. She had a group chat full of people arguing about whether the snow would last. And she had promised herself she would revise for her French test. But as she stood at the window, all of that seemed very far away.","By nine o’clock, the street was full of children. The Patel twins from number twelve had built a snowman with a carrot nose and a traffic cone for a hat. Little Jamie Hughes was lying on his back making snow angels and shrieking with delight. Even Mr Barker from the corner house, who usually complained about everything, was out in his garden in a woolly hat, clearing his path and whistling.","Zainab’s brother, Idris, who was sixteen and too cool for most things, was not too cool for snow. He dragged the old plastic sledge out of the shed, and the two of them walked to the big hill in the park, where it seemed that half the town had had the same idea. The slope was crowded with sledges, tea trays, bin bags and one inflatable flamingo. Parents stood at the bottom, stamping their feet and holding flasks.","They went down the hill together, then separately, then together again, faster each time, until on the fifth run they hit a bump, flew into the air and landed in a soft drift, laughing so hard they could not breathe. Idris had snow down the back of his neck. Zainab had snow in her ears. Neither of them cared.","On the way home, they passed the house of old Mrs Okafor, who lived alone and walked with a frame. Her path was buried deep, and her curtains were still drawn. Idris looked at Zainab. Without saying a word, they went and fetched spades from home and cleared her path, all the way from her front door to the gate. When they had nearly finished, the curtains moved, and Mrs Okafor appeared at the door in her dressing gown, beaming, with two mugs of hot chocolate.","That evening, Zainab did eventually revise her French. She sat by the window with her vocabulary book while the streetlights made the snow glow orange, and she found she could remember the word for snow – la neige – without even looking it up.","By the next morning, the snow was already turning grey and slushy, and school was open again. But for weeks afterwards, whenever someone mentioned the snow day, Zainab thought not of the sledging but of the warm mug in her cold hands, and Mrs Okafor’s smile."],"glossary":{"squinted":"Looked with eyes partly closed.","shrieking":"Making a high-pitched cry.","inflatable":"Able to be filled with air.","drift":"A bank of snow piled up by the wind.","beaming":"Smiling widely.","slushy":"Partly melted and watery."},"questions":[{"q":"Why was school closed?","o":["The heating broke","Heavy snow","A teachers’ strike","A holiday"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“SCHOOL CLOSED TODAY DUE TO HEAVY SNOW.”"},{"q":"What does the simile “flakes as big as moths” suggest?","o":["The snowflakes were large, soft and fluttering","The snow was dirty","The snow was falling fast and hard","Moths were flying"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Moths are soft and flutter, like large drifting flakes."},{"q":"What does the writer mean by “the special silence that only snow can make”?","o":["Snow muffles sound, making everything unusually quiet","Everyone was asleep","Zainab was deaf","Snow makes a loud noise"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“As if someone had turned the volume of the whole world down.”"},{"q":"What does Mr Barker whistling in the snow suggest?","o":["The snow had put even grumpy people in a good mood","He was angry","He was lost","He was calling his dog"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"He usually complained, but now he was whistling."},{"q":"Why did Zainab and Idris clear Mrs Okafor’s path?","o":["She paid them","They realised she lived alone, used a frame and could not clear it herself","Their mum told them to","They wanted hot chocolate"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"She lived alone and walked with a frame, and her path was buried."},{"q":"What does Zainab remember most about the snow day?","o":["The sledging","The French test","The kindness shared with Mrs Okafor","Missing school"],"a":2,"skill":"Summary","why":"She thought “not of the sledging but of the warm mug … and Mrs Okafor’s smile.”"}]},{"id":"y150","title":"The Moon: Our Closest Neighbour","kind":"Science","theme":"space","text":["On a clear night, the Moon is the brightest object in the sky. It has guided travellers, inspired poets, marked out calendars and pulled the tides for as long as there have been people to notice. Yet it does not shine with its own light at all. Like a mirror, it simply reflects light from the Sun.","The Moon is about 384,000 kilometres from Earth – roughly the distance you would travel if you drove around the world nine and a half times. It is about a quarter of the width of the Earth, making it one of the largest moons in the solar system compared with the planet it orbits. Its gravity is only about one-sixth of the Earth’s, which is why astronauts could bound across its surface in great leaps.","Most scientists believe the Moon formed about 4.5 billion years ago, when a planet-sized object, perhaps as large as Mars, smashed into the young Earth. The collision threw out an enormous cloud of molten rock and debris, which gradually clumped together to form the Moon. Rocks brought back by the Apollo astronauts support this idea, because they are made of very similar material to the Earth’s outer layers.","The Moon’s surface is grey and dusty, covered in craters made by asteroids and comets crashing into it. Because the Moon has almost no atmosphere and no weather, these craters do not wear away, and some are billions of years old. The large dark patches you can see with your eyes are called maria, from the Latin for ‘seas’, because early astronomers thought they might be oceans. In fact, they are vast plains of hardened lava from ancient volcanic eruptions.","The Moon takes about 27 days to orbit the Earth, and it also takes about 27 days to spin once on its axis. Because these two times are the same, the Moon always keeps the same side facing us. The side we never see from Earth is often wrongly called the ‘dark side’; in fact it gets just as much sunlight as the near side. It is simply the far side.","As the Moon travels around the Earth, we see different amounts of its sunlit half, which is why it seems to change shape. These changes are called phases. At new moon, the sunlit side faces away from us and the Moon is almost invisible. Over the next two weeks it grows, or waxes, through a crescent and a half moon to a full moon, when the whole sunlit side faces us. Then it shrinks, or wanes, back to new. The whole cycle takes about 29.5 days, which is where the word ‘month’ comes from.","The Moon’s gravity pulls on the Earth’s oceans, creating a bulge of water on the side facing the Moon and another on the opposite side. As the Earth spins, coastlines pass through these bulges, giving most places two high tides and two low tides each day.","Humans first walked on the Moon in 1969, and twelve astronauts visited it between then and 1972. Now space agencies are planning to return, this time to explore the south pole, where frozen water may be hidden in permanently shadowed craters – water that could one day help people live and work on the Moon."],"glossary":{"reflects":"Sends back light from a surface.","orbits":"Travels around a planet or star in a curved path.","molten":"Melted by great heat.","debris":"Scattered pieces of broken material.","craters":"Large round holes made by impacts.","atmosphere":"The layer of gases around a planet.","phases":"The different shapes of the Moon we see during a month.","waxes":"Grows larger.","wanes":"Gets smaller."},"questions":[{"q":"Why does the Moon shine?","o":["It burns like a star","It reflects light from the Sun","It glows from volcanoes","It is made of ice"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“It simply reflects light from the Sun.”"},{"q":"Why could astronauts leap so high on the Moon?","o":["They had special boots","The Moon’s gravity is only about one-sixth of Earth’s","There is no air","They were very fit"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Its gravity is “only about one-sixth of the Earth’s.”"},{"q":"What evidence supports the idea that the Moon formed from a collision with Earth?","o":["Moon rocks are made of very similar material to the Earth’s outer layers","There are footprints on the Moon","The Moon is round","The Moon causes tides"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Apollo rocks “are made of very similar material to the Earth’s outer layers.”"},{"q":"Why is the ‘dark side’ of the Moon a misleading name?","o":["It actually receives as much sunlight; it is just the far side we never see","It is very bright","It does not exist","It is always dark"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“It gets just as much sunlight as the near side. It is simply the far side.”"},{"q":"Why do we always see the same side of the Moon?","o":["The Moon does not spin","It takes the same time to spin once as to orbit the Earth","The Earth blocks the other side","The other side is hidden by clouds"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Because the orbit and spin both take about 27 days."},{"q":"Why are space agencies interested in the Moon’s south pole?","o":["It is warmer","Frozen water may be hidden there, which could help people live on the Moon","It has the biggest craters","It faces the Sun all the time"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"“Frozen water may be hidden in permanently shadowed craters.”"}]},{"id":"y151","title":"Dragonfly","kind":"Poem","theme":"garden","text":["For two years it lived in the dark of the pond,\na mud-coloured thing with a hunter’s jaw,\ncreeping on six legs through the weed,\nsnatching tadpoles, fearing the heron’s shadow,\nknowing nothing of air.","Then, one June morning, it climbed a reed\ninto a world it had never seen –\nlight, wind, the enormous sky –\nand clung there,\nshaking.","Its back split open like a seed.\nSlowly, slowly, something pulled itself free:\ncrumpled wings, wet and folded,\na long body, pale as glass,\nhanging from its own old skin.","All morning it waited.\nThe sun dried it. Blood pumped into its wings\nuntil they spread, stiff and shining,\nveined like leaves,\nlike stained-glass windows.","And then – no lesson, no practice,\nno one to show it how –\nit flew.\nA blue spark over the water,\nstitching the summer air.","I think of it sometimes,\nwhen I am waiting for something to begin.\nHow long it spent in the dark,\nnot knowing it had wings."],"glossary":{"heron’s":"Belonging to a heron, a tall wading bird with long legs that eats fish and frogs.","reed":"A tall, thin plant that grows in or near water.","crumpled":"Crushed or creased.","veined":"Marked with thin lines.","stitching":"Sewing; here, darting back and forth like a needle."},"questions":[{"q":"Where does the dragonfly spend its first two years?","o":["In a tree","Underwater in a pond","In the sky","In a garden shed"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“For two years it lived in the dark of the pond.”"},{"q":"What does “knowing nothing of air” suggest?","o":["The young dragonfly has no idea of the life it will have later","It cannot breathe","It is about to die","It is very clever"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Underwater, it knows nothing of flying, which comes later."},{"q":"“Its back split open like a seed.” What does this simile suggest?","o":["Something new and alive is emerging, as a plant grows from a seed","The dragonfly is injured","The dragonfly is a plant","Seeds are dangerous"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"A splitting seed releases new life, just as the adult dragonfly emerges."},{"q":"Why are the wings compared to stained-glass windows?","o":["They are made of glass","They are delicate, patterned and shining with colour when light passes through","They are heavy","They are in a church"],"a":1,"skill":"Language","why":"Stained glass is beautiful, patterned and glowing, like the veined wings."},{"q":"What is surprising about the dragonfly’s first flight?","o":["It happens with no lesson, practice or teacher","It fails","It flies backwards","It takes years to learn"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“No lesson, no practice, / no one to show it how – / it flew.”"},{"q":"What does the speaker take from the dragonfly in the final stanza?","o":["That waiting in difficult times may come before discovering hidden abilities","That ponds are dangerous","That insects are frightening","That summer is too short"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"They think of it “when I am waiting for something to begin … not knowing it had wings.”"}]},{"id":"y152","title":"The Silk Road","kind":"History","theme":"desert","text":["There was never one single road called the Silk Road. Instead, the name describes a vast web of trade routes that, for more than 1,500 years, linked China with Central Asia, India, Persia, the Middle East and Europe. These routes stretched for around 6,400 kilometres, across deserts, mountains and grasslands, and they carried not only goods but ideas, religions, inventions and diseases between the great civilisations of the ancient and medieval world.","The routes became important around the second century BC, when the Chinese Han dynasty sent an envoy named Zhang Qian west to find allies against nomadic raiders. He returned with news of rich kingdoms beyond China’s borders, and of their magnificent horses. Soon, Chinese merchants were trading westwards, and the name ‘Silk Road’ was invented much later, in 1877, by a German geographer, Ferdinand von Richthofen.","Silk was the most famous product. For centuries, only the Chinese knew how to make it from the cocoons of silkworms, and they guarded the secret closely; revealing it could be punished by death. In Rome, silk was so fashionable and expensive that some politicians complained it was draining the empire’s wealth. According to legend, the secret was eventually smuggled out of China in the sixth century AD by monks who hid silkworm eggs inside hollow walking sticks.","But silk was only one of many goods. Westward went porcelain, tea, paper and spices. Eastward travelled gold, silver, glass, wool, horses and grapes. Few merchants travelled the whole route. Instead, goods passed from trader to trader in a long chain, increasing in price at every step. Merchants travelled in large groups called caravans, with hundreds of camels, for protection against bandits. The two-humped Bactrian camel was especially valuable, because it could carry heavy loads, survive freezing winters and go for days without water.","Along the way, great trading cities grew rich, such as Samarkand and Bukhara in modern Uzbekistan, and Kashgar on the edge of the Taklamakan Desert, whose name is sometimes said to mean ‘you go in, but you don’t come out’. Travellers rested in caravanserais, roadside inns built around courtyards where animals and goods could be kept safe for the night.","Ideas travelled too. Buddhism spread from India into China along the Silk Road. Paper-making and printing moved west from China, eventually transforming learning in Europe. Mathematics, astronomy and medicine were shared between Arab, Persian, Indian and Chinese scholars. Musical instruments, recipes and styles of art crossed continents.","Not everything that travelled was welcome. Historians believe that the Black Death, which killed so many people in Europe in the 1340s, spread west from Central Asia along these trade routes.","In the thirteenth century, the Mongol Empire controlled most of the land routes and kept them relatively safe, and travellers such as the Venetian Marco Polo journeyed all the way to China. But as sea routes became faster and cheaper from the fifteenth century onwards, the overland Silk Road declined. Today, China’s Belt and Road Initiative aims to build new railways, ports and roads across many of the same regions – a modern echo of the ancient routes."],"glossary":{"dynasty":"A series of rulers from the same family.","envoy":"A messenger sent by a ruler on a special mission.","nomadic":"Moving from place to place rather than living in one spot.","cocoons":"Silky cases that some insects spin around themselves.","smuggled":"Moved secretly and illegally.","porcelain":"Fine, hard, white pottery.","caravans":"Groups of people travelling together with animals across a desert.","caravanserais":"Roadside inns for travellers and their animals.","declined":"Became less important or smaller."},"questions":[{"q":"What was the Silk Road?","o":["A single long road paved with silk","A web of trade routes linking China with Asia, the Middle East and Europe","A river in China","A Roman road"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The name describes a vast web of trade routes.”"},{"q":"When was the name ‘Silk Road’ first used?","o":["In the second century BC","In 1877, by a German geographer","By Marco Polo","By the Romans"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It was “invented much later, in 1877, by a German geographer.”"},{"q":"Why did the Chinese guard the secret of silk-making so closely?","o":["It was dangerous","It made silk rare and valuable, giving China great wealth","Silkworms were sacred","The Romans asked them to"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Only China knew the secret, so silk was extremely expensive abroad."},{"q":"Why did goods become more expensive as they travelled?","o":["They were passed from trader to trader, increasing in price at each step","Camels charged a lot","Taxes were banned","They were damaged"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Goods passed “in a long chain, increasing in price at every step.”"},{"q":"Which of these was spread along the Silk Road but was NOT welcome?","o":["Buddhism","Paper-making","The Black Death","Mathematics"],"a":2,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Not everything that travelled was welcome … the Black Death.”"},{"q":"Why did the overland Silk Road decline?","o":["Silk went out of fashion","Sea routes became faster and cheaper","Camels died out","The Mongols closed it"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"“As sea routes became faster and cheaper … the overland Silk Road declined.”"}]},{"id":"y153","title":"Magnets: The Invisible Force","kind":"Science","theme":"lab","text":["Hold two magnets close together and you can feel something strange: an invisible push or pull, with nothing touching. Turn one magnet round and the pull becomes a push. Magnetism is one of the fundamental forces of nature, and without it, much of modern life – from electricity to computers to hospital scanners – would be impossible.","Every magnet has two ends, called poles: a north pole and a south pole. The rule is simple. Opposite poles attract, pulling towards each other. Like poles – north and north, or south and south – repel, pushing each other away. If you cut a bar magnet in half, you do not get a separate north and south; you get two smaller magnets, each with its own north and south pole.","The space around a magnet where its force can be felt is called its magnetic field. You can see the shape of a field by sprinkling iron filings on paper over a bar magnet: they arrange themselves into curved lines running from one pole to the other. The field is strongest at the poles, where the lines are closest together.","Only a few materials are magnetic – that is, attracted to a magnet. The most common are iron, nickel and cobalt, and alloys such as steel that contain iron. Most other metals, including aluminium, copper, gold and silver, are not magnetic, which is why recycling centres can use giant magnets to separate steel cans from aluminium ones. Wood, plastic and glass are not magnetic either.","The Earth itself is a giant magnet. Deep inside the planet, liquid iron in the outer core swirls around, generating a magnetic field that extends far into space. This is why a compass works: its needle is a tiny magnet that lines up with the Earth’s field, pointing roughly north. Navigators have used compasses for at least a thousand years; the first ones were made in China from a magnetic stone called lodestone. Many animals, including migrating birds, sea turtles and some fish, can sense the Earth’s magnetic field and use it to find their way across thousands of kilometres. Curiously, the Earth’s magnetic poles are not fixed. They slowly wander, and over the history of the planet, they have completely swapped places many times.","In 1820, a Danish scientist, Hans Christian Ørsted, noticed that a compass needle moved when he switched on an electric current in a wire nearby. He had discovered that electricity creates magnetism. A wire coiled around an iron core and connected to a battery becomes an electromagnet, which can be switched on and off. Electromagnets are used in scrapyard cranes to lift cars, in doorbells, in loudspeakers and in the motors that turn everything from washing machines to electric cars. A few years later, Michael Faraday showed that the reverse is also true: moving a magnet near a wire produces electricity. This is how power stations generate electricity today.","Magnets even help doctors see inside the body. MRI scanners use extremely powerful magnets – tens of thousands of times stronger than a fridge magnet – along with radio waves to create detailed images of organs, without using harmful X-rays. Not bad for an invisible force."],"glossary":{"fundamental":"Basic and very important.","attract":"To pull towards.","repel":"To push away.","filings":"Tiny pieces of metal.","alloys":"Metals made by mixing two or more metals.","navigators":"People who plan and direct a route, especially at sea.","lodestone":"A naturally magnetic stone.","migrating":"Travelling from one region to another at certain seasons.","electromagnet":"A magnet made by passing electricity through a coil of wire."},"questions":[{"q":"What happens when two north poles are brought together?","o":["They attract","They repel","Nothing","They become one magnet"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Like poles … repel, pushing each other away.”"},{"q":"What happens if you cut a bar magnet in half?","o":["You get a separate north and south","You get two smaller magnets, each with a north and south pole","It stops being magnetic","It explodes"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“You get two smaller magnets, each with its own north and south pole.”"},{"q":"Why can recycling centres use magnets to separate cans?","o":["Steel is magnetic but aluminium is not","Aluminium is magnetic","Both are magnetic","Magnets crush cans"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Steel contains iron and is magnetic; aluminium is not."},{"q":"Why does a compass point north?","o":["It is pulled by the Sun","Its needle is a tiny magnet that lines up with the Earth’s magnetic field","It is heavy at one end","It is programmed to"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Its needle is a tiny magnet that lines up with the Earth’s field.”"},{"q":"What did Ørsted discover?","o":["That electricity creates magnetism","That magnets are made of iron","That the Earth is round","How to make compasses"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“He had discovered that electricity creates magnetism.”"},{"q":"What is the tone of the final sentence, “Not bad for an invisible force”?","o":["Light-hearted and admiring","Angry","Sad","Confused"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"It is a playful, approving comment on magnetism’s many uses."}]},{"id":"y154","title":"Harriet Tubman: Conductor on the Underground Railroad","kind":"Biography","theme":"forest","text":["Harriet Tubman was born into slavery in Maryland, in the United States, around 1822. She was named Araminta Ross, and as a child she was ‘hired out’ to other enslaved people’s owners, forced to work as a nursemaid, checking muskrat traps in icy rivers and labouring in the fields. She was often beaten. When she was about twelve, she was hit on the head by a heavy metal weight thrown by an overseer at another enslaved person. The injury nearly killed her and caused seizures, headaches and sudden sleeping spells for the rest of her life.","In 1849, fearing that she was about to be sold and separated from her family, she escaped. Travelling mostly at night, she walked about 145 kilometres north to Pennsylvania, a state where slavery was illegal. She later described her feelings when she crossed the line into freedom: ‘I looked at my hands to see if I was the same person. There was such a glory over everything.’ She took the name Harriet, after her mother, and Tubman, the surname of her husband.","But freedom alone did not satisfy her. Her family was still enslaved. Over the next eleven years, Tubman returned to Maryland around thirteen times to lead others to freedom, guiding about seventy people – including her parents, brothers and their families – and giving instructions that helped dozens more escape on their own.","She travelled along the Underground Railroad. This was not an actual railway, but a secret network of routes and safe houses, run by free black people and white supporters, who sheltered people escaping slavery. People used railway words as code: guides were ‘conductors’, escaping people were ‘passengers’ and the safe houses were ‘stations’. Tubman became its most famous conductor.","Her journeys were extremely dangerous. Rewards were offered for the capture of escaping people, and after the Fugitive Slave Act of 1850, people who escaped could be captured even in free states and returned to slavery, so Tubman led many of them all the way to Canada. She travelled in winter, when the nights were long, and set off on Saturday nights, because notices about runaways would not appear in newspapers until Monday. She used disguises, sang songs with hidden messages and followed the North Star. She carried a gun, both for protection and, it is said, to encourage frightened passengers not to turn back and endanger everyone. She later said proudly: ‘I never ran my train off the track, and I never lost a passenger.’","During the American Civil War, Tubman worked for the Union army as a cook, nurse and spy. In 1863, she guided a raid on plantations along the Combahee River in South Carolina, which freed more than 700 enslaved people. She was the first woman to lead an armed assault in the war.","After the war, she settled in Auburn, New York, where she cared for elderly people, campaigned for women’s right to vote and opened a home for the aged. She died in 1913. She is remembered as one of the bravest Americans who ever lived – a woman who, having won her own freedom, risked it again and again so that others could share it."],"glossary":{"overseer":"A person who supervised enslaved workers.","seizures":"Sudden attacks of illness in which a person may shake or lose consciousness.","enslaved":"Forced to be a slave, owned by another person.","network":"A group of connected people or places.","fugitive":"A person who is running away, especially from the law.","disguises":"Clothes or appearances used to hide who you are.","plantations":"Large farms where crops such as cotton were grown, often by enslaved people.","assault":"A violent attack."},"questions":[{"q":"What caused Harriet Tubman’s lifelong seizures and headaches?","o":["A disease","Being hit on the head by a heavy weight thrown by an overseer","A fall from a horse","Lack of food"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"She was “hit on the head by a heavy metal weight thrown by an overseer.”"},{"q":"Why did Tubman escape in 1849?","o":["She feared being sold and separated from her family","She wanted to see Canada","Her owner set her free","She was bored"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Fearing that she was about to be sold and separated from her family, she escaped.”"},{"q":"What was the Underground Railroad?","o":["A real train under the ground","A secret network of routes and safe houses that helped people escape slavery","A railway company","A tunnel to Canada"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It was “a secret network of routes and safe houses.”"},{"q":"Why did Tubman set off on Saturday nights?","o":["It was her day off","Notices about runaways would not appear in newspapers until Monday","The moon was brightest","Trains ran on Saturdays"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"It gave them a head start before newspapers could spread the alarm."},{"q":"What does her quotation “I never lost a passenger” show?","o":["Her pride in her skill and success at keeping people safe","She was a train driver","She lost her map","She was boastful about money"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"She proudly notes that everyone she guided reached safety."},{"q":"What does the final sentence emphasise about Tubman?","o":["She was mainly a spy","She repeatedly risked her own freedom to free others","She disliked her family","She was famous for cooking"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"Having won her freedom, she “risked it again and again so that others could share it.”"}]},{"id":"y155","title":"The Lantern Festival","kind":"Story","theme":"city","text":["Every year, on the fifteenth day of the first month of the Chinese calendar, Grandma Lin made lanterns. This year, for the first time, she was making them in Manchester.","She had arrived from Fujian in October to live with Mei’s family, and she had not liked anything about England so far. Not the rain, which she said was ‘lazy’, because it never properly fell but just hung about. Not the food, which she said tasted of cardboard. Not the cold, grey streets, where nobody greeted anybody. She spoke almost no English, and Mei spoke only a little of the Fujianese dialect Grandma used. Mostly, they sat in the same room and said nothing.","But when Mei came home from school on Tuesday, the kitchen table had disappeared under red paper, bamboo sticks, glue, scissors and string. Grandma looked up, and for the first time since she had arrived, she smiled.","‘Yuánxiāo,’ she said, and pointed at the calendar. Then she pointed at a chair.","Mei sat down.","For the next three evenings, they made lanterns. Grandma did not explain; she showed. She split bamboo with a knife, faster than Mei could follow, and bent it into shapes – a round lantern, a fish, a rabbit with long ears. She showed Mei how to glue the paper so it did not wrinkle, how to cut paper tassels and how to fold a strip of gold paper into tiny repeated patterns. When Mei made a mistake, Grandma clicked her tongue and fixed it without a word. When Mei got something right, Grandma nodded once, sharply, which Mei came to understand was the highest possible praise.","On the second evening, Grandma began to talk while she worked, slowly, partly to herself, using some words Mei knew and many she did not. Mei caught enough to understand that Grandma was describing the Lantern Festival in her village when she was a girl – the streets full of light, the riddles written on lanterns for people to guess, the sweet sticky rice balls called tangyuan, the dragon dancers. Mei asked her phone to translate the words she did not know, and Grandma laughed at the phone’s strange robot voice. It was the first time Mei had heard her laugh.","On the night of the festival, they hung their lanterns in the front window: eleven of them, red and gold, glowing from the candles inside. Mei had written riddles on slips of paper and tied them to the bottom, in English and in shaky Chinese characters that Grandma had corrected twice.","Then something unexpected happened. People stopped. A woman walking her dog stopped, and then a man coming home from work, and then two girls from Mei’s school. They pointed and smiled. Someone knocked on the door to ask what the lanterns were for. Mei explained, and Grandma, standing behind her, insisted on giving them each a bowl of tangyuan. By the end of the evening, there were nine neighbours in the kitchen, guessing riddles, eating rice balls and asking Grandma questions that Mei translated as best she could.","Later, when everyone had gone, Grandma stood at the window looking at the lanterns, and the street, and the soft lazy rain falling through the light.","‘Maybe,’ she said, in careful English, ‘is not so bad here.’"],"glossary":{"dialect":"A form of a language spoken in a particular area.","bamboo":"A tall plant with hollow woody stems.","tassels":"Bunches of threads tied at one end, used as decoration.","riddles":"Puzzling questions with clever answers.","insisted":"Said firmly that something must happen."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did Grandma think English rain was ‘lazy’?","o":["It never properly fell but just hung about","It was very heavy","It only fell at night","It was warm"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“It never properly fell but just hung about.”"},{"q":"What does the silence between Mei and Grandma at the start show?","o":["They disliked each other","A language barrier and unfamiliarity kept them apart","They were tired","They were angry"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Grandma spoke almost no English and Mei spoke little of Grandma’s dialect."},{"q":"How did Grandma teach Mei to make lanterns?","o":["By giving long explanations","By showing her rather than explaining","By using a video","By writing instructions"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Grandma did not explain; she showed.”"},{"q":"What does Grandma’s single sharp nod mean?","o":["She is angry","It is her highest form of praise","She is tired","She disagrees"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Mei understood it was “the highest possible praise.”"},{"q":"How do the lanterns change things for Grandma?","o":["They help her connect with her granddaughter and new neighbours","They make her want to go home","They make her ill","They annoy the neighbours"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"She laughs with Mei and welcomes nine neighbours into the kitchen."},{"q":"Why is it significant that Grandma speaks in English at the end?","o":["It shows she is beginning to accept and feel more at home in England","She has forgotten her own language","She is angry","She wants to leave"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Her careful English words, ‘is not so bad here’, show a change of heart."}]},{"id":"y156","title":"Bats: Masters of the Night","kind":"Non-fiction","theme":"forest","text":["As the sun sets on a summer evening, look up. Around streetlights, over rivers and along the edges of woods, small dark shapes flicker and swerve through the dusk, too fast to follow. They are bats – the only mammals in the world that can truly fly.","Bats are remarkably successful animals. There are more than 1,400 species, making up about a fifth of all mammal species on Earth. They live on every continent except Antarctica. The largest, the flying foxes of Asia and Australia, have wingspans of up to 1.7 metres; the smallest, Kitti’s hog-nosed bat from Thailand, weighs about two grams – less than a penny – and is one of the smallest mammals in the world.","A bat’s wing is really a hand. If you look at its skeleton, you can see the same bones as in your own hand, but with the fingers hugely lengthened, and a thin, stretchy skin called a membrane stretched between them. This lets bats change the shape of their wings as they fly, making them extraordinarily agile.","Many bats hunt in the dark using echolocation. As they fly, they make very high-pitched calls, usually too high for humans to hear, and listen for the echoes bouncing back from objects around them. From these echoes, a bat can work out the size, shape, distance and speed of an insect, and even its texture, and catch it in mid-air. Some bats can detect an object as thin as a human hair. A single common pipistrelle, Britain’s most widespread bat, can eat around 3,000 tiny insects such as midges in one night.","There are seventeen species of bat that breed in the UK, and all of them eat insects. In summer, females gather in groups called maternity roosts, often in the roofs of houses, barns and churches, where they give birth to a single baby, called a pup. In winter, when insects are scarce, British bats hibernate in cool, quiet places such as caves, tunnels and old buildings, slowing their heart rate and body temperature to save energy.","Bats are hugely important to ecosystems and to people. Insect-eating bats control pests that damage crops and spread disease. In tropical regions, fruit- and nectar-eating bats pollinate plants and spread seeds; bananas, mangoes, avocados and the agave plant used to make tequila all depend on bats. The seeds that bats drop help rainforests to regrow after trees are cut down.","Despite this, bats have a bad reputation, and they face many threats. Old buildings where they roost are renovated or demolished, hedgerows and ponds where they feed are lost, and pesticides reduce the insects they eat. Artificial light can confuse them and disturb their feeding. Many bat populations in Britain declined sharply during the twentieth century. As a result, all British bats and their roosts are now protected by law, and it is illegal to disturb them.","You can help bats by planting flowers that attract night-flying insects, such as honeysuckle and evening primrose, by creating a pond, and by turning off unnecessary outside lights. And on a warm evening, if you stand quietly at dusk, you may be lucky enough to watch these remarkable hunters at work."],"glossary":{"mammals":"Warm-blooded animals that feed their young with milk.","membrane":"A thin, flexible layer of skin or tissue.","agile":"Able to move quickly and easily.","echolocation":"Finding things by sending out sounds and listening for echoes.","maternity":"To do with mothers and giving birth.","scarce":"In short supply.","hibernate":"To spend the winter in a deep sleep-like state.","pollinate":"To carry pollen from one flower to another.","renovated":"Repaired and improved."},"questions":[{"q":"What makes bats unique among mammals?","o":["They are the smallest","They are the only mammals that can truly fly","They only eat fruit","They live underwater"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The only mammals in the world that can truly fly.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer say “A bat’s wing is really a hand”?","o":["It has the same bones as a hand, with long fingers and skin between them","Bats can hold things","Bats wear gloves","Bats walk on their wings"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Its skeleton shows the same bones as a hand, with lengthened fingers and a membrane."},{"q":"How does echolocation work?","o":["Bats see in the dark","Bats make high calls and listen for echoes to locate objects","Bats follow the Moon","Bats smell insects"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"They make high-pitched calls and listen for echoes bouncing back."},{"q":"What do British bats do in winter?","o":["Fly south","Hibernate in cool, quiet places","Eat fruit","Build nests"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“British bats hibernate in cool, quiet places.”"},{"q":"Which of these depends on bats, according to the article?","o":["Apples","Bananas and mangoes","Potatoes","Wheat"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Bananas, mangoes, avocados and the agave plant … all depend on bats.”"},{"q":"Why are all British bats protected by law?","o":["They are dangerous","Their populations declined sharply because of lost roosts, feeding areas and insects","They are pets","They spread disease"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Threats caused bat populations to decline, so they were given legal protection."}]},{"id":"y157","title":"Should Children Have Smartphones at Eleven?","kind":"Discussion","theme":"computer","text":["For many children in the UK, starting secondary school and getting a smartphone happen at about the same time. Surveys suggest that around nine in ten eleven-year-olds have a phone of their own. But in recent years, a growing number of parents, teachers and campaign groups have questioned whether this is a good idea. Some parents have even made pacts with other families to delay giving their children smartphones until they are fourteen or older. So should eleven-year-olds have smartphones?","Those in favour point to safety. Many Year 7 pupils travel to school alone for the first time, often on buses or trains, and a phone lets them contact their parents if they are late, lost or in trouble. Parents can use location-sharing apps to check that their children have arrived safely. For many families, this peace of mind is the main reason for buying a phone.","Supporters also argue that smartphones are simply part of modern life. Much of children’s social life – arranging to meet, sharing jokes, keeping up with friends – now happens on messaging apps. A child without a phone, they say, may feel left out and isolated. Phones are also useful for learning: for checking homework platforms, looking up information, using language apps and creating videos and music.","Those who want to delay smartphones raise serious concerns. Some researchers argue that the rise in anxiety, depression and loneliness among teenagers in the 2010s was linked to the spread of smartphones and social media, although other researchers say the evidence is not yet clear. Many parents worry about children being exposed to harmful content, such as violent or upsetting images, and about online bullying, which can follow a child home and continue at any time of day or night.","Critics also point to the effect on sleep and concentration. Notifications and endless scrolling can make it hard to focus on homework and to switch off at bedtime, and many children take their phones to bed. Some studies suggest that heavy phone use can reduce the time children spend outdoors, reading, playing sport or talking face to face.","Many people suggest a middle way. A basic phone that can make calls and send texts, but has no internet access or social media, can give parents peace of mind about safety without the risks of a smartphone. Others recommend setting clear family rules: no phones in bedrooms at night, no phones at mealtimes, limits on screen time and apps, and open conversations about what to do if something online feels wrong. Many schools now ban phones during the school day, or ask pupils to keep them switched off in their bags.","There is no single right answer, and every family and every child is different. What most experts agree on is that it is not just about whether a child has a phone, but about how it is used – and that children and parents should talk honestly about the benefits and the risks. What do you think is the right age?"],"glossary":{"pacts":"Agreements between people to do or not do something.","isolated":"Alone and separated from others.","platforms":"Websites or apps where people share or use content.","anxiety":"A feeling of worry and nervousness.","exposed":"Put in contact with something harmful.","notifications":"Alerts on a phone telling you something has happened.","concentration":"The ability to focus your attention."},"questions":[{"q":"About how many eleven-year-olds in the UK have their own phone, according to surveys?","o":["One in ten","Half","Nine in ten","All of them"],"a":2,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Around nine in ten eleven-year-olds have a phone of their own.”"},{"q":"What is the main safety argument for giving children phones?","o":["They can play games on the bus","They can contact parents if lost or in trouble, and parents can check they arrived","Phones are cheap","Phones make children popular"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"A phone lets them contact parents, and location apps show they arrived safely."},{"q":"Why might a child without a phone feel left out?","o":["Much social life now happens on messaging apps","Teachers ignore them","They cannot do PE","They have no friends at all"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Arranging to meet and keeping up with friends happens on messaging apps."},{"q":"Why is online bullying described as especially difficult?","o":["It only happens at school","It can follow a child home and continue at any time","It is easy to stop","It is always anonymous"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It “can follow a child home and continue at any time of day or night.”"},{"q":"What is suggested as a ‘middle way’?","o":["A basic phone without internet, or clear family rules about use","Banning all technology","Giving every child the newest smartphone","Only using phones at school"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"A basic phone, or rules such as no phones in bedrooms, are suggested."},{"q":"What do most experts agree on, according to the conclusion?","o":["Every child should have a smartphone at eleven","How a phone is used matters, and families should talk openly about benefits and risks","Phones should be banned","Only teachers should decide"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"“It is not just about whether a child has a phone, but about how it is used.”"}]},{"id":"y158","title":"The Spanish Armada, 1588","kind":"History","theme":"ship","text":["In the summer of 1588, the largest fleet that had ever sailed in European waters left Spain. It was made up of around 130 ships carrying about 30,000 soldiers and sailors, and its aim was simple: to invade England and overthrow its queen, Elizabeth I. The Spanish called it ‘la felicísima armada’ – the most fortunate fleet. History would remember it rather differently.","Relations between England and Spain had been getting worse for years. Spain was a Catholic country, and its king, Philip II, wanted to restore Catholicism in England, which Elizabeth had made Protestant. Philip had once been married to Elizabeth’s half-sister, Mary I, and had even proposed to Elizabeth herself. He was also furious that English ‘sea dogs’ such as Francis Drake had been attacking Spanish treasure ships returning from the Americas, often with Elizabeth’s secret approval, and that England was helping Protestant rebels fighting against Spanish rule in the Netherlands. The final straw came in 1587, when Elizabeth agreed to the execution of the Catholic Mary, Queen of Scots, whom many Catholics regarded as the rightful queen of England.","Philip’s plan was for the Armada to sail up the English Channel to the Netherlands, collect a large Spanish army waiting there under the Duke of Parma, and ferry it across to invade England. The Armada was commanded by the Duke of Medina Sidonia, an experienced administrator but not a sailor; he had begged the king not to give him the job.","When the Armada was sighted off Cornwall on 19 July, a chain of beacons was lit along the coast to carry the news to London. According to a famous story, Drake was playing bowls on Plymouth Hoe and insisted on finishing his game before going out to fight. Whether or not that is true, the tide meant that the English ships could not leave harbour immediately anyway.","The Spanish ships sailed up the Channel in a tight crescent formation, which the English found hard to break. The English ships were smaller, faster and easier to handle, and they had long-range guns, so they could attack from a distance without getting close enough for the Spanish soldiers to board them. For a week, the two fleets fought a series of running battles, but neither side won a decisive victory.","Then the Armada anchored off Calais to wait for Parma’s army, which was not ready. On the night of 7 August, the English sent eight old ships, set on fire and packed with gunpowder, drifting towards the anchored fleet on the wind and tide. These fireships caused panic. Many Spanish captains cut their anchor cables and scattered into the night. The next day, at the Battle of Gravelines, the English attacked the scattered fleet and did serious damage.","Strong winds then drove the Armada north into the North Sea. Unable to sail back down the Channel, the Spanish ships had to go all the way around Scotland and Ireland to get home. There, they met terrible storms. Many ships were wrecked on the rocky coasts of Scotland and Ireland. Of the 130 ships that had set out, only around 60 or 70 returned to Spain, and perhaps half of the men died, many from hunger, disease and shipwreck rather than battle.","In England, the victory was celebrated as a sign of God’s favour. A medal was struck reading ‘He blew with His winds, and they were scattered.’ Elizabeth had earlier given a famous speech to her troops at Tilbury: ‘I know I have the body of a weak and feeble woman; but I have the heart and stomach of a king, and of a king of England too.’"],"glossary":{"fleet":"A group of ships sailing together.","overthrow":"To remove someone from power by force.","restore":"To bring back.","administrator":"A person who manages and organises things.","beacons":"Fires lit on high ground as signals.","formation":"A particular arrangement of things, such as ships.","decisive":"Settling something clearly.","fireships":"Ships set on fire and sent towards enemy ships.","feeble":"Weak."},"questions":[{"q":"What was the aim of the Spanish Armada?","o":["To trade with England","To invade England and overthrow Elizabeth I","To explore America","To rescue Mary, Queen of Scots"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“To invade England and overthrow its queen, Elizabeth I.”"},{"q":"Which event was “the final straw” for Philip II?","o":["Drake’s game of bowls","The execution of Mary, Queen of Scots","A storm","Elizabeth’s refusal to marry him"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The final straw came in 1587, when Elizabeth agreed to the execution of … Mary, Queen of Scots.”"},{"q":"Why was Medina Sidonia perhaps a poor choice of commander?","o":["He was not a sailor and had not wanted the job","He was English","He was too young","He was ill"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"He was “not a sailor; he had begged the king not to give him the job.”"},{"q":"What advantage did the English ships have?","o":["They were larger","They were smaller, faster, easier to handle and had long-range guns","They carried more soldiers","They had more ships"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"English ships “were smaller, faster and easier to handle, and they had long-range guns.”"},{"q":"Why were the fireships so effective?","o":["They caused panic and scattered the anchored Spanish fleet","They sank every Spanish ship","They carried soldiers","They blocked the harbour"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Captains cut their anchors and “scattered into the night.”"},{"q":"According to the article, what destroyed most of the Armada?","o":["English cannons alone","Storms and shipwrecks on the long journey home around Scotland and Ireland","Spanish mutinies","Parma’s army"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"Many ships were wrecked in storms; many men died “from hunger, disease and shipwreck rather than battle.”"}]},{"id":"y159","title":"The Pied Piper of Hamelin","kind":"Legend","theme":"city","text":["In the year 1284, so the story goes, the town of Hamelin in Germany had a terrible problem. Rats. There were rats in the bakeries and rats in the barns. Rats ran across the tables while people ate their dinner, and nibbled holes in the cheeses, and bit babies in their cradles. They fought the dogs and killed the cats. They squeaked so loudly that nobody could hear themselves speak.","The townspeople went to the town hall and shouted at the mayor and the councillors. ‘What are we paying you for?’ they demanded. ‘Get rid of the rats, or we will get rid of you!’ The mayor, a fat, comfortable man who did not like being shouted at, promised a reward of a thousand gold coins to anyone who could free the town of its plague.","The next morning, a stranger walked into the council chamber. He was tall and thin, with a long coat of many colours – half red, half yellow – and he carried a slender wooden pipe. ‘I can rid you of your rats,’ he said, ‘for the price you have offered.’","‘A thousand gold coins? Done!’ said the mayor, delighted.","The piper stepped into the street and began to play. It was a strange, wavering tune, and as soon as the first notes sounded, something began to stir in every cellar and attic in Hamelin. Out came the rats – big rats, small rats, brown rats, black rats, grey and tawny rats, fathers, mothers, uncles and cousins – tumbling out of houses in their thousands and following the piper down the street. He led them to the River Weser, waded into the water still playing, and every single rat followed him in and was drowned.","The town rang its bells for joy. But when the piper came back to collect his thousand coins, the mayor looked at the empty streets and changed his mind. The rats were gone. They could hardly come back. Why pay so much for a little music?","‘A thousand coins? That was a joke,’ said the mayor. ‘Take fifty and be grateful.’","The piper’s face grew very still. ‘You made a promise,’ he said quietly. ‘People who break their promises to me find that I play a different tune.’","The mayor laughed in his face.","So the piper stepped back into the street and put his pipe to his lips once more. This time, the music was sweeter than anything anyone in Hamelin had ever heard. And this time, it was not the rats that came. It was the children. Out of every house they ran, laughing and dancing, boys and girls, all the children of the town, following the piper up the street, past their frozen, horrified parents, who found that they could not move or call out. The piper led the children out through the town gate towards a mountain called the Koppelberg. A door opened in the side of the mountain. The piper walked in, and the children followed. Then the door closed, and they were never seen again.","Only one child was left behind: a little boy who was lame and could not keep up. For the rest of his life, he told how the piper had promised them a wonderful land, where the fruit was sweeter and the flowers were brighter, and how he had watched the mountain close before he could reach it.","Nobody knows for certain what lies behind the legend. A record from Hamelin from the 1380s says simply that 130 children left the town in 1284. Historians have suggested many explanations – a disease, a children’s crusade, or young people leaving to settle in new lands in the east. Whatever the truth, the story has lasted more than seven hundred years, with a message that is as true now as it was then: if you make a promise, keep it."],"glossary":{"councillors":"Members of a council that runs a town.","plague":"Here, a huge number of pests causing trouble.","chamber":"A large room used for meetings.","slender":"Thin.","tawny":"An orange-brown colour.","lame":"Unable to walk normally because of an injured leg or foot.","crusade":"Here, a religious journey or expedition.","legend":"An old story that may or may not be true."},"questions":[{"q":"What reward did the mayor promise?","o":["Fifty gold coins","A thousand gold coins","A house","Free food for life"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“A reward of a thousand gold coins.”"},{"q":"How does the writer show how serious the rat problem was?","o":["By listing all the places rats were and the harm they did","By describing the mayor","By describing the river","By describing the piper’s coat"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Rats were in bakeries, barns, tables, cheeses and cradles."},{"q":"Why did the mayor refuse to pay the full reward?","o":["He had no money","The rats were gone and he thought he could get away with paying less","The piper was rude","The rats came back"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“The rats were gone. They could hardly come back. Why pay so much?”"},{"q":"What does the piper mean by “I play a different tune”?","o":["He will play music for a party","He will take revenge in a different way","He will leave town","He knows many songs"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"He warns that promise-breakers will suffer; he then leads away the children."},{"q":"Why was one child left behind?","o":["He was asleep","He was lame and could not keep up","He did not like music","His parents held him"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“A little boy who was lame and could not keep up.”"},{"q":"What is the message of the legend, according to the writer?","o":["Never trust musicians","If you make a promise, keep it","Rats are dangerous","Children should stay indoors"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"The final sentence states this message."}]},{"id":"y160","title":"Mountain Rescue Dogs","kind":"Non-fiction","theme":"mountain","text":["It is a winter night in the Scottish Highlands. Snow is falling, the wind is gusting at sixty kilometres per hour, and somewhere on the mountainside a walker is lost. Visibility is down to a few metres. The rescue team will search for hours. But one member of the team will cover more ground than all the others put together, and she will do it with her nose. She is a search dog.","Mountain rescue in the UK is carried out mostly by volunteers. Around 3,500 people belong to mountain rescue teams in England, Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland, and they are called out by the police when someone is lost, injured or overdue in the hills. Many teams work alongside specially trained dogs, usually Border Collies, Labradors, German Shepherds or Springer Spaniels.","A dog’s sense of smell is extraordinary. Humans have around six million scent receptors in their noses; dogs have up to 300 million, and the part of their brain that analyses smells is, in proportion, around forty times larger than ours. Every human constantly sheds tiny flakes of skin and gives off scent, which drifts away on the wind. Search dogs are trained to detect this ‘air scent’ and follow it back to its source. Unlike a tracking dog, which follows the trail of one particular person, an air-scenting search dog looks for any human scent in an area, so it can find someone even if nobody knows where they went.","One trained dog can search an area that would take a line of twenty human searchers to cover, and dogs work just as well at night and in fog as in daylight. A dog can often detect a person from several hundred metres away, and even locate someone buried under snow after an avalanche.","Training takes a long time. It usually begins when the dog is a puppy and takes around two to three years before the dog is fully qualified. The dog first learns to find its handler, then other people hiding in easy places, and eventually ‘bodies’ – volunteers playing the part of lost walkers – hidden in difficult terrain, at night and in bad weather. When the dog finds someone, it is trained to return to its handler and bark, then lead the handler back to the person. Each find is rewarded with play or a favourite toy; to the dog, a search is the best game in the world.","The dogs must also be calm around helicopters, sheep and other animals, and fit enough to work for hours in rough, steep country. Their handlers, who are also volunteers, must be skilled mountaineers and navigators. Dog and handler train together constantly, and the bond between them is crucial: the handler needs to read the dog’s body language to know when it has picked up a scent.","Every year, mountain rescue teams in the UK respond to thousands of incidents, and search dogs play a vital part. Many people owe their lives to a wagging tail and a very good nose.","If you go walking in the mountains, remember the advice that rescue teams give: check the weather forecast, take a map and compass and know how to use them, carry warm clothing, food, water and a torch, and always tell someone where you are going and when you expect to be back."],"glossary":{"visibility":"How far you can see.","volunteers":"People who work without being paid.","overdue":"Not arrived by the expected time.","receptors":"Cells that detect something, such as smells.","analyses":"Examines something carefully to understand it.","terrain":"An area of land and its features.","handler":"A person who trains and works with an animal.","crucial":"Extremely important.","incidents":"Events, especially emergencies."},"questions":[{"q":"Who carries out most mountain rescue in the UK?","o":["The army","Volunteers","Paid firefighters","The police alone"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Mountain rescue in the UK is carried out mostly by volunteers.”"},{"q":"How many scent receptors can a dog have?","o":["About six million","Up to 300 million","About forty","About twenty"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Dogs have up to 300 million.”"},{"q":"How is an air-scenting dog different from a tracking dog?","o":["It searches for any human scent in an area rather than following one person’s trail","It follows footprints","It only works in daylight","It cannot smell well"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It “looks for any human scent in an area.”"},{"q":"Why are search dogs so valuable in bad weather and at night?","o":["They use smell, which works as well in darkness and fog as in daylight","They have torches","They never get cold","They can see in the dark"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“Dogs work just as well at night and in fog as in daylight.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer say that “to the dog, a search is the best game in the world”?","o":["Because each find is rewarded with play or a favourite toy","Because dogs dislike work","Because searches are short","Because dogs win prizes"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Training rewards each find with play, so the dog enjoys searching."},{"q":"What is the purpose of the final paragraph?","o":["To give practical safety advice to walkers","To describe a rescue","To advertise dog food","To explain dog training"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It lists advice from rescue teams for anyone walking in the mountains."}]},{"id":"y161","title":"The Old Bridge","kind":"Poem","theme":"bridge","text":["I have stood here four hundred years\nwith my feet in the river\nand my back to the weather,\ncarrying whatever you gave me.","Packhorses first, their bells chiming,\nsalt and wool and Sunday bread;\nthen carts, then coaches, wheels grinding\nmy stones to a shine;\nthen the soldiers, marching to a war\nthat took the young men and gave back\nonly some of them.","I have felt the slow heart of the river\nbeat against my piers\nin drought and in flood,\nthe year the water rose to my lips\nand the year it shrank to a thread\nand children walked beneath me\non the dry stones, laughing.","Lovers have carved their names in me.\nBoys have dived from me into the dark pool\nthat their mothers warned them about.\nAn old man stood on me every morning for thirty years\nand fed the ducks,\nand then one morning he did not come.","Now the lorries go by on the new road\nthree fields away, and I am left to the walkers,\nthe dogs, the slow cyclists, the heron\nstanding on one leg in my shadow.","I am not sorry.\nI have carried enough.\nLet me stand here a while longer\nwith my feet in the river,\nlistening to the water\ntell me where it has been."],"glossary":{"packhorses":"Horses used to carry loads on their backs.","chiming":"Ringing like a bell.","piers":"The supports that hold up a bridge.","drought":"A long period with little or no rain.","carved":"Cut into a surface."},"questions":[{"q":"Who is the speaker of the poem?","o":["A river","An old bridge","A walker","An old man"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The bridge speaks: “I have stood here four hundred years / with my feet in the river.”"},{"q":"Why does the poet give the bridge “feet” and a “back”?","o":["To personify it, making it seem like a patient living being","Because it walks","Because it is made of wood","To describe its shape exactly"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Personification lets the bridge speak and feel."},{"q":"How does the second stanza show the passing of time?","o":["It lists what crossed the bridge over the centuries, from packhorses to carts to soldiers","It describes the seasons","It tells the age of the river","It lists the bridge’s builders"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"Packhorses, carts and coaches, then soldiers show history moving forward."},{"q":"What is suggested by “a war / that took the young men and gave back / only some of them”?","o":["Many soldiers did not return from the war","The soldiers got lost","The war was short","The bridge was destroyed"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Only some returned, implying many died."},{"q":"What does the line “and then one morning he did not come” suggest?","o":["The old man had probably died","He moved house","He forgot","He was ill for a day"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"After thirty years of daily visits, his sudden absence suggests his death."},{"q":"How does the bridge feel at the end of the poem?","o":["Angry at being replaced","Content to rest after carrying so much","Afraid of falling down","Lonely and bitter"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"“I am not sorry. / I have carried enough.”"}]},{"id":"y162","title":"William Shakespeare: The Man Behind the Plays","kind":"Biography","theme":"stage","text":["William Shakespeare is the most famous writer in the English language, yet surprisingly little is known for certain about his life. We have no letters written by him, no diaries and only a few signatures. Most of what we know comes from official records: baptisms, marriages, property deals and lawsuits. The rest comes from his extraordinary plays and poems.","Shakespeare was baptised in Stratford-upon-Avon, a market town in Warwickshire, on 26 April 1564. His birthday is traditionally celebrated on 23 April, St George’s Day. His father, John, was a glove-maker who became an important figure in the town, and his mother, Mary Arden, came from a farming family. William probably attended the local grammar school, where boys studied long hours, mostly learning Latin and reading classical writers such as Ovid, whose stories would later inspire his plays.","At eighteen, he married Anne Hathaway, who was twenty-six. They had three children: Susanna, and twins called Hamnet and Judith. Then, for several years, he disappears from the records. Historians call this period the ‘lost years’, and nobody knows what he did. When he reappears in 1592, he is in London, and he is already well enough known as a playwright that a jealous rival, Robert Greene, called him ‘an upstart crow’.","Shakespeare became a member and part-owner of an acting company, the Lord Chamberlain’s Men, which later became the King’s Men when James I came to the throne. He acted in plays as well as writing them, and he was a shareholder in the Globe Theatre. This made him wealthy: in 1597 he bought New Place, the second-largest house in Stratford.","Over about twenty-five years, he wrote around thirty-eight plays, as well as 154 sonnets and several longer poems. His plays are usually divided into comedies, such as A Midsummer Night’s Dream and Twelfth Night; tragedies, such as Romeo and Juliet, Hamlet, Macbeth and King Lear; and histories about English kings, such as Henry V and Richard III. In 1596, his son Hamnet died, aged eleven. Some scholars think his grief may be reflected in later plays, including Hamlet, whose title is almost the same as his son’s name.","Shakespeare’s influence on the English language is enormous. He is credited with being the first to write down hundreds of words and phrases, some of which he may have invented, including ‘lonely’, ‘bedroom’, ‘eyeball’, ‘fashionable’ and ‘swagger’, and many phrases we still use without thinking, such as ‘wild-goose chase’, ‘break the ice’, ‘in a pickle’, ‘all of a sudden’ and ‘the be-all and end-all’.","In about 1613, Shakespeare seems to have retired to Stratford. He died there on 23 April 1616, aged fifty-two, and was buried in Holy Trinity Church. His gravestone carries a curse on anyone who moves his bones. In his will, he famously left his wife his ‘second-best bed’ – which may have been the bed they slept in, as the best bed was usually kept for guests.","Seven years after his death, two of his fellow actors, John Heminges and Henry Condell, collected thirty-six of his plays and published them in a single volume, now known as the First Folio. Without it, around half of his plays, including Macbeth and The Tempest, might have been lost for ever. In its introduction, his friend and rival Ben Jonson wrote that Shakespeare ‘was not of an age, but for all time’. Four centuries later, he seems to have been right."],"glossary":{"baptised":"Welcomed into the Christian Church in a ceremony with water.","classical":"From ancient Greece or Rome.","playwright":"A person who writes plays.","upstart":"A person who has suddenly become important and is thought to be too proud.","shareholder":"A person who owns part of a business.","sonnets":"Poems of fourteen lines with a set rhyme pattern.","scholars":"People who study a subject in great depth.","credited":"Recognised as having done something.","folio":"A large book made from sheets of paper folded once."},"questions":[{"q":"Why is so little known about Shakespeare’s life?","o":["He lived in secret","No letters or diaries survive, only official records","He was not famous","His house burned down"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“We have no letters written by him, no diaries.”"},{"q":"What are the ‘lost years’?","o":["Years when his plays were lost","A period when he disappears from the records","Years he spent at sea","His years at school"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“For several years, he disappears from the records.”"},{"q":"What does Robert Greene calling him ‘an upstart crow’ suggest?","o":["Greene admired him","Greene was jealous of this newcomer’s success","Shakespeare liked birds","Shakespeare was a criminal"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"A ‘jealous rival’ insulted the newly successful playwright."},{"q":"Why might Hamlet be linked to Shakespeare’s own grief?","o":["The title is almost the same as the name of his son, who died aged eleven","It is a comedy","Hamlet was his father","It was written before Hamnet was born"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Some scholars think his grief for Hamnet is reflected in Hamlet."},{"q":"Why was the First Folio so important?","o":["It contained his letters","Without it, around half of his plays might have been lost","It was the first book ever printed","It was written by Shakespeare himself"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Without it, around half of his plays … might have been lost for ever.”"},{"q":"What did Ben Jonson mean by ‘not of an age, but for all time’?","o":["Shakespeare’s work would remain relevant for ever","Shakespeare lived a very long time","Shakespeare did not know his age","Shakespeare wrote about time"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"His writing would last beyond his own era, as it has for four centuries."}]},{"id":"y163","title":"Photosynthesis: How Plants Make Food from Light","kind":"Science","theme":"garden","text":["Almost every living thing on Earth depends on a single chemical process that takes place quietly, every day, in the leaves of plants. It is called photosynthesis, and without it, there would be no food, no oxygen to breathe and very little life at all.","Animals, including humans, have to eat to get energy. Plants are different. They make their own food, using just three simple ingredients: carbon dioxide from the air, water from the soil and energy from sunlight. The word ‘photosynthesis’ comes from Greek and means ‘putting together with light’.","Photosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves. Inside the cells of a leaf are tiny structures called chloroplasts, which contain a green pigment called chlorophyll. Chlorophyll is very good at absorbing red and blue light from the Sun, but it reflects green light – which is why most leaves look green. The energy captured by chlorophyll is used to split water molecules and to join carbon dioxide molecules together to make glucose, a type of sugar. Oxygen is given off as a waste product.","Scientists sum this up in a word equation: carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen, using light energy. The leaf is perfectly designed for the job. It is broad and flat to catch as much sunlight as possible, and thin so that gases can move in and out easily. On the underside of the leaf are thousands of tiny holes called stomata, which open to let carbon dioxide in and oxygen and water vapour out. Veins carry water up from the roots and carry sugars away to the rest of the plant.","The plant uses the glucose it makes in several ways. Some is used straight away for energy, through respiration. Some is turned into starch, which is stored in places such as roots, seeds and tubers – a potato is a store of starch. Some is used to build cellulose, the tough material that makes up plant cell walls. Plants also combine glucose with minerals from the soil, such as nitrates, to make proteins for growth.","The speed of photosynthesis depends on several things: how much light there is, how much carbon dioxide is in the air and the temperature. Farmers who grow tomatoes or lettuces in greenhouses sometimes add extra carbon dioxide and use lamps and heaters to make their plants grow faster.","Photosynthesis is the starting point of almost every food chain. When you eat a carrot, you are eating energy that the carrot plant captured from the Sun. When you eat beef, you are eating energy that grass captured, which the cow then ate. Even fossil fuels such as coal, oil and gas are made from the remains of plants and tiny organisms that photosynthesised millions of years ago. And every breath you take contains oxygen released by plants and algae.","Scientists estimate that around half of the world’s oxygen comes from tiny floating algae in the oceans called phytoplankton. So the next time you walk past a tree or look at the sea, remember: something there is quietly turning sunlight into life."],"glossary":{"chloroplasts":"Tiny structures in plant cells where photosynthesis happens.","pigment":"A substance that gives something its colour.","chlorophyll":"The green substance in plants that absorbs light.","absorbing":"Taking in or soaking up.","molecules":"The smallest units of a substance, made of atoms joined together.","glucose":"A type of sugar that living things use for energy.","stomata":"Tiny holes in leaves that let gases in and out.","respiration":"The process by which living things release energy from food.","tubers":"Thick underground stems where plants store food, such as potatoes.","phytoplankton":"Tiny plant-like organisms that float in water."},"questions":[{"q":"What are the three ingredients plants need for photosynthesis?","o":["Soil, water and air","Carbon dioxide, water and sunlight","Oxygen, sugar and light","Seeds, rain and wind"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Carbon dioxide from the air, water from the soil and energy from sunlight.”"},{"q":"Why do most leaves look green?","o":["They are painted","Chlorophyll reflects green light","They contain green sugar","They absorb green light"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Chlorophyll “reflects green light – which is why most leaves look green.”"},{"q":"Why are leaves broad, flat and thin?","o":["To look nice","To catch lots of sunlight and let gases move in and out easily","To stop insects eating them","To store water"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Broad and flat to catch sunlight; thin so gases can move easily."},{"q":"What is a potato, according to the article?","o":["A root that makes oxygen","A store of starch","A type of leaf","A seed"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“A potato is a store of starch.”"},{"q":"Why might greenhouse farmers add extra carbon dioxide?","o":["To make plants grow faster by speeding up photosynthesis","To kill pests","To keep the greenhouse cool","To change the colour of plants"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The speed of photosynthesis depends on carbon dioxide levels."},{"q":"Why does the writer say photosynthesis is “the starting point of almost every food chain”?","o":["Because the energy in almost all food comes originally from sunlight captured by plants","Because plants eat animals","Because food chains start in the ocean","Because animals photosynthesise"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"Carrots, beef and even fossil fuels all trace their energy back to photosynthesis."}]},{"id":"y164","title":"The Railway Age","kind":"History","theme":"victorian","text":["In October 1829, crowds gathered at Rainhill, near Liverpool, to watch an extraordinary competition. The directors of the new Liverpool and Manchester Railway had offered a prize of £500 for the best locomotive – a steam engine that could pull trains along rails. Five machines entered the Rainhill Trials. One was powered by a horse walking on a treadmill. The winner was Rocket, built by George Stephenson and his son Robert, which pulled a carriage of passengers at an astonishing thirty miles per hour – faster than any human had ever travelled on land.","Railways themselves were not new. For centuries, horses had pulled wagons along wooden or iron rails in mines and quarries. What changed everything was steam. In 1804, the Cornish engineer Richard Trevithick built the first steam locomotive to run on rails, at an ironworks in Wales. George Stephenson, the son of a colliery worker who had taught himself to read at eighteen, built engines for coal mines in the north-east, and in 1825 his locomotive Locomotion No. 1 pulled the first train on the Stockton and Darlington Railway, the world’s first public railway to use steam.","The Liverpool and Manchester Railway, which opened in 1830, was the first to run entirely on steam power and to carry passengers between two cities on a regular timetable. Its opening was marred by tragedy: the politician William Huskisson stepped onto the track and was struck by Rocket, becoming one of the first people to be killed in a railway accident. But the railway was a huge success.","What followed was ‘railway mania’. Investors rushed to fund new lines, and between 1830 and 1850 more than 6,000 miles of track were laid across Britain. Armies of labourers called navvies dug cuttings, built embankments and blasted tunnels through hills, often in dangerous conditions and with little more than picks, shovels and gunpowder. The engineer Isambard Kingdom Brunel built the Great Western Railway from London to Bristol, and Robert Stephenson built great bridges.","Railways transformed Britain. Journeys that had taken days by stagecoach now took hours. Coal, iron and manufactured goods could be moved cheaply across the country, powering industry. Fresh food such as milk, fish and vegetables could be carried quickly into cities, improving people’s diets. Newspapers and letters could be delivered across the country overnight.","Railways even changed time. Before the railways, towns kept their own local time based on the sun, so that Bristol was about ten minutes behind London. This made timetables confusing, so in 1840 the Great Western Railway adopted London time, and by 1880 a single standard time was used across the whole of Britain.","Ordinary people could now travel for pleasure. In 1841, Thomas Cook organised a railway excursion for around 500 people from Leicester to Loughborough for a temperance meeting, and went on to found one of the world’s first travel companies. Seaside resorts such as Blackpool, Brighton and Scarborough boomed as families poured out of the cities for day trips and holidays.","Within a few decades, railways had spread across the world, built with British engines and British engineers. The age of steam lasted until the 1960s, when British Railways replaced steam locomotives with diesel and electric trains. But you can still ride behind steam on heritage railways across Britain – and Rocket itself is preserved in the Science Museum in London."],"glossary":{"locomotive":"A railway engine.","treadmill":"A moving belt or wheel that is walked on.","colliery":"A coal mine and its buildings.","marred":"Spoiled.","investors":"People who put money into a business hoping to make a profit.","navvies":"Labourers who built railways and canals.","embankments":"Raised banks of earth that carry a railway or road.","excursion":"A short trip, especially for pleasure.","temperance":"Not drinking alcohol.","heritage":"Valued things from the past that are preserved."},"questions":[{"q":"Which locomotive won the Rainhill Trials?","o":["Locomotion No. 1","Rocket","A horse-powered engine","The Great Western"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The winner was Rocket, built by George Stephenson and his son Robert.”"},{"q":"Why was thirty miles per hour so astonishing in 1829?","o":["It was faster than any human had ever travelled on land","It was the speed limit","Horses were faster","Trains were usually faster"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It was “faster than any human had ever travelled on land.”"},{"q":"What does the fact that Stephenson taught himself to read at eighteen suggest?","o":["He was determined and self-made despite a humble background","He was lazy at school","He was wealthy","He disliked books"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Rising from a colliery family to great engineer shows determination."},{"q":"How did railways improve people’s diets?","o":["Fresh food could be carried quickly into cities","Food became more expensive","People ate on trains","Farms moved into cities"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Fresh food such as milk, fish and vegetables could be carried quickly into cities.”"},{"q":"Why did railways lead to a single standard time across Britain?","o":["Local times differed from town to town, making timetables confusing","The Queen ordered it","Clocks were invented","Trains needed sunlight"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Bristol was ten minutes behind London, so timetables were confusing."},{"q":"How did railways change leisure for ordinary people?","o":["They could travel for pleasure, and seaside resorts boomed","They had to work longer","They stopped taking holidays","They could only travel for work"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"Thomas Cook’s excursions and booming seaside resorts show travel for pleasure."}]},{"id":"y165","title":"Owls: The Silent Hunters","kind":"Non-fiction","theme":"forest","text":["A field mouse creeps through long grass on a moonless night. It cannot see the owl. It cannot hear it. It has no idea it is being hunted until, in a fraction of a second, two sets of sharp talons close around it. The barn owl has caught its prey by sound alone, in almost total darkness, without making a whisper of noise. How?","Owls are birds of prey, like hawks and eagles, but most species hunt at night. There are around 250 species around the world, and five breed regularly in Britain: the barn owl, tawny owl, little owl, long-eared owl and short-eared owl. The tawny owl, which lives in woodland and is the commonest, makes the famous ‘twit-twoo’ call – although the ‘twit’ (actually more of a ‘ke-wick’) is usually made by the female and the ‘twoo’ by the male, answering each other.","An owl’s first secret weapon is its hearing. A barn owl’s face is shaped like a dish, made of stiff feathers that collect sound and funnel it towards the ears, rather like a satellite dish. Even more remarkably, in many owls one ear is placed higher on the head than the other. A sound reaches the two ears at very slightly different times and volumes, and the owl’s brain uses these tiny differences to work out exactly where the sound is coming from – up or down as well as left or right. A barn owl can pinpoint a mouse rustling under snow or leaves.","Its second weapon is silence. Most birds make a whooshing noise as they fly, caused by air rushing over their feathers. Owl feathers are specially adapted to stop this. The leading edge of the main wing feathers has a soft, comb-like fringe that breaks up the airflow, the back edge is fringed too, and the surface of the feathers is velvety. The result is almost completely silent flight, so that the prey cannot hear the owl coming – and the owl can hear the prey. Engineers have studied owl feathers to design quieter fans and aircraft.","Owls also have huge eyes, which gather as much light as possible. They are so large that they cannot move in their sockets. Instead, an owl turns its whole head. Thanks to extra vertebrae in its neck and special blood vessels that keep blood flowing to the brain, an owl can turn its head about 270 degrees.","Owls swallow small prey whole. They cannot digest bones, fur or feathers, so a few hours later they cough up these remains as a neat, compact pellet. By carefully pulling apart owl pellets, scientists – and school pupils – can find tiny skulls and bones and work out exactly what the owl has been eating.","Barn owls declined sharply in Britain during the twentieth century as farming changed, rough grassland where voles live disappeared, old barns were converted into homes and many owls were killed by traffic. Conservation groups and farmers have helped them recover by leaving strips of rough grass along field edges and putting up nest boxes. If you are out at dusk in the countryside, look for a pale, ghostly shape floating low over a field. You may be watching one of the most perfectly designed hunters in nature."],"glossary":{"talons":"Sharp, hooked claws of a bird of prey.","prey":"An animal hunted by another for food.","funnel":"To direct through a narrow space.","pinpoint":"To find the exact position of something.","adapted":"Changed over time to suit the environment.","velvety":"Soft and smooth like velvet.","vertebrae":"The small bones that make up the spine.","digest":"To break down food in the body.","compact":"Closely and neatly packed together.","conservation":"Protecting animals, plants and nature."},"questions":[{"q":"How many owl species breed regularly in Britain?","o":["250","Five","Two","Twenty"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Five breed regularly in Britain.”"},{"q":"Who usually makes the ‘twit’ and the ‘twoo’ in a tawny owl call?","o":["The male makes both","The female makes the ‘twit’ and the male the ‘twoo’","Two males","Two females"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The ‘twit’ is usually the female and the ‘twoo’ the male."},{"q":"Why is it useful that one of an owl’s ears is higher than the other?","o":["It helps the owl work out exactly where a sound comes from, including up and down","It looks better","It keeps the ears warm","It helps the owl fly"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Tiny differences in timing and volume reveal the sound’s exact position."},{"q":"Why is silent flight an advantage for an owl?","o":["Prey cannot hear it coming, and the owl can hear its prey","It saves energy","It keeps the owl warm","It helps it sleep"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The prey cannot hear the owl coming – and the owl can hear the prey.”"},{"q":"What can scientists learn from owl pellets?","o":["How old the owl is","Exactly what the owl has been eating","Where the owl nests","How fast the owl flies"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"From bones in pellets they “work out exactly what the owl has been eating.”"},{"q":"Why did barn owls decline in Britain?","o":["Changes in farming, loss of rough grassland and barns, and traffic","Too many nest boxes","Cold winters only","Hunting by eagles"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"The final paragraph lists these causes."}]},{"id":"y166","title":"Underground: The Story of the Tube","kind":"History","theme":"city","text":["By the 1850s, London was the largest city in the world, and its streets were choked with traffic. Horse-drawn omnibuses, carts, cabs and hundreds of thousands of pedestrians jammed the roads, and it could take longer to cross central London than to travel by train from Brighton. Something had to be done. The solution, proposed by a lawyer named Charles Pearson, sounded to many people like madness: put the trains underground.","The world’s first underground railway, the Metropolitan Railway, opened on 10 January 1863, running about six kilometres between Paddington and Farringdon. It was built using a method called ‘cut and cover’: workers dug a deep trench along the line of a street, built the tunnel walls and roof of brick, and then covered it over again. This caused enormous disruption, as roads were torn up for months. The trains were pulled by steam locomotives, and although the engines were designed to reduce smoke, the tunnels were filled with choking fumes. Despite this, on its first day around 38,000 people rode the new railway, and it was a huge success.","To build deeper lines without digging up the streets, engineers developed tunnelling shields – large metal cylinders that protected workers as they dug forwards, while the tunnel behind was lined with cast-iron rings. The resulting round tunnels gave the system its famous nickname: the Tube. In 1890, the City and South London Railway opened as the world’s first deep-level underground railway to use electric trains, which meant no more smoke. Its carriages had only tiny windows high up, because the designers thought there was nothing to see in a tunnel, and passengers called them ‘padded cells’.","Over the following decades, more lines were built by competing private companies, and in 1933 they were joined together under a single public organisation, London Transport. The famous Underground map, designed in 1931 by an engineering draughtsman named Harry Beck, was a stroke of genius. Beck realised that passengers did not need to know the real distances between stations or the exact routes of the lines – only the order of the stations and where to change. His map used only straight lines at right angles and forty-five degrees, with stations evenly spaced, like a diagram of an electric circuit. At first the company was nervous, but the map was a hit, and it has inspired transport maps around the world.","During the Second World War, the Tube played an unexpected role. When German bombs began to fall on London during the Blitz in 1940, thousands of people sheltered on the platforms of deep Tube stations every night, sleeping on the hard floors as the bombs fell above. At first, the government tried to stop this, but people simply bought tickets and refused to leave. Eventually bunks, toilets and refreshment trains were provided. Some stations were also used for secret purposes; an unfinished section of the Central Line was turned into an underground aircraft-parts factory.","Today, the London Underground has eleven lines and 272 stations, and on a busy day it carries around five million passenger journeys. The deepest station, Hampstead, is fifty-eight metres below ground. Some abandoned ‘ghost stations’ still lie hidden along the lines, and you can sometimes glimpse their tiled platforms from a passing train. More than a century and a half after the first trains ran, the idea that once sounded like madness is the beating heart of one of the world’s great cities."],"glossary":{"omnibuses":"An old word for buses.","pedestrians":"People walking.","disruption":"Disturbance or problems that stop something working normally.","fumes":"Smoke or gases that are unpleasant to breathe.","cylinders":"Tube-shaped objects.","draughtsman":"A person who makes detailed technical drawings.","refreshment":"Food and drink.","abandoned":"Left empty and no longer used."},"questions":[{"q":"Why was an underground railway needed in London?","o":["The streets were jammed with traffic","It rained too much","People wanted adventure","There was no room for houses"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"London’s streets “were choked with traffic.”"},{"q":"What was the ‘cut and cover’ method?","o":["Digging a trench along a street, building the tunnel, then covering it over","Cutting through hills","Covering trains with cloth","Digging deep tunnels with shields"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Workers “dug a deep trench … built the tunnel … and then covered it over again.”"},{"q":"Why is the system nicknamed ‘the Tube’?","o":["The trains are long","The deep tunnels dug with shields were round","It is painted like a tube","It carries water"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"The round tunnels “gave the system its famous nickname.”"},{"q":"What was clever about Harry Beck’s map?","o":["It showed exact distances","It showed only the order of stations and connections, using simple straight lines","It was drawn by hand","It showed the streets above"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"Passengers only needed the order of stations and where to change, so he used straight lines and even spacing."},{"q":"What does the people’s behaviour during the Blitz show?","o":["They were determined to find safety, even against government wishes","They wanted to travel","They liked sleeping on floors","They were lost"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“People simply bought tickets and refused to leave.”"},{"q":"What does the final sentence suggest?","o":["An idea that once seemed mad is now essential to London","The Tube should close","London has no other transport","The Tube is a ghost railway"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"It contrasts the ‘madness’ of the original idea with the Tube as “the beating heart” of London."}]},{"id":"y167","title":"The Treehouse Pact","kind":"Story","theme":"forest","text":["The treehouse had been built by Ollie’s dad and Ollie’s uncle when they were twelve, in the big oak at the bottom of the field behind Gran’s house. It was older than Ollie’s mum and dad’s marriage, older than the new road, older than almost everything. It had a rope ladder, a trapdoor, a window made from an old picture frame and, carved into the main beam, the words: THE OAK GANG – MEMBERS ONLY – NO GROWN-UPS EVER.","Ollie, Priya and Sam had claimed it the summer they were nine. Now they were twelve, and it was the summer before secondary school, and they had made a pact. On the last night of the holidays, they would sleep in the treehouse. All night. On their own.","‘We’ll be at different schools,’ said Priya, who had got into the grammar school in town. ‘Everything’s going to be different. So we should do something we’ll remember.’","Gran agreed, after a lot of persuading, as long as they took a torch, a phone, three sleeping bags and the walkie-talkie that connected to her kitchen.","At first, it was brilliant. They ate crisps and chocolate and Gran’s flapjacks. They told terrible jokes. They played cards by torchlight until the batteries started to fade. Through the picture-frame window, they watched the sky turn orange, then purple, then a deep, glittering black.","Then, around eleven, the night began to make noises.","An owl screeched, so close it seemed to be inside the treehouse. Something heavy moved through the undergrowth below. Branches creaked. The oak, which had seemed so solid in daylight, swayed gently in the breeze, and every time it moved, the whole treehouse groaned.","‘It’s just a badger,’ said Sam, who had not said anything for a while. ‘Probably.’","Nobody slept. They lay in their sleeping bags, staring at the dark, listening. And somewhere in the middle of the night, in the dark, they started to talk properly – not jokes, but real things. Sam admitted that he was scared of starting at the big comprehensive, because his brother had had a horrible time there. Priya said that she was terrified she would not be clever enough at her new school, and that everyone would find out. Ollie said that his mum and dad were arguing a lot lately, and that sometimes he came to Gran’s just to get away from the shouting. He had never told anyone that before.","Nobody laughed. Nobody said anything stupid. Priya reached across in the dark and squeezed his hand, and Sam said, ‘That’s rubbish, mate,’ which, from Sam, meant a lot.","At about four in the morning, the birds started singing – one at first, then a few, then hundreds, until the whole field seemed to be shouting. The sky turned grey, then pink. The three of them climbed out onto the platform and sat with their legs dangling, wrapped in sleeping bags, watching the sun come up over the hedges.","‘We should make a new pact,’ said Priya. ‘Whatever happens at our new schools, we meet here. Every holiday. Even when we’re old.’","They shook on it. Then Ollie took out his penknife and, carefully, underneath THE OAK GANG, he carved their three initials and the year.","Twenty years later, there would be other children in the treehouse. But the initials would still be there."],"glossary":{"trapdoor":"A door in a floor or ceiling.","pact":"An agreement or promise between people.","persuading":"Convincing someone to agree.","undergrowth":"Bushes and plants growing beneath trees.","comprehensive":"A secondary school that takes pupils of all abilities.","initials":"The first letters of a person’s names."},"questions":[{"q":"Who built the treehouse?","o":["Ollie and his friends","Ollie’s dad and uncle when they were twelve","Gran","A builder"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It “had been built by Ollie’s dad and Ollie’s uncle when they were twelve.”"},{"q":"Why did the friends want to sleep in the treehouse?","o":["Their houses were being painted","To do something memorable before going to different schools","Because Gran told them to","To watch for badgers"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Priya says, “we should do something we’ll remember.”"},{"q":"How does the writer create a change of mood around eleven o’clock?","o":["By describing frightening night noises – an owl, something moving, creaking branches","By describing the sunrise","By having them fall asleep","By describing their food"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"“Then, around eleven, the night began to make noises,” followed by unsettling sounds."},{"q":"Why might the friends have talked honestly in the dark?","o":["The darkness and being awake together made it easier to share fears","They were told to","They were bored of cards","They wanted to scare each other"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"In the dark, unable to sleep, they began “to talk properly.”"},{"q":"What does Sam’s comment “That’s rubbish, mate” mean in context?","o":["He is being rude","It is his way of showing real sympathy","He thinks Ollie is lying","He wants to go home"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“From Sam, meant a lot” – it shows he cares."},{"q":"What does the final sentence suggest?","o":["The friendship and memory will last, even as time passes","The treehouse will fall down","The friends will forget each other","Other children will remove the initials"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"Twenty years later the initials “would still be there,” symbolising lasting friendship."}]},{"id":"y168","title":"From Bean to Bar: How Chocolate Is Made","kind":"Non-fiction","theme":"jungle","text":["The average person in the UK eats around eight kilograms of chocolate a year – about 160 bars. But few people stop to think where it comes from. The answer is a small tree that grows in the humid shade of tropical rainforests, and the journey from that tree to your sweet wrapper is long, complicated and fascinating.","Chocolate comes from the seeds of the cacao tree, Theobroma cacao. Its name means ‘food of the gods’, and the people of Central America would have agreed. The ancient Olmec, Maya and Aztec peoples were drinking chocolate thousands of years ago, though not as we know it. They ground roasted cacao beans into a paste, mixed it with water, chillies, maize and spices, and poured it from one cup to another to make it frothy. It was bitter, not sweet. Cacao beans were so valuable that the Aztecs used them as money: a turkey might cost a hundred beans.","When Spanish explorers brought cacao to Europe in the sixteenth century, Europeans added sugar and it became a fashionable drink for the rich. Solid eating chocolate came much later. In 1847, the British company J. S. Fry & Sons made the first chocolate bar, and in 1875 Daniel Peter in Switzerland created milk chocolate by adding powdered milk.","Today, cacao trees grow in a band around the equator, and about seventy per cent of the world’s cocoa comes from West Africa, especially Côte d’Ivoire and Ghana. The trees are delicate: they need warmth, rain and shade, and their flowers are pollinated by tiny flies called midges. Cacao pods, about the size of a rugby ball, grow directly from the trunk and branches. Each pod contains around thirty to fifty beans surrounded by a sweet white pulp.","Harvesting is done mostly by hand, on small family farms. Farmers cut down the ripe pods with machetes, split them open and scoop out the beans. The beans are then heaped up and covered with banana leaves to ferment for about a week. Fermentation is essential: it develops the chocolate flavour and changes the colour of the beans from purple to brown. Next, the beans are spread out in the sun to dry, then packed into sacks and shipped around the world.","In the factory, the beans are roasted, which deepens the flavour. Then their shells are cracked off, leaving pieces called nibs. The nibs are ground into a thick, dark paste called cocoa liquor. This can be pressed to separate cocoa butter (a pale fat) from cocoa powder. To make chocolate, cocoa liquor, extra cocoa butter, sugar and, for milk chocolate, milk are mixed together. The mixture is then ‘conched’ – stirred and rolled for many hours, sometimes days – to make it smooth. Finally it is ‘tempered’, carefully heated and cooled so that it sets glossy and snaps cleanly, then poured into moulds.","There is a less sweet side to the story. Most cocoa farmers are poor, earning only a small share of the price of a chocolate bar, and child labour on cocoa farms remains a serious problem. Cutting down rainforest to plant cacao also damages the environment. Fairtrade and other certification schemes aim to pay farmers a fairer price and support better conditions. So next time you unwrap a bar of chocolate, you might want to check the label – and remember the long journey it has made to reach you."],"glossary":{"humid":"Warm and damp.","equator":"An imaginary line around the middle of the Earth.","pollinated":"Had pollen carried to it so it can produce fruit or seeds.","pulp":"A soft, wet mass.","machetes":"Large heavy knives.","ferment":"To change chemically, often with the help of tiny organisms.","nibs":"Small pieces of crushed cacao bean.","conched":"Stirred and rolled for a long time to become smooth.","certification":"An official guarantee that something meets certain standards."},"questions":[{"q":"What does ‘Theobroma’ mean?","o":["Brown bean","Food of the gods","Sweet seed","Tree of life"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Its name means ‘food of the gods’.”"},{"q":"How was the ancient chocolate drink different from modern chocolate?","o":["It was bitter, mixed with chillies and spices, not sweet","It was solid","It was made with milk","It was blue"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It was mixed “with water, chillies, maize and spices … It was bitter, not sweet.”"},{"q":"What does the fact that the Aztecs used cacao beans as money suggest?","o":["Cacao beans were very valuable","Beans were worthless","The Aztecs had no gold","Turkeys were cheap"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"They were “so valuable that the Aztecs used them as money.”"},{"q":"Why is fermentation essential?","o":["It develops the chocolate flavour and changes the beans’ colour","It makes the beans lighter","It kills the trees","It adds sugar"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“It develops the chocolate flavour and changes the colour of the beans.”"},{"q":"How is the main section of the article organised?","o":["In the order of the process, from tree to bar","In alphabetical order","From the newest company to the oldest","As a list of brands"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It follows harvesting, fermenting, drying, roasting, grinding, conching and tempering."},{"q":"Why does the writer mention the “less sweet side” of chocolate?","o":["To raise awareness of farmers’ poverty, child labour and environmental damage","To say chocolate is not tasty","To advertise a brand","To explain tempering"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"It highlights problems and encourages readers to check for fair-trade labels."}]},{"id":"y169","title":"Oliver Twist Asks for More","kind":"Retelling","theme":"victorian","author":"Retold from Charles Dickens","text":["Oliver Twist was born in a workhouse – a grim building where the very poorest people were sent to live and work when they had nowhere else to go. His mother died moments after he was born, and nobody knew who she was. So Oliver was given a name chosen alphabetically by the parish beadle, a pompous official named Mr Bumble, who named the orphans in order: Swubble, Twist, Unwin, Vilkins.","For his first nine years, Oliver was ‘farmed out’ to a branch workhouse run by a woman named Mrs Mann, who was paid to look after the children but kept most of the money for herself. The children were half-starved, cold and frequently beaten. On his ninth birthday, Oliver was brought back to the main workhouse, where he was set to work picking oakum – unpicking old, tarred ropes so the fibres could be reused.","The workhouse was run by a board of gentlemen, very fat and comfortable themselves, who had decided that the poor were coming to the workhouse because they liked it too much. So they made it as unpleasant as possible. In particular, they reduced the food to three meals of thin gruel a day, with an onion twice a week and half a roll on Sundays.","The boys ate in a large stone hall, with a copper pot at one end, from which the master, in an apron, ladled out the gruel. Each boy had one small bowl, and no more. The bowls never needed washing: the boys polished them with their spoons until they shone, and then sat staring at the copper with hungry eyes, sucking their fingers in case any drops of gruel had splashed onto them.","For three months, Oliver and his companions suffered the tortures of slow starvation. At last they grew so desperate that they held a meeting and drew lots to decide who should walk up to the master after supper and ask for more. The lot fell to Oliver.","That evening, the gruel was served and swallowed in an instant. The boys whispered and winked at Oliver; his neighbours nudged him. Child as he was, he was desperate with hunger and reckless with misery. He rose from the table, walked up to the master, basin and spoon in hand, and said, somewhat alarmed at his own boldness:","‘Please, sir, I want some more.’","The master was a fat, healthy man, but he turned very pale. He stared in astonishment at the small rebel for some seconds, and then clung to the copper for support. The assistants were paralysed with wonder, the boys with fear.","‘What!’ said the master at length, in a faint voice.","‘Please, sir,’ replied Oliver, ‘I want some more.’","The master aimed a blow at Oliver’s head with the ladle, pinioned him in his arms and shrieked for the beadle. The board was in solemn session when Mr Bumble rushed into the room in great excitement. ‘Oliver Twist has asked for more!’ he cried. There was a general start. Horror was depicted on every face. One gentleman in a white waistcoat declared with confidence: ‘That boy will be hung.’","Oliver was locked in a dark room, and a notice was pasted on the gate offering five pounds to anyone who would take him off the workhouse’s hands.","Dickens wrote Oliver Twist in 1837–39 partly to attack the New Poor Law of 1834, which had created harsher workhouses. By making readers laugh at the board and cry for Oliver, he made them angry about the way the poor were treated – and that anger helped to bring about change."],"glossary":{"workhouse":"A place where very poor people lived and worked in harsh conditions.","parish":"A local area with its own church and officials.","beadle":"A minor parish official.","pompous":"Behaving in a self-important, overly serious way.","oakum":"Loose fibre obtained by unpicking old ropes.","gruel":"A thin, watery porridge.","reckless":"Not caring about danger or consequences.","pinioned":"Held someone’s arms tightly so they could not move.","depicted":"Shown."},"questions":[{"q":"How did Oliver get his surname?","o":["It was his father’s name","Mr Bumble named orphans in alphabetical order","He chose it himself","His mother wrote it down"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Mr Bumble named the orphans alphabetically: Swubble, Twist, Unwin…"},{"q":"Why did the board make the workhouse so unpleasant?","o":["They had no money","They thought the poor were coming because they liked it too much","The government ordered it","To make the boys stronger"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They decided “the poor were coming to the workhouse because they liked it too much.”"},{"q":"What does the detail about the bowls never needing washing show?","o":["The boys were very tidy","The boys were so hungry they licked every drop","The bowls were new","The cook washed them"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"They polished them with spoons until they shone, showing extreme hunger."},{"q":"How was Oliver chosen to ask for more?","o":["He volunteered","The boys drew lots","The master chose him","He was the oldest"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They “drew lots … The lot fell to Oliver.”"},{"q":"How does Dickens make the adults’ reaction seem ridiculous?","o":["By exaggerating their horror at a small, polite request – the master clings to the copper and a gentleman predicts hanging","By describing their kindness","By making them laugh","By making them give Oliver more"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Their extreme shock at a hungry child’s request is comic and shows their cruelty."},{"q":"According to the final paragraph, why did Dickens write Oliver Twist?","o":["To entertain only","Partly to attack harsh workhouse laws and make readers angry about the treatment of the poor","To describe his childhood","To advertise workhouses"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"He wrote “partly to attack the New Poor Law … that anger helped to bring about change.”"}]},{"id":"y170","title":"Speech: Why I Volunteer","kind":"Speech","theme":"city","text":["Good afternoon, everyone. Two years ago, if you had told me that I would spend my Saturday mornings sorting tins of beans in a church hall, I would have laughed. Saturday mornings were for sleeping. Now they are my favourite part of the week. I want to tell you why, and I want to persuade at least some of you to join me.","I volunteer at the Riverside Food Bank. Every week, families come to collect parcels of food: pasta, rice, tins of soup and vegetables, cereal, tea, toiletries, and, if we have them, a few treats for the children. Some of these families have lost their jobs. Some are working but their wages do not cover their rent and bills. Some are dealing with illness or have just escaped a difficult situation. What they have in common is that, for a while, they cannot afford to feed themselves. It could happen to anyone.","My job is not dramatic. I check the dates on donated food. I sort tins onto shelves. I pack bags. Sometimes I make tea and chat to people while they wait. But I have learned that small jobs matter. When a little girl came in last winter and found a chocolate bar in her bag, her whole face lit up. Her mum cried. She told me it was the first treat they had been able to give her in a month. I think about that every time I am tempted to stay in bed.","Volunteering has given me more than I have given. I have learned to talk to people of every age and background. I have become more confident – two years ago, I could hardly speak to an adult I did not know. I have learned to organise, to work in a team and to stay calm when things get busy. These are things they do not always teach you in lessons, and they will help me at college and in a job. There is good research to back this up, too: studies have found that people who volunteer often report feeling happier and less stressed. Helping others turns out to be good for you.","Maybe the food bank is not for you. That is fine. There are so many ways to volunteer. You could help at an animal rescue centre, read with younger children at the library, join a litter-pick on the beach, help out at a parkrun, or visit an elderly neighbour who does not see many people. Many charities welcome young volunteers, and some, like the Duke of Edinburgh’s Award, give you recognition for it.","Some of you might be thinking that you are too busy, with homework, clubs and everything else. I understand. But volunteering does not have to take a lot of time. One hour a week, or even one morning a month, can make a real difference. And you might find, as I did, that the time you give comes back to you in ways you never expected.","So here is my challenge. Before the end of this term, find one way to give an hour of your time to someone else. Ask your form tutor, check the noticeboard, or come and find me – I will happily bring you along on Saturday. I promise you will not regret it.","Thank you for listening."],"glossary":{"donated":"Given as a gift, especially to a charity.","toiletries":"Things such as soap and toothpaste used for washing.","tempted":"Wanting to do something, especially something you know you should not.","recognition":"Being given credit or praise for something.","challenge":"A difficult task that tests you; also, an invitation to try something."},"questions":[{"q":"Where does the speaker volunteer?","o":["An animal shelter","The Riverside Food Bank","A library","A hospital"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“I volunteer at the Riverside Food Bank.”"},{"q":"Why does the speaker say “It could happen to anyone”?","o":["To show that people use food banks for many reasons, such as job loss or illness, and should not be judged","To frighten the audience","To advertise food","To complain about prices"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"They list varied reasons families need help."},{"q":"Why does the speaker tell the story of the little girl and the chocolate bar?","o":["To show how small acts of kindness can make a big difference","To show chocolate is expensive","To describe their own favourite food","To criticise the girl’s mum"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"The story makes the audience feel the impact of a small gesture."},{"q":"Which skill does the speaker say volunteering has given them?","o":["Confidence talking to adults","Cooking","Driving","Playing an instrument"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“I have become more confident – two years ago, I could hardly speak to an adult I did not know.”"},{"q":"How does the speaker deal with the objection that people are too busy?","o":["Agrees they should not volunteer","Understands but argues even an hour a week can help","Says homework is unimportant","Ignores it"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"“I understand. But volunteering does not have to take a lot of time.”"},{"q":"What is the speaker’s ‘challenge’ to the audience?","o":["To give an hour of their time to someone else before the end of term","To donate money","To stop sleeping in","To run a marathon"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"“Find one way to give an hour of your time to someone else.”"}]},{"id":"y171","title":"Snowfall","kind":"Poem","theme":"storm","text":["It started in the night\nwhile the town was sleeping –\nno knock, no warning,\njust a hush\nlike a held breath.","By morning it had rewritten everything.\nThe cars were loaves of bread.\nThe hedges were sleeping animals.\nThe bins wore tall white hats\nlike chefs.","The road we take to school,\nthat grey, tired road,\nwas a clean page\nwaiting for the first word.","We wrote it with our boots:\na wobbly line of footprints,\nthen another, then a dog’s,\nthen the long double stripes of a sledge\nand a circle where somebody fell.","All day the sky kept falling,\nsoft and slow,\nfilling in our mistakes,\nas if the world were saying,\nstart again, start again.","By evening the streetlights\nhad turned it to gold,\nand the town was so quiet\nyou could hear the snow\nlanding on itself."],"glossary":{"hush":"A quiet silence.","loaves":"More than one loaf of bread.","chefs":"Professional cooks, who often wear tall white hats.","rewritten":"Written again in a different way; here, completely changed.","wobbly":"Unsteady; moving from side to side."},"questions":[{"q":"When did the snow start to fall?","o":["In the afternoon","In the night","At breakfast","During school"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“It started in the night / while the town was sleeping.”"},{"q":"What does “The cars were loaves of bread” suggest?","o":["The cars were covered in rounded humps of snow","The cars were brown","The cars smelled of bread","The cars were warm"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Snow-covered cars look like rounded loaves – a metaphor."},{"q":"Why does the poet describe the road as “a clean page / waiting for the first word”?","o":["The fresh, untouched snow is like blank paper waiting to be marked","The road is made of paper","Children write on the road","The road leads to the library"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Unmarked snow resembles a blank page; footprints become the ‘writing’."},{"q":"How does the fourth stanza continue the idea of writing?","o":["Footprints, paw prints and sledge tracks are described as marks written on the snow","It describes a book","It describes a letter","It describes graffiti"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"“We wrote it with our boots” extends the page metaphor."},{"q":"What does “filling in our mistakes … start again” suggest about the falling snow?","o":["Fresh snow covers marks, like a chance for a fresh start","The children made spelling mistakes","The snow is melting","The snow is dangerous"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"New snow covers tracks and falls, as if offering a new beginning."},{"q":"What is the effect of the final image, hearing “the snow / landing on itself”?","o":["It emphasises how incredibly silent and peaceful the town has become","It shows the snow is loud","It shows a storm is coming","It is a joke"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Only total silence would let you hear snow landing on snow."}]},{"id":"y172","title":"Joan of Arc: The Girl Who Led an Army","kind":"Biography","theme":"castle","text":["In 1429, a teenage peasant girl from a small village in eastern France persuaded the heir to the French throne to give her armour, a horse and command of an army. Within a few months, she had lifted a siege, won a string of battles and seen him crowned king. Two years later she was dead, burned at the stake by her enemies. Her name was Jeanne d’Arc – known in English as Joan of Arc.","Joan was born in about 1412 in the village of Domrémy. At that time, France was in the middle of the Hundred Years’ War against England, which had been going on, on and off, since 1337. English armies, allied with the powerful Duke of Burgundy, controlled much of northern France, including Paris. The heir to the French throne, Charles, had not even been crowned, and his cause seemed almost hopeless.","From the age of about thirteen, Joan said she heard voices and saw visions of saints – Saint Michael, Saint Catherine and Saint Margaret – who told her that God had chosen her to drive the English out of France and see Charles crowned. When she was about sixteen, she travelled to the nearby town of Vaucouleurs and asked the local commander to take her to Charles. He laughed and sent her home. She came back, and kept coming back, until at last he agreed.","Joan cut her hair short and dressed in men’s clothes for the dangerous journey across enemy territory to the town of Chinon, where Charles was staying. According to legend, Charles tested her by disguising himself among his courtiers, but she walked straight up to him. After she was questioned by church scholars, he decided to trust her.","Joan was sent with an army to Orléans, an important city that the English had been besieging for months. Her arrival transformed the mood of the French soldiers. Wearing white armour and carrying a banner, she rode at the front of attacks, and even when she was wounded by an arrow in the shoulder she returned to the fight. Within nine days, the English abandoned the siege. Further victories followed, and in July 1429 Charles was crowned King Charles VII in Reims Cathedral, with Joan standing beside him.","Her success did not last. An attack on Paris failed, and in May 1430 she was captured by Burgundian soldiers at Compiègne and sold to the English. King Charles made no attempt to rescue her. The English wanted to discredit her, and in 1431 she was put on trial by a church court in Rouen, accused of heresy – holding beliefs against the teachings of the Church – and of wearing men’s clothing. For months, educated churchmen tried to trap her with questions. Joan, who could not read or write, often answered with remarkable cleverness. When asked if she knew she was in God’s grace, a question with no safe answer, she replied: ‘If I am not, may God put me there; and if I am, may God so keep me.’","She was found guilty, and on 30 May 1431, aged about nineteen, she was burned at the stake in the market square of Rouen. Twenty-five years later, a new trial declared her innocent, and in 1920 she was made a saint. Today she is one of France’s national heroines, remembered for her extraordinary courage and her belief that one young person could change the course of history."],"glossary":{"heir":"A person who will inherit a title or throne.","siege":"Surrounding a city to force it to surrender.","allied":"Joined together with others for a shared aim.","visions":"Things seen in a dream or trance, sometimes believed to be holy.","territory":"An area of land controlled by a ruler or group.","courtiers":"People who attend a king or queen at court.","besieging":"Surrounding a place to capture it.","discredit":"To make people stop trusting or respecting someone.","heresy":"Beliefs that go against the official teaching of a religion."},"questions":[{"q":"What war was France fighting when Joan was born?","o":["The Wars of the Roses","The Hundred Years’ War against England","The First World War","The Crusades"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“France was in the middle of the Hundred Years’ War against England.”"},{"q":"What did Joan say her voices told her to do?","o":["Become a nun","Drive the English out of France and see Charles crowned","Become queen","Move to England"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They told her “to drive the English out of France and see Charles crowned.”"},{"q":"What does Joan’s repeated return to Vaucouleurs show about her?","o":["She was determined and would not give up","She was lost","She wanted to live there","She was afraid"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“She came back, and kept coming back, until at last he agreed.”"},{"q":"How did Joan’s arrival affect the siege of Orléans?","o":["It transformed the soldiers’ mood, and the English abandoned the siege within nine days","It made no difference","The French surrendered","The city was destroyed"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"Her arrival “transformed the mood,” and “Within nine days, the English abandoned the siege.”"},{"q":"Why was the question about God’s grace a trap?","o":["Saying yes would seem arrogant and saying no would admit guilt","It was too long","She did not understand French","It was about money"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"It had “no safe answer,” so either reply could be used against her."},{"q":"How was Joan’s reputation changed after her death?","o":["She was forgotten","A new trial declared her innocent and she was later made a saint","She was declared a traitor","She was blamed for losing the war"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“A new trial declared her innocent, and in 1920 she was made a saint.”"}]},{"id":"y173","title":"When the Rivers Rise: Flooding in Britain","kind":"Geography","theme":"rain","text":["In the winter of 2015–16, a series of storms – named Desmond, Eva and Frank – brought record-breaking rain to northern England and Scotland. In Cumbria, more than 340 millimetres of rain fell in a single day at Honister Pass. Rivers burst their banks, bridges were swept away and thousands of homes in towns such as Carlisle, Keswick and York were flooded, some for the second or third time in a decade. People returned to find their furniture ruined, their walls soaked and their possessions covered in mud and sewage.","Flooding is the most common natural hazard in the UK. The Environment Agency estimates that around one in six properties in England is at risk of flooding from rivers, the sea or surface water. And with climate change bringing wetter winters and more intense downpours, the risk is growing.","There are several kinds of flooding. River flooding happens when more water enters a river than it can carry, so it spills over its banks onto the surrounding floodplain. Coastal flooding is caused by high tides, storm surges and big waves, especially when strong winds and low air pressure push seawater against the coast. Surface water flooding, sometimes called flash flooding, happens when heavy rain falls so fast that it cannot soak into the ground or drain away, and it pours across streets and into buildings.","Whether a river floods depends on many factors. If the ground is already saturated after weeks of rain, or frozen, it cannot absorb any more water, so the rain runs straight into rivers. Steep slopes send water downhill quickly. Towns and cities make the problem worse: concrete, tarmac and roofs are impermeable, which means water cannot soak through them, so rain rushes into drains and rivers far faster than it would across fields or woodland. Cutting down trees has a similar effect, because trees intercept rain and their roots help the soil to soak it up.","There are two main ways to manage flood risk. The first is hard engineering: building flood walls, embankments, barriers and reservoirs, or straightening and deepening river channels to carry water away faster. The Thames Barrier, which opened in 1982, protects London from tidal surges with ten huge steel gates that can be raised across the river. Hard engineering can be very effective, but it is expensive and can sometimes simply move the problem further downstream.","The second approach is soft engineering, often called natural flood management. This works with nature to slow the flow of water. Planting trees on hillsides, restoring wetlands and peat bogs, and building small ‘leaky dams’ out of logs across streams all help to hold water back in the upper parts of a river, releasing it gradually. Letting some fields flood on purpose can protect towns downstream. In Pickering, North Yorkshire, a combination of tree planting, leaky dams and a small storage area helped to protect the town from flooding in 2015, when many nearby places were badly hit.","Individuals can prepare too. People living in at-risk areas can sign up for free flood warnings, keep important documents in waterproof bags and fit flood doors or barriers. But the most important lesson is simple: never walk or drive through floodwater. Just thirty centimetres of flowing water can float a car, and fifteen centimetres can knock an adult off their feet."],"glossary":{"hazard":"A danger or risk.","floodplain":"Flat land next to a river that floods when the river overflows.","surges":"Sudden powerful rises, especially of sea water.","saturated":"Completely soaked so that no more water can be absorbed.","absorb":"To take in or soak up.","impermeable":"Not allowing water to pass through.","intercept":"To catch or stop something on its way.","reservoirs":"Large lakes used to store water.","restoring":"Bringing back to a former condition."},"questions":[{"q":"What is the most common natural hazard in the UK?","o":["Earthquakes","Flooding","Volcanoes","Hurricanes"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Flooding is the most common natural hazard in the UK.”"},{"q":"What causes surface water (flash) flooding?","o":["High tides","Heavy rain falling too fast to soak in or drain away","Melting glaciers","Rivers drying up"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Rain “falls so fast that it cannot soak into the ground or drain away.”"},{"q":"Why do towns and cities make flooding worse?","o":["They have too many trees","Concrete and tarmac are impermeable, so water rushes into drains and rivers quickly","They are built on hills","They have no rivers"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Impermeable surfaces stop water soaking in, so it runs off quickly."},{"q":"What is a drawback of hard engineering?","o":["It is cheap","It can be expensive and may move the problem downstream","It plants trees","It never works"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It “is expensive and can sometimes simply move the problem further downstream.”"},{"q":"What does the example of Pickering show?","o":["Natural flood management can successfully protect a town","Leaky dams never work","Pickering always floods","Trees cause floods"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Natural measures “helped to protect the town from flooding in 2015.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer give figures about thirty and fifteen centimetres of water?","o":["To show how dangerous even shallow floodwater can be","To give the height of the Thames Barrier","To explain rainfall","To describe a swimming pool"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"They support the warning never to walk or drive through floodwater."}]},{"id":"y174","title":"The Mayflower Voyage","kind":"History","theme":"ship","text":["On 16 September 1620, a small, battered merchant ship called the Mayflower sailed out of Plymouth harbour in Devon, heading west across the Atlantic Ocean. On board were 102 passengers, crammed into a space below deck about the size of a tennis court, along with around thirty crew, chickens, goats, dogs and supplies for the journey. They were bound for North America, where they hoped to build a new life. They are remembered today as the Pilgrims, and their voyage became one of the founding stories of the United States.","About a third of the passengers were members of a religious group known as Separatists. They believed the Church of England had not gone far enough in reforming itself and wanted to worship in their own way, which was against the law in England. Some had already fled to the Netherlands, but they worried their children were losing their English identity, so they decided to start again in America. The rest of the passengers, whom the Separatists called ‘Strangers’, were travelling for other reasons, such as the hope of land and work.","The voyage was meant to begin in August with a second ship, the Speedwell, but the Speedwell leaked so badly that both ships had to turn back twice. Eventually, the Mayflower set off alone, very late in the year. The crossing took sixty-six days. Autumn storms battered the ship, and at one point a main beam cracked, threatening to split the hull; the passengers repaired it with a great iron screw they had brought with them. The passengers lived in damp, dark, cramped conditions, many of them seasick, eating hard biscuits, salted meat and dried fish. One passenger, John Howland, was swept overboard in a storm but managed to grab a rope trailing in the water and was hauled back aboard. A baby was born at sea and named Oceanus.","On 9 November they sighted land – Cape Cod, in what is now Massachusetts, further north than they had planned. Before going ashore, the men signed an agreement called the Mayflower Compact, promising to make and obey fair laws for the good of the colony. It is often seen as an early step towards self-government in America.","They chose a site for their settlement and named it Plymouth. But their first winter was devastating. They had arrived too late to plant crops, and many were weakened by the voyage. Disease, cold and hunger swept through the settlement, and by the spring, about half of the passengers and crew had died.","The land was not empty. It was home to the Wampanoag people, whose villages in the area had been devastated by an epidemic of disease brought by earlier European traders a few years before. In March 1621, a Wampanoag man named Tisquantum, or Squanto, who had been kidnapped by an English explorer years earlier and had learned English, came to help. He showed the settlers how to plant maize, using fish as fertiliser, and where to fish and hunt. The Wampanoag leader, Massasoit, agreed a treaty of peace with the settlers.","In the autumn of 1621, after their first harvest, the Pilgrims held a three-day celebration, which around ninety Wampanoag men attended, bringing deer. This feast is remembered as the ‘first Thanksgiving’, now a national holiday in the United States. But the peace did not last. As more and more English settlers arrived, they took over land, and in the decades that followed, conflict and disease devastated the Native American peoples of New England. For many Wampanoag people today, the arrival of the Mayflower is a day of mourning rather than celebration."],"glossary":{"battered":"Damaged by repeated blows or rough use.","separatists":"People who wanted to separate from the official church.","identity":"Who someone is; the qualities that make a group what it is.","hull":"The main body of a ship.","cramped":"Too small and crowded.","compact":"Here, a formal agreement.","colony":"A settlement in a new land controlled by another country.","epidemic":"A disease that spreads quickly to many people.","fertiliser":"A substance added to soil to help plants grow.","mourning":"Showing great sadness for a death or loss."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did the Separatists want to leave England?","o":["They wanted gold","They wanted to worship in their own way, which was illegal in England","They were sailors","They were sent by the King"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They “wanted to worship in their own way, which was against the law in England.”"},{"q":"Why did the voyage start so late in the year?","o":["The Speedwell leaked so badly they had to turn back twice","They were waiting for the King","They had no food","The Mayflower was being built"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The Speedwell leaked and “both ships had to turn back twice.”"},{"q":"What does John Howland’s story show about the voyage?","o":["It was extremely dangerous","It was calm and safe","Swimming was popular","Ropes were not needed"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"He was swept overboard in a storm and survived only by grabbing a rope."},{"q":"What was the Mayflower Compact?","o":["A map of America","An agreement to make and obey fair laws for the colony","A ship’s logbook","A peace treaty with the Wampanoag"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"The men promised “to make and obey fair laws for the good of the colony.”"},{"q":"How did Tisquantum help the settlers?","o":["He gave them a ship","He showed them how to plant maize and where to fish and hunt","He built their houses","He took them back to England"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“He showed the settlers how to plant maize … and where to fish and hunt.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer end with the Wampanoag view of the Mayflower?","o":["To show that the same event can have very different meanings for different peoples","To praise the Pilgrims","To describe Thanksgiving food","To explain how ships work"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"For many Wampanoag people it is “a day of mourning rather than celebration.”"}]},{"id":"y175","title":"The Secret Garden: The Key","kind":"Retelling","theme":"garden","author":"Retold from Frances Hodgson Burnett","text":["When Mary Lennox was sent to Misselthwaite Manor to live with her uncle, everybody said she was the most disagreeable-looking child ever seen. She had a thin little face and a thin little body, thin light hair and a sour expression. She had been born in India, where her parents had been too busy and too fond of parties to pay her much attention, and she had been looked after by servants who did whatever she told them. So she had grown up selfish, spoilt and very cross. Then a terrible outbreak of cholera swept through the house. Her parents and almost all the servants died, and Mary was found alone, forgotten, in the empty bungalow.","Now she was in Yorkshire, in an enormous, gloomy old house on the edge of a great moor, with a hundred rooms that were nearly all shut up and locked. Her uncle, Mr Craven, was a hunchbacked, unhappy man who travelled abroad most of the time and did not want to see her. Nobody seemed to want her at all.","The only person who talked to her properly was the young housemaid, Martha, a cheerful, rosy-cheeked Yorkshire girl who did not do whatever Mary said and who told her to put on her own clothes. ‘Tha’ doesn’t know how to do anything for thysel’?’ she asked in astonishment. Martha told Mary to go outside and play in the gardens, and she mentioned something curious. One of the gardens had been locked up for ten years. Mr Craven had shut it after his wife died, locked the door and buried the key. Nobody had been inside since.","Mary had never cared about anything much before. But she began to think about the locked garden. Each day she wandered along the paths and the high walls, looking for a door. She made friends, in a prickly sort of way, with the old gardener, Ben Weatherstaff, and with a bold little robin with a red breast, who followed her about and seemed to want to talk. The fresh, wild wind from the moor made her cheeks pink, and she found, to her surprise, that she was hungry at mealtimes.","One day, after a rainy spell, Mary was walking beside a flowerbed when she saw the robin hopping about on a heap of freshly turned earth, where a dog had been digging for a mole. Something was half buried in the soil. It looked like a ring of rusty iron. Mary picked it up. It was an old key, which looked as if it had been buried a long time.","‘Perhaps it has been buried for ten years,’ she said in a whisper. ‘Perhaps it is the key to the garden!’","But where was the door? She looked at the walls covered in thick ivy, and could see nothing. Then, a few days later, a gust of wind swung aside a loose trailing spray of ivy, and the robin flew up to the top of the wall – and Mary saw something underneath the leaves. It was a round knob. It was the handle of a door.","With her heart thumping, she pushed back the ivy and found the keyhole. She put the key in. It needed two hands to turn it, but it did turn. Then she took a long breath and looked behind her to see if anyone was coming. No one was.","She pushed back the door, which opened slowly, slowly, and slipped through it. Then she shut it behind her and stood with her back against it, looking about her and breathing quite fast with excitement and wonder.","She was standing inside the secret garden."],"glossary":{"disagreeable-looking":"Looking unpleasant and bad-tempered.","cholera":"A dangerous disease caught from dirty water.","bungalow":"A one-storey house.","moor":"A wide area of open, wild land covered with grass and heather.","hunchbacked":"Having a back that is curved outward at the top.","prickly":"Here, easily annoyed or unfriendly.","spray":"Here, a small branch with leaves or flowers.","gust":"A sudden strong rush of wind."},"questions":[{"q":"Why had Mary grown up selfish and spoilt?","o":["Her parents ignored her and servants did whatever she told them","She was very rich","She lived in Yorkshire","She went to boarding school"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Her parents paid little attention, and servants “did whatever she told them.”"},{"q":"How is Martha different from Mary’s servants in India?","o":["She does whatever Mary says","She does not obey Mary’s every wish and tells her to dress herself","She is very strict and unkind","She speaks only Hindi"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Martha “did not do whatever Mary said and … told her to put on her own clothes.”"},{"q":"Why had the garden been locked for ten years?","o":["It was dangerous","Mr Craven shut it after his wife died","The gardener lost the key","It was full of weeds"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Mr Craven had shut it after his wife died.”"},{"q":"What changes in Mary as she spends time outside?","o":["Her cheeks become pink and she feels hungry – she is growing healthier and more interested","She becomes more selfish","She becomes ill","She stops going outside"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The moor wind made her cheeks pink and “she was hungry at mealtimes.”"},{"q":"What part does the robin play in the story?","o":["It leads Mary to the key and then to the door","It steals the key","It frightens Mary","It belongs to Martha"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"It hops near the buried key and flies up to the hidden door."},{"q":"How does the writer build suspense as Mary opens the door?","o":["Slow, careful actions: two hands to turn the key, a long breath, the door opening slowly","By having Mary run away","By describing the weather","By introducing a villain"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Slowing the action builds excitement before the reveal."}]},{"id":"y176","title":"Elephants: Gentle Giants","kind":"Non-fiction","theme":"jungle","text":["The elephant is the largest animal living on land. An adult male African savanna elephant can stand four metres tall at the shoulder and weigh six tonnes – as much as about eighty adult humans. Yet elephants can move almost silently on the soft, cushioned pads of their feet, and they are among the most intelligent, social and emotional animals on Earth.","There are three species of elephant: the African savanna elephant, the smaller African forest elephant and the Asian elephant. You can tell African and Asian elephants apart by their ears. African elephants have huge ears, shaped a little like the continent of Africa, which they flap to cool themselves down; blood vessels in the ears release heat. Asian elephants have smaller, rounder ears, and often only the males have long tusks.","An elephant’s trunk is one of the most remarkable tools in the animal kingdom. It is a fusion of the nose and upper lip, contains around 40,000 muscles and has no bones at all. An elephant uses its trunk to breathe, smell, drink, eat, trumpet, greet other elephants and even snorkel when swimming. It is strong enough to rip a branch off a tree, yet sensitive enough to pick up a single peanut. An elephant can smell water several kilometres away.","Elephants live in close family groups led by the oldest female, called the matriarch. The group is usually made up of related females and their young; adult males tend to live alone or in small groups of bachelors. The matriarch’s knowledge is vital. She remembers where to find water and food in times of drought, sometimes from decades earlier, and leads the family there. The saying ‘an elephant never forgets’ has some truth in it.","Baby elephants are looked after by the whole family. A calf is born after a pregnancy of about twenty-two months – the longest of any land mammal – and can weigh over a hundred kilograms at birth. Its aunts, sisters and cousins help to protect it and teach it how to use its trunk, which at first flops about uncontrollably.","Elephants communicate in many ways: with trumpets, roars and rumbles, many of them so low that humans cannot hear them. These low rumbles travel through the air and the ground for kilometres, and elephants may detect them through sensitive cells in their feet. Elephants also show behaviour that looks very much like grief. When a family member dies, others may stand over the body, touch it gently with their trunks, and return to the bones months or years later.","Elephants play a crucial role in their ecosystems. They are sometimes called ecosystem engineers. By pulling down trees, they open up grasslands; by digging for water, they create waterholes used by other animals; and by eating fruit and travelling long distances, they spread seeds through their dung.","Sadly, elephants are endangered. Their numbers have fallen dramatically, mainly because of poaching for their ivory tusks and the loss of their habitats to farms and settlements, which also brings them into conflict with people. Many countries have banned the ivory trade, and conservation projects are working to protect elephants and help them live alongside local communities. Protecting elephants means protecting the whole web of life that depends on them."],"glossary":{"savanna":"A large grassy plain with few trees, found in hot countries.","fusion":"Two or more things joined to make one.","matriarch":"A female who leads a family or group.","bachelors":"Here, adult males without a mate.","drought":"A long period with little or no rain.","ecosystems":"All the living things in an area and how they affect each other.","poaching":"Illegally hunting or catching animals.","habitats":"The natural homes of animals or plants.","conservation":"Protecting animals, plants and nature."},"questions":[{"q":"How can you tell an African elephant from an Asian elephant?","o":["African elephants have huge ears; Asian elephants have smaller, rounder ears","African elephants are smaller","Asian elephants have no trunk","They look identical"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"African elephants have huge ears; Asian elephants have “smaller, rounder ears.”"},{"q":"How do elephants’ ears help them stay cool?","o":["They fan away flies","Blood vessels in the ears release heat as they flap","They hold water","They block the sun"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Blood vessels in the ears release heat.”"},{"q":"What does the contrast between ripping off a branch and picking up a peanut show about the trunk?","o":["It is both very strong and very sensitive","It is weak","It can only do one thing","It has bones"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"These examples show its strength and delicacy."},{"q":"Why is the matriarch so important?","o":["She is the strongest fighter","She remembers where to find food and water, even from decades earlier","She has the longest tusks","She is the youngest"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“She remembers where to find water and food in times of drought.”"},{"q":"How do elephants act as ‘ecosystem engineers’?","o":["They build dams","They open grasslands, create waterholes and spread seeds","They plant trees deliberately","They build nests"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"Pulling down trees, digging for water and spreading seeds in dung shape the environment."},{"q":"What are the two main threats to elephants?","o":["Cold weather and disease","Poaching for ivory and loss of habitat","Other elephants and lions","Pollution and noise"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Mainly because of poaching for their ivory tusks and the loss of their habitats.”"}]},{"id":"y177","title":"Diary: Climbing Ben Nevis","kind":"Diary","theme":"mountain","text":["Friday\nWe drove up to Fort William today – eight hours in the car with my little sister singing the same song on repeat. Tomorrow is The Day. Dad has been training for months, and so have I, sort of: we walked up Snowdon at Easter and I did the Three Peaks of Yorkshire with Scouts. But Ben Nevis is the highest mountain in Britain, 1,345 metres, and the guidebook says the weather on top can be dangerous even in summer. Mum made us pack waterproofs, hats, gloves, a map, a compass, a first-aid kit, a head-torch, a whistle and enough food for a small army. ‘You can always turn back,’ she said. ‘The mountain will still be there.’","Saturday – morning\nWe set off from the visitor centre in Glen Nevis at seven o’clock. The path started gently, through fields with sheep, then began to zigzag steeply up the side of the mountain. It is called the Mountain Track, but most people call it the ‘tourist path’, which makes it sound easy. It is not easy. The path is made of loose rocks and big stone steps, and it just keeps going up. After an hour my legs were burning and I was too hot in my jacket. By the halfway lake, Lochan Meall an t-Suidhe (which I definitely cannot pronounce), the sun had disappeared and the clouds had come down.","Saturday – later\nAbove the lochan, the path turned into a long series of zigzags across a huge slope of scree. People call them the ‘zigzags of doom’. I counted them for a while, then gave up. We were inside the cloud now, so everything was grey and damp, and I could only see about twenty metres. Every so often a figure would loom out of the mist coming down, say ‘nearly there!’ and disappear again. Everybody says ‘nearly there’. Nobody means it.","I was really struggling at this point. My legs felt like jelly and I wanted to stop. Dad noticed and we sat on a rock and ate the emergency chocolate (it was an emergency). He said the trick on big hills is not to look at the top, just at the next twenty steps, then the next twenty. So that’s what I did.","Saturday – the summit!\nWe reached the top at 11:50. It is a huge, flat, stony plateau, covered in old snow patches even in June, with the ruins of an old weather observatory and a big pile of stones called a cairn with a trig point on top. We could not see a thing. Not a single mountain. Just white cloud in every direction, and wind so cold it hurt my ears. A man from Germany offered to take our photo. In it, we look soaked, pink-faced and incredibly happy.","Dad was very careful on the summit. He made me stay away from the edges, because there are enormous cliffs on the north side, hidden in the cloud, and he used the map and compass to find exactly the right bearing back to the path. He said people get lost up here every year because they walk off in the wrong direction in mist. It was a bit scary, actually, how easy it would be.","Saturday – evening\nComing down was harder on my knees than going up, and it took nearly four hours. But halfway down, the cloud suddenly tore open, and there was the whole of Glen Nevis below us, green and gold in the sunshine, with the river shining at the bottom. It was the best view I have ever seen, and I think it was better because we had worked so hard for it.","Total time: nine hours. Total blisters: three. Would I do it again? Ask me when my legs work."],"glossary":{"scree":"A mass of small loose stones covering a slope.","loom":"To appear as a large, unclear shape, often in a threatening way.","summit":"The top of a mountain.","plateau":"An area of high, flat land.","observatory":"A building for studying the weather or stars.","cairn":"A pile of stones marking a summit or path.","bearing":"A direction measured with a compass."},"questions":[{"q":"How high is Ben Nevis, according to the diary?","o":["1,345 metres","3,000 metres","1,085 metres","800 metres"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Ben Nevis is the highest mountain in Britain, 1,345 metres.”"},{"q":"What does Mum mean by “The mountain will still be there”?","o":["It is fine to turn back if things are dangerous; you can try another time","The mountain might fall down","They should go home now","Mountains move"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"She is reassuring them that safety matters more than reaching the top."},{"q":"Why does the writer say “Nobody means it” about “nearly there”?","o":["Walkers say it to encourage others even when the top is still far away","People are lying to be cruel","Nobody had reached the top","They were speaking German"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Everybody says it, but the summit is still a long way off."},{"q":"What advice does Dad give for climbing big hills?","o":["Run as fast as you can","Don’t look at the top, just at the next twenty steps","Always take the steepest route","Never stop for food"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The trick on big hills is not to look at the top, just at the next twenty steps.”"},{"q":"Why is Dad so careful on the summit?","o":["There are huge hidden cliffs and it is easy to walk the wrong way in mist","He is afraid of heights","He wants a better photo","He has lost his map"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"There are “enormous cliffs on the north side,” and people get lost in mist."},{"q":"Why does the writer think the view of Glen Nevis was so special?","o":["They had worked so hard to earn it","It was on television","It was sunny all day","They took a cable car"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"“I think it was better because we had worked so hard for it.”"}]},{"id":"y178","title":"Space Junk: The Rubbish Above Our Heads","kind":"Science","theme":"space","text":["Look up on a clear night, and the sky seems peaceful and empty. It is not. Circling the Earth, travelling faster than a bullet, are millions of pieces of rubbish: dead satellites, spent rocket stages, flecks of paint, bolts, a lost glove and even a toolbag dropped by an astronaut in 2008. This is space junk, or orbital debris, and it is becoming one of the biggest challenges of the space age.","Since the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1 in 1957, humans have sent more than 15,000 satellites into orbit, along with the rockets that carried them. Many satellites stop working after a few years but stay in orbit for decades, or even centuries. Upper stages of rockets are often left circling too. Over time, these objects break up – through explosions of leftover fuel, collisions and the harsh conditions of space – creating clouds of smaller fragments.","Space agencies track more than 30,000 pieces of debris larger than about ten centimetres. But scientists estimate there are around a million pieces between one and ten centimetres across, and more than a hundred million pieces smaller than one centimetre. They may sound tiny, but in low Earth orbit, debris travels at around 28,000 kilometres per hour, about eight kilometres every second. At that speed, a fleck of paint can chip a window, and a fragment the size of a marble can hit with the force of a bowling ball dropped from a tall building. A piece the size of a tennis ball could destroy a satellite.","The International Space Station, which carries astronauts, has to move out of the way of large pieces of debris several times a year. Its most vulnerable parts are protected by special shields made of layers of material that break up small particles. On a few occasions, when debris was spotted too late, the astronauts on board have had to shelter in their return capsules in case the station was hit.","Two events in particular made the problem much worse. In 2007, China tested an anti-satellite missile by deliberately destroying one of its own old weather satellites, creating more than 3,000 trackable fragments. In 2009, an active American communications satellite, Iridium 33, crashed into a dead Russian satellite, Kosmos 2251, at a speed of about 42,000 kilometres per hour, producing another cloud of debris.","Some scientists warn of a nightmare called the Kessler Syndrome, named after the NASA scientist Donald Kessler, who described it in 1978. If there is enough debris, one collision could create fragments that cause more collisions, which create more fragments, in a chain reaction. Eventually, some orbits could become so crowded with junk that they would be too dangerous to use for generations. That would affect things we rely on every day: weather forecasting, satellite navigation, television, internet links and climate monitoring.","The problem is growing quickly, as companies launch thousands of new satellites to provide internet connections from space. To tackle it, many space agencies now require new satellites to be moved out of busy orbits or brought down to burn up in the atmosphere within a few years of the end of their missions. Engineers are also testing ways to clean up existing junk, using nets, harpoons, robotic arms and magnets to catch old satellites and drag them down. In 2018, a British-led experiment called RemoveDEBRIS successfully fired a net and a harpoon at test targets in space.","Space, it turns out, is not as big as we thought. Like the oceans, it is a shared resource, and if we want to keep using it, we will have to learn to keep it clean."],"glossary":{"orbital":"To do with an object’s path around a planet.","debris":"Scattered pieces of broken material.","fragments":"Small pieces broken off something.","vulnerable":"Easily harmed or damaged.","particles":"Very tiny pieces.","deliberately":"On purpose.","syndrome":"A set of problems that happen together.","navigation":"Finding the way.","resource":"Something useful that can be used by people."},"questions":[{"q":"What is space junk?","o":["Rocks from the Moon","Dead satellites, rocket stages and fragments left orbiting the Earth","Stars that have died","Rubbish thrown from planes"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It includes “dead satellites, spent rocket stages, flecks of paint, bolts.”"},{"q":"Why can even tiny pieces of debris be dangerous?","o":["They are poisonous","They travel at about eight kilometres per second, so they hit with great force","They are very heavy","They are radioactive"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"At that speed, a marble-sized fragment hits like a dropped bowling ball."},{"q":"Why does the writer compare a marble-sized fragment to a bowling ball dropped from a tall building?","o":["To help readers understand the enormous force of a high-speed impact","Because bowling balls orbit the Earth","To describe its shape","To make readers laugh"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"A familiar image helps readers grasp the surprising power of tiny debris."},{"q":"What is the Kessler Syndrome?","o":["A space illness","A chain reaction of collisions creating more and more debris","A type of satellite","A rocket engine"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"One collision creates fragments that cause more collisions “in a chain reaction.”"},{"q":"Why would the Kessler Syndrome affect everyday life?","o":["We rely on satellites for weather, navigation, TV and internet","It would block the Sun","It would make rockets cheaper","It would cause earthquakes"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Unusable orbits would disrupt services we depend on daily."},{"q":"What comparison does the writer make in the final paragraph?","o":["Space is like the oceans – a shared resource we must keep clean","Space is like a desert","Space is like a city","Space is empty"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"“Like the oceans, it is a shared resource.”"}]},{"id":"y179","title":"The Last Piano","kind":"Story","theme":"music","text":["The piano in the corner of Platform 4 at Birmingham New Street had been there for three years. It was an old upright, painted in bright flowers by a local art group, with a sign on top that said: PLAY ME, I’M YOURS. Most days, someone stopped to play: businessmen bashing out pop songs while they waited for their trains, a little girl picking out ‘Twinkle, Twinkle’ with one finger, a homeless man who played the most beautiful jazz anyone had ever heard and then shuffled away without a word.","Leon had never played it. He walked past it every day on the way to school, eyes down, rucksack on, earphones in. He had played the piano once, a long time ago, before his dad got ill. His dad had taught him on the old keyboard in the front room, sitting beside him on the stool, guiding his fingers. After the funeral, Leon had put the keyboard in the loft and not touched it since. That was two years ago.","Then, one Monday, there was a new sign on the piano. THIS PIANO WILL BE REMOVED ON FRIDAY. THANK YOU TO EVERYONE WHO PLAYED IT.","Leon stopped. He did not know why the sign made his chest feel tight. It was only a battered old piano in a train station. But all that week, he found himself slowing down as he walked past, glancing at it, then hurrying on.","On Friday, his train was cancelled. The platform filled up with irritated commuters checking their phones. And the piano stood in the corner, its painted flowers fading, with the sign on top: TODAY IS THE LAST DAY.","Leon put his rucksack down. He did not quite decide to. His feet just took him to the stool, and he sat, and lifted the lid, and looked at the keys. Yellowed. Chipped. One of the black notes was missing completely.","He put his hands on the keys. For a moment they shook so much that he nearly stood up again. Then, very quietly, he began to play the piece his dad had taught him first – the slow opening of ‘Clair de Lune’ by Debussy, his dad’s favourite. He was out of practice. He stumbled over chords, missed notes, had to stop and start again. But his fingers remembered more than he did.","Something changed in the station. A woman with a suitcase stopped. Then a man in a suit lowered his phone. Two students who had been laughing fell quiet. Leon did not see any of it. He had his eyes half closed, and somewhere in the music he could almost feel someone sitting beside him on the stool, guiding his hands.","When he finished, there was a silence. Then the platform burst into applause – real applause, from about forty people. Leon looked up, startled, his face burning, and saw that the woman with the suitcase was wiping her eyes.","An old man in a flat cap came over. ‘My wife used to play that,’ he said quietly. ‘Thank you, son.’","The train came. Leon caught it. That night, he climbed into the loft, found the keyboard under a pile of boxes and carried it down to the front room. His mum stood in the doorway and watched him plug it in. She did not say anything. She did not need to.","The next Monday, the corner of Platform 4 was empty. But Leon walked past it with his head up, humming."],"glossary":{"upright":"An upright piano has its strings arranged vertically in a tall case.","shuffled":"Walked slowly, dragging the feet.","irritated":"Annoyed.","commuters":"People who travel regularly to work or school.","chords":"Groups of musical notes played together.","applause":"Clapping to show approval."},"questions":[{"q":"Why had Leon stopped playing the piano?","o":["He was not interested","His dad, who taught him, had died","His keyboard broke","He had no time"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"After his dad’s funeral, he put the keyboard away and had not touched it for two years."},{"q":"Why did the sign about the piano’s removal make Leon’s chest feel tight?","o":["It reminded him of losing something precious and of the music he had stopped playing","He was ill","He wanted to buy it","He disliked the station"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The coming loss echoes his grief and the music he had shut away."},{"q":"How does the writer show that Leon did not really plan to play?","o":["“He did not quite decide to. His feet just took him to the stool.”","He bought a ticket","He practised all week","His mum told him to"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"His body acts before his mind decides, showing an instinctive, emotional choice."},{"q":"What does “his fingers remembered more than he did” mean?","o":["His hands remembered the music through muscle memory even though he felt out of practice","His fingers could talk","He forgot the piece completely","He was reading sheet music"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Despite mistakes, his hands recalled the piece his dad taught him."},{"q":"How does Leon’s playing affect the people in the station?","o":["They ignore him","They stop, fall silent and are moved, then applaud","They complain about the noise","They ask him to stop"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"People stop, a woman wipes her eyes, and about forty people applaud."},{"q":"What does Leon bringing the keyboard down from the loft show?","o":["He is ready to start playing again and to remember his dad through music","He wants to sell it","He is moving house","He is angry with his mum"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"He returns to music, and he walks past the platform “with his head up, humming.”"}]},{"id":"y180","title":"The History of Writing","kind":"History","theme":"books","text":["Humans have been talking for perhaps a hundred thousand years or more. But for almost all of that time, nobody could write anything down. Every story, law, recipe and family history had to be remembered and passed on by word of mouth. Then, around 5,000 years ago, in what is now Iraq, people invented writing – and changed the world.","The first writing was not invented to record poetry or stories. It was invented by accountants. In the cities of Sumer, in Mesopotamia, between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers, temples and rulers needed to keep track of grain, sheep, beer and taxes. At first, they used small clay tokens to represent goods. Then they began pressing marks into tablets of wet clay. By around 3200 BC, these marks had developed into a full writing system. Scribes used a reed cut to a wedge-shaped point to press marks into the clay, so the writing is called cuneiform, from the Latin word for ‘wedge’. The clay tablets were then dried in the sun or baked. Hundreds of thousands have survived, recording everything from shopping lists to the world’s oldest surviving great story, the Epic of Gilgamesh.","Writing was invented independently in several places. In Egypt, around the same time as in Mesopotamia, people developed hieroglyphs, a system of beautiful picture-like signs carved on temples and painted on papyrus, an early form of paper made from reeds. In China, writing appears by around 1200 BC, on bones and turtle shells used to ask questions of ancestors and gods; modern Chinese characters descend from this script. In Central America, the Maya developed their own writing system independently, carving it on stone monuments and painting it in folding books.","Early writing systems used hundreds or thousands of signs, each standing for a whole word or a syllable, and learning them took years. Only a small group of trained scribes could read and write. The great breakthrough was the alphabet, in which each sign stands for a single sound. Around 1800 BC, workers in Egypt who spoke a Semitic language borrowed some Egyptian signs to represent the sounds of their own language. This idea was developed by the Phoenicians, a trading people from the eastern Mediterranean, whose alphabet of twenty-two letters spread through their trade routes. The Greeks adapted it around 800 BC, adding letters for vowel sounds. The Romans adapted the Greek alphabet, and the Roman, or Latin, alphabet is the one you are reading now. The word ‘alphabet’ itself comes from the first two Greek letters, alpha and beta.","For thousands of years, books had to be copied by hand, which made them rare and expensive. In medieval Europe, monks spent years copying a single Bible onto parchment. Then, in about 1440, a German goldsmith named Johannes Gutenberg developed a printing press with movable metal type. Books could now be produced quickly and cheaply. Within fifty years, millions of books had been printed across Europe, and ideas spread faster than ever before. In England, William Caxton set up the first printing press in 1476.","Today, writing surrounds us – on screens, signs, packets and phones. More people can read and write than ever before, although millions of adults around the world still cannot. Every time you send a message, you are using an invention that began with a scribe in Mesopotamia, counting sheep on a lump of clay."],"glossary":{"accountants":"People who keep records of money and goods.","tokens":"Small objects used to represent something else.","scribes":"People whose job was to write and copy documents.","cuneiform":"An ancient writing system of wedge-shaped marks.","independently":"Separately, without help or influence from others.","hieroglyphs":"Pictures and symbols used as writing in ancient Egypt.","papyrus":"An early kind of paper made from reeds.","syllable":"A part of a word with one vowel sound.","parchment":"Material made from animal skin, used for writing on."},"questions":[{"q":"Why was writing first invented in Mesopotamia?","o":["To write poems","To keep records of goods such as grain, sheep and taxes","To send letters to friends","To write laws"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“It was invented by accountants … to keep track of grain, sheep, beer and taxes.”"},{"q":"Why is cuneiform called by that name?","o":["It comes from the Latin word for ‘wedge’, describing the shape of the marks","It was invented by Cuneus","It means ‘clay’","It means ‘sheep’"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Cuneiform, from the Latin word for ‘wedge’.”"},{"q":"What does it mean that writing was invented “independently” in several places?","o":["Different civilisations developed writing separately, without copying each other","Writing was invented by one person","Only Egypt had writing","Writing spread from China"],"a":0,"skill":"Vocabulary","why":"Egypt, China and the Maya each developed their own systems."},{"q":"Why was the alphabet such a great breakthrough?","o":["Each sign stood for a single sound, so far fewer signs were needed and writing was easier to learn","It used pictures","It was very beautiful","It was carved on stone"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Earlier systems had hundreds or thousands of signs; the alphabet needed far fewer."},{"q":"Where does the word ‘alphabet’ come from?","o":["The first two Greek letters, alpha and beta","The Phoenicians’ king","A Latin word for ‘letters’","An Egyptian god"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The word ‘alphabet’ itself comes from the first two Greek letters, alpha and beta.”"},{"q":"What was the effect of Gutenberg’s printing press?","o":["Books could be produced quickly and cheaply, so ideas spread faster","Books became rarer","Monks copied more Bibles","Writing was banned"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"“Books could now be produced quickly and cheaply … ideas spread faster than ever before.”"}]},{"id":"y181","title":"Rain on the Roof","kind":"Poem","theme":"rain","text":["Tonight the rain has come to visit.\nIt knocks on the skylight with small cold knuckles,\nfirst politely, one tap, two,\nthen all its fingers at once,\ndrumming, drumming,\nas if it has something urgent to tell us.","It runs down the window in long silver threads,\nunravelling the streetlight.\nIt gargles in the gutter,\nchatters in the drainpipe,\nslaps the patio with flat wet hands\nand fills the bucket we forgot\nwith a deep, hollow note.","Somewhere out there\nit is soaking the dog’s ball in the garden,\nwashing the chalk drawings off the pavement,\nfilling the pond to its brim,\nmaking the snails come out\nto wander like slow old men\nalong the wall.","And in here, under the slates,\nunder the duvet,\nI am dry and warm\nand listening.","There is no sound on Earth\nthat makes a bed feel safer\nthan rain\non the roof\nat night."],"glossary":{"skylight":"A window in a roof or ceiling.","knuckles":"The joints of the fingers.","unravelling":"Coming apart; here, the light seems pulled into threads.","gargles":"Makes a bubbling sound, like water in the throat.","gutter":"A channel along the edge of a roof that collects rainwater.","slates":"Thin flat pieces of grey rock used to cover roofs.","duvet":"A thick, soft quilt used as a bed cover."},"questions":[{"q":"How does the rain arrive in the first stanza?","o":["Suddenly and violently from the start","Politely at first, then drumming with all its fingers","Silently","Only as snow"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“First politely, one tap, two, / then all its fingers at once.”"},{"q":"Which technique is used in “It knocks on the skylight with small cold knuckles”?","o":["Personification","Alliteration only","A rhetorical question","A list of facts"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"The rain is described as a person knocking with knuckles."},{"q":"Which words in stanza two are examples of onomatopoeia?","o":["“gargles” and “chatters”","“silver” and “threads”","“window” and “streetlight”","“forgot” and “bucket”"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"‘Gargles’ and ‘chatters’ imitate the sounds of water."},{"q":"What does the simile “like slow old men” suggest about the snails?","o":["They move slowly and steadily","They are very fast","They are grumpy","They are lost"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Slow old men walk carefully and unhurriedly, like snails."},{"q":"How does stanza four contrast with stanza three?","o":["It moves from the wet world outside to the dry, warm speaker inside","It describes a storm at sea","It moves to the morning","It describes the dog"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"Outside everything is soaked; “in here … I am dry and warm.”"},{"q":"What feeling does the final stanza express?","o":["Fear of the storm","A sense of comfort and safety","Boredom","Anger at the weather"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"Rain on the roof makes “a bed feel safer” than anything."}]},{"id":"y182","title":"Nikola Tesla: The Man Who Lit the World","kind":"Biography","theme":"lab","text":["Every time you plug something into a wall socket, you are using an idea that a young Serbian engineer had while walking in a park in Budapest in 1882. His name was Nikola Tesla, and although he died poor and almost forgotten, his inventions helped to create the modern electrical world.","Tesla was born in 1856 in Smiljan, a village in what is now Croatia. His father was a priest, and his mother, though she could not read, was a brilliant inventor of household gadgets. Tesla later said he inherited his talent from her. As a boy, he had an extraordinary memory and could picture complicated machines in his mind in perfect detail, and he claimed he could design, build and test a machine entirely in his imagination before making it.","At that time, electricity was mostly produced and used as direct current, or DC, which flows steadily in one direction. DC was difficult to send over long distances without losing a lot of energy. Tesla became fascinated by alternating current, or AC, which rapidly changes direction many times a second. AC can be easily changed to a high voltage for travelling long distances and then back to a lower, safer voltage for homes. The problem was that nobody had built a practical motor that could run on AC. Walking in the park that day in 1882, reciting poetry, Tesla suddenly saw the solution: a rotating magnetic field. He drew the design in the dust with a stick.","In 1884, Tesla emigrated to the United States with, according to legend, four cents in his pocket. He went to work for Thomas Edison, the famous inventor of the light bulb, who was building a business supplying DC electricity. The two men did not get on. Tesla later claimed that Edison had promised him $50,000 to improve his DC generators and then refused to pay, saying, ‘You don’t understand our American humour.’ Tesla left, and for a time he was so poor that he worked digging ditches.","In 1888, the businessman George Westinghouse bought Tesla’s patents for AC motors and the AC system. What followed became known as the ‘War of the Currents’. Edison, who had invested heavily in DC, campaigned fiercely against AC, claiming it was too dangerous. But AC was more efficient, and in 1893 Westinghouse and Tesla won the contract to light the Chicago World’s Fair with AC, a dazzling display of 100,000 lamps. Soon afterwards, they built a great hydroelectric power plant at Niagara Falls, sending electricity to the city of Buffalo, forty kilometres away. AC had won, and it is still the system used across the world today.","Tesla’s imagination never stopped. He developed the Tesla coil, which produces spectacular high-voltage sparks and is still used in science demonstrations; he demonstrated radio-controlled boats in 1898, decades before remote controls; and he worked on wireless communication and even wireless power. He dreamed of a tower that could send electricity through the air without wires, but his backers lost faith and the project was abandoned.","Tesla was brilliant but difficult. He worked obsessively, slept little, never married, had many strange habits and often fell out with business partners. Many of his later ideas were impractical, and he died alone and in debt in a New York hotel room in 1943. For years his name was largely forgotten. Today, though, it is famous again: the international unit of magnetic field strength, the tesla, is named after him – and so is a well-known make of electric car."],"glossary":{"inherited":"Received a quality from a parent.","alternating":"Changing back and forth regularly.","voltage":"The force that pushes electricity through a circuit.","reciting":"Saying aloud from memory.","emigrated":"Left your own country to live in another.","patents":"Legal rights to be the only one allowed to make or sell an invention.","efficient":"Working well without wasting energy.","hydroelectric":"Producing electricity from the power of moving water.","obsessively":"Thinking about or doing something all the time."},"questions":[{"q":"What advantage does AC have over DC, according to the article?","o":["It is easier to change to high voltage for long distances and back down for homes","It flows in one direction","It needs no wires","It is older"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"AC “can be easily changed to a high voltage for travelling long distances and then back.”"},{"q":"What did Tesla realise in the park in Budapest?","o":["How to build a light bulb","How a rotating magnetic field could make an AC motor work","How to fly","How to make radio"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Tesla suddenly saw the solution: a rotating magnetic field.”"},{"q":"What does the story about Edison and the $50,000 suggest about their relationship?","o":["They were close friends","There was distrust and conflict between them","Edison was generous","Tesla owed Edison money"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Tesla claimed Edison refused to pay a promised sum; “the two men did not get on.”"},{"q":"What was the ‘War of the Currents’?","o":["A battle at Niagara Falls","A fierce competition between AC (Tesla and Westinghouse) and DC (Edison)","A real war in Serbia","A river race"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Edison campaigned against AC while Westinghouse and Tesla promoted it."},{"q":"How did the Chicago World’s Fair help AC win?","o":["It was lit by 100,000 AC lamps, showing AC worked spectacularly","It banned DC","Edison lost a race there","It was cancelled"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Lighting the fair with AC was a dazzling public demonstration of its success."},{"q":"How does the writer present Tesla’s character?","o":["Brilliant and imaginative but difficult and obsessive","Lazy and unoriginal","Wealthy and relaxed","Shy and unskilled"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"“Tesla was brilliant but difficult. He worked obsessively.”"}]},{"id":"y183","title":"The Terracotta Army","kind":"History","theme":"desert","text":["In March 1974, farmers in Shaanxi province, in northern China, were digging a well during a drought when their spades struck something hard. They pulled out pieces of baked clay: a head, an arm, part of a body. They had no idea that they had discovered one of the greatest archaeological finds in history: an army of thousands of life-sized clay soldiers, buried for more than 2,200 years to guard the tomb of China’s first emperor.","That emperor was Qin Shi Huang. Born in 259 BC, he became king of the state of Qin at the age of thirteen. At that time, China was divided into seven warring kingdoms. Through ruthless military campaigns, Qin conquered them all, and in 221 BC he declared himself the First Emperor of a united China. The name ‘China’ may come from ‘Qin’ (pronounced ‘chin’).","Qin Shi Huang was a powerful and terrifying ruler. He standardised weights, measures, coins and the written script across the whole empire, built roads and canals, and joined together earlier walls to form an early version of the Great Wall. But he was also brutal. He ordered books he disapproved of to be burned, and forced hundreds of thousands of people to work on his building projects, many of whom died. He was obsessed with death and spent years searching for an elixir of immortality. Ironically, some historians think the mercury he took, believing it would help him live for ever, may have contributed to his death in 210 BC.","Work on his tomb began soon after he became king and is said to have involved around 700,000 workers. The burial mound itself, which rises like a hill, has never been opened. The ancient historian Sima Qian, writing about a century later, described an underground palace containing models of the emperor’s whole empire, with rivers of flowing mercury and crossbows set to fire at intruders. Scientists have found unusually high levels of mercury in the soil around the mound, suggesting this account may be true. Archaeologists have decided not to excavate it until they can be sure they will not damage what lies inside.","The army was buried in pits about 1.5 kilometres east of the mound. So far, archaeologists have found three main pits containing an estimated 8,000 soldiers, along with 130 chariots, around 520 horses and hundreds more cavalry horses, though most have not yet been fully excavated. Remarkably, no two soldiers’ faces seem to be exactly the same. Each figure was made from parts produced in workshops – legs, bodies, arms, heads – then assembled, and the faces and hairstyles were finished individually by hand. The soldiers vary in height, uniform and pose according to their rank: archers kneel, infantry stand ready, officers wear elaborate headdresses and generals are taller than the rest. Many originally held real bronze weapons, some of which are still sharp.","When they were made, the warriors were brightly painted in reds, greens, blues and purples. But when the figures are dug up and exposed to the dry air, the paint curls and flakes away within minutes. This is one reason that excavation proceeds so slowly and carefully.","Today, the Terracotta Army is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, visited by millions of people each year. The silent ranks of soldiers, standing in their ancient battle formation, are a powerful reminder of the ambition – and the fear of death – of the man who first united China."],"glossary":{"archaeological":"To do with the study of the past through remains.","ruthless":"Without pity; cruel.","standardised":"Made the same everywhere.","brutal":"Very cruel and violent.","elixir":"A magical liquid believed to cure illness or give eternal life.","immortality":"Living for ever.","intruders":"People who enter a place without permission.","excavate":"To dig carefully to uncover remains.","infantry":"Soldiers who fight on foot.","formation":"An arrangement of people or things in a pattern."},"questions":[{"q":"How was the Terracotta Army discovered?","o":["By archaeologists searching for it","By farmers digging a well during a drought","By a satellite","By tourists"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Farmers … were digging a well during a drought when their spades struck something hard.”"},{"q":"What did Qin Shi Huang standardise across his empire?","o":["Weights, measures, coins and the written script","Clothes and food","Religions","Languages spoken at home"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He “standardised weights, measures, coins and the written script.”"},{"q":"Why is it ironic that Qin Shi Huang took mercury?","o":["He believed it would make him live for ever, but it may have helped cause his death","Mercury was cheap","He hated mercury","It made him stronger"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"He sought immortality, yet the mercury “may have contributed to his death.”"},{"q":"Why have archaeologists not opened the burial mound?","o":["It is too small","They want to be sure they will not damage what is inside","It is empty","It is under the sea"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They will not excavate “until they can be sure they will not damage what lies inside.”"},{"q":"How were the soldiers made so that each looks different?","o":["Each was carved from a single stone","Parts were made in workshops and assembled, then faces and hair finished individually by hand","They were cast from real soldiers","They were all identical"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Parts were assembled and “the faces and hairstyles were finished individually by hand.”"},{"q":"Why does excavation proceed so slowly?","o":["The workers are tired","The original paint flakes away within minutes in dry air","Visitors get in the way","The soldiers are heavy"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“The paint curls and flakes away within minutes. This is one reason that excavation proceeds so slowly.”"}]},{"id":"y184","title":"The Hidden Door","kind":"Fantasy","theme":"castle","text":["Nobody had ever noticed the door in the school library, which was strange, because it was bright blue.","Rafael noticed it on a wet Wednesday lunchtime, when he had come to the library to avoid football. It was tucked between the shelf of atlases and the shelf of books about volcanoes, and it was small – he would have to duck to go through it – with a round brass handle and a tiny keyhole shaped like a star. He was sure it had not been there yesterday.","‘Miss Okonkwo,’ he said to the librarian, ‘where does that door go?’","Miss Okonkwo looked up from her desk, followed his finger and frowned. ‘What door?’","Rafael looked back. The door was still there, bright and blue and obvious. But Miss Okonkwo was already looking at her screen again. ‘There’s no door there, Rafael,’ she said. ‘Just the atlases.’","He waited until she went to help a Year 9 boy with the printer. Then he went over and turned the handle.","It opened.","On the other side was not a cupboard or a corridor but a staircase, spiralling down into warm golden light. It smelled of old paper and something sweet, like toasted almonds. Rafael looked back at the library – grey light, rain on the windows, the printer jamming again – and went down.","At the bottom was a library. But it was a library like nothing he had ever seen. The shelves went up so high that they disappeared into a sky full of stars. Books flew between the shelves like slow birds, flapping their pages. A river of ink ran down the middle of the floor, and on its banks sat readers of every kind: a girl in a Victorian dress, an old man in a spacesuit, a knight with his helmet off, a fox wearing spectacles. Every one of them was reading.","‘You found the door,’ said a voice. A small woman with silver hair and a cardigan full of pencils was standing beside him. She looked oddly like Miss Okonkwo, if Miss Okonkwo had been a hundred years older and a great deal more cheerful. ‘Most people don’t. You have to really need it.’","‘Need what?’ said Rafael.","‘A story,’ said the woman. ‘Somewhere to go. What were you running away from?’","Rafael thought about football, and about the boys who laughed when he missed the ball, and about how he never quite knew what to say to anyone at this school. He did not say any of it. But the woman nodded as if he had.","She held out a book. It had no title on the cover, only a small star-shaped hole, like the keyhole. ‘Every reader who comes here gets one,’ she said. ‘It will be whatever you need it to be.’","Rafael opened it. The first page said: Chapter One. The boy who found the blue door.","When the bell rang for afternoon lessons, Rafael was sitting in the school library by the atlases, holding a book with a star on the cover. There was no door behind him. But in his pocket, though he did not remember putting it there, was a small brass key, shaped like a star.","He went to maths with the book under his arm. At the next table, a quiet girl he had never spoken to glanced at it and her eyes widened. She reached into her bag and, without saying anything, showed him a book of her own. It had a star on the cover too."],"glossary":{"atlases":"Books of maps.","spiralling":"Winding round and round in circles.","spectacles":"Glasses.","cardigan":"A knitted jumper that opens down the front."},"questions":[{"q":"Why did Rafael go to the library at lunchtime?","o":["To do homework","To avoid football","To meet a friend","To print something"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He had come “to avoid football.”"},{"q":"What is strange about Miss Okonkwo’s reaction to the door?","o":["She cannot see it, even though it is bright blue","She opens it","She tells him to go through","She is frightened of it"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"She says, “There’s no door there … Just the atlases.”"},{"q":"How does the writer make the hidden library feel magical?","o":["Books flying like birds, shelves vanishing into a starry sky and a river of ink","Describing computers","Describing ordinary bookshelves","Describing a broken printer"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"These impossible images create a magical setting."},{"q":"According to the woman, why do most people not find the door?","o":["It is too small","You have to really need it","It is locked","It only opens at night"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Most people don’t. You have to really need it.”"},{"q":"What is significant about the first page of Rafael’s book?","o":["It tells his own story, as if the book is about him","It is blank","It is a maths textbook","It is about volcanoes"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“Chapter One. The boy who found the blue door” – the book is about Rafael."},{"q":"What does the ending with the quiet girl suggest?","o":["Rafael may have found a friend who shares his secret","The girl stole his book","The library is closing","Rafael is in trouble"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"She has a star book too, hinting at a new friendship for someone who felt alone."}]},{"id":"y185","title":"The Great Journeys: Bird Migration","kind":"Science","theme":"nature","text":["Every spring, a small bird weighing about as much as a teaspoon of sugar arrives in a British barn after flying around 10,000 kilometres from South Africa. It is a swallow, and it has crossed the Sahara Desert, the Mediterranean Sea and the length of Europe to reach the very same building where it nested the year before, perhaps even the same beam. In autumn, it will make the whole journey in reverse. Bird migration is one of the most astonishing events on the planet.","Around 4,000 species of bird – about forty per cent of all bird species – migrate regularly. They travel to take advantage of seasonal food supplies. In summer, northern places such as Britain, Scandinavia and the Arctic have long days and an explosion of insects, seeds and fish, perfect for raising chicks. In winter, food becomes scarce and the weather harsh, so many birds fly south to warmer places. Others, such as many geese, ducks and waders, come to Britain from the frozen north to spend the winter here, where it is milder.","Some journeys are almost unbelievable. The Arctic tern, a slender white seabird, travels from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back every year, following the summer from one end of the world to the other. Its zigzagging route can add up to around 70,000 kilometres a year. Over a thirty-year life, that is about the same as flying to the Moon and back three times. The bar-tailed godwit holds the record for the longest non-stop flight: one tagged bird flew more than 13,000 kilometres from Alaska to Tasmania without landing, for eleven days.","To prepare, migrating birds eat huge amounts of food and store it as fat, sometimes almost doubling their body weight. Some species even shrink their digestive organs before departure to save weight, and regrow them on arrival. Many birds migrate at night, when the air is cooler and calmer and there are fewer predators, and they can feed during the day.","How do they find their way? Scientists believe birds use several methods together. Many navigate using the sun, adjusting for its movement across the sky. Night-migrating birds use the stars; experiments in planetariums have shown that young birds learn to recognise the patterns of stars that rotate around the Pole Star. Birds can also sense the Earth’s magnetic field, possibly using special molecules in their eyes, so they may, in a sense, ‘see’ magnetic directions. They also use landmarks such as coastlines, rivers and mountain ranges, and some may even use smells and low-frequency sounds, such as waves crashing on distant shores.","Migration is dangerous. Birds face storms, exhaustion, predators and the challenge of crossing vast deserts and seas with no food or water. Human activity adds further dangers: loss of wetlands where they rest and refuel, collisions with buildings and power lines, and illegal hunting. Climate change is also disrupting the timing of migration, so that some birds arrive after the peak of the insects they need to feed their young.","Conservationists are working to protect the network of habitats that migrating birds depend on along their routes. Because a bird may spend its summer in one country, its winter in another and stop in a dozen more along the way, protecting migratory birds requires countries to work together. A swallow in your barn, after all, belongs to three continents."],"glossary":{"migration":"Regular journeys made by animals from one region to another.","seasonal":"Happening at a particular time of year.","scarce":"In short supply.","waders":"Long-legged birds that feed in shallow water.","slender":"Thin and graceful.","digestive":"To do with breaking down food in the body.","navigate":"To find the way.","planetariums":"Buildings with a domed ceiling where images of stars are shown.","conservationists":"People who work to protect nature."},"questions":[{"q":"Where does the swallow in the opening paragraph spend the winter?","o":["Scandinavia","South Africa","The Arctic","Alaska"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It flies “around 10,000 kilometres from South Africa.”"},{"q":"Why do many birds come to Britain in summer?","o":["It is the warmest place on Earth","Long days and plentiful food make it perfect for raising chicks","They are lost","There are no predators"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Long days and “an explosion of insects, seeds and fish, perfect for raising chicks.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer compare the Arctic tern’s lifetime travels to flying to the Moon and back three times?","o":["To help readers grasp the enormous distance","Because terns go to the Moon","To show terns are fast","To describe the tern’s colour"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"A familiar huge distance makes the number easier to imagine."},{"q":"How do birds prepare for migration?","o":["They sleep for weeks","They eat a lot and store fat, and some shrink their digestive organs","They grow longer wings","They drink seawater"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They store fat and some “shrink their digestive organs before departure.”"},{"q":"Which of these is NOT given as a way birds navigate?","o":["The sun","The stars","The Earth’s magnetic field","Following aeroplanes"],"a":3,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Sun, stars, magnetism, landmarks, smells and sounds are given – not aeroplanes."},{"q":"What does “A swallow in your barn, after all, belongs to three continents” mean?","o":["Protecting migrating birds requires many countries to cooperate","Swallows are owned by three countries","Swallows have three nests","Barns are found on three continents"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"It sums up why countries must work together to protect migrating birds."}]},{"id":"y186","title":"Should We Send Humans to Mars?","kind":"Discussion","theme":"space","text":["Mars has fascinated people for centuries. Through early telescopes, astronomers imagined canals built by Martians. Science-fiction writers filled it with invaders and lost civilisations. Today, robotic rovers trundle across its red, dusty surface, and space agencies and private companies talk seriously about sending humans there, perhaps within the next twenty years. But should we? It is a question that divides scientists and the public alike.","Those in favour argue, first, that Mars is the most important place to search for life beyond Earth. Billions of years ago, Mars had rivers, lakes and possibly oceans. If life ever began there, its traces may still be preserved in the rocks. Robots like NASA’s Perseverance rover are collecting samples, but supporters say that human geologists could do in a few days what robots take years to do, because humans can make quick decisions, notice the unexpected and adapt.","Second, supporters say that exploration drives innovation. The Apollo programme that put astronauts on the Moon led to advances in computers, materials, medicine and engineering that changed everyday life. A mission to Mars could inspire a new generation of scientists and engineers and push technology forward in areas such as clean energy, recycling and food production – all of which would also help us on Earth.","Some go further and argue that humanity needs a ‘backup’. If a catastrophe – an asteroid strike, a pandemic, a nuclear war – ever threatened civilisation on Earth, a self-sustaining human settlement on Mars could ensure that humans survive. The entrepreneur Elon Musk has made this one of the main reasons for his company SpaceX’s plans.","Opponents raise serious objections. The journey itself is extremely dangerous. A one-way trip would take six to nine months, and outside the protection of Earth’s magnetic field, astronauts would be exposed to high levels of cosmic radiation, increasing the risk of cancer. Long periods in low gravity weaken bones and muscles and can damage eyesight. On Mars, the air is thin and mostly carbon dioxide, the average temperature is about minus sixty degrees Celsius, and dust storms can cover the whole planet for weeks. Any emergency would be millions of kilometres from help.","Critics also point to the enormous cost. A crewed mission to Mars could cost hundreds of billions of pounds. Some argue that this money would be better spent solving problems on Earth, such as poverty, disease and climate change. And they question the ‘backup planet’ idea: even after a disaster, Earth would remain far more habitable than Mars. As one scientist put it, ‘There is no Planet B.’","There are ethical concerns too. If microbes exist on Mars, human visitors could contaminate the planet with Earth bacteria, destroying the very evidence we hoped to find – or, in theory, bring something harmful back.","Between these views lies a middle path: continuing to explore Mars with robots for now, while developing the technology, safety and international cooperation needed for humans to follow when the risks are better understood.","What do you think? Is going to Mars a giant leap for humanity, or an expensive distraction from the problems of our own planet?"],"glossary":{"civilisations":"Societies with their own culture and organisation.","preserved":"Kept in its original state.","innovation":"New ideas, methods or inventions.","catastrophe":"A sudden disaster causing great damage.","pandemic":"A disease that spreads across many countries.","self-sustaining":"Able to continue without outside help.","entrepreneur":"A person who sets up businesses.","radiation":"Energy given off in rays or particles, which can be harmful.","habitable":"Suitable for living in.","ethical":"To do with what is morally right or wrong."},"questions":[{"q":"Why is Mars thought to be a good place to look for life beyond Earth?","o":["It is the closest planet","It once had rivers, lakes and possibly oceans","It has trees","It is warm"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Billions of years ago, Mars had rivers, lakes and possibly oceans.”"},{"q":"Why might human geologists be better than robots on Mars?","o":["They can make quick decisions, notice the unexpected and adapt","They are cheaper","They need no food","They can fly"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Humans “can make quick decisions, notice the unexpected and adapt.”"},{"q":"What is the ‘backup’ argument for going to Mars?","o":["A settlement on Mars could help humanity survive a disaster on Earth","Mars has spare water","Mars could store computer data","Astronauts need a holiday"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"A settlement “could ensure that humans survive” a catastrophe on Earth."},{"q":"Which danger of a Mars journey does the writer mention?","o":["Cosmic radiation increasing cancer risk","Too much oxygen","Hot weather","Crowded spaceships"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Astronauts “would be exposed to high levels of cosmic radiation.”"},{"q":"What does ‘There is no Planet B’ mean in this context?","o":["Even after a disaster, Earth would be far more liveable than Mars, so we must protect Earth","There are only two planets","Mars is called Planet B","Scientists are confused"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"It challenges the ‘backup planet’ idea by saying Earth is irreplaceable."},{"q":"How is the text organised?","o":["Arguments for, arguments against, a middle path, then a question to the reader","A story about an astronaut","Only arguments for","A timeline of Mars missions"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"It presents both sides, a compromise and ends by asking the reader’s view."}]},{"id":"y187","title":"Book Review: The Clockwork Sea","kind":"Review","theme":"books","text":["The Clockwork Sea by Hana Moreland · Adventure / Fantasy · Ages 10–14 · ★★★★½","Some books grab you by the collar on the first page and refuse to let go. The Clockwork Sea is one of them. It opens with a twelve-year-old girl, Tilda Brack, hanging upside down from the rigging of a ship that sails not on water but on an ocean of gears, cogs and turning brass wheels, which grind and clatter beneath the hull as if the whole world were one enormous machine. By the end of the first chapter, she has lost her father, gained a mechanical parrot that only speaks in riddles and been accused of a crime she did not commit. I read the whole book in two days and was sorry when it ended.","The world-building is the novel’s greatest strength. Moreland has created something genuinely original: a world where the sea is a vast clockwork engine that must be wound every hundred years, or it will stop, and with it all the tides, seasons and winds. The guild of Keepers who once looked after it has vanished, and the great mechanism is beginning to slow. Moreland describes this world with such vivid detail – the smell of oil on the wind, the ticking that never stops, the brass whales that surface from the gears – that I could almost hear it.","Tilda is a wonderful heroine. She is brave and clever, but she is also stubborn, impatient and sometimes wrong, which makes her feel real. Her friendship with Ossian, a quiet boy who can mend anything mechanical but cannot bring himself to tell the truth about his own past, is beautifully written, and their arguments are often very funny. The parrot, Gizmo, steals every scene it appears in, and its riddles are cleverly woven into the plot, so readers can try to solve them before the characters do.","The plot moves at a cracking pace, with storms, chases, a mutiny and a breathtaking sequence in which Tilda must climb inside the turning gears of the sea itself. There are some genuinely surprising twists; I will not spoil them, but one about halfway through made me gasp out loud on the bus.","My only criticisms are small. The villain, Admiral Grell, is a little too obviously evil, and I would have liked to understand more about why he wants to stop the sea. A few chapters in the middle, set in the floating city of Coggleport, slow the story down with too much description. Some younger readers may also find the vocabulary challenging in places, although there is a helpful glossary at the back.","Underneath the adventure, the book has something important to say about responsibility: about the things that keep our world running that we take for granted until they start to fail, and about the courage it takes to look after them. Readers who enjoy Philip Pullman or Katherine Rundell will find a lot to love here.","Verdict: An inventive, exciting and moving adventure with a heroine you will root for. I am already counting down the days until the sequel."],"glossary":{"rigging":"The ropes that support a ship’s masts and sails.","mechanical":"Worked by machinery or moving parts.","genuinely":"Really; truly.","guild":"An organisation of people with the same skill or trade.","mechanism":"A set of moving parts in a machine.","heroine":"The main female character in a story, often admired.","mutiny":"When sailors rebel against their officers.","sequence":"A series of events in a particular order.","verdict":"A final judgement or opinion.","sequel":"A book or film that continues the story of an earlier one."},"questions":[{"q":"What rating does the reviewer give the book?","o":["Three stars","Four and a half stars","Five stars","Two stars"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“★★★★½”."},{"q":"What does “grab you by the collar on the first page” mean?","o":["The book is exciting and immediately gripping","The book is badly made","The book is about clothes","The book is frightening"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"The metaphor shows how quickly the story captures the reader."},{"q":"According to the reviewer, what is the novel’s greatest strength?","o":["Its world-building","Its villain","Its length","Its pictures"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The world-building is the novel’s greatest strength.”"},{"q":"Why does the reviewer think Tilda feels real?","o":["She is perfect","She is brave and clever but also stubborn, impatient and sometimes wrong","She is based on a real person","She never makes mistakes"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Her flaws make her believable."},{"q":"Which of these is a criticism the reviewer makes?","o":["The villain is too obviously evil","The heroine is boring","The plot is too slow throughout","There is no ending"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The villain, Admiral Grell, is a little too obviously evil.”"},{"q":"Why does the reviewer mention Philip Pullman and Katherine Rundell?","o":["To suggest readers who like those authors may enjoy this book","To criticise them","To say they wrote this book","To list the reviewer’s friends"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Comparisons to known authors help readers decide if the book suits them."}]},{"id":"y188","title":"The Windrush Generation","kind":"History","theme":"ship","text":["On 21 June 1948, a ship called the Empire Windrush docked at Tilbury, on the River Thames in Essex. On board were around a thousand passengers, including several hundred people from Jamaica, Trinidad and other Caribbean islands. Newspaper photographers recorded the moment they came down the gangplank: men in smart suits and hats, women in their best dresses, carrying suitcases that held everything they owned. One of them, the calypso singer Lord Kitchener, sang for the cameras a song he had composed on the voyage: ‘London Is the Place for Me’.","These passengers, and the people from the Caribbean who followed them over the next quarter of a century, have become known as the Windrush generation. Their arrival is often seen as the beginning of modern multicultural Britain.","Why did they come? After the Second World War, Britain was badly damaged and short of workers. Cities needed to be rebuilt, and new public services, such as the National Health Service founded in July 1948, needed staff. The Caribbean islands were part of the British Empire, and their people were British citizens with the right to live and work in Britain. Many had served in the British armed forces during the war; some of the Windrush passengers were ex-servicemen returning to the country they had fought for. Others came because jobs were scarce at home, and adverts in the Caribbean encouraged people to come to Britain, which many had been taught at school to think of as the ‘mother country’.","The new arrivals worked in hospitals, on buses and trains, in factories and on building sites. They helped to rebuild Britain and to keep its public services running. By the early 1970s, around half a million people from the Caribbean had settled in Britain.","The welcome they received was often cold. Many faced racism in the streets, at work and when looking for somewhere to live. Signs in some lodging-house windows read ‘No Irish, no Blacks, no dogs’. Some were refused service in pubs or shops, and many had to take jobs below their qualifications. In 1958, there was violence against black residents in Notting Hill in London and in Nottingham.","Caribbean communities responded with resilience and creativity. They set up churches, social clubs and ‘partner’ savings schemes, in which friends pooled money so that each could take turns to receive a lump sum for a house deposit. In 1959, Claudia Jones, a Trinidad-born activist and journalist, organised a Caribbean carnival in London to celebrate Caribbean culture and bring people together after the riots. It grew into the Notting Hill Carnival, now one of the largest street festivals in Europe. Caribbean music, food, writing and art became part of British life.","Decades later, the story took a shameful turn. In 2018, it emerged that some members of the Windrush generation, who had lived in Britain legally for fifty years or more, had been wrongly classified as illegal immigrants because they lacked official paperwork. Some lost their jobs, homes or access to healthcare, and some were detained or deported. The scandal led to a government apology and a compensation scheme.","Since 2018, 22 June has been celebrated as Windrush Day, honouring the contribution of the Windrush generation and their descendants to the history and life of Britain."],"glossary":{"gangplank":"A movable board for walking on and off a ship.","calypso":"A style of Caribbean music with lively rhythms and clever lyrics.","multicultural":"Including people of many different cultures.","citizens":"People who legally belong to a country and have its rights.","scarce":"In short supply.","resilience":"The ability to recover from difficulties.","activist":"A person who campaigns for change.","classified":"Put into a category.","deported":"Forced to leave a country.","descendants":"Children, grandchildren and later generations."},"questions":[{"q":"When did the Empire Windrush arrive at Tilbury?","o":["21 June 1948","1 July 1958","22 June 2018","9 May 1945"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“On 21 June 1948.”"},{"q":"Why did Britain need workers after the Second World War?","o":["Cities needed rebuilding and new services like the NHS needed staff","There were too many jobs in factories only","Everyone had retired","The King asked for them"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Cities needed rebuilding and the NHS “needed staff.”"},{"q":"Why did many Caribbean people think of Britain as the ‘mother country’?","o":["They had been taught this at school, as the islands were part of the British Empire","Their mothers lived there","They were born in Britain","It was a popular song"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"The islands were part of the Empire and people “had been taught at school” to see Britain this way."},{"q":"What does the sign ‘No Irish, no Blacks, no dogs’ reveal?","o":["Open racism and discrimination faced by new arrivals","A pet-friendly policy","A welcoming attitude","A rule for pubs only"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"It shows the racism they faced when looking for somewhere to live."},{"q":"How did the Notting Hill Carnival begin?","o":["As a Caribbean carnival organised by Claudia Jones in 1959 to celebrate culture and bring people together","As a government event","As a sports day","As a protest about buses"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Claudia Jones organised a carnival in 1959 after the riots."},{"q":"What was the Windrush scandal of 2018?","o":["People who had lived legally in Britain for decades were wrongly treated as illegal immigrants","The ship was found","A new carnival was banned","A film about Windrush was released"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"They were “wrongly classified as illegal immigrants because they lacked official paperwork.”"}]},{"id":"y189","title":"Lost in the Maze","kind":"Story","theme":"garden","text":["The maze at Hollingbrook Hall was three hundred years old, made of yew hedges taller than a grown man and so thick you could not see through them. A sign at the entrance said that the average visitor took twenty minutes to reach the centre. Eli and his younger cousin Bea had been inside for fifty-five.","‘We’ve been past this bench before,’ said Bea, pointing. ‘Three times.’","‘No, we haven’t,’ said Eli. ‘That was a different bench.’","It was the same bench. Somebody had carved a small heart into the arm of it, and Eli had seen it three times. He was twelve and Bea was eight, and he was supposed to be looking after her while their parents had coffee in the café, and he had said, very confidently, ‘I’ll get us to the middle in ten minutes.’ That was before the maze had swallowed them.","At first it had been fun. They had raced along the paths, laughing, taking turns at random. But the hedges all looked the same, the paths kept bending, and every turn seemed to lead either to a dead end or to somewhere they had already been. The sky above was grey and heavy, and now, as the first drops of rain began to fall, the maze felt less like a game and more like a trap. Somewhere on the other side of a hedge, a group of children were shrieking with laughter, but Eli could not work out how to get to them.","Bea’s lip started to wobble. ‘I want to go back.’","‘We can’t go back,’ said Eli, louder than he meant to. ‘I don’t know where back is.’","Bea burst into tears.","Eli felt terrible. He knelt down on the wet gravel. He wanted to panic himself, but he could see that if he did, Bea would be even more frightened. So he took a deep breath and tried to think. Then he remembered something he had read once, in a puzzle book, years ago.","‘Bea,’ he said. ‘I know a trick. A secret maze trick. Do you want to know it?’","She sniffed and nodded.","‘You put one hand on the hedge,’ he said, putting his right hand on the prickly yew, ‘and you never, ever take it off. You just keep walking and follow the hedge wherever it goes, even into the dead ends. And eventually, you have to come out. It always works for this kind of maze.’","It was slow. The wall-follower method took them into every dead end in the maze, and some of them twice. Bea started counting the dead ends out loud – ‘Number eleven!’ – and by number fifteen she was giggling. They found a lost glove, a feather and a beetle as shiny as a jewel. Eli kept his hand on the hedge the whole time, even when it scratched him.","And then, quite suddenly, the hedge turned a corner and they were in the middle: a small square of grass with a stone sundial and a wooden bench, and their parents, who had come looking for them, sitting on it under an umbrella.","‘There you are!’ said Eli’s mum. ‘We were starting to worry.’","‘We weren’t lost,’ said Bea importantly. ‘Eli knows a secret trick.’","Eli did not tell her that the trick only worked because the maze’s walls were all connected, or that it was not really a secret, or that for a while he had been just as scared as she was. He just sat on the bench and let her tell the story, which, by the time they reached the café, had grown to include a dragon."],"glossary":{"yew":"An evergreen tree with dark needles, often used for hedges.","confidently":"In a way that shows you feel sure of yourself.","shrieking":"Making a high-pitched cry.","panic":"Sudden, overwhelming fear.","prickly":"Covered in sharp points.","sundial":"An instrument that shows the time using the shadow cast by the sun."},"questions":[{"q":"How long had Eli and Bea been in the maze when the story begins?","o":["Twenty minutes","Fifty-five minutes","Ten minutes","Three hours"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Eli and his younger cousin Bea had been inside for fifty-five.”"},{"q":"What does the repeated bench with the carved heart show?","o":["They were going round in circles","There were many benches","Someone was following them","They had found the centre"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Seeing the same bench three times shows they were lost and repeating paths."},{"q":"How does the writer show the mood changing from fun to fear?","o":["The sky grows grey, rain begins and the maze feels “more like a trap”","They find the centre immediately","The sun comes out","They start a race"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Weather and the image of a trap create a sense of growing danger."},{"q":"Why did Eli not panic when Bea cried?","o":["He realised panicking would frighten her more","He was not worried at all","He was angry","He knew where the centre was"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“He could see that if he did, Bea would be even more frightened.”"},{"q":"How does the wall-follower method work?","o":["Keep one hand on the hedge and follow it, even into dead ends, until you come out","Always turn left","Climb over the hedges","Follow the noise"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“You put one hand on the hedge … and you never, ever take it off.”"},{"q":"What does Eli’s decision to let Bea tell the story show?","o":["He is kind and lets her enjoy feeling brave, even keeping his own fear private","He wants to forget the maze","He is too tired to talk","He is jealous of her"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"He does not correct her and quietly lets her enjoy the adventure."}]},{"id":"y190","title":"Light and Rainbows","kind":"Science","theme":"rain","text":["Light is the fastest thing in the universe. It travels at almost 300,000 kilometres per second – fast enough to go around the Earth seven and a half times in a single second. Light from the Sun takes about eight minutes to reach us, so when you look at the Sun (which you should never do directly), you are seeing it as it was eight minutes ago.","Light travels in straight lines, which is why shadows form: when an opaque object, which light cannot pass through, blocks the light, it leaves a dark area behind it. Transparent materials, such as clear glass and water, let light pass straight through, while translucent materials, such as frosted glass or tissue paper, let some light through but scatter it, so you cannot see clearly through them.","We see objects because light bounces off them and enters our eyes. This is called reflection. Smooth, shiny surfaces like mirrors reflect light in a very regular way, so we see a clear image. Rough surfaces scatter light in all directions. A mirror reflects light at the same angle it arrives: the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection. This is why you can see round corners with a periscope.","When light passes from one transparent material into another – from air into water, for example – it changes speed, and this makes it bend. This bending is called refraction. It is the reason a straw in a glass of water looks broken, and why a swimming pool looks shallower than it really is. Lenses in glasses, cameras and microscopes use refraction to focus light.","What looks like white light is actually a mixture of many colours. In 1666, Isaac Newton showed this by shining a narrow beam of sunlight through a glass prism. The different colours in white light bend by slightly different amounts when they are refracted: violet light bends the most and red light the least. So the prism spread the light out into a band of colours, called a spectrum: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo and violet. You can remember the order with the phrase ‘Richard Of York Gave Battle In Vain’. Newton then used a second prism to combine the colours back into white light, proving that the colours were part of the light, not created by the glass.","A rainbow is a natural spectrum. It appears when the sun is shining behind you and rain is falling in front of you. Each raindrop acts like a tiny prism. Sunlight enters the drop and is refracted, splitting into colours; it reflects off the back of the drop; and it is refracted again as it leaves, spreading the colours further. Each colour reaches your eyes from drops at a slightly different angle, which is why the colours appear in bands, with red on the outside and violet on the inside. Because the light leaves the drops at an angle of about forty-two degrees, a rainbow is always a circle centred on the point directly opposite the sun. From the ground, the horizon usually hides the bottom half, but from an aeroplane you can sometimes see a complete circular rainbow.","Sometimes a fainter second rainbow appears outside the first. This double rainbow is caused by light reflecting twice inside each raindrop, and its colours are in reverse order, with red on the inside. The sky between the two bows looks darker, a band called Alexander’s band after the Greek philosopher who first described it.","And because every person sees light from a different set of raindrops, no two people ever see exactly the same rainbow. Each one is yours alone."],"glossary":{"opaque":"Not letting light pass through.","transparent":"Letting light pass through so you can see clearly.","translucent":"Letting some light through but not clearly.","incidence":"The angle of incidence is the angle at which light hits a surface.","periscope":"A tube with mirrors used to see over or around things.","refraction":"The bending of light as it passes from one material into another.","prism":"A clear glass shape that splits light into colours.","spectrum":"The band of colours produced when white light is split.","horizon":"The line where the land or sea seems to meet the sky."},"questions":[{"q":"How long does sunlight take to reach the Earth?","o":["About eight seconds","About eight minutes","About eight hours","Instantly"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Light from the Sun takes about eight minutes to reach us.”"},{"q":"Why do shadows form?","o":["Light travels in straight lines and is blocked by opaque objects","Light bends around objects","Objects give off darkness","The sun moves"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Light travels in straight lines … when an opaque object … blocks the light.”"},{"q":"Why does a straw in water look broken?","o":["The water is dirty","Light bends (refracts) as it passes from water into air","The straw really bends","Reflection from the glass"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Refraction “is the reason a straw in a glass of water looks broken.”"},{"q":"How did Newton prove that the colours were part of white light?","o":["He painted them","He used a second prism to combine the colours back into white light","He looked at the sun","He used a mirror"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Newton then used a second prism to combine the colours back into white light.”"},{"q":"Where must the sun be for you to see a rainbow?","o":["In front of you","Behind you, with rain in front","Directly overhead","Below the horizon"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"A rainbow appears “when the sun is shining behind you and rain is falling in front of you.”"},{"q":"What does the writer mean by “Each one is yours alone”?","o":["Every person sees light from different raindrops, so no two people see exactly the same rainbow","Rainbows can be bought","Only one person can see a rainbow","Rainbows follow you home"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“No two people ever see exactly the same rainbow.”"}]},{"id":"y191","title":"Year Seven","kind":"Poem","theme":"city","text":["In September I was small.\nThe corridors were rivers\nthat swept me the wrong way,\nand every door was a question\nI didn’t know the answer to.","I carried my whole life in a rucksack:\nnew pencil case, new planner,\nnew shoes rubbing new blisters,\na map I couldn’t read,\na name nobody knew.","In October I learned the shortcut\nbehind the science block.\nIn November, someone saved me a seat.\nIn December, I laughed so hard in French\nthat I had to pretend I was coughing.","In the spring, the days grew longer,\nand so, I think, did I.\nI stopped checking my timetable.\nI knew which stairs were quickest,\nwhich teachers liked a joke,\nwhich dinner lady gave extra chips.","Now it is July,\nand the Year Sixes are visiting,\nsmall in their stiff new blazers,\nholding their maps upside down,\nlooking at the corridors\nthe way I used to.","One of them stops me and asks\nwhere Lab 4 is.\nAnd I tell her – upstairs,\nit’s always upstairs –\nand watch her go,\nand remember."],"glossary":{"planner":"A small book for writing homework and plans.","blisters":"Small bubbles on the skin caused by rubbing.","shortcut":"A quicker way to get somewhere.","blazers":"Jackets worn as part of a school uniform."},"questions":[{"q":"How did the speaker feel in September?","o":["Confident and calm","Small, lost and unsure","Bored","Angry"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"“In September I was small” and every door was a question."},{"q":"What does the metaphor “The corridors were rivers / that swept me the wrong way” suggest?","o":["The crowds of pupils carried the speaker along, out of control","The school was flooded","The speaker liked swimming","The corridors were pretty"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Like a strong current, the busy corridors pushed the speaker where they did not want to go."},{"q":"How does the third stanza show the speaker settling in?","o":["Each month brings a small sign of belonging – a shortcut, a saved seat, shared laughter","It lists homework","It describes a holiday","It shows the speaker getting more lost"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"October, November and December each bring a step towards feeling at home."},{"q":"What does “the days grew longer, / and so, I think, did I” suggest?","o":["The speaker grew in confidence and maturity, not just height","The speaker got taller only","The days were boring","The speaker slept more"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Growth here means growing as a person over the year."},{"q":"Why does the speaker notice the Year Sixes in July?","o":["They remind the speaker of how they felt at the start of the year","They are noisy","They are lost on purpose","They are the speaker’s friends"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"They look at the corridors “the way I used to.”"},{"q":"What is the effect of the final word, “remember”?","o":["It shows the speaker recognising how far they have come and feeling for the newcomer","It means the speaker forgot something","It tells the reader to remember homework","It ends the poem with a joke"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Helping the new pupil, the speaker recalls their own first days."}]},{"id":"y192","title":"Rachel Carson: The Woman Who Spoke for Nature","kind":"Biography","theme":"nature","text":["In 1962, a quiet American scientist published a book that began with a fable. It described a town in the heart of America where, one spring, something strange happened. The birds stopped singing. Apple trees bloomed, but no bees buzzed among the blossom, so no fruit formed. Chickens laid eggs that would not hatch. Fish died in the streams. ‘No witchcraft, no enemy action had silenced the rebirth of new life in this stricken world,’ she wrote. ‘The people had done it themselves.’ The book was called Silent Spring, and it changed the way the world thought about nature.","Its author, Rachel Carson, was born in 1907 on a farm in Pennsylvania, where she spent her childhood exploring woods and streams and writing stories about animals. She studied biology and became one of the few women of her time to work as a scientist for the US government, at the Bureau of Fisheries. She was also a gifted writer, and in the 1950s her book The Sea Around Us, which described the oceans in beautiful, poetic language, became a bestseller.","In the years after the Second World War, a powerful new chemical called DDT was being sprayed across America to kill insects. It had been used during the war to kill mosquitoes and lice that spread diseases such as malaria and typhus, and it had saved many lives. Now it was sprayed on farms, forests, gardens and even towns, often from aeroplanes, to kill crop pests and mosquitoes. People were told it was completely safe.","Carson began to receive letters from people who were worried. One friend wrote that after her land had been sprayed, songbirds had died in her garden. Carson spent four years researching the effects of pesticides. She discovered that DDT did not simply kill the insects it was aimed at. It killed many other insects too, including helpful ones. It washed into rivers and killed fish. Most worryingly, it did not break down quickly. It built up in the bodies of animals, becoming more concentrated at each step of the food chain. Small creatures absorbed it, fish ate the small creatures, and birds of prey such as eagles and peregrine falcons ate the fish. DDT made the shells of their eggs so thin that they broke when the parents tried to sit on them, and populations of these birds crashed.","Silent Spring caused an uproar. Chemical companies attacked Carson fiercely. They called her hysterical and unscientific, and threatened to sue her publisher. Some critics mocked her for being a woman and unmarried. But her research was careful and her evidence was strong. What her attackers did not know was that, while she was writing the book and defending it, Carson was secretly fighting cancer. She testified before the US Congress in 1963, calmly arguing that people had a right to be protected from poisons in their environment.","Rachel Carson died in 1964, aged just fifty-six. She did not live to see the results of her work. But Silent Spring inspired the modern environmental movement. In 1970, the United States created the Environmental Protection Agency, and in 1972 it banned the use of DDT on crops; many other countries did the same. The bald eagle, America’s national bird, which had been close to extinction, slowly recovered.","Carson taught the world that humans are part of nature, not separate from it, and that what we do to the natural world, we ultimately do to ourselves."],"glossary":{"fable":"A short story that teaches a lesson.","stricken":"Badly affected by trouble or illness.","bureau":"A government department or office.","pesticides":"Chemicals used to kill insects and other pests.","concentrated":"Made stronger by being gathered closely together.","uproar":"A noisy, angry public reaction.","hysterical":"Uncontrollably emotional (often used unfairly as an insult).","testified":"Gave evidence formally, for example to a government committee.","extinction":"When a species dies out completely."},"questions":[{"q":"What did the fable at the start of Silent Spring describe?","o":["A town where nature had fallen silent because of human actions","A war","A happy farm","A trip to the sea"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Birds stopped singing and life was silenced: “The people had done it themselves.”"},{"q":"Why was DDT originally seen as useful?","o":["It killed mosquitoes and lice that spread diseases such as malaria and typhus","It made crops sweeter","It cleaned rivers","It fed birds"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It “had been used during the war to kill mosquitoes and lice that spread diseases.”"},{"q":"Why did DDT harm birds of prey such as eagles?","o":["It built up through the food chain and made their eggshells too thin","They ate it directly from sprays","It made them fly too high","It made them lose their feathers"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"DDT became more concentrated up the food chain and thinned eggshells."},{"q":"How did chemical companies respond to Silent Spring?","o":["They thanked Carson","They attacked her fiercely, calling her hysterical and threatening to sue","They stopped making DDT immediately","They ignored it"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"They “attacked Carson fiercely” and threatened her publisher."},{"q":"What does Carson’s secret illness add to our understanding of her?","o":["She showed great courage, continuing to defend her work while seriously ill","She did not care about the book","She stopped working","She was afraid of Congress"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"She testified calmly while secretly fighting cancer."},{"q":"What lasting effect did Silent Spring have?","o":["It inspired the modern environmental movement and led to DDT being banned","It made DDT more popular","It was quickly forgotten","It only affected one town"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"It inspired the movement, led to the EPA and a ban on DDT on crops."}]},{"id":"y193","title":"The Treasure of Sutton Hoo","kind":"History","theme":"ship","text":["In the summer of 1939, as Britain prepared for war, a self-taught archaeologist named Basil Brown was digging into a large grassy mound on a country estate at Sutton Hoo, near Woodbridge in Suffolk. The landowner, Edith Pretty, had long wondered what lay inside the mounds on her land. Local legends spoke of buried treasure. What Brown found was one of the most important archaeological discoveries ever made in Britain.","As he carefully dug, Brown began to notice rows of rusted iron rivets in the sandy soil. They were arranged in neat curving lines. The wood they had once held together had long since rotted away, but the sand had kept its shape, like a ghostly imprint. Brown realised that he was uncovering a ship – a huge wooden ship, about twenty-seven metres long, which had been dragged up from the River Deben and buried in a trench beneath the mound. It had been used as a grave for someone very important.","In the middle of the ship was a burial chamber, and it was crammed with treasures. There was a magnificent gold belt buckle, decorated with interlacing snakes and animals, weighing more than 400 grams. There were shoulder clasps of gold set with hundreds of tiny pieces of red garnet, cut so precisely that they still sparkle. There was a purse lid decorated with gold and garnets, containing thirty-seven gold coins from France. There were silver bowls and a great silver dish from the Byzantine Empire, thousands of kilometres away; drinking horns; a lyre; spears, a sword and a shield; and an iron helmet decorated with bronze panels showing warriors, with a face mask whose eyebrows, nose and moustache together form the shape of a flying dragon.","Strangely, no body was found. For a long time, some people wondered whether the ship was a memorial rather than a grave. But scientific tests later showed that the acidic sandy soil had simply dissolved the body completely. Most historians believe the person buried there was Rædwald, a powerful king of East Anglia who died around AD 624. The treasures show that the Anglo-Saxons, who had once been dismissed as rough barbarians living in the ‘Dark Ages’, had skilled craftsmen, great wealth and trading links stretching across Europe and beyond.","The discovery came at a tense time. Within weeks, the Second World War had begun. The treasures were packed up and stored safely in a disused London Underground tunnel for the rest of the war. The site itself was used for military training, and tanks were driven over the mounds. After the war, Edith Pretty generously gave all the treasure to the British Museum, where it can be seen today. It was one of the largest gifts ever made to the museum by a living person.","For many years, Basil Brown received little credit for his discovery. Professional archaeologists from Cambridge and the British Museum had taken over the excavation once they realised how important it was, and his name was often left out of the official reports. Today his role is fully recognised, and his story, along with Edith Pretty’s, was told in a film called The Dig in 2021.","The helmet, painstakingly rebuilt from more than 500 rusted fragments, has become one of the most famous objects in Britain – a fierce face looking out at us across fourteen centuries."],"glossary":{"archaeologist":"A person who studies the past by digging up remains.","rivets":"Short metal pins used to hold pieces of material together.","imprint":"A mark or shape left by pressing.","interlacing":"Crossing over and under one another.","garnet":"A deep red precious stone.","byzantine":"From the Byzantine Empire, the eastern part of the Roman Empire.","lyre":"An ancient stringed musical instrument.","acidic":"Containing acid, which can dissolve or wear things away.","barbarians":"People thought of as uncivilised.","painstakingly":"With great care and effort."},"questions":[{"q":"How did Basil Brown realise he had found a ship?","o":["He found the wooden planks intact","Rows of iron rivets in the sand showed the ship’s shape","Edith Pretty told him","He found the sail"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Rusted rivets “arranged in neat curving lines” showed the ghostly shape."},{"q":"Why was no body found in the grave?","o":["It had been stolen","The acidic sandy soil had dissolved it completely","It was a fake grave","The body was buried elsewhere"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Tests showed “the acidic sandy soil had simply dissolved the body.”"},{"q":"What do the Byzantine silver bowls tell us about the Anglo-Saxons?","o":["They had trading links stretching far across Europe and beyond","They made all their own silver","They had never travelled","They lived in the Byzantine Empire"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Goods from thousands of kilometres away show far-reaching trade."},{"q":"How did the discovery change ideas about the ‘Dark Ages’?","o":["It showed the Anglo-Saxons had skilled craftsmen, great wealth and wide trade","It proved they were barbarians","It showed they had no kings","It showed they could not build ships"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"The treasures show they were not rough barbarians but skilled and wealthy."},{"q":"Why did Basil Brown receive little credit for many years?","o":["He asked not to be named","Professional archaeologists took over and his name was often left out of reports","He made mistakes","He sold the treasure"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"His “name was often left out of the official reports.”"},{"q":"What does the final sentence suggest about the helmet?","o":["It is a powerful link between people today and the distant past","It is broken and worthless","It was made recently","It belongs to Basil Brown"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"Its face looks out “across fourteen centuries”, connecting us to the past."}]},{"id":"y194","title":"Creatures of the Deep","kind":"Science","theme":"sea","text":["More people have walked on the Moon than have travelled to the deepest point of the ocean. The deep sea is the largest habitat on Earth, but it is also the least explored. We have better maps of the surface of Mars than of our own ocean floor. Down there, in a world of crushing pressure, freezing cold and total darkness, live some of the strangest creatures on the planet.","As you descend through the ocean, sunlight fades quickly. Below about 200 metres, there is not enough light for plants or algae to photosynthesise. This is the twilight zone. Below about 1,000 metres, no sunlight reaches at all; the only light is made by living things. This is the midnight zone. Deeper still are the abyssal plains, vast flat areas of mud between 3,000 and 6,000 metres down, and deepest of all are the ocean trenches. The deepest point, Challenger Deep in the Mariana Trench in the western Pacific, is nearly eleven kilometres down. If Mount Everest were placed inside it, its summit would still be more than two kilometres under water.","Life in the deep must cope with extreme conditions. The pressure at the bottom of the Mariana Trench is more than a thousand times that at the surface – like having the weight of fifty jumbo jets pressing down on you. The water is just above freezing. Food is scarce, because almost everything depends on ‘marine snow’: a constant, slow drift of dead plankton, droppings and other remains sinking down from the waters above.","Many deep-sea creatures make their own light, a process called bioluminescence. They use it to attract prey, find mates, confuse predators or communicate. The anglerfish dangles a glowing lure from a long spine on its head, like a fishing rod, to attract smaller fish towards its enormous, tooth-filled mouth. Some squid squirt out clouds of glowing liquid instead of ink to dazzle attackers. In the midnight zone, most of the light is blue, because blue light travels furthest in water.","Because food is so hard to find, many deep-sea animals have huge mouths and stretchy stomachs, so they can eat anything they come across, even prey bigger than themselves. The gulper eel has a mouth so large that it can swallow a fish almost its own size. Others save energy by moving very slowly. Some deep-sea creatures grow to enormous sizes, a phenomenon called deep-sea gigantism; the giant squid can reach thirteen metres long and has the largest eyes in the animal kingdom, each the size of a dinner plate.","Some of the most remarkable ecosystems on Earth surround hydrothermal vents – cracks in the sea floor where volcanic activity heats water to over 350 degrees Celsius and pumps out minerals. Here, bacteria use chemicals from the vents, not sunlight, to make food, in a process called chemosynthesis. Around them live giant tube worms two metres long, white crabs, blind shrimp and clams. When these vents were discovered in 1977, they changed our understanding of where life could exist, and some scientists think life on Earth may have begun in places like these.","Scientists believe there may be hundreds of thousands, or even millions, of deep-sea species still undiscovered. On almost every expedition, they find something new. But the deep sea is already under threat from fishing with heavy nets, pollution, plastic, which has been found even in the Mariana Trench, and proposals to mine the sea floor for metals. It may be the last great wilderness on Earth – and we are only just beginning to explore it."],"glossary":{"habitat":"The natural home of an animal or plant.","descend":"To go down.","photosynthesise":"To make food using sunlight, as plants do.","abyssal":"To do with the very deep parts of the ocean.","bioluminescence":"Light produced by living things.","lure":"Something used to attract an animal, especially to catch it.","phenomenon":"Something that happens or exists, especially something unusual.","hydrothermal":"To do with hot water heated inside the Earth.","chemosynthesis":"Making food using energy from chemicals rather than sunlight.","wilderness":"A wild area left in its natural state."},"questions":[{"q":"What is surprising about how much we know about the deep sea?","o":["We have better maps of Mars’s surface than of our ocean floor","We know everything about it","It is very shallow","It has been fully explored"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“We have better maps of the surface of Mars than of our own ocean floor.”"},{"q":"What would happen if Mount Everest were placed in the Challenger Deep?","o":["It would stick out of the water","Its summit would still be more than two kilometres under water","It would float","It would fill the trench exactly"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Its summit would still be more than two kilometres under water.”"},{"q":"What is ‘marine snow’?","o":["Frozen seawater","A slow drift of dead plankton, droppings and remains sinking from above","Snow falling on the sea","White fish"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It is “a constant, slow drift of dead plankton, droppings and other remains.”"},{"q":"How does the anglerfish use bioluminescence?","o":["To see in the dark","To attract prey with a glowing lure","To keep warm","To find its way home"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It “dangles a glowing lure … to attract smaller fish.”"},{"q":"Why did the discovery of hydrothermal vents change scientists’ understanding?","o":["They showed life could exist using chemicals instead of sunlight","They proved fish can fly","They showed the sea is hot everywhere","They proved there is no life in the deep"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Bacteria there use chemicals, not sunlight, so life can exist in unexpected places."},{"q":"What is the writer’s main concern in the final paragraph?","o":["The deep sea is boring","The deep sea is already threatened by fishing, pollution and mining before it is fully explored","Too many scientists visit it","It is too cold for exploration"],"a":1,"skill":"Summary","why":"It is threatened by nets, pollution, plastic and mining proposals."}]},{"id":"y195","title":"The School Play","kind":"Story","theme":"stage","text":["When the cast list for the school play went up on the drama noticeboard, Amira read it three times to make sure. She was not Juliet. She was not the Nurse, or Lady Capulet, or even a servant with one line. Next to her name, at the very bottom, it said: AMIRA HASSAN – PROMPT.","‘What’s a prompt?’ asked her friend Grace.","‘The person who sits at the side of the stage,’ said Amira, ‘and whispers the lines when people forget them.’ She tried to sound as if she did not mind. She minded very much. She had spent the whole audition practising Juliet’s balcony speech in front of her bedroom mirror. She knew it by heart.","For six weeks, she sat on a plastic chair in the wings with the script on her knees, following every word with her finger. She knew the whole play by the end of the first fortnight. She knew where Romeo always forgot his lines in Act Two, and where Mercutio rushed his jokes. She noticed that Juliet, played by a Year 9 girl called Esme, went quiet and shaky whenever she had to say the word ‘banishèd’. Without being asked, Amira began writing little notes in the margin of her script: ‘Tom – breathe before the fight scene.’ ‘Esme – look at the audience, not the floor.’ Once, when Mr Davies the drama teacher was busy, she quietly suggested to Romeo that he might stand further downstage so that people at the back could hear him. He tried it. It worked.","On opening night, the hall was full. Amira sat in the wings in the dark, her script lit by a tiny torch, her heart thumping as though she were going on stage herself.","The first act went perfectly. Then, in the balcony scene, Esme froze.","She stood at the top of the wooden balcony in her long white dress, in the spotlight, and said, ‘O Romeo, Romeo…’ and stopped. Nothing came. Amira could see her face, white with terror, her hands gripping the rail. In the audience, someone coughed. The silence stretched.","Amira did not look at her script. She did not need to. She leaned forward and whispered, clear and calm, ‘Wherefore art thou Romeo?’","Esme did not move.","So Amira did something she had never done in rehearsal. Softly, from the darkness of the wings, so that only Esme could hear, she began to say the whole speech – not loudly, not as a prompt, but slowly and gently, just a few words ahead, the way you might walk beside someone across a narrow bridge. ‘Deny thy father and refuse thy name…’","Esme turned her head very slightly towards the sound. Then she took a breath and joined in. For a few lines, Amira’s whisper and Esme’s voice went together. Then Esme’s voice grew stronger, and Amira let her own fade away, until Esme was speaking alone, and speaking beautifully.","At the end of the show, the cast took their bows to a standing ovation. Amira stayed in the wings, as prompts do. But after the final bow, Esme walked off the stage, straight past the others, and pulled her out by the hand into the light.","‘This,’ she told the audience, ‘is the reason there was a balcony scene at all.’","The applause got louder. Amira, standing there in her black prompt clothes, blinking in the spotlight, thought that perhaps, after all, she had not minded so very much."],"glossary":{"cast":"All the actors in a play.","audition":"A short performance to try to get a part.","wings":"The sides of a stage, hidden from the audience.","margin":"The blank space around the edge of a page.","downstage":"Towards the front of the stage, nearest the audience.","rehearsal":"A practice before a performance.","ovation":"A long burst of applause; a standing ovation is when the audience stands to clap."},"questions":[{"q":"What role was Amira given?","o":["Juliet","The Nurse","Prompt","Romeo"],"a":2,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“AMIRA HASSAN – PROMPT.”"},{"q":"How did Amira really feel about being the prompt at first?","o":["Delighted","Disappointed, though she tried to hide it","Relieved","Angry with Grace"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"“She tried to sound as if she did not mind. She minded very much.”"},{"q":"What do Amira’s notes in the margin show about her?","o":["She paid close attention and quietly helped others improve","She was bored","She wanted Esme to fail","She was copying the script"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"She noticed each actor’s difficulties and wrote helpful advice."},{"q":"Why does Amira say the whole speech softly instead of just one prompt?","o":["Esme was frozen with fear and needed gentle support to start again","She wanted to steal the scene","She had forgotten her script","Mr Davies told her to"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"A single prompt did not work, so she guided Esme through the lines."},{"q":"What does the simile “the way you might walk beside someone across a narrow bridge” suggest?","o":["Amira was gently supporting Esme through a frightening moment","They were outside","Amira was leading Esme off stage","The stage was very narrow"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"Walking beside someone on a narrow bridge suggests calm, close support."},{"q":"What does the ending suggest about Amira’s feelings?","o":["She realises her role was valuable and her disappointment has faded","She is still angry","She wants to quit drama","She wishes Esme had failed"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"She thinks “perhaps, after all, she had not minded so very much.”"}]},{"id":"y196","title":"How to Survive a Night in the Wild","kind":"Instructions","theme":"forest","text":["Nobody plans to get lost. But every year, walkers in Britain’s hills and forests take a wrong turn, are caught out by darkness, or are injured and unable to walk out. Most are found quickly. Knowing what to do while you wait can make the difference between an uncomfortable night and a dangerous one. The following guidance is based on advice from mountain rescue teams and survival experts.","1. Stop, think and stay put.\nThe moment you realise you are lost, stop walking. Panic makes people hurry, and hurrying leads to falls and to getting even more lost. Sit down, have a drink and something to eat, and calm down. If you have told someone your route and when you will be back – which you always should – rescuers will start searching where you were expected to be. Unless you are in immediate danger, or you are completely sure of the way back, it is usually best to stay where you are.","2. Call for help.\nIf you have a phone with signal, call 999 or 112 and ask for the police, then mountain rescue. Give your location as accurately as you can; many phones can show your grid reference or coordinates. If there is no signal, try sending a text, which sometimes gets through when calls do not. Keep your phone warm and switch off apps to save the battery. The international distress signal is six blasts on a whistle, or six flashes of a torch, repeated every minute.","3. Stay warm.\nIn Britain, the biggest danger is not wild animals or hunger, but cold. Hypothermia, when the body’s core temperature falls too low, can happen even in summer if you are wet and exposed to the wind. Put on every layer you have, including a hat and gloves; a lot of body heat is lost from the head. Get out of the wind behind a wall, a large rock, a bank or dense trees. Sit on your rucksack, a foam mat or a pile of dry bracken, because the cold ground draws heat from your body faster than the air does. If you are in a group, huddle together to share warmth.","4. Make a shelter.\nIf you have a survival bag – a large, strong plastic bag, usually bright orange – get inside it, leaving your face out. It will keep off wind and rain. If not, you can build a simple shelter by leaning branches against a fallen tree or low branch to make a lean-to, and covering it with leaves, bracken or pine branches. Make it small; a small space is easier to keep warm.","5. Be seen and heard.\nRescuers may search with helicopters, drones and dogs. Wear or lay out anything brightly coloured. In daylight, a mirror or the screen of your phone can flash sunlight towards a helicopter. Shout or whistle at regular intervals, then listen for a reply.","6. Drink, but be careful.\nYou can survive for weeks without food but only a few days without water. Ration what you have. Water from fast-flowing streams in the hills is safer than still water, but it may still contain germs; boil it if you possibly can.","7. Keep your spirits up.\nA night outside can feel very long. Talk, sing, plan what you will eat when you get home. Staying positive genuinely helps survival.","Above all, remember that the best survival skill is preparation. Check the weather forecast. Take a map and compass and know how to use them. Carry warm and waterproof clothing, food, water, a torch, a whistle, a fully charged phone and a small first-aid kit. And always tell someone where you are going."],"glossary":{"guidance":"Advice or information about how to do something.","accurately":"Correctly and precisely.","coordinates":"Numbers that show an exact position on a map.","distress":"Danger or serious trouble.","hypothermia":"A dangerous drop in body temperature.","exposed":"Not protected from wind and weather.","bracken":"A large fern that grows on hills and in woods.","intervals":"Gaps of time between events.","ration":"To limit the amount used so that it lasts."},"questions":[{"q":"What is the first thing you should do when you realise you are lost?","o":["Run to find the path","Stop walking, sit down and think","Climb a tree","Shout as loudly as possible"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The moment you realise you are lost, stop walking.”"},{"q":"Why is it usually best to stay where you are?","o":["Rescuers will search where you were expected to be, and moving can make things worse","It is more comfortable","You will find food","The weather will improve"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"If someone knows your route, rescuers search there; hurrying causes falls and confusion."},{"q":"What is the international distress signal?","o":["Three whistle blasts","Six blasts on a whistle or six torch flashes, repeated every minute","One long shout","Waving both arms"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Six blasts on a whistle, or six flashes of a torch, repeated every minute.”"},{"q":"According to the guide, what is the biggest danger in Britain?","o":["Wild animals","Hunger","Cold and hypothermia","Getting bored"],"a":2,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The biggest danger is not wild animals or hunger, but cold.”"},{"q":"Why should you sit on your rucksack or bracken rather than the ground?","o":["It is softer","The cold ground draws heat from your body faster than the air","To stay dry from rain","To be seen more easily"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The cold ground draws heat from your body faster than the air does.”"},{"q":"Why is this text organised as numbered steps with headings?","o":["To make the advice clear and easy to follow, even in a stressful situation","Because it is a poem","To tell a story","To make it longer"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"Instructions use numbered headings so readers can quickly find and follow each step."}]},{"id":"y197","title":"Speech: The Planet We Will Inherit","kind":"Speech","theme":"nature","text":["Good morning. I want to begin by asking you to imagine something. Imagine it is the year 2075. You are about sixty years old. Your grandchildren are sitting with you, and one of them asks: ‘When you were young, and scientists told everyone the planet was warming, what did you do?’","What will you say?","I ask because we are the generation that will live with the consequences of the decisions being made right now. The adults in charge today will mostly not be here in 2075. We will. The world of 2075 is our world. So I believe we have the right – and the responsibility – to speak up about it.","Let me be clear about the facts, because facts matter. The world is warming because of the greenhouse gases released by burning coal, oil and gas, and by cutting down forests. Scientists from almost every country agree on this. We are already seeing the effects: hotter summers, more extreme floods and droughts, melting ice, rising seas and wildlife struggling to adapt. In 2022, temperatures in Britain passed forty degrees for the first time on record. These are not predictions for the far future. They are happening now.","But I did not come here to make you feel frightened or hopeless. Fear on its own makes people freeze. I came to tell you that there is a great deal of reason for hope – and a great deal we can do.","Look at what has already changed. Twenty years ago, solar and wind power were expensive and rare. Today, they are among the cheapest ways to make electricity in the world. In 2024, Britain closed its last coal power station, which would have seemed impossible a generation ago. Electric cars are becoming normal. Scientists are developing new ways to store energy, to grow food and to capture carbon. Every one of these changes happened because people decided that things could be different.","So what can we do, as students? Some things are small but they add up. We can waste less food, which is responsible for a huge share of emissions. We can repair things instead of throwing them away, buy fewer clothes, walk, cycle or take the bus when we can, and switch off what we are not using. Here at school, we can ask for more recycling, for solar panels on the roof, for a school garden and for plant-based options in the canteen. We can plant trees. We can protect the green spaces that we have.","But individual actions are not enough on their own. The biggest changes need governments and businesses to act. And they listen when enough people speak up. You are not too young to write to your MP, to sign a petition, to join an eco-club or to talk to your family about what they could change. Young people around the world have already shown that their voices can be heard.","Change is hard. It takes effort and patience. Sometimes it will feel as though nothing you do makes any difference. But think again about that grandchild in 2075. I want to be able to look them in the eye and say: ‘We knew. And we did something.’","Let us be the generation that turned it around. Thank you."],"glossary":{"consequences":"Results or effects of an action.","responsibility":"A duty to deal with something.","predictions":"Statements about what will happen in the future.","emissions":"Gases released into the air.","petition":"A written request signed by many people asking for change.","individual":"Done by one person."},"questions":[{"q":"How does the speaker open the speech?","o":["With a statistic","By asking the audience to imagine being asked about their actions by a grandchild in 2075","With a joke","With a quotation from a scientist"],"a":1,"skill":"Structure","why":"The speech begins with an imagined future conversation."},{"q":"Why does the speaker say “The world of 2075 is our world”?","o":["Young people today will be living with the results of decisions made now","The speaker owns the world","Adults will live longer","2075 is a long way off"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Today’s adults mostly will not be there; today’s students will."},{"q":"Why does the speaker say they did not come to make people frightened?","o":["Because fear on its own makes people freeze rather than act","Because there is nothing to fear","Because they are frightened","Because the facts are wrong"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Fear on its own makes people freeze.”"},{"q":"What evidence does the speaker give for hope?","o":["Cheaper solar and wind power, the closure of Britain’s last coal power station and electric cars","Colder winters","Fewer scientists","Less recycling"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"These examples appear in the sixth paragraph."},{"q":"Why does the speaker say individual actions are not enough on their own?","o":["The biggest changes need governments and businesses to act","Individuals cannot recycle","Students are too young","Nothing can be done"],"a":0,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“The biggest changes need governments and businesses to act.”"},{"q":"How does the ending link back to the beginning?","o":["It returns to the grandchild in 2075, giving the answer the speaker hopes to give","It repeats the statistics","It introduces a new topic","It asks for money"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"The circular structure gives a powerful, memorable conclusion."}]},{"id":"y198","title":"Wonderful Things: The Tomb of Tutankhamun","kind":"History","theme":"desert","text":["On 26 November 1922, in the Valley of the Kings in Egypt, an English archaeologist named Howard Carter stood in front of a sealed doorway. He had been searching for the tomb of an almost unknown pharaoh for five years, and his wealthy sponsor, Lord Carnarvon, had nearly given up. Now Carnarvon and his daughter, Lady Evelyn, stood behind him as Carter made a small hole in the top corner of the door, pushed a candle through and peered inside. Hot air escaped from the chamber, making the flame flicker. Then, as his eyes grew used to the light, details began to emerge from the darkness: strange animals, statues and gold – everywhere the glint of gold.","‘Can you see anything?’ asked Carnarvon anxiously.","‘Yes,’ Carter replied. ‘Wonderful things.’","The tomb belonged to Tutankhamun, a pharaoh who had become king around 1332 BC, at the age of about nine, and died about ten years later, aged around eighteen or nineteen. He was not a particularly important king in his own time. But because his tomb was small and hidden beneath the rubble from a later tomb, it had escaped the robbers who had looted almost every other royal tomb in the valley. It was the most complete royal burial ever found in Egypt.","Inside were more than 5,000 objects, crammed into four rooms: gilded couches shaped like animals, chariots, thrones, jewellery, clothes, weapons, board games, model boats, musical instruments, jars of food and wine, and even 130 walking sticks. There were also two small coffins containing the mummified bodies of the king’s stillborn daughters. Every object was carefully recorded, photographed and drawn before it was moved, and the work of clearing the tomb took ten years.","In the burial chamber stood four great golden shrines, fitted one inside another like nesting boxes. Inside the last of them was a stone sarcophagus, and inside that were three coffins, also nested. The innermost was made of solid gold, weighing about 110 kilograms. Inside lay the mummy of Tutankhamun, and over his head and shoulders was the famous funeral mask, made of more than ten kilograms of gold, inlaid with blue lapis lazuli, coloured glass and precious stones. It has become one of the most recognisable objects in the world.","In April 1923, Lord Carnarvon died in Cairo of blood poisoning after an infected mosquito bite. Newspapers, eager for a sensational story, claimed that he had been killed by the ‘curse of the pharaoh’, a punishment for disturbing the boy king’s rest. The legend spread quickly, and stories of a curse still appear today. But there is no evidence for it: Howard Carter himself, who spent ten years working in the tomb, lived until 1939.","Modern scientific studies, including CT scans and DNA tests, have taught us more about Tutankhamun. He seems to have had a club foot and other health problems, and walked with a stick, which may explain all those walking sticks in his tomb. Several of his relatives’ mummies have been identified. Scientists have also found evidence that he had malaria. How he died, though, is still debated: possibly from an infection after a broken leg, combined with malaria.","Today, most of the treasures are displayed in the Grand Egyptian Museum near the pyramids of Giza. Tutankhamun himself still lies in his tomb in the Valley of the Kings, the only pharaoh to remain where he was buried – a boy king who was almost forgotten, and who has become the most famous pharaoh of all."],"glossary":{"sponsor":"A person who pays for a project.","peered":"Looked closely, as if it was hard to see.","looted":"Robbed, especially of valuable things.","gilded":"Covered with a thin layer of gold.","stillborn":"Born dead.","shrines":"Holy places or containers for sacred things.","sarcophagus":"A large stone coffin.","inlaid":"Decorated with pieces of another material set into the surface.","sensational":"Causing great excitement or shock."},"questions":[{"q":"What did Carter famously say when he first looked into the tomb?","o":["“It’s empty!”","“Wonderful things.”","“Gold everywhere!”","“We are too late.”"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“Yes,” Carter replied. “Wonderful things.”"},{"q":"Why had Tutankhamun’s tomb escaped robbers?","o":["It was guarded by soldiers","It was small and hidden beneath rubble from a later tomb","It was cursed","It was in a different country"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"His tomb “was small and hidden beneath the rubble from a later tomb.”"},{"q":"Why did clearing the tomb take ten years?","o":["Every object was carefully recorded, photographed and drawn before being moved","The workers were lazy","There was a war","The tomb was flooded"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Careful recording of over 5,000 objects took a long time."},{"q":"How does the writer describe the arrangement of the shrines and coffins?","o":["Fitted one inside another, like nesting boxes","Side by side in a row","Piled up randomly","Hidden in different rooms"],"a":0,"skill":"Language","why":"They were “fitted one inside another like nesting boxes.”"},{"q":"What does the writer say about the ‘curse of the pharaoh’?","o":["It is proven","There is no evidence for it – Carter lived until 1939","It killed Carter","It is mentioned in hieroglyphs"],"a":1,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"“There is no evidence for it: Howard Carter himself … lived until 1939.”"},{"q":"What might explain the 130 walking sticks found in the tomb?","o":["He collected them as a hobby","He had a club foot and health problems and walked with a stick","They were weapons","They were gifts for guests"],"a":1,"skill":"Inference","why":"Scans show he “walked with a stick, which may explain all those walking sticks.”"}]},{"id":"y199","title":"The Fisherman and the Genie","kind":"Folk tale","theme":"sea","author":"Retold from The Thousand and One Nights","text":["There was once a poor old fisherman who lived with his wife and three children by the sea. Every morning, he cast his net exactly four times, never more, and lived on whatever he caught. One day, he cast his net and pulled up a dead donkey. The second time, he pulled up a basket full of mud and stones. The third time, nothing but broken pots and shells. He raised his eyes to the sky, cast his net for the fourth and last time, and felt it grow heavy. When he hauled it in, he found a large copper jar, its neck sealed with lead and stamped with the seal of the great King Solomon.","‘I will sell this in the market,’ thought the fisherman, ‘for at least ten pieces of gold.’ But first he wanted to see what was inside. He took out his knife and worked the lead seal loose. He turned the jar upside down and shook it. Nothing came out. Then, from the mouth of the jar, rose a thin wisp of smoke. It rose and spread until it reached the clouds and covered the beach, and then it gathered itself together and became a genie, so tall that his head was in the sky and his feet on the sand. His eyes burned like lamps and his voice was like thunder.","‘Choose,’ roared the genie, ‘how you will die!’","‘Die?’ said the fisherman, trembling. ‘But I have just set you free! Why would you kill me?’","The genie explained. Long ago, he had rebelled against King Solomon, who had imprisoned him in the jar and thrown it into the sea. For the first hundred years, the genie had promised that whoever freed him would be made rich for ever. Nobody came. For the second hundred years, he had promised to open the treasures of the earth to his rescuer. Nobody came. For the next four hundred years, he had promised to grant his rescuer three wishes. Still nobody came. ‘Then I became furious,’ said the genie, ‘and I swore that whoever freed me, I would kill. You freed me. So choose your death.’","The fisherman thought quickly. He was poor and old, but he was not stupid.","‘Very well,’ he said. ‘But before I die, please answer one question, by the great name of Solomon. I do not believe you were ever in that jar. Look at it! It could hardly hold one of your hands or one of your feet. How could your whole body fit inside?’","‘You do not believe I was in the jar?’ roared the genie.","‘I will never believe it,’ said the fisherman, ‘unless I see it with my own eyes.’","The genie, insulted, shook himself, turned back into smoke and began to pour himself into the jar, until every last wisp had disappeared inside. ‘There!’ he called from within. ‘Now do you believe me?’","Quick as lightning, the fisherman snatched up the lead seal and jammed it back on the neck of the jar.","‘Choose,’ he said, ‘how you will die! I shall throw you back into the sea, and I shall build a house on this beach and warn every fisherman never to pull you out again.’","The genie begged and pleaded, promising great riches if the fisherman would only let him go. The story goes on, through many twists and further tales, until the genie keeps his word and the fisherman becomes wealthy. But it is that first moment that people remember: a poor old man, facing a giant, who saved himself not by strength but by his wits."],"glossary":{"cast":"To throw a fishing net or line.","hauled":"Pulled with effort.","seal":"A mark pressed into wax or metal to show who owns or closed something.","wisp":"A thin streak of smoke or hair.","genie":"A magical spirit from Arabian stories.","rebelled":"Fought against someone in authority.","imprisoned":"Locked up.","insulted":"Made to feel offended.","wits":"Cleverness and quick thinking."},"questions":[{"q":"How many times did the fisherman cast his net each day?","o":["Once","Three times","Four times","As many as he liked"],"a":2,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“He cast his net exactly four times, never more.”"},{"q":"What does the repeated pattern of the first three catches add to the story?","o":["It builds anticipation before the important fourth catch","It shows he was a bad fisherman","It describes the weather","It introduces the genie early"],"a":0,"skill":"Structure","why":"Three disappointing catches build suspense for the jar."},{"q":"Why did the genie want to kill the person who freed him?","o":["He was hungry","After centuries of waiting, his gratitude had turned to fury and he swore to kill his rescuer","The fisherman insulted him first","King Solomon ordered it"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"He became furious and “swore that whoever freed me, I would kill.”"},{"q":"How does the fisherman trick the genie?","o":["He pretends not to believe the genie fitted in the jar, so the genie goes back in to prove it","He runs away","He fights the genie","He asks for three wishes"],"a":0,"skill":"Summary","why":"Insulted, the genie pours himself back into the jar, and the fisherman seals it."},{"q":"Why is the genie’s pride his weakness?","o":["He cannot bear to be doubted, so he walks into the trap","He is too tall","He is afraid of water","He forgets the seal"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"Feeling insulted, he proves his point and gets trapped."},{"q":"What is the main message of this tale?","o":["Cleverness can defeat great strength","Never go fishing","Genies are always kind","Always open sealed jars"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"He “saved himself not by strength but by his wits.”"}]},{"id":"y200","title":"Letter to My Future Self","kind":"Letter","theme":"books","text":["Bedroom desk, 11:15 p.m.\nThe last night of Year 7","Dear Future Me,","Hello. It’s me – you, I mean – from the end of Year 7. I don’t know how old you are when you’re reading this. Maybe you’re in Year 11, stressing about exams. Maybe you’re twenty-five and you’ve found this in a box when you were moving house. Maybe you’re really old and you’ve forgotten you ever wrote it. Whoever you are, I wanted to tell you some things before I forget them myself.","This year was a lot. In September, I was so nervous I didn’t sleep the night before school. I thought everyone would already know each other and that I would be the only person who was lost. It turned out that almost everyone was lost. On the first day I walked into a Year 10 science lesson by mistake and sat down before I realised. Everybody laughed, and I wanted to disappear. Now it’s one of my favourite stories.","Here are some things I learned this year, in no particular order.","Things that seem like the end of the world usually aren’t. I failed my first maths test (32%) and cried in the toilets. Mr Osei told me that a test is just a map that shows you where you still need to go. I got 78% in the summer exam. Please remember that, Future Me, next time something goes wrong.","The people who end up being your friends are not always the ones you expect. On the first day, I was sure I would be friends with the loud, funny group at the back. Instead, my best friend is Hana, who I sat next to in English because there were no other seats. She is quiet and draws dragons in the margins of everything and knows more about volcanoes than our geography teacher. I hope we are still friends. If we’re not, please message her.","Reading is good for you. I didn’t used to like reading, but this year I read every day, sometimes only for ten minutes, and somewhere along the way I started to enjoy it. I read about Shackleton and Malala and the Mary Celeste and a girl who could talk to machines. I think I know more about the world than I did, and I definitely know more words. (‘Resilient’. ‘Perseverance’. ‘Ambiguous’. See?)","Being brave doesn’t mean not being scared. I was terrified before I spoke in assembly about the school council. My hands were shaking so much that my notes rattled. I did it anyway. That’s what brave is, I think: being scared and doing it anyway.","Here are some questions for you. Did you keep playing the guitar? Did you ever go to Japan? Are you kind to people? Are you still curious? I hope you are. I hope you haven’t become someone who thinks they know everything, because the best bits of this year were all the moments when I realised I didn’t.","I don’t know what happens next. Year 8, I suppose, and then everything after that. But I wanted you to know that, at the end of Year 7, I was mostly happy, a bit tired, slightly better at maths and very proud of myself. I hope you are proud of me too.","Look after yourself. Look after Mum. And go to bed earlier than I do.","Love from,\nYou (aged 12 and three-quarters)","P.S. If you’ve lost the stuffed penguin, it’s in the box under the bed. You’re welcome."],"glossary":{"stressing":"Feeling worried and under pressure (informal).","margins":"The blank spaces around the edge of a page.","resilient":"Able to recover quickly from difficulties.","perseverance":"Continuing to try even when something is difficult.","ambiguous":"Able to be understood in more than one way.","curious":"Wanting to know or learn about things."},"questions":[{"q":"Who is the letter addressed to?","o":["A teacher","The writer’s future self","Hana","Mum"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"It begins “Dear Future Me.”"},{"q":"What lesson did the writer learn from failing the first maths test?","o":["Maths is impossible","Things that seem like the end of the world usually aren’t; a test shows where you still need to go","Never take tests","Cry more often"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"Mr Osei called a test “a map that shows you where you still need to go,” and the writer improved to 78%."},{"q":"How did the writer become friends with Hana?","o":["They were in the same club","They sat together in English because there were no other seats","They lived next door","They met on holiday"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“I sat next to [her] in English because there were no other seats.”"},{"q":"What does the writer think being brave means?","o":["Never feeling scared","Being scared and doing it anyway","Speaking loudly","Winning arguments"],"a":1,"skill":"Retrieval","why":"“That’s what brave is, I think: being scared and doing it anyway.”"},{"q":"Why does the writer hope Future Me hasn’t become someone who “knows everything”?","o":["Because the best moments came from realising how much there was still to learn","Because clever people are boring","Because knowledge is dangerous","Because teachers know everything"],"a":0,"skill":"Inference","why":"“The best bits of this year were all the moments when I realised I didn’t.”"},{"q":"What is the overall tone of the letter?","o":["Warm, reflective and hopeful, with touches of humour","Angry and bitter","Formal and serious","Frightened"],"a":0,"skill":"Evaluation","why":"It reflects on growth, ends proudly and includes jokes like the P.S. about the penguin."}]}];
